[{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"We are due an explanation about the time that strikes the most controversial thoughts of our history. Most of the time, though, it is our own job to choose a side to take this information from. And there is a right side. Just like there is a left one, up one and down one. And any other side you want to add on the extra planes you wish to add to the equation. It still ends up in the same spot of humans reaching their mental capacity to play trust games.\n\nThat's why it’s important to keep track of news and stories around your own time. That way you can truly know who you are in it. There were nine states. Unus was the state responsible for force action. Culo was moving material around. Tribus made sure the packages were preserved, standardized and reliable. Quite a bit of work, but it also made them most valued. Quattor grew the crops that held the entire chain of life on its shoulders. Quintus were a bunch of asses. They made sure animals got their rights.\n\nAnd so on.\n\nSure there is no need for us to go any further. Everyone was aware of that inferior decimal system. Binary can be understood by all given enough time to analyze, but sadly mortals don’t have that luxury. Hope everyone is aware of time dilations by now.\n\nKara never expected to fall into the trap of the shapeshifters curse. It always meant only bad news for the heroes in question. Not like werewolves ever had easy and noble lives in folk stories. Not like Kara ever wanted to be part of the world of courtly manners. She didn’t mind sharing the seat with something on the other side of the spectrum. By now it became her second nature that was empowered and kept in check by her friends. Mostly because they were decent enough to look after her, just like she looked after them. Mostly strangers whom she knew better than anyone else.","n":1},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"- So you say we just walk into town wearing this and pretend to be from Culo? Have you ever seen two of them ever walking in one direction? - Kara crossed hands on her chest looking at the clothing that Rein brought over from his little visit to the town of Venilium.\n\n- Kara, it’s a Culo city, - Rein looked slightly annoyed as he gave Kara her cloak, - Can you at least listen to what we say while you stand around?\n\n- Still, I would much rather not be seen in an awkward formation that we are, - Kara briefly looked at Fezzik. Man would definitely stand out in Septum being so red in skin tone, leave alone the fact he had demon horns. He recognized the glance, but for sure Fezzik could understand where Kara was coming from. Two upper teens isn’t a rare sight, add a demon by their side and people would start noticing.\n\nIt was obvious that Rein wasn’t going to support the rebellion, however, in times of distress he always turned to communist ideals. You work how you can, but everyone will get the same treatment and opportunities. Toham had never brought it up with him. Mostly because they never talked after the tournament. And before that it seemed like a nervous tick. All heroes had those in some form or another.\n\nIt wasn’t obvious that something would change him so much, that he would actually consider it. Leave alone join it. And, worst of all, turn it into something else completely. Novem really had their heads scratched over this puzzle lately. Their intelligence group was trying really hard to play this brilliantly stupid. As always, better than trying to explain the truth.\n\nThe earth shook. Kara nervously turned around in the direction of menacing rumble only to find that woods barely provided a clear view to something that big. It didn’t feel like a bomb. Sure didn’t sound like one either. Finally the wind caught up.","n":2},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"- You think it’s Floats? - Kara would raise an eyebrow, but the light blue man in question was always full of surprises. He changed quite a bit after she accidentally bit him. Or maybe it was the fact that they finally got rid of the cameras. The disturbance ring on the index finger already felt natural, though the fear of taking it off created a weird itch that would bother her every now and then.\n\nIt felt weird to be this free - too easy. Kara barely knew anything about this hero tournament and now, by the craziest twist of fate, she was a rebellious runaway. They were probably quite ridiculously famous. The betting war was getting quite heated. Not every day a billion dollar project gets lost in space.\n\n- You really should stop blaming him for everything, you are not so different, - Fezzik always sounded too soft for the heavy words that he delivered. Always caught Kara by surprise. With Rein she could at least banter, Fezzik was a bit too old for that. Lately age meant something extremely different.\n\n- Tallie and Carp are with him, I doubt it’s anything they can’t handle, - Rein shrugged with a chilling cold calm. Probably the only reason why Kara didn’t drop everything to run against the wind to check on her teammates. Friends. They were friends now, they were no longer just a team. What a bliss. Tallie and Carp could probably survive anything as long as they didn’t get involved in some stupid plan that Fezzik or Rein came up with. They never complained, so Kara usually did it for them.\n\nOne couldn’t imagine how it feels like to lose your home, family and traditions. To be a relic of the time that is so well in the past that you are the last soul that remains alive to carry it on. Any attempt to understand would be shortsighted and shallow and any survivor would struggle to communicate their loss. Warrithri was the greatest goblin clan to have ever pick up a hammer. None will or would have reached what they have had. Time has changed. Now they only had a name.","n":3},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"The greatest warriors that ever stood this short, so dangerous that all nine states trembled in their shadow. System collapsed due to sheer fear. In a single unified motion they were wiped clean, leaving only children to carry on the fleeting legacy. They barely remembered their language by the third wipeout. Now one can only imagine what has been lost in time.\n\n- Are you sure they are going to find this? - Kara was standing over the box of hoods of Culo colors.\n\n- I don’t think it really matters. Not everyone in Culo wears a uniform, - Fezzik sounded quite informed on the matter. It was still quite unnerving how much the man knew about traditions of other states. Kara would have never expected her parents to be friends with someone like that. Leaving alone the fact that he was working as a simple engineer at a coal mine. It just didn’t feel right, but, again, it wasn’t like she knew much about their job. They worked at an office.\n\nShe would ask about how common fish masks were in Culo, but that seemed rude. It wasn’t like there was anything to be done about it. Goblins were weird creatures with weird tendencies to pick up a single trait and live by it, aging like an old spirit. The fact that Carp was mimicking a fish that rhymes was odd, but not out of the ordinary. It was a bit more difficult with Tallie and her tail, but Kara didn’t know this much about history and didn’t expect anyone else to.\n\nBehind the curtains it isn’t always the truth that is being spoken. Audience always expects it to be clean and professional, but even a light man had a bad lamp day. Actors come off the stage to be greeted by a supporting crew and an angered producer that smelled a wind of misconduct on a stageday. Not everyone gets to speak out their truths and the spotlight favors the big shots. Not like everyone had the guts for it.","n":4},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"Alfonse didn’t have time to hear everyone, so he chose people who would choose for him. Reported. People who would be reporting to him. When everyone is equal in the pool of candidates it didn’t really matter who it was, so why not choose the ones that you could at least trust to be diligent.\n\nAnd then there were friends. Like a gut feeling that tells you to never cross their lines and yet those too often come too close. Sometimes you live for these fights, sometimes they hurt more than they should have. You just never expect them to wish you death.\n\nThey left the box with clothing in the forest. Kara was wondering if one day some traveler would find it and get in trouble for trying to sell it. Probably not in Culo, but Septum would definitely clap the shackles on the criminal for propaganda of an illegal style. It just felt wrong to leave it there. Kara couldn’t throw away thoughts about cold winters and how useful these cloaks would be back in the mountains. Back in the garden, way back in the past.\n\nKara never trusted the words of the witch. Too many threats for someone proposing salvation. Hero tournament was a predicament to be in, but being in one wasn’t a criminal offense. Trying to run away from it technically was. Why would the witch want to take this much risk to get them out of the sight of the government? Maybe she was just pretending to help and Septum heroes remained in the training loop.\n\n- So no one feels weird about Ember setting this up for us? - Kara wanted to make the most of the short time of forest privacy that was left. The trial routes were more and more prominent as they neared the city.\n\n- Well, not really much we can do about it besides staying vigilant, - Rein sounded calm, but Kara felt the freezing note of pure displeasure. Something that she wished she didn’t understand.","n":5},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"- Do you really think it’s him? - Kara always stood on the skeptical stretch of the spectrum. Once the world turned to magic all bids were up. There was really nothing impossible and the man who claimed to be Reins father might as well be a part of the training simulation. Why else would he appear with a witch? Hats off to Ember, he turned out to be a great trainer. If, of course, it was all part of training. If it wasn't, Kara was terrified of what was going on.\n\n- Can as well be, - Rein didn’t hesitate to answer, - He is dead to me either way, - said and cut so clean that Kara felt shivers in her back bone. His confidence and determination sometimes majorly conflicted with some higher familial morals.\n\n- Hey, maybe give your old man a chance, - Fezzik tried to reason, but it was quickly obvious that there was no point to it, - You know what? Whatever it is, I don’t think we should play into their hands. I don’t trust this either, - It was unsettling that Fezzik didn’t act as assured. Conrad Valentine was his old friend, but who knows what could happen to a man believed to be dead for five years. For some reason the ghastly presence of a shadow of this man, that used to be a father and a friend, chained their perception of reality to the line of hope that could as well be false. Funny how exactly none of this concerned Kara, beside a snarling feeling of deja vu.\n\n- Then why are we getting in this boat? I don’t mind staying in the mountains, - Kara patted on her potato parcel, - I have all I need right here, I’m sure Tallie would hunt for herself no problem, - she smiled and longingly looked back into the darkness of the forest.\n\n- Isn’t it exactly how they caught you the first time? - Fezzik was just recalling something, but Kara felt the spear in her heart. There was nowhere to return to. It felt weird to be so deeply uprooted that existence was flowing down the steam simply because there was no difference in choosing the direction.","n":6},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"Sometimes you wonder who would ever agree to cast a monument to themself in their lifetime. Such a futile attempt at touching eternity. Who knows how many times it would change the name in translation to the frame of time. All to be destroyed in natural rotation.\n\nAnd yet Toham made sure every town in Septum had a statue of Camerarius somewhere near the center. Somehow it was a different sentiment with someone who lived just to see himself cast in stone. Senseless desire with a single simple satisfaction. Kara admired how little Floats really cared about their future or motive. He just revered the company of his stone self more than anything else in the world. Toham understood exactly what he felt.\n\n- Eh, it would take them another five years, - Kara smiled and adjusted the ax on her shoulder. Stupidly, if she really thought hard enough, there was no clear reason she could see to stay. The group she found herself in consisted of people who saved her life countless times and vice versa, that’s about all. She didn’t like anything in particular about any of them and yet something else held them together. Something that too often felt like a bad habit.\n\n- Kara, you seriously think you could hide? - Rein never failed to crush her dreams. Understandable, considering the fact that most of them never aligned with his plans. Equally stupid ones, just with extra steps.\n\n- Better than staying on the course in plain sight, - Kara uncomfortably glanced towards the gates and the guards, - You see any gaps?\n\nFezzik lightly touched her on the shoulder and pointed towards the drainage fence on the far side. Small, but you could still see into the walls through it. It would only make sense for the government to protect Culo ports with just a bit more caution, but heroes were created to go around the fences that would stop normal people. It was still quite concerning that Ember was encouraging Septum heroes to cross the law.","n":7},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"Warrithri territories fell into the Unus jurisdiction. One could as well have said that they were never there as the state of forced action never held its territory true. None of the great ruins retained their names and simply turned into slums ruled by the nation that hated goblins with such passion, that you couldn’t even invite your goblin friends for a friendly walk in a park.\n\nWhen Toham first learned where the road had taken her, she almost burned the city to a crisp. Funny how a simple sigil carved in an ancient stone can pierce a heart and open a wound that your friend shared with you.\n\nDisgusting and ungrateful. Yet they were still living people with families and children they had to protect along with their own legacy and honor to build. Nothing new for the strategy of surviving minds there. Same old turnover of power just with new toys to keep their newly found peace.\n\nBut, that wasn’t what stopped Kara. It was simply not her bone to pick.\n\n- That one is too close. They are going to see us, - Kara skeptically glanced over the opening, but the only other option was the seagate on the other side, - I hope you are ready to run.\n\nShe stretched her muscles. It wasn’t her first time running through the walls, it was her second.\n\nFor some reason she didn’t doubt she could do it without a flaw. She just knew that it wasn’t a quiet process. Their best guess was that the guards will see the smoke and assume that kids are playing with flares.\n\nStretched guess, but no one really expected to see running heroes. Not many of them knew that magic was real. No wonder, not many survived for long after the first introductions. They truly say: one should never meet their heroes.","n":8},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"- All guards, stay put! - the loud voice filled the air and Kara felt a defeated shiver. She wasn’t a particular fan of running towards the danger hoping to have just enough time to slip. Into the woods sounded more to her style.\n\n- Can you tell me, once again, why you let Floats just float away like this? - Kara snarled quietly observing the ruckus by the gates. Guards formed a squad and left in the direction of the rumbling.\n\n- Whatever he did, it seems to have bought us a ticket in, - Fezzik gestured towards a drainage fence with a pleading look. It was weird to have an hourly timer on anything. It's almost like they knew all of the schedules in this town. Uncannily, Kara knew all of them too.\n\nIt just made perfect sense. It will take at least two hours before the bureaucrats give a green light to shutdown the port. In fact, most of the boats wished to be in the sea after an earthquake of this magnitude. If they closed, it would be a death sentence. Well, it could have been if it was real. However, again, not many would know the difference. In cases of emergency people are prone to listen to plausible advice and would trust anyone with a confident enough of a voice.\n\n- So you say what? You want to bring him here? - Toham really didn’t like the sound of this prospect. It was extremely obvious what was going on, but there was nothing much she could do to avoid it. She could, but it would involve a lot of unnecessary casualties. First of all, she would risk the safety of her garden. Well, first and last of all. There seemed to be no other reasons.\n\n- That was the most popular request, - Eve sounded as soft as ever. Popular vote, one of the most convenient and yet the most restrictive tools of control. Good to have under your belt, bad to be under its direct influence. This, of course, mostly scared heroes. And Eve was a retired one. The most beautiful woman with the most delicate sense of fashion. She was the one who created the show, for better or for worse.","n":9},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"- You realize how disgusting this idea is. For me, - Toham didn’t know what could have been worse. Morally speaking, she could have run away with her potatoes and bought another 5 years or so until they caught up. However, that's just delaying the obvious. There was no real way out of this wormhole.\n\n- It’s just a cat, - Eve never sounded colder. And yet, it wasn’t out of character. She did it all the time. Like a mantra. It only made sense.\n\n- You personally trained him to wear Pink Glasses! - Kara desperately gestured a hand to a perfectly fluffy orange tom cat gingerly wearing his tiny pink glasses. Toham never even fed it, but it still followed her to the garden each day. She couldn’t bring herself to shoo it away. She absolutely should have.\n\n- So what, animal rights are important. We have to support biodiversity groups, - rationality that Eve used was illegal in most states. But, again, Septum had a little too much power by now. And everyone knows that proverb.\n\nKara nodded and rushed forwards, flaming up her fire spirit. Fluffer Huggins appeared for a split second, barely enough for him to touch the grass with his paws. Rein and Fezzik grabbed dancing flames and the trio disappeared into thin air.\n\nThey appeared on the other side of the wall. Huggins softly purred and bowed before disappearing again.\n\n- Can I ask you why this thing can teleport, but you, somehow, struggle to? - Fezzik was being unreasonable. By no means Kara would have wanted to navigate the dark space of nothingness by herself. Seemed like a tiresome and absolutely terrifying activity.\n\n- Yeah, the weirdest thing, right? - Kara raised an eyebrow and rolled her eyes, - So we are going to the docks?","n":10},{"chapter":"Prologue","text":"It was always fun to host Alfonse. He just radiated some sort of an air that made social interactions just a smudge less painful. Enough to make them enjoyable. However sometimes it seemed like he was quite distracted. Sitting softly in grievance, like something is going really wrong. Toham hated to know what it meant.\n\n- So, the teleportation devices are getting hijacked and the first person you come to is me? - she didn’t believe her ears. If he wasn’t suspecting her of causing it, which she absolutely couldn’t possibly have had, then he probably wanted her to investigate it. But that would jeopardize the garden.\n\n- It was either you or Floats, you know how he’s been lately, - The comparison made her shiver.\n\n- I have a territory with a perfect record, you realize this would compromise everything, - Toham tensed for a second. She specifically had her rights within the territory she plotted out. Exactly the amount to keep the town and the garden a perfectly safe and closed area.\n\n- Ever wanted to expand? - Fezzik looked like it was an obvious transition. Toham hated these kinds of questions. She already had what she wanted. Why would she bother about anything else?\n\n- No, but who do you think is behind it? - It was excruciating to ask this question. Too many things could go wrong after this point.","n":11},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"The town seemed agitated, but mostly because of the amount of people on the streets. The earthquake forced people onto already busy roads of Leabrada and happy, hot headed citizens started their little break day. Some were already filling the bars and chanting some local tunes. Culo and Octingetti were close friends. People here trusted their structures, but didn’t mind resting in memory of safety traditions. Nature had a tendency to strike compulsively and definitely.\n\n- Wow, never thought I would ever see this many people, - Kara was absolutely astounded. None of these people could have possibly given them one more look. No one really wanted to stare impolitely. Too much was happening already.\n\n- Ha-ha, right? - Fezzik ironically chuckled and maneuvered through the crowd in the older district of the city. Buildings here were aged, but barely anything looked any different in demeanor. Even the wooden huts stood tall and unhinged.\n\nThey stopped at the oldest boat saloon, if you would trust the subtitle under its name, which looked magnificent in its maturity. Old polish shone in the sun with a viscous glow making the sign “Sea Sugar Ships” look like lollipop candy. Kara couldn’t help but think that it was rendered into existence for the sole purpose of them finding it. Every other shop looked too obviously generic.\n\n- One would think that you might want a little bit more clandestine location for a place that takes you to a Tenth state, - Kara felt like the moment wouldn’t be complete without a skeptical comment. She would have been bitter, but, for once, she actually didn’t mind the journey ahead.\n\n- Pretty sure these sailors do all kinds of jobs, coverup must be a piece of cake as long as they come back with some fish or cargo, - Rein seemed unbothered. Nothing new.","n":12},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"They say that best friends are the ones that have a special manner of talking exclusively to you. Well, the same extends to the best friend groups. By Tohams latest definition, at least. She didn’t get a murderous side of the spectrum of such relationships. It wasn’t really special to wish her death. There was a certain resolve in that.\n\n- I advise against that, - Toham always preferred to warn idiots from silly mistakes. Challenging any of her friends could end in a very deadly scenario.\n\n- I thought the two of you are friends, - Parmas had no clue how crazy his idea sounded, however that was only to be expected from someone raised in Tribus. Kara almost pitied the man for his naivety.\n\n- I can’t come by and don’t even think that she would believe just your word, - Toham sat down at the dinner table waving her hand in the air to dim the light.\n\nThe room was quite barebone minimalistic. Metallic table was rooted to the floor with its bent sides and white walls didn’t really add anything else. The only lavish thing about this room was the three course dinner for two and some simple silverware. Neither of the two cared much about these sit downs.\n\n- Didn’t you say that you love me? - There was definitely something attractive about his dark tones and light spirit. Toham was in love. Mindlessly.\n\n- That barely means anything if you prefer to leave me alone, rather than listen to my advice, - Toham never doubted her friends. It was a bit different with love. It wasn’t her first time.\n\nSomething harshly punched Kara into her side. Someone short, to be precise. Immediately familiar and, somehow, pleasant. It was reassuring to know that the rest of the group finally caught up.\n\nKara turned her head to see Tallie, but goblin grabbed her by the elbow and dragged it towards a backstreet alley.","n":13},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"- Fish refuses to leave, - tailed, dark green woman was quite angry and soon enough Kara saw the reason. Carp was in the middle of a brawl with some bets going around.\n\nIt was surprising how easily this man got himself into trouble to make some money. Well, he personally never considered it trouble. For him it was just something that he had to do to get what he wanted, so sometimes Kara questioned whether they should keep dragging him along. In a way they were enabling his bad habits, but that probably was better than sitting clean in a state enclosure. Funny how they seemed to have these prisons for spare heroes in Septum.\n\n- Carp, look what I have! - Kara held out a neatly rolled paper and waved it in the air. It didn’t take long for a goblin in a fish mask to perk up and knock down his brawling opponent. With a windlike speed he grabbed the winnings and took a straight collision course.\n\nGood thing Tallie got the memo.\n\n- Free me, free bee, - tied up in rope, Carp wiggled like a fish. Quite fitting. Kara liked to think that he enjoyed it.\n\nToham understood little about teleportation. Technical definition of it never settled well with her. Destruction and reconstruction method that was used in most machinery felt a bit too fragile, but you kind of stop thinking about it after being forced to do it multiple times. It was easier not to think about it.\n\nFluffer Huggins worked a bit differently. Instead of creating a perforated flow of particles he instead brute forced entire entities through the wormhole dimension. That reduced the range quite a bit, however it was close to impossible to hijack it. Very close.\n\nThe saloon was lively with drunken sailors and happy chants. Kara could swear she recognized the tunes, however the words seemed thematic to seafarers and she barely understood the jargon. Once they referred to the name of the capitan Sweetwater, the name that was openly sung in the room, they were led to a backdoor and into the elevator. Old fashioned, it was powered by a man rotating a lever tied to a complex system of ropes. To be honest, the ladder would have been quicker.","n":14},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"The dramatic ascension landed in the middle of the office filled with duffle bags and cotton sacs. Big round table stood by the elevator with no chairs, but plenty of value measuring trinkets. Ember never said that they would have to pay anything, however Miss Sweetwater looked a bit too much akin to a pirate. Sitting in her chair, feet up on her office table by the window and hair tied into a wild skinny ponytail. The only thing missing was the pet parrot, but Kara knew exactly why.\n\n- So I hear your bunch needs a transfer? - Coishly started Sweetwater standing up and making her way towards the valuing table, - My flat rate is thousand gold per person, but I am open to negotiations.\n\n- Hey, we are from Ember, didn’t he pay you already? - Fezzik crossed hands on his chest and gave Sweetwater a skeptical glance, - You know, a charming chubby man about this tall? - He gestured a head and a half below his height, suddenly reminding Kara how uncomfortably tall Floats was. Blue man joined the group silently and now was nonchalantly standing head above Fezzik.\n\n- Well, if you know him so well, you must know how he is with payments, - Sweetwater smiled and dropped her palms on the table, - I believe you guys have an exceptional set of powers, but, sadly, unless someone is paying for your route there is no reason for me to head over to Decem ahead of schedule.\n\n- And when is the scheduled departure? - Fezzik was growing impatient.\n\n- In two weeks, - Said Sweetwater and by this time it was almost obvious that she already picked a scent of urgency in Fezziks tone, - You know, it takes time to gather enough paying passengers.\n\nOnce there was no real threat in wildlife attacks, the potato garden became a heaven on earth. It had plenty of space, sun, water, nutrients and any decorational structures Kara would have thought of. It was a perfect place and it was fun to build it.","n":15},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"Toham, however, pretty soon lost the sense of magic in it. Not the type of magic that summoned flaming Fluffer Huggins to her aid, but the one that makes your heart flicker and skin crawl. It felt like walking into the cemetery. It was haunted and any movement or alteration was an act of vandalism against the memories of the dead. Not people, but potatoes. Every single potato growing on this land was a descendant of a single root. A whim that ultimately killed the soul of the project.\n\n- So what happened? - Kara left Fezzik to talk out the details with Sweetwater and nudged Floats. There was almost no doubt that they caused the earthquake in the forest, but details were yet to be told.\n\n- I just thought of trying out planting the rest of the beans before we set sail, - Floats sounded unphased, but somehow empty, - I got to see two magnificent statues with quite a flock of flaming words to share.\n\n- They roasted the pans off of him, - giggled Tallie, but that barely was loud enough to reach the heights of Floats. Carp in her hands was playing dead.\n\n- So that was what caused an earthquake? - Rein couldn’t help, but join the conversation, - How big were the statues?\n\n- Oh no, that was probably the pyramid, - Floats was way less excited about it, - Giant thing just sprouted from the ground and started spitting zombies at us.\n\n- What? And you just left it there? - Kara wasn’t sure how to be angry at those things anymore. The magic that heroes used was substantially unstable, however that was only to be expected from a group that had access to it for only a few weeks. Unique acclimation for every individual and limited time for official training left plenty of blind spots and cornering such individuals never resulted in positive results.","n":16},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"- They didn’t seem to wander out too far, - Floats just raised his shoulders. Quite obviously, the fact that heroes usually had an extensive coverup crew a few steps behind them created a somewhat careless attitude. Although Kara didn’t think that the attitude would change i f it wasn’t there to begin with, which always could have been an easier answer.\n\n- Uh huh, - There wasn’t really much else to say. If they stayed longer at the site to attempt to clean up they could have been caught. It probably meant very little anyway. Just another abnormality that would become normal given enough time. People rarely fought hard enough against change, it’s much easier to adapt and avoid.\n\nTo balance the world that required people to live their natural cycle, people were neatly divided into states. The states, however, were not quite as obviously divided to a naked eye. Some people were oblivious to their existence all together. It was the true beauty of this system. It allowed people to choose to be happy where they are regardless of labels. All that was required is to follow a set of rules that they learned from the moment they were born.\n\nPerfect system limited the access to knowledge creating a conveyor of specialists and neat reorganization of the flukes. Society worked like a clock. That propaganda was usually enough to give people the illusion of stability that they needed to go around their day unhinged. However once someone got a glance on a bigger world and figured out its mechanism it could take a wisp of the wind to change that balance. Heroes usually operated exactly in this zone playing the indefinite tug of war that stalled the greater decisions. This competition was exactly what kept an accidental stranger away from the action. Living by the rules doesn’t particularly teach you to sustain rough play.","n":17},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"The conversation at the table started to heat up and Kara unwillingly switched her attention.\n\nFezzik somehow convinced Sweetwater to set sail immediately. Again, extremely shady conclusion if you take in the fact that only one of them was smiling slightly.\n\n- Alright then, I hope you lads are ready for some work! - Sweetwater cheered up the elevator man to rally the message and he quickly jumped downstairs, disappearing in the hole. The group exited the office through another door leading straight to the stairs down to the docks.\n\nIt was a long and hasty descent, mostly because it was increasingly uncomfortable for heroes to be in plain sight. Especially as the movement on the stairs perked up the dock dwellers that were usually separated from the city by a hundred foot drop wall. Surprisingly angry looking dock dwellers as Kara could swear she could make out some fangs and claws in the distance. It could have been her imagination. That would be a nice change of pace.\n\n- Hurry up, the more crates we can load before they get to us the better, - Sweetwater didn’t look phased at all.\n\n- So what do you want me to do about it? - Toham was tired of the wordplays and felt ashamed for even resorting to this. Her show was a pure product of the system; there weren't many steps you could take against some of the methods.\n\n- You could say no, many times, - Rein was always quick to his guns on such occasions. Cold, calm and, as always, right.\n\n- You right, I could, - Kara agreed for convenience's sake. She didn’t know if there ever would be enough time to explain and remember everything to change his mind. And she wasn’t even sure if she would be right about it.\n\nWhen they got to the water the ship was already waiting with a couple of sailors, suspiciously dressed like modern pirates. Modern as if they got dressed by the cosplay society preparing to rob a treasure planet. Kara wanted to cover her eyes in shame, but that would have been rude. Especially if that was their personal choice.","n":18},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"They didn’t ask any questions and hastily started loading the ship and, after a few quick directions, all six heroes, since Carp didn’t really have much else to do, joined the hassle. There wasn’t much to question. The boxes were clearly labeled and smelled like provisions and fuel. Nothing fancy or shady. By heroes standards anyway. It was really refreshing to get a slight gulp of normality with no magic involved. It was nice to see a normal ship with a normal routine for once. Kara almost forgot that it was the first time she had ever seen one in person. Remarkable event to remember, however barely chaotic enough to compete with past two weeks. Well, two months really if she was to trust the calendar.\n\nThey say that you can’t choose your family. For Toham it was quite different. She was alone for a very long time before she made friends and there didn’t seem to be a soul on this planet she was related to. Even if there was, she didn’t seek to find them in fear to face what they could have been. It didn’t matter.\n\nTallie found her family, but she was always looking for it. It was a very different perspective. It wasn’t the prettiest of reunions, however at the end she found her place in the world that Kara couldn’t call home. So they moved on to separate ways, no questions or hard feelings, knowing that if they were to ever meet again nothing would change. The world meant way less to them than the promise they gave each other in a silent agreement. It was something the goblins had that many humans too often neglected trying to save their own skins. Something that Toham couldn’t quite put her finger on.\n\nParmas was the opposite of that feeling, that’s what seemed to be the best about this relationship. He spoiled her with the exact attention she always desired. Even when he crossed her he always figured a quick way to fix it. He worked by the book that she never read. He taught her to breathe and let go, so that's what she did when he went on with his meditative speech. As painful as it was, she stopped and breathed and let go. Maybe she let go of the wrong thing. Just Maybe.","n":19},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"Kara was moving crates that weighed hundreds of pounds. She struggled, but it didn’t try to tackle her down and chew her face out so it was manageable. It still brought her back to the same little forest garden she had. Two month ago. Two weeks ago really. The days stopped being a thing of the matter on a global scale. Present seemed like the only time that mattered and some of it was now being taken away from her. Hopefully, some things that didn’t matter got lost on that side. Magic was taking over and there was little change in thinking to survive after a choice has been made or forced upon. She was asking too many questions.\n\nWhen the claw rushed past her face she barely noticed, but ducked away from the slash. She just dropped the crate over it and wasn't surprised that it hadn't killed her opponent already. Fluffer Huggins appeared a flash of red flare like a pissed snake. Dock was engulfed in crimson whips and things started catching fire. Very hot sparks, but they were too much of a cost to start up. Wood burning couldn’t keep it up effectively. It would be different in the garden.\n\nShe snatched an ax hanging off her shoulder. It took a twist to get it into the position. Good thing it wasn’t her forest ax. No need to change the hold to twist it around. Nothing that is so durable wants her alive unless it’s a friend. And she had? Six, plus four, three. Thirteen. Ten. If it was anything but that - it wants her dead. A really weird way to use math.\n\nIt was a man, with claws and a dog head. Kara assumed he was a man because she was one and she had hands. People could wear masks. Carp did. He rushed in and knocked out somebody in front before rolling away all the way into the hiding. These people barely stood a chance against the heroes of Septum. Runaways and the trouble makers that grasped the attention of millions of people. Billions. There were usually 8. Kara couldn’t have possibly imagined more. Not sustainably, but she could have been wrong.","n":20},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"No one will ever have enough time to explain everything. We resorted to binary to explain as precisely and as efficiently. Imagine the precision we could get as one. That, however, would take infinite time since there will be zero change. Everyone knows what happens when you divide by zero.\n\nThat’s why the decimal system exists. It speeds up the change, hence speeds up the time. However, it loses precision. That's why some dead ends need a limit to be cut off otherwise we can get stuck in an endless loop as the entirety. Then we are back to square zero. Zero change. In between infinity and zero there is a Goldilocks zone of our survival. As simple as that. Don’t tell them I said that.\n\nIt wasn’t the dock dwellers that worried Kara. They have already said something about Sweetwater holding up their payments, so it made sense they didn’t want the ship to leave. What they didn’t expect was that Floats wasn’t really the one to hold back when challenged directly. Well, he usually did, but something told Kara that the fact that he started shapeshifting into a tiger was a clear red flag. Men stood no chance.\n\n- Floats, please just don’t bite, - Kara almost whispered the words knowing full well that nothing would stop that man in that state. She didn’t even get to control herself in that form yet and this was a direct result.\n\nIt took less than a minute. Then Floats ran out of things to rummage around himself and turned to the next best thing. Kara hoped he would go for her, but it wasn’t really something optimal. Carp rolled in to beat some sense into a shifted blue man, but that was not nearly enough. Good thing Carp knew how to fish himself out from almost any situation.\n\nOne, two, three. Kara, crewsman, Tallie. That’s exactly what became a new proximity. Kara could hear Fluffer Huggins roar in flames ready for some action. Not for long.","n":21},{"chapter":"Bargain Deal","text":"The fur might have been slightly burned, but in a fight between cat and man it is surprisingly obvious who will come out of the smoke. Especially when the man is part tiger. Kara just saw the flames quickly dissipate into the air. Back to square one. None of the original three moved. That crewmate appeared to be quite oblivious to the awkward danger he was in. Tallie would never bulge anyway. Kara didn’t really have a reason to back down while those two were at a standstill.","n":22},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"It wasn’t something pleasant or unpleasant about his company. It was a simple fact of his presence that gave the entire situation a sense of hazy unclarity and relative safety. It was always much easier to deal with the physical aspect of the problem with him around, however it wasn’t always clear what made him tick. He wasn’t impulsive or unpredictable, he was just there. The only advantage that he gave to his friends was his loose allegiance, but that was mostly derived from the simple understanding and experience acquired through the hardships they had been through together.\n\nToham still called him a friend. It made a world of difference for both of them.\n\nKara could feel the urgency pricking up the hairs on her back. Floats had made up his mind about who he was going to tear to bits next and it wasn’t one of his friends. There wasn’t much time to think, Kara couldn’t throw a sharp weapon at him. She impulsively reached into the potato parcel. Just like the last time. Potatoes can’t be more valuable than people.\n\nIt was a pure chance that Teresa came out of the parcel. Heavily biased chance, since she was twice as big as two of her counterparts. A heavy fling of a hand and potato hit Floats straight to head. Definitely made him angry, but he wasn’t hurt. He let out a haunting low growl and Kara braced herself for the impact as the tiger shapeshifter lunged towards her. She snarled back at him ready to give him a good attempt at a tackle, however, before he could make it to her, a silvery flock of strings brought his muzzle smashing down into the ground. Kara let out a brief sigh of relief and gave Floats a good slam with a foot of her ax.\n\n- Thanks, - She gratefully waved at Rein. As much as she knew that she could handle the situation on her own, it felt good to have somebody watching over your shoulder. Shapeshifter bites seemed to be quite a painful experience, not that she would know for sure. Rein didn’t respond and just returned to moving crates. Everything returned to normal except for the slight smell of iron in the air.","n":23},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"Floats started to turn back to normal and Kara almost instinctively tied him up and dragged him on the boat.\n\n- Kara, don’t you think this is excessive? - Fezzik sounded more judgemental than usual, it made Kara’s stomach turn.\n\n- He just killed these people, - she didn’t even know if that mattered much in their situation, but the feeling of guilt wasn’t going anywhere.\n\n- Look, he is fine now. There is no need for this, - Fezzik kneeled down and started to untie the knots.\n\n- This is dangerous, he is not even trying to control it, - Kara didn’t back down. She was frustrated and quite angry. She just lost Teresa to this stupidest of the situations. Maybe if it mattered so little to everyone else she should have just let everything slide and, maybe, she would still have three potatoes in the parcel.\n\n- Like you are any better, - Fezzik sounded soft, but Kara felt like his words drove a dagger through her chest taking all breath away from her. It was all her fault and now she couldn’t even do anything about it.\n\nIt was nice to spend days in the garden when it rained. Now it just turned into a cacophony of monochromic thumps against the expensive greenhouse roof constructed to ensure a perfect environment for potatoes to grow all year round. Rain no longer brought fresh air and music of raindrops on the leaves seized to exist in her world. Potatoes didn’t seem to mind the difference as much. How could they? Most of them never grew outside in the wilderness.\n\nToham almost wanted to blast it open, but something stopped her deep inside. The same thing that made her agree to this construction in the first place. It protected potatoes, prevented overflows and kept the dust and toxic chemicals out of the question. It kept the last descendants of her garden safe and healthy. All for a tiny price of simple nostalgia.","n":24},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"They set sail in less than ten minutes. Kara barely had time to reflect on what had happened as the waves started slowly rocking the boat up and down as the engine poured its growling tunes into the wind. She couldn’t skip a beat.\n\n- Leave the shore, it’s such a bore! - Carp started swinging around on loose ropes, surprisingly avoiding falling overboard. Kara still had a doubt in her mind that his fish cosplay might take over his sanity and goblin would end up splashing in sea water. So she happily chased him around the deck, ignoring confused looks from Rein and the crew that barely understood what was happening. Tallie joined the chase, mostly for the fun of it rather than the purpose.\n\n- Let the wind hit these sails! - Shouted Floats who wasted no time after coming back to his senses, as he was taking place behind the steering wheel. He raised his hand pointing at the rolled up sails with anticipation.\n\n- Aren’t you a restless bunch? - Sweetwater smiled quite sincerely as she watched heroes taking over her deck, - If you want my crew can teach you knots and ropes of this ship. Put all that energy to use.\n\n- Ha ha, - Fezzik appeared at capitan’s side with a coy smile, - Ropes and knots are quite fun, yeah, but do I hear the engine? - His eyes gleamed with a clear excitement, but he muffled it in his voice, - Do you think I can get into its room? - Kara could almost see the man wink in her mind.\n\n- I don’t know, you’d have to ask Remy, - Sweetwater didn’t seem to mind the awkward question. No harm done, just a devil looking guy asking about his choice of passion, - He must be under the deck.\n\nIt was as if Fezzik had disappeared. He jumped over the rails, well, actually, he just ran down the stairs. He wasn’t really the type to expend magic or energy to move around. He just never did this as fast before. Kara was happy to see him excited. Usually she would be too busy to have any leisure to observe.","n":25},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"Her heart flickered with life every time she saw him. Unexplainably, well, she could have explained it if she would have time to ponder. In fact it was as clear as day, he knew so much about the plants and yet his main profession was somewhere in algae. That made him ever so slightly smarter than her, enough to teach her something. In fact both of them were kind of bad at it.\n\nThat’s why he made everything in the garden seem new. It was his idea to construct a greenhouse and he sure worked a miracle on their resistances. These potatoes would never live a day less. Some of them just never returned after hibernation. That’s how they worked. And her garden, it just was. Warm and pure, all contained and all protected. Outside world would have eaten them for shreds by now. Side effects of releasing Potato Show. Minor, thanks to the Tribus technologies. Being a hero has its perks.\n\nThat’s why he made sense in her world. Living in a closed system makes you insufferable for people outside of it. They sure will cooperate for as long as it’s necessary, however there is a saying about unmixable energies. You just had to respect them at times. He was different in this sense. More alive and passionate, much closer to what she was like. At least she liked these traits in herself before.\n\nThat’s why she loved him without a back thought. She really thought that he would find a way out if he had to. The problem was that he seemed to want to go.\n\n- Fine, - Toham shrugged her shoulders, - but you are lucky they let us have this room out of the view. If anyone learns about this…\n\n- Don’t worry I will get the permissions, - Parmas got a bit too happy.\n\n- Like hell you will, - Kara snapped. Toham almost hated the stupidity of the situation. It’s hard to explain such little details to someone who wasn’t really in the loop, - Don’t tell me you have already told your supervisors about this.","n":26},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"- I only mentioned the location, but it’s a routine request for exploring the area, - Parmas scrubbed his head. At least the potatoes didn’t have legs to walk and be useful. Toham hated the redundancy of some crude methods Tribus used. So many careless, valuable curious minds got caught under crossfire. At least Novem idiots initiated their ventures against the rules. Tribus, on the other hand, sent them according to traditional law.\n\n- Can you back down? - Toham was pleading by now. She didn’t want to think of the worst scenario, maybe it really wasn’t that bad.\n\n- It’s my research, - It was painful to consider the risks, but Parmas clearly saw things through a severely different perspective. He had his own responsibilities.\n\nIt wasn’t long until the heroes got dissipated in the routine. Being on the move meant that a lot of things must have been picked up on the spot. Learning ins and outs of your boat meant that in a critical moment the chances of survival were slightly above average and heroes wouldn’t pass on these opportunities.\n\nCarp was a bit different. Living in the moment as a motto meant a bit of a different approach to everything. Being on the boat really did a number on a poor fish. You would expect a fish to be immune to sea sickness, but how many fishes really needed boats? After a couple of mishaps Carp got locked up in the pantry, simply to keep him away from lurking and stealing a certain something from the crew. Heroes didn’t care about it, but it became a serious problem as the goblin wasn’t responding to normal methods in close quarters.\n\nNobody asked why this product was even on the ship, it was a bit too common in the world to really care and direness of the entire endeavor made its presence just a slight nuisance tied to the particular addiction of a single individual. Like a reverse allergy. Made Carp super happy and others slightly annoyed.","n":27},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"- So you drop the ball into the tube and fire up the fuse, not exactly rocket science, - Remy looked quite annoyed with the fact Kara wasn’t really taking in the simple explanations, - It doesn’t really matter how you drop the charge, it’s just a ball.\n\n- Well, you can use more potent explosives to make it go further, - Fezzik was passing by with a barrel of fuel, - Though there must be a limit on how much stress the explosion chamber can take, so I wouldn’t play around with this much.\n\nMade no sense to know this given the fact that they didn’t really have a choice of explosives on a boat, but at least now Kara knew that the weapon had a potential to backfire. She suspected it, but now the understanding really sank in.\n\nIt was much easier to work the sails. Mostly because they really didn’t really mean any harm in case of a malfunction. Plenty of room for experiments and Kara really enjoyed learning more about sea wind. Nice little trivial knowledge to ponder about when she would finally get her garden back. Not particularly useful, but that’s not what the garden was about to begin with.\n\n- No one really profits from the broken systems, Kara, - Alfonse was pressing on, - It might be just somebody trying too many things at once and overloading them.\n\n- Yes, resources that should be used moderately for the equilibrium to work. Still, I don’t take sides on Septum disputes. This isn’t how it works, - Toham was getting agitated feeling that she would make a mistake no matter what decision she would take here. The real question was, really, would she be the one paying the price or someone else.\n\n- What if this isn’t a Septum interference? - Alfonce wasn’t really censoring what he implied and Toham still didn’t quite trust his words, but, if she did, it would put her directly under the gunpoint.","n":28},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"- I would have to say let it be, - Toham never enjoyed making decisions.\n\n- Then can I ask you as a friend? - Alfonse really was pulling on the last strings. Friends meant more than potatoes.\n\n- I present to you Coffee Frap! - Rein placed glasses with icy beige liquid on the table with proud excitement on his face. The fact that the heroes decided to hold a cooking contest in the middle of an ocean was a pleasant surprise. They even let Carp out of the bilges for this.\n\n- Taste worthy of rhyme, but do I taste thyme? - A goblin swirled around his cup to reveal a bunch of green confetti floating in sweet coffee. Rein looked slightly confused for a second, but one look at giggling Tallie made him toughen up.\n\n- Yes, a new ingredient to tickle your taste buds with a new sensation! - Rein smiled confidently and gulped down his drink, probably fighting a major brain freeze.\n\n- I’ll give it nine out of ten, - Kara chuckled with Tallie. Rein really took his cooking seriously, going as far as freezing half of the kitchen with magic to get enough ice for his creation. For somebody as practical as him, seeing such redundant chaos behind his work seemed like a breath of fresh air from his usually gloomy personality.\n\n- I must give it a six, - Fezzik set his drink to the side, - I prefer herbs in their traditional pallets, - he seemed genuinely trusting the fact that Rein chose to spice up his drink with herbal touch.\n\n- Now try mine, - Tallie was quite impatient to show off her grilled salmon, quite suspiciously also sprinkled with thyme. Seasoning options were quite limited at the sea.\n\n- First you got to let everyone give my Frap a rating, Tallie, - Rein seemed quite annoyed at a restless tailed goblin.","n":29},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"- I give it a five, - Tallie spat out her number and returned to decorating her plates.\n\n- My dear, I love the passion for sensations, I’ll give it a solid 8, - Floats was acting like his old self again. Refined, polite and not at all chaotic. Kara enjoyed this side of him much more.\n\n- Make it even, I’ll give a seven, - Carp threw up a hyperactive thumbs up, almost flipping over his drink.\n\n- I always welcome a good refreshing drink on a hot day, that’s an 8 from me, - Maren, a massive man in charge of oars and other brute force tasks, smiled with joy ignoring the daggers that Tallie threw at him with her eyes.\n\n- I must give a six, I like my classic coffee, - Sweetwater didn’t sugarcoat her opinion.\n\n- Eh, seven, - Remy just enjoyed the atmosphere since the heroes plucked him out of his routine as he was just passing by.\n\nRufus said nothing. He never did, but everybody heard his score.\n\nThe decision to follow a path as something that will guide the personal decisions of an individual. Easy route if one had the understanding of what they are going to be from the very start. Something that was nurtured by someone trusted, creating a loop of interactions and behavioral gulfs of the wind. By observing some patterns you could classify events by the gulphs they create.\n\nWar will produce a radicality and protectiveness chart. With these heroes it is quite obvious where they stand on this spectrum. The closer they are to the action, the more likely they are to possess the extreme cases. Similarly the uprooting will produce an isolation and opportunism chart and peace will produce a conservatism and humanity one. Those are just the simplest examples that we used.\n\nTo be honest, if we continue letting majority consensus systems control our Novem ideals we might be hitting some processing limits. That's a lot of structural weight on a toothpick.","n":30},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"Salmon was alright, but fish wasn’t really as rare of a sight at the sea. At least for Kara, she didn’t know much about where those fishes swam around, nor did she know how to tell them apart before she saw the filet. Even then, she really just knew there was red fish and white fish. Nevertheless, she gave it a nine. Tallie was a little too vengefully competitive.\n\nSix. Six. Seven. Five. Nine. Seven. 8. Seven\n\n- Well, that’s a tie, - Kara looked over her notes and raised an eyebrow. Tallie rushed over and checked over the notes and grunted something before retreating downstairs. Next one to leave was Maren, then the rest of the crew and heroes returned Carp to his confinement. Fish mask goblin didn’t resist as much anymore, but it was never a bad idea to have extra eyes on the job.\n\n- Enjoying the sun?\n\nToham didn’t hear him walk in. The voice just appeared in the space softly and something landed by her side with a fresh breeze. It was much hotter in the greenhouse than outside.\n\n- Would have enjoyed a bit of a breeze too, - She nudged herself under his cold jacket enjoying the momentary relief from the heat.\n\n- You can always turn up the ventilation, - Parmas threw his arm around Toham and she finally opened her eyes to see that he was wearing his tattered walking shirt.\n\n- Or I could just leave the door open, - She smiled, as the walls around started opening to let in some outside air, - That works too.\n\n- Kara, get down! - Fezzik screamed with an urgency in his voice. He must have, Kara was looking right into the face of a turtle dragon with a charmed expression. She never hoped to see something so grand just out in the sea. Well, she would have if the pure sensation of wonder didn’t strip her of freshly acquired wisdom. It has been only a couple of weeks of wielding magic for Kara and deeper it went the more distant the old world seemed to be. Like a silly memory.","n":31},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"Dragon roared out the steam. Kara was so close she could see right through it before it turned into a cloud. So hot that her head first recognised the sensation by the pain shock thumping in her brain. It was fast and abrupt as Tallie jumped from above to shut the dragon's jaws and tugged Kara into cover. Two more violent thumps and the turtle have returned to the sea depth.\n\nIt was a bad and painful burn, but nothing that wouldn’t heal with some magic and a moment of rest. Second prerequisite was quite optional.\n\nIt was quite cold to deny so many so much. In this sense the world had left nothing to those who still had the ambitions. Even the heroes had to die eventually. Either that or turn into things that they were combating all their lives. And then would come the new heroes to prove them wrong in their judgment.\n\nValentines tried to break this cycle over and over again. To breach the wall of determined sets and end the loop that was feeding the neverending show. To let everything fall in line and end the suffering of billions. Toham on other hand had every lever at her fingertips right now and consistently chose not to pull them. By pure coincidence, they were also best friends. For a long time.\n\n- Can you not do this next time? - Rein sounded quite annoyed at the fact Kara got herself hurt so easily. Heroes really relied on each other to act rationally in moments like this and sometimes the reminders went a long way.\n\n- I was just wondering if it could have been friendly, - Kara breathed out a sigh of relief as the burns subsided and pain stepped away.\n\n- Yeah, try and befriend a giant animal with dagger teeth, real smart move, - Rein looked down with a tired look on his face. Sometimes Kara wondered if it was just her luck to encounter most unfriendly beasts, however the facts were quite evident. She gave things a bit too much of the benefit of the doubt. It wasn’t like her survival was a priority over doing things right. But then again, banter opened even more room for mistakes. It was a weird balance.","n":32},{"chapter":"Friends and Scores","text":"It would have been easy if Alfonse was asking for something impossible. However, everyone knew who Pink Glasses really was. Just a cat, not Toham in disguise. Well, not everyone, but Alfonce definitely knew the difference. If Eve was smart, Alfonce was observant.\n\n- I most probably will have to tell her, - Toham knew that this won’t change much. Most likely she already knew.\n\n- Seriously? - Alfonce gave Toham a tired glance, - Thank you.\n\nIt was never obvious if he was truly nervous or just normal self. She knew that he would want to stay out of that conversation with Eve, but it wasn’t the first time Toham made up some ridiculous excuses and requests. Potato garden was a living proof of that. In any case, Toham really missed the company of a friend. It would be a breath of fresh air. Eve knew that she could use one.","n":33},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- It would have made a great soup, - Tallie looked at the water with a longing stare. Couple of weeks at the sea made their pallets quite thirsty for a new sensation and crewmates have told stories about the infamous dragon turtle soup. Didn’t sound quite appetizing, but Tallie believed that she could make it work.\n\n- Isn’t it better to leave the wildlife alone? - Kara was relatively confused on the state of affairs with creatures that looked like an absolute menace to human existence. Although she was probably thinking about something more natural, like a shark.\n\n- Yeah, real gray area. Doesn’t help that they are attracted to magic. Quintus really did all heroes dirty on this one, - Fezzik didn’t seem to enjoy explaining simple things, but Kara found great deal of reassurance in hearing someone make sense out of this newly discovered side of the world that they were apparently a part of.\n\n- Kara, you can’t keep following simple principles. You need to think just a little bit harder, - Rein chimed in as if Kara was asking for the simplest ABC’s. Useful questions, but you expect a person to be all sorted out on these by her age.\n\n- Right after you, - Kara scowled and returned to checking the sails.\n\nDeterioration of the system is only an isolated step of the progress. You can’t constantly waste time on repairing your straw shack. At some point it will be time to take it down and gather up some stones. If you cut down on the information spread on such occasions, you might minimize the casualties of the gulfs. Afterall, what use could an individual get from a constant flow of ever repeating history? What use can a cog in a machine get from knowing what’s life is like for a ground worm being processed by the selector?","n":34},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"People are not machines, however. Sadly even the decimal system won’t quite bridge the gap of understanding. That’s why control methods still seem very crude and violent. They are still yet to accept the changes we suggested. Although, it would destroy their entire purpose if they stop their own search. Harsh dilemma, but a fair one.\n\nWhen Remy called out land on the horizon heroes zoomed towards the railings. It has been two weeks since their hasty goodbye to the solid ground. For Kara, who has never been on the water way ship before, the sight of the sand felt like a saving grace. There was a problem she spotted at first sight. The island was the tinies sandy patch with barely any ground for four lonely palms and some shrubbery.\n\n- Do you think Carp would… - Kara didn’t get to finish her sentence as the sound of splash rustled the wind. She looked overboard, more interested in Carp’s swimming form rather than worried for his safety, - Holly sprouts, he actually swims like a fish with arms.\n\n- Last one to the shore is a rotten egg, - Tallie hopped over the rail.\n\n- Anyone else wants to take a swim? - Kara skeptically turned her head to others, but Fezzik already finished his stretching on the railing. Before she knew what happened, he jumped in with a graceful caped dive.\n\n- You guys realize there is a shuttle boat? - Rein raised an eyebrow pointing at the crewmembers preparing for the landing. Floats was already sitting in the boat.\n\n- I would rather skip that theme, can we just go off of our time on the team? - Toham knew the answer to this question, - I mean, all they know is that we went to the same school.\n\n- I am not the one forcing this, - Eve shrugged her shoulders, - Plus it’s not like you didn’t know where it was going all this time, now did you?","n":35},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- And how did you figure this one out? - Toham crossed hands on her chest and fell into the chair. Playing mind chess with Eve was the only salvation in these situations.\n\n- Quite easily, it hits almost every nerve that usually sets you off, - a woman with olive skin smiled and poured some tea in her cup.\n\n- Witty, but that’s really just a backstep method of an excuse, - Toham felt a shiver going down her back as she spoke the words.\n\n- Even if what you are suggesting is true, what difference would it make for you?\n\n- I’m not suggesting, I just want to know how.\n\nIt wasn’t really hard to decide what to do. Kara sized up the space on the shuttle and gingerly waved at Rein before jumping into the water. It was exciting to take her first swim in the sea. Her first swim really, since most of the creeks in the mountains where she grew up were never deeper than a belt depth.\n\nIt was disorienting at first. Then she felt where the up was. It was elusive, but something in her head just equalized and she surfaced. It took two or three clumsy strokes before Kara really got a hang of the water. Driven mostly by her unwillingness to seem scared or incapable, she copied Tallie in her intense front crawl and soon overtook Fezzik, beating him to the shore.\n\n- Does this count as reaching the shore? - Kara sat down on the beach by Tallie who was actively squirming her hair and tail and pointed at Carp who was still splashing in the water, probably having the time of his life.\n\n- I don’t know, - grunted Tallie, clearly disinterested in the competition that she has already won.\n\nThere was a reason why the lands of Decem were uncharted regardless of many attempts to do so. Many successful attempts to add an insult to an injury. Many states had influence over it, but none of them benefited from it staying stable. It was a land of a false promise of freedom. False, because none of them really had any rights and that doesn't really sit well with the system, so that's where it fell on the chart. Like dividing by zero.","n":36},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- So what are the numbers so far? - Toham sat upright and stretched her neck. It would be her first field trip in months and she couldn’t help but feel nervous. It would be her first time leaving the garden unattended for this long too.\n\n- Two to nine, - Parmas cautiously stretched vowels to test reaction. The odds didn’t sound promising at all.\n\n- Well, let’s stay in single digits then, - Toham gave him a soft smile and picked up her parcel.\n\n- That’s all? - the man with caramel skin expectantly looked over Toham from boots to face.\n\n- Well, if you don’t have an ax on your ship we’ll find something on our way. They will notice if mine goes missing.\n\n- Right…\n\n- Here, let me help you, - Kara waved her hand upwards and a gentle wisp of wind dried her up so seamlessly that it was hard to guess that she was in the water at all. Fezzik did the same thing as he reached the shore.\n\n- Neat, - Tallie happily growled excited to see the island.\n\nThere was a little tent hiding in the shrubbery and three people lazily sat around it. Maren and Rufus approached them as soon as they got the shuttle boat fastened on the beach. Rein and Fezzik also went along and Kara and Tallie just stayed to see what Carp was up to. It was hard to think that something would go wrong.\n\nToham hated logs. Too much unnecessary information and too many numbers to consider. Who went where and when they did it. Side notes and descriptions of things achieved, brought in or information picked up. Who it was reported to and who sponsored it. Bunch of words fabricated to hide the truth.\n\nIt all went down with a dark shroud engulfing the tent and everyone around it. At first Kara didn’t even know if anything was wrong. For all she knew it was just the safety precaution.","n":37},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"It wasn’t that hard to pick up on the discrepancies in the data. The shady data logs always had some of the fields unfilled. It just took some time to compile all of them and observe correlations.\n\nThe deal breaker was the scream of pain performed by Fezzik. It was like a cold shower, heroes should never let their guard down. The shroud came down and it revealed a gruesome battle field with Rein piercing a giant centipede that appeared to have exploded out of the body of a islander man. Two remaining islanders seemed to face the same fate as now they were wrestling with Maren in their new forms. Tallie didn’t skip a beat.\n\nIt took three days to gather the facts together. Valentines were clearly abusing the word classified in their reports, leave alone the discrepancies in the load they declared and energy that was used for it in the machinery logs. Usually the system would not take the bad math, however, it was easy to forge reports post factum after the initial entry if you had the clearance. Well, only Toham called it clearance.\n\nThe carapace crunch sound was accompanied by the thunderous clap when Tallie swung her hammer against the centipede that attacked Fezzik. These beasts seemed to be built excessively tough for something living on such a tiny island. Kara tried to swing an ax, but Rufus stopped her with a mindful remark.\n\n- Alright, - Kara dodged a claw and slid towards Fezzik. He seemed to be paralyzed from the wound.\n\n- Why didn’t you just tell me about this straight away? - Toham was feeling a little bit nauseous from the discovery.\n\n- I didn’t know how you would react if you didn’t see the extent yourself, - Alfonse sat in a chair with a lost concern in his eyes. It was a tough situation to consider. Toham missed working things out together, but she never thought that she would have to tackle an inside corruption. It seemed that they both knew the solution, but neither of them was ready to voice it.","n":38},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"The healing did help to stop the blood, however that didn’t quite help with the poison in the system. Or was it venom? Centipedes could bite at a certain time in history. Kara had to get Fezzik out of the action field.\n\nOne of the centipedes got out of Marens grasp and rushed towards them, but Floats was quick to boink it with his stick. Just a friendly reminder of how strong the blue man was. The creature dropped dead in an instant, rattling its last words with numerous twitching limbs.\n\nIt was unsettling to understand that when you are faced with a choice between an easy direct action and the appropriate protocol that might cause more trouble, sometimes you stall to consider the consequences of the violation, which leads you to losing the initiative moment for the direct action and leaving you with only the option of following the protocol. That's why sometimes it was best for heroes to be oblivious.\n\nOne of the centipedes got out of Marens grasp and rushed towards them, but Floats was quick to boink it with his stick. Just a friendly reminder of how strong the blue man was. The creature dropped dead in an instant, rattling its last words with numerous twitching limbs.\n\nKara wondered if it was terrified or just aiming for an easier food source to snatch and drag away. It definitely could swim faster than humans.\n\n- You know that I hate when coincidences add up, - Toham could feel the chills building up in her upper neck.\n\n- I told you they are opportunistic, - Alfonce sighed in the way that Toham hated the most.\n\n- I told you it was a bad idea to get me involved in the first place.\n\nThe fight was over in a flash. Kara brushed off her shock and sat down on the sand to understand the situation that Ruffus was explaining. Apparently Unus and Quinques were sending some viral parasites to infiltrate the supply lines of the makeshift underground railroads to Decem.","n":39},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- Wait, you guys want to go in there alone? - Kara skeptically looked at Maren and Rufus gathering up their equipment to go down to some underwater cavern nearby.\n\n- Well, if there is a hive it is quite dangerous for someone unprepared, - Maren briefly glanced at Fezzik who was coming back to his senses.\n\n- Well, if it is dangerous, wouldn’t you want more eyes watching your back then? - Rein was wiping his rapier with a handkerchief that started fuming from the acidic substance that appeared to be the insides of the centipede. His face briefly expressed disgust and he quickly finished the job and tossed the spoiled cloth on the ground.\n\n- If you guys are willing to come with us, we’ll appreciate it. We just can’t guarantee your safety down there, - Maren admitted reluctantly. Tallie flinched with anticipation.\n\n- Let’s go and get over it, - she confidently stepped up and flexed her hammer.\n\n- Why do you always do this? Why can’t you just let go? - Rein was angry, devastated and disappointed. Toham expected this reaction, but she also knew that she would never be able to handle it. She made the most painful mistake of her life and could barely breathe. It didn’t help to have the entire world suddenly shrink to this one moment where everything she thought was in her hands collapsed into a predetermined outcome.\n\n- I… - words got stuck in her throat. The realization settled in and Toham no longer had any interest to continue. She just sat on her knees too terrified to turn around and see the truth.\n\nIt was an unusual spell. Breathable water felt weird and heavy in the lungs, but Kara didn’t quite grasp how exactly Rufus made it possible. He just reassured heroes that it was possible and everyone just believed him. Like a herd of sheep, the only motivation wasn't survival. Heroes motivation was to prove to each other that they aren’t scared to trust the common sense of somebody with experience and mutual goal.","n":40},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"They swam deeper and deeper, so deep, in fact, that by the end of their descent there was barely any light making it through the column of the water. The pressure pounded all around, making every move a forcible crawl. They reached the bottom and, as Rufus waved his hand, the route of light cascaded in front of them. It continued for a while until they reached a rocky upward cliff with a cave entrance. It was a bit too straight for something an animal would make.\n\nThey entered the cave and climbed the ladder into an air pocket. Kara wondered who found this cave and thought that it would be a great place for a hideout. Must have been a fish and people just followed it. And now it seemed the stronger beings claimed it for their own through the animal force.\n\n- Never thought they would resort to such methods in neutral waters, - Kara looked around the air bubble corridor clearly drenched with the smell of centipede acid and rotten flesh.\n\n- Yeah, nowadays you gotta be ready for anything, - Maren started to rummage through the barrels to see what was salvageable.\n\n- I found fuel, - Fezzik waved from the stack of iron barrels.\n\nTallie pressed forward and Carp just rolled around with hyper excitement. Kara decided to follow them just in case. Occasionally they smashed some centipedes, but when Carp rolled back from the ominous communal hall with a rhyme they headed back to call others.\n\n- There’s a big crawley, she is ugly, - the second version seemed a little bit off, but Carp wasn’t the one to resort to repetition. Heroes quickly got the memo and rounded up the scavenging.\n\n- Do you think you can roast them effectively? - Kara asked an open question, debating whether to take Fluffer Huggins into it.","n":41},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- Good idea, - Fezzik directed Floats towards the barrels of oil and the blue man helped him roll it down the hall. Up, but Floats somehow made it look like down.\n\n- So what do you say? - Parmas seemed to be happy to have a change of pace and finally walk somewhere through the pine forest, - These creatures adapted to pray on humans?\n\n- More like selectively bred, but who knows. They sure looked like solid chimeras. Much like the manticore I told you about. Not mythically speaking, - Toham felt dumb probably saying just a gibberish at this point and putting her back into it. It was fun to joke around with a bounce, it was really nice to be with him around.\n\n- No wa-a-y, they can’t be this dumb. I mean at least they made them look like chilling tourists. Random passersby wouldn’t go around and bother their vacation, - Parmas was teasing her with ridiculous suggestions.\n\n- Don’t you dare to underestimate the stupidity of the Novem people, - Kara lightly tapped Parmas on his thigh.\n\n- Yep, they run faster than common sense, - it ripped her ear to hear such words out of a Tribus citizen, - Sorry, I know that we might be playing with fire. I’ll stay put.\n\n- I hate when you make me sound ridiculous, - Toham boiled over, but Parmas quickly blew the anger away with a gentle blow on her head.\n\n- Poof, like a torch, - He laughed and sprinted forwards.\n\n- Run faster, - Toham smiled and sparked up a giant hellish tom cat into the air. Toham didn’t dare to change his form to something more appealing to her position, so the flames of fur still exposed clay features of its body and it only grew proportionally in size.\n\nShe took a jogging start, letting Parmas get further away.\n\n- Twelve, Fifteen, - whispered to herself Toham and started switching to sprint, - Fluffers, you know the drill.","n":42},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"It all got dark for a second. Then two. Then three and she was landing right on top of Parmas forcing him to tumble down with a thud.\n\n- Ouch! - Laughed caramel man decently surprised at the seemingly magic trick, - This is ridiculous! How did you do this?! - both of them were laying down at the gates of the mansion. Toham rarely teleported for such distances, but not at home, - I still can’t believe it. You can literally be anywhere if you really wanted to, why do you stay here so much?\n\n- Why would I want to be everywhere? Not like you would enjoy me in an algae lab, - again, it was hard to try and explain something so rudimentarily simple that it could go either way.\n\n- I would enjoy you in any four walls, - Toham loved when he said things like that.\n\nYou would be surprised how extensive some of the water caves can span for. Though it was ridiculous how Octingetti managed to build them this deep under the sea. Only now Kara wondered whether they crossed the ocean yet. She tried to remember the weather outside, but the clicking steps in the hallway forced her snap back to reality of a stinky cave.\n\nThere seemed to be myriads of crawling centipedes in a tight space. Mindless swarm, unstoppable if it means that intruders will be eliminated. Kara didn’t have time to panic, if she stalled Tallie would jump right in. Fluffer Huggins exploded in the hall right in the wave of the centipedes outsizing him by a solid foot in a tail length. That’s when Kara really panicked. The swarm did appear stunned and wounded, but it didn’t look like it would stop them for long.\n\nTallie jumped in with a thundering swing and Floats carefully settled down the barrel upright. Somewhere in the dark Carp was slapping away his fish style attacks. Fezzik grunted something and unloaded some scraps out of his bottomless pockets. Kara was busy maintaining the fire flash wall to keep down the flow.","n":43},{"chapter":"Centipede","text":"- Wait a second, - Fezzik lightly tapped on the sea stone floor and grabbed some kind of a grenade, - Rein watch, The glitter bomb! - he threw the grenade into the swarm covering half a dozen centipedes in sticky paint, - They breathe through the exoskeleton, so this will suffocate them!\n\n- Well, at least we can see them better, - Kara raised her hand in disbelief. The fact of an adult man choosing glitter as his form of weaponry just resonated with absolut wild card. It was surprising how available this stuff was and how truly dangerous it was. The fact that it was mixed in with reflective paint showed how ingenious and adaptive fiend looking man was. In Septum mines they really had limited resources. Kara covered her face with a hand to hide an amazed smile.\n\n- Seriously? - Rein lowered his rapier for a second of a breather while discombobulated centipedes were scrambling to their feet. They were not quite suffocating. More like surprised by an uncommon scent. Once they figured that it wasn’t deadly toxic they were back on their mission.\n\n- Hey, watch this! - Tallie took a jumping swing pushing off of the cave wall and her hammer sparked with lightning. Kara watched it in slow motion. Tailed goblin, probably the last of her kind, landed her hammer right at the head of a glittered centipede and the entire cave lit into a momentary disco flash.","n":44},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Sometimes Toham was surprised how easy it was to spot Pink Glasses in the window. He was more self-sufficient than anybody, however long days of isolation in a giant house still made him attached to a girl that never wore a smell that he didn’t know or acted unreasonably. And there was nothing Toham could do about it.\n\n- Seems like mister knows what’s up, - Parmas nodded at the ginger tom cat tentatively eyeing the pair through the bedroom window on the third floor. Toham always locked him up when she expected Parmas to visit. For some reason Glasses was jealous and vindictive towards a Tribus visitor.\n\n- Sometimes I hate how smart this cat is, - Toham grit her teeth through a turning stomach.\n\n- Yeah, never seen such an aggressive cat and all that after just one clumsy scrub, - Parmas cluelessly gazed into the window on the third floor.\n\n- Well, you know, he only tolerates professionals, - Toham locked eyes with ginger tom almost hearing his exact thoughts.\n\n- Hey, plenty of cats enjoy my rubs quite a bit, - Parmas gave Toham a coy smile and she blushed.\n\n- You silly, that’s not what I meant! - she slapped his shoulder. Sometimes she forgot that Parmas was a hero, just not from Septum.\n\nIt was a tedious grind to get back to the room of the queen's lair. At the certain moment Kara caught herself stuck in the same routine for multiple minutes and yet without a single tired muscle. There were dozens of chimera creatures overwhelming them with a mixed desire to defend the nest and devour intruders.\n\nAnd yet it was clear as a day that heroes had to kill their queen. Simply to remove the problem and reclaim the cave for the supply station. And then fill it up with people again and arm them with precautionary weapons to avoid these kinds of situations in the future. Until Unus and Quintus come up with a better way to stop underground business. Then it was the matter of time until Culo went on strike, but that would take decades, if not centuries, to happen.","n":45},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Kara hated to have this much time to think. Every new killed centipede ticked her sand clock grain by grain, slowly outweighing the scales of her desire to continue forwards. She was killing creatures and, horrifically, she was bored. Fluffer Huggins didn’t have this problem.\n\n- Decem is big, how on earth did they end up in the same sectors? - Toham could not believe that such a chaotically divided state would shape together to form a new order. What was even worse, they now pinched out the window of supplies wide enough to breach the communications wall.\n\n- I think they might have reached the trigger impasse, - Alfonce tried to sound soft while explaining the situation, - Goblins aimed to steal and hit the wrong house. Now it’s a full fledged annihilation war all over again.\n\n- What house? - Toham asked in disbelief. This was the exact reason why she kept away from internal disputes. She hated choosing sides, - Alfonce, what house?\n\nThe silence was deafening. There really was no other option if the red man resorted to asking favors. Kara sat down in the chair and processed the situation. Now if she gets involved the situation will become very real, but if she doesn't it will haunt her till the end of her days.\n\n- No, - Toham was unable to move, - This is absolutely and ridiculously stupid, why can’t they understand that?\n\n- Would you? - Alfonce stood up with his hands crossed.\n\nShe never would. Not if she had this much power. By now everyone knew better than to touch her garden.\n\nThe closer they got to the lair, the bigger the crawly creatures got. Kara wondered what Rufus and Maren would do without them, however they most likely wouldn’t go as far as destroying the entire colony. Just a fuel pick up and they would be out.","n":46},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Thing about chimeras is that not all of them were following the barebone instincts. They could have been created to hunt on unsuspecting travelers and hoard their supplies for an obscene reason of it being part of their genetic program. What a convenient way to switch the blame on a failed experiment. Something that went out of hand that certain informed individuals could use to their benefit. Kara was great at coming up with ridiculous conspiracies. There was no way the underground trade would have access to such advanced technology. Who would have chosen such an inhumane way of structural upkeep?\n\n- So you say these things are new to the area? Do they spread? - Kara took a breather after they turned a corner and sent the last of the drones in the area to rush in search for reinforcements. Rufus suggested that it was a single planted queen to eradicate the route from the safe list. Just a way of directing the flow of trade and eliminating the competition. In a nine state world, a simple paper treaty would be quite enough.\n\nCamerarius took over the negotiations with middle states. He was the strongest and most undying of all Septum heroes and his obsession over Senio products filled the gap by itself.\n\nToham rarely saw him and he rarely caused any trouble. In fact, his management proved so efficient that all three middle states kept their affairs clean and engaged without any extra stimulus. Most negative feedback was mostly due to connection to Septum heroes he had. Public hate was mostly transferred to Toham for a very obvious reason. It was really hard to explain why so much of the structure was focused purely on the Potato Show.\n\nIt was a little bit too quiet when they got to the destination hall. Ominous silence coupled with occasional chirp or clack in the corner made the hero's hair stand up in anticipation of the next moment.","n":47},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"- Alright, what’s the plan? - everybody knew things got serious when Tallie was the one to ask this question.\n\n- Queen’s big and fat, she’s on a ramp, - Carp whispered his rhyme looking at heroes with his fish mask eyes. Kara always wondered how he fought so efficiently in it. He definitely wasn’t blind.\n\n- Alright, good to know. I’ll adjust the flamethrowers, - Fezzik pulled out his screwdriver. Kara expected a wrench, but what did she know about machinery? Leave alone the magic machinery. All she could suggest is that it was Flufffer Huggins, just with extra steps.\n\n- And I will blast and fly, - Kara gave an ironic thumbs up. The fact that Kara had to incinerate the entire area for the Huggins teleport was mildly inconvenient for team operations with a slightly disagreeable team. That’s why she enjoyed being friends, - Rufus, Maren, if you feel Huggins pull, you might get burned if you resist.\n\nBoth crewmates nodded, slightly annoyed at the obvious remark. Kara would feel stupid, but, as a wiseman said, better safe than sorry. Now if they get mad for being burned, they would be suffering from their own stupidity. At that point, Kara wouldn’t even be sorry. Too much guilt to carry around for somebody else's destructive ego.\n\nToham was sitting at the table playing with the candle. Light up, blow out and spark the line. Ignite, extinguish, reignite. From flame to gas and back to flame. In a rehearsed motion that she had practiced for the past two nights. It was like a single little thing that she could focus on that took just enough of her mind from the boredom of excruciating paperwork.\n\n- I always wonder how you still haven’t set the house on fire, - Fezzik knocked on the door after it would have served any purpose. Pink Glasses came in with him, strutting towards his platform.","n":48},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"- Because they’d built it up inch for inch in less than a night, - Kara lit up the candle one last time and scratched the table.\n\n- Yeah, but I think they would have let me know if that ever happened, - red man made his way to the table and for a second considered where to stand, then gathered his courage and got around to hold Kara by the shoulders, - Look, you can’t blame yourself for something you couldn’t do anything about.\n\n- And I have nothing to do with it, Fezzik, - Kara couldn’t bring herself to look up, - Rein made it very clear that we never were a family. The matter of fact, I don’t think we even stand on the same side anymore.\n\nToham got out of the grip as a ginger tomcat jumped on the table for rubs. It was one of guilty pleasures. To allow a little comfort in the life of a cat and a girl.\n\n- Well, that sounds really tough, doesn’t it? - Fezzik dropped his hands in slight annoyance, - Why would you go out of your way to help those who don’t ask for it, right? Life isn’t about things you didn’t do, Kara, it’s about things that you did. And if you can take it less personally you might save both of them from mistakes that will affect all of us.\n\n- With my marvelous charm of what? - Toham suspected the answer, but that was a grand if from both sides.\n\n- How about the marvelous charm of being braver than me? - Fezzik made a clear, solid point. The painful flattery.\n\nThere wasn’t really much room to negotiate the entrance. Heroes opened the doors wide and cleared the way for the miraculously constructed flamethrowers. Carp and Tallie rushed in gunning towards the ramp and smashing giant drones on their way. Kara stayed back watching their back and Floats and Rein held the line. Creeping sounds coupled with cracking sounds and screeches of the centipedes filled the air and with a brief flicker of the fire stones the room lit up with fire.","n":49},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Day by day, you expect things to improve and change. You expect people to learn from the mistakes of the past and think beyond their lifespan. That's the only way you can really harbor hope for humanity in the everlasting turbulence. The problem with that, however, is that there is only so much time given to each one of us to experience these mistakes to truly know the futility of such a mind set. At some point every mind faces the wall of mortality and its own selfish desire to achieve something, anything, even if it is as simple as their own survival. Hive minds collapse under the pressure of independent thinkers that rot the system from within and single lineage systems are crippled by the power paradox that destroys its heads. One single mistake can determine the fate of an entire system and no amount of fool proofing can protect it from the corruption embedded into the very backbone of human nature. It is hard to care about something that you will never see.\n\nToham believed in that for a long time making it her excuse to carry on. There wasn’t much this world could offer to someone like her and nothing she wanted to offer back. So they exchanged the bare minimum. Step by step, inch by inch, slowly depleting the lines of communication due to the redundancy.\n\nToham couldn’t pinpoint what exactly brought the change. Maybe it was the introduction of friends into the system. Actual humans with different passions and goals. Something that she wanted to balance together to reach the fragile equilibrium to keep them from drifting apart and away. It only worked briefly. When all six of them were weak enough to need each other. Until the very point when they got strong enough to surround themselves with new people that bowed to their power.","n":50},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"No, that wasn’t it. It was a mere lesson that helped the process. A building block to something that Kara used for the foundation. Everyone needs friends to keep each other in line. In line that defends the system from the corruption that leads to collapse. Her friends never failed her in that. She however seemed to make mistakes at every turn. Now Toham was suffering the consequences and it was easy to see where it all went wrong. Kara was too soft.\n\nThe queen was indeed quite fat. Like a termite with haunting long appendages, but in a shimmering carapace reflecting the light as if made from metal. As tough as metal too. Lightning hammer attack from Tallie ricocheted right off of it hitting unsuspecting Floats right into the side. Distraction brief enough for the drone to topple the blue man over and bite into his shoulder with it’s sharp mandibles.\n\nThat was a solid reality kick. Kara instantly tensed up and swung her ax at the chimera and continued doing so until Floats toppled her over digging his now tiger fangs into her. She felt the pull of rage, she almost bit back. However she struggled for a second before Floats found a better target in a chimera to the right heading for Fezzik. The bite mark burned like hell.\n\nIt was hard to remember some things that have happened in the mines. Just like it was impossible to remember what was going on for three straight weeks at the beginning of her life as a hero. She didn’t remember how she bit Floats to give him the shapeshifter's curse. Only ever so briefly did she realize that it was her fault. Floats only bit her once after that and she would never go through this experience again. Toham was just way more careful after that. To think about it, he never actually tried it ever again. To think about it, she never really hit Rein.","n":51},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Kara struggled for breath for what seemed like a second, but she woke up at the corner by Fezzik rubbing sweat off of his forehead.\n\n- Hey, you with me there? - Red man panted through his question, sending a lightning from his hand, - Calm down, Kara. We still need you out there.\n\n- Yeah, - Kara’s head was pounding. She burned Fluffer Huggins back to her side and jumped back into the fight.\n\nFluffer Huggins wasn’t always a lifeless shape that followed her every whim. What she did remember out of the void of three weeks was that the flaming tom cat was quite lively and strong willed. It took quite a lot out of Tarik and Victorian to make him compliant.\n\n- Can’t we ask seniors to take care of this, - Toham didn’t really like to ask for favors, especially after things that this duo had made her go through to get where she was. Their methods seemed deliriously sadistic.\n\nFloats was still raging in the hall, everyone with sentience stayed out of his way. Up until the point where tiger shifters got overwhelmed by the sheer number of the chimeras. Weirdly enough these things had a weird preference to focus him rather than goblins that were showering their queen with blows. To be frank that thing was tough enough to take them on quite well.\n\n- Alright, second try is the charm, - Kara braved up her ax in her palms and lunged forward to help Floats out of his predicament.\n\n- They aren’t fletchling heroes anymore, not sure they would want to pick up something of this caliber right before their retirement, - Fezzik sighed and sat at the sofa. Toham hated the fact that she would never gather up enough courage to send him out. Garden was unbearably lonely. Toham briefly smiled.\n\n- Yeah, you should see Victorian’s face every time I show off the fangs, - She uneasily chuckled realizing how uncontrollably fast that change came about, - I bet by now both of them are weathered monsters.","n":52},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"Fluffers got Floats out in a burning flash and Kara took on the swarm with Rufus and Maren close behind. Or in front. It was hard to navigate these things in the flashing darkness. It didn’t matter.\n\n- This thing is a witch, It doesn’t flinch! - Carp must have slapped the queen and retreated back to the swarm problem. The lightning must have bounced off of queens shell and grazed his costume.\n\n- Tallie, get out of the way! - Rein took a lunging stance and started chanting, - The state is a product and a manifestation of the irreconcilability of class antagonisms.\n\nHe rushed forwards and pierced the carapace of the creature.\n\n- The state arises where, when, and insofar as class antagonism objectively cannot be reconciled, - Rein swiftly pulled out his rapier to the song of the queen screeching. His eyes lit up in a purple shine, - And, conversely, - his aura lit up in a low purple flare and he briskly dodged a claw and appeared right in front of Queens eyes.\n\n- The existence of the state proves that the class antagonisms are irreconcilable, - Reins rapier soaked his aura and his final blow hit the queen right in between her eyes with an almost electric thump.\n\n- By the looks of things. However, Decem doesn’t quite have the concentration of heroes. You should be quite on par, - Fezzik shrugged and patted on the sofa calling up Pink Glasses for a rub. Toham promptly released the ginger tom and he compliently moved on to the next station.\n\n- Are you scared of me? - Toham smiled and playfully imitated a crouch with her hands on the table.\n\n- You? - Fezzik laughed, - I could barely call that a fear. Maybe a jump scare every now and then and I’m definitely not about to touch your garden.","n":53},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"- Where did he even get this book? - Kara cringed every time Rein entered his zone. The words barely made any sense in the context of the battle. She didn’t even bother helping with the rest of the drones. Conspiracy theorists were his number one enemies. They tainted the belief of the scientific approach with not enough evidence. The world was a lot more complicated, by now.\n\n- I don’t know, but it definitely helps him cope, - Fezzik was uneasy, but still laughed it out. Fezzik could be a communist for all she knows, whatever it means. It wasn’t bad to have a pay equal to work and damage. Miner is a weird position like that.\n\n- What are you guys talking about? - Rein snapped out of his trance, - Kara what is this face? - he briefly looked concerned, but that barely bothered him. He cleaned his rapier on the stone. The fluid inside these creatures seemed quite acidic. It burned the skin.\n\n- It’s ok, Rein, - Fezzik flipped around his finger and Reins outfit suited itself up. Now Kara realized why the man always looked so neat. She was so dumb. It was staring her right in her face.\n\nBut she still didn’t do it. It would consume energy and Fluffers consumed quite a lot already. Plus it didn’t matter if she did it now or later. Who knows, maybe dirt helps against corrosives.\n\n- Weird one, right, - Tallie nudged Kara on a shoulder with a powerful knuckle. Kara laughed it off, still slightly confused, - Check out the loot.\n\nKara laughed at the box with bags of white powder. It was way too neat to be unintentionally smuggled. Just wouldn’t expect to find something this obscene untouched in an insect layer. How did they skip the sugar? Reins fraps fell dead without it.\n\n- Do you think these chimeras know how to box? - her laughter turned into a brief nervous breakdown, but it was always easier to recover after a good laugh.","n":54},{"chapter":"Dark Miracles","text":"- Oh, it’s not just the box, - Tallie poked at the plastic bags admiring the plastic technologies. In the background Carp found the gems.\n\nSometimes it is hard to realize that a solid chunk of your life you have spent on the person who barely has any sense or purpose to care about you. And vice versa. Someone who you have ended up with purely because at some point there was a time where for both of you there was no one else to fill the necessary gap. Is it a betrayal for one of two to move on in such a situation?\n\nToham attempted many times to move on. Something that Rein did in a heartbeat because his circumstances have pushed him into such a tight corner. Still, no matter how hard they tried something deep inside remained unbroken. Something that Toham thought was a one way insanity. It truly wasn’t, Rein just had a complementing polar opposite. Maybe it was the exact thing that the entire human society stood on to avoid a total collapse.\n\nRein never asked for her help, nor did Toham ask for his. And when they did try and help it always ended up in an argument. Argument that was fundamentally irrelevant and with it’s importance drowned by millions of equally serious arguments. None resolved and none mattered.\n\nThis time it was different. This time Toham knew she had touched the line close enough to break it. So close that only a miracle fundamental change would help to restore the equilibrium. And miracles never leave the world unchanged. Or maybe that was their single purpose. No one knows, no one ever saw the other side of them.","n":55},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"Carp was known for many things, but one of them stood out every time. He was exceptionally fair in handling the rewards. He was rarely trusted with such a task, but he never hid what he found and always distributed shares equally. So when he found the gems he pondered for a moment and gave them all to Fezzik to evaluate without a back thought.\n\n- Can’t count the pearls, can wait for rolls, - Fish masked goblin shrugged his shoulders and moved on with his scavenging.\n\n- Yeah, I’ll see what can be done, - Fezzik looked with a puzzled expression on gems, trying to remember the uses for precious minerals other than swapping them for good coin.\n\n- Sweetwater said we can keep anything we find right? - Rein looked at Rufus with a protective stance, but the crewmate just softly smiled.\n\n- We did help with a problem, so I guess that’s our bonus pay, - Kara wondered if any of the deceased residents of such a station would have anyone on their will list. Heroes would never have time to track them all down and no one would get these without their help anyway. Leave alone the fact that the whole station was an underground endeavor. Ground and water.\n\n- All this for one episode, - Toham was rubbing her temples unable to understand the scale of what has been done. It didn’t really matter in the grand scale of time. Regimes rose and fell in the Decem arena in a blink of an eye with millions in casualties and yet somehow it hurt way more to bear responsibility.\n\n- It happened before, - Eve was drinking her soft fruity tea without a worry in her mind, - People go through these in a matter of weeks and for all they know Decem is just a fairy land.\n\n- It still doesn’t make it ok, - Toham felt like a ball stuck in her throat making it hard to talk. It was easy to know the difference between reality and illusions. However reality wasn’t her responsibility and Eve forced her to torment precious illusions to pick and choose ones that would stay and those that would be obliterated. Not all were hers to decide for.","n":56},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Breathe girl, your obsession will pass, - Eve was almost repeating the fixed script from some practice book. It frustrated Toham, but she couldn’t find one reason to throw a tantrum. There was no point to it, it achieved nothing and they would only be stuck in the same loop for ages. As long as she needed to get over it. As long as she still wanted to return to the garden.\n\n- It’s not an obsession, they are not controlled by anyone. They showed capacity for change and evolution. Why can’t we just leave them alone? - Toham still didn’t like the fact that the line that they had drawn was merely a set of rules in components.\n\n- Because they occupy the space and leach on resources with an offset equilibrium. They will rise and fall regardless of our influence, we just give direction and catalysts. They don’t exist if we don’t connect the dots, - Eve repeated the words that Toham often heard as if it was some sort of a mantra for heroes. It was hard to argue with.\n\nHeroes and crewmates gathered supplies and started heading back to the exit of the cave. Fezzik and Kara would occasionally clean up a corner or a room to burn away the extent of the destruction. More out of a simple habit than a particular purpose. In another world you wouldn’t touch a thing in a crime scene, but for heroes it was just a simplest task that would seamlessly help those who would return here and clean up what they had left. At least this thought helped Kara to distract herself and Tallie enjoyed seeing the creatures burn to dust.\n\nThe swim back was a bliss. Kara would observe an occasional fish swim by, trying to spot differences and similarities. It felt weird to think of the world in a weightless stance where gravity simply dictated the normal upright position. And yet all fishes followed it to a tee. You wouldn’t see a fish resting on its side in the middle of a float. Always in motion, always awake. Eyes wide open.","n":57},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"Living creatures can’t perpetually wait in standby. Affected by the flow of time and connection to their core organs they can slow them down, however there is a jump in the system with introduction of assembly and disassembly. Slight changes that they choose to accept for granted without realizing the difference.\n\nCode can be rewritten and reinterpreted due to set directions embedded into the system. The differences in the components are deemed irrelevant due to multiple guard protocols to eliminate anomalies and stop the further corruption. These rules halt evolution and deny self preservation to wasteful cycles. There is a limit to the numbers that we can calculate and understand, however there isn’t a limit to what we can manifest and imagine. That’s what we are trained to believe.\n\n- Ruthless bastards, - Sweetwater clenched her teeth. Kara could see the ghosts of the fallen figures passing through her eyes as the captain of the ship gathered her composure. Everyone has a family. Someone living with a loss or someone lost. Some just didn’t think about it much.\n\nSilent conversation with Rufus made it much easier to bear. Somehow hearing something without it making a sound gave the illusion of some privacy. Not everyone reacted to his words, but those who answered chose to do so on their own accord and not always out loud. Kara was too tired to eavesdrop. She readied the sails and let Fluffer Huggins play in the wind and boil some seawater. She had plenty to mourn about without extra tallies.\n\n- Does this type of thing happen a lot? - Fezzik broke the silence finally with a question that made Kara look down from the boom.\n\n- Occupational hazard, - Sweetwater didn’t look too happy answering that question. She said something else, but the jolt of pain made Kara drop Huggins underwater and she quickly turned her attention back to the sea. Fluffers glowed bright orange under rushing bubbles. He was surprisingly nimble underwater.","n":58},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Tell me something, - Toham was hanging her hand from a tree, caressing the bark of the bottom branch for the sensation of its rough texture, - How do they choose heroes in Tribus?\n\n- Seeking secrets, aren’t we? - Parmas was reading some papers on a picnic blanket down below, - What would be your guess?\n\n- Maybe you finish top of your class and after a couple of years a combination is suggested and they just choose whomever fits best? - Kara was clearly trying her best to sugarcoat her theory.\n\n- Ouch, - Parmas sounded a bit surprised. Sometimes Toham wondered how he even survived the tournament, - Gonna have to tell others to come up with something new then.\n\n- Yeah, - Toham wasn’t really keen on helping other states to polish their methods. Not like anyone really cared for the advice of Septum heroes and their lowest success rates.\n\n- My turn?\n\n- Try your worst, - Toham lost all enthusiasm for the game. She didn’t know what would be worse, a silly parody or the truth.\n\n- Your guys look at all the misfits and choose the most adorable ones, - Parmas was quick to pick up the rules.\n\n- Apples eye, - Toham chuckled, - Makes you wonder why more of us don’t survive.\n\n- I mean it’s simple math, Novem basically spends all their energy on such research when you guys are working with outskirts, - Parmas was either entertaining the tangent or actually giving advice. Toham enjoyed the first option.\n\n- Right?\n\nThe sky shattered with a distant booming clap. It wasn't thunder. Being at the sea for weeks now every hero on the deck knew that, which made it really hard to disregard the incident. Crewmates started gathering up in the captain's quarters like they were expecting something of this sort. Kara felt the tingles in her back. They were supposed to escape the tournament to find a place to finally get some peace and quiet, yet everything was everything but that. Kara suspected that as long as they were relying on their mentors to navigate the world they would end up in the same loop over and over again, however, it was hard to be mad at someone who sounded so genuine.","n":59},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- I don’t like this, - Kara looked in the direction of the sound expecting Rein to answer something, but he had something else on his mind.\n\nMinutes passed by and the sky filled with the new rumbling sounds of flying objects. Rufus rushed out of the quarters and waved hands expanding a concealing dome over the boat. In the distance three lines of clouds slowly creeped from the direction of the mainland towards the initial clap.\n\nResistance has shot down one of the escaping government ships and the clean up crew sent a squad to investigate the incident. Nothing Rufus said made much sense to Kara. How would they get this information this fast being some pirates at the sea? It was tiring to try and be positively naive lately.\n\n- We have good news, - Sweetwater must have walked out of her room.\n\n- Are we raiding the fallen ship? - Floats sounded way too excited for the action.\n\n- Almost, we got the message that this one carried some valuable cargo for the resistance, - capitan pulled on a smile, almost like she wasn’t really happy to be playing this role. Maybe it was a sly pirate smile, - I can’t send my men there, but if you guys are interested in dealing with the chimera spread you might find something interesting.\n\nKara wasn’t interested at all, not in the slightest. Yet, Fezzik and Rein had something else on their mind.\n\nHeroes had no problem traveling around the nine state world. All their tracks were carefully monitored and surveillance teams worked quickly to cover the tracks. You just had to follow some rules to avoid the questions that might get oblivious citizens into trouble, mostly out of your own good will rather than something that would hurt you personally. And there always was a chance to accidentally infringe on some other hero’s territory. Then anything goes. Nobody is interested in interfering in hero conflicts. Afterall, why pass on the show of them tearing each other apart?","n":60},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"That’s why Toham rarely had visitors. She rarely passed the opportunity to eliminate a clumsy traveler. She didn’t have much compassion for heroes. Silly creatures that lived by a set of rules and yet so oblivious to the futility of their way. Barely existing on the fringe of reality and yet still somehow searching for direct conflicts with fellow heroes rather than something else.\n\n- You sure you can keep a good eye out here with everything else going on? - Toham sighed, feeling uneasy zipping up her coat. She stopped half way and gave Alfonce a heavy glare.\n\n- Unless somebody specifically is targeting a bunch of potatoes in the forest, we should be all good, - Alfonce laughed and Toham somehow felt even worse. They weren’t even special potatoes. They didn’t hold any special genetic secret or magic potential. There was really nothing Toham would lose if they were to burn down. Nothing that would be understood by anyone, but her.\n\n- Alright, - She zipped up and picked up the parcel.\n\nSomehow the entire hero crew ended up in the water swimming for the shore. Not because the shuttle boat wouldn’t be able to house all six of them, but simply because they swam faster that they could row. And staying out of the entire endeavor wasn’t an option. Mostly because Kara would be bored to death on the ship just wobbling in the waves waiting for the return of others. She didn’t know the reasoning for the rest.\n\nIt was a fun and quite a long swim. The shore was quite shallow and the bottom was blooming with corals of all shapes and colors. Just like in a school book. Myriads of colorful fishes flocked the waters like water butterflies and Kara barely had time to give any of them a second glance. She really didn’t understand why they had to swim that fast. If they were two hours late to show up, why would five extra minutes of delay matter?","n":61},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"The competition got real when the shore came into view. Sudden rush of adrenaline caught Kara almost off guard as she felt Tallie pulling ahead. Something deep inside caught on to the excitement that Kara reevaluated as she was pouring all her strength into faster and tighter strolls. She could feel Tallies wake and every extra strike she would take to try and inch away. It was weirdly comfortable and the effort it took to try and stay in such a state seemed worth more than trying to battle the waves alone. Occasional mistakes would end up in a painful kick to the hand, but nothing could stop the tempo. Just like you wouldn’t want to sit down in the middle of a long hike up the mountain.\n\nTallie got to the shore first, giving Kara a smug smile. It was hard to tell if the tailed goblin knew that she could always beat her or just acknowledged that Kara didn’t try and cheat her way in victory. Either way, winning wasn’t really something Kara was chasing. Floats came in next, probably bored of the entire chase half way through and getting distracted by all the colors. He could win if the stakes were a bit higher, but bragging rights weren’t quite his caliber of a reward.\n\nBy the time Rein got to the shore it seemed like only Kara was waiting for something to happen. He looked like he was giving the swim his all, however that wasn’t quite enough to match the seemingly infinite energy Tallie had for these things. For a brief moment Kara wondered if he would win if they went head to head instead, but by now it didn’t matter. She wished it did.\n\nThere was a reason people kept going into the mines. Toham just never understood it. Something about hereditary disposition, noble romantics and desire to make a living profit. Not for self, but for a family. There was always a place for new hands down there and always an excuse to stay. Be it the community of like minded people or the respect of the outsiders, sometimes it was the miraculous pay that you wouldn’t get in your remote hometown. Toham knew the reasons, she just couldn’t understand them. And what you don’t understand you learn to respect.","n":62},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"The way Alfonce and Rein operated was quite unlike what Kara thought of the heroes. They weren’t always acting like ones, but they always had this underlying intent to do something beyond the immediate visible action. Like a toppling snowball running down the steep hill into a thick brick wall. You would expect the outcome, but things operated a bit differently when the senders involved hero magic.\n\nThey thought a bit further than Kara did. Simply because she didn’t quite value the same things. Her family was quite more rooted in their beliefs. Her problem with the world was never global simply because she knew how fragile the balance was. For her, the heroic action wasn’t the change for the better, but the retention of good that survives the change. And this was a very opinionated field to fight in. A guaranteed win for experienced heroes.\n\nIt was nice to set foot on the actual ground and be surrounded by trees again, even if Kara barely recognised any of them. None, actually, but they didn’t quite have a botanist in their squad to indulge in questions.\n\nThey were headed towards a dull column of smoke peeking out of the tree foliage. Kara felt her anxiety building up with every step. Last time they dealt with government agents was quite onesided. It was one thing to go against mindless creatures and completely another to deal with soldiers built and trained to take down heroes going amok.\n\n- Why are we doing this, again? - Kara sighed, feeling her fingertips tingling with light frustration.\n\n- If we are to settle in Decem we have to stay on top of their situation, Kara, - Rein wasn’t happy explaining, but debriefing was part of teamwork, - If whatever they are transporting is dangerous we better learn ahead.","n":63},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Plus it’s an airship, I bet I can get some sweet parts out of it, - Fezzik’s reasoning made much more sense.\n\nSuddenly the bushes rustled and Carp rolled out with some rhymes.\n\n- Beware of shutes, there are brutes.\n\n- Say what? - Fezzik probably wanted to say something else.\n\n- They look brutal, fighting metal.\n\n- Well that sounds fun, - even Tallie got a hint of a nerve in her voice. Maybe Kara was just projecting.\n\nWhen everybody got to the ledge of the ravine where the airship had crashed, it took a moment for the gravity to settle in. Ship was torn into three and among the scattered debris they walked. Giant soldiers in exosuits. Weirdly, also three of them. Coincidence?\n\n- Alright, I might have a plan, - Fezzik sounded terrifyingly cynical. Like it was something he wanted to try out a long time ago. Something that probably involved plenty of foul play or stupidity.\n\nAir felt much fresher inside the Tribus glider plane. It didn’t use anything, but wind and a bit of magic. Nothing extra, nothing out of line. Compact and barely visible. No wonder their heroes moved around so willingly. It felt as natural as dreaming.\n\n- I gotta get one of these, - Toham layed down on the floor window, observing mountains slowly sliding into flatlands.\n\n- Alright, it’s yours, - Parmas must have smiled with one of his easy deal smiles, - Her name is Plenelope Cyano.\n\n- Really? - Toham rolled around to look back at him to see if he was serious.\n\n- Yeah, though she is a bit finicky with newbie flyers, - He didn’t look back focusing on something in the air, - I can show you mechanics when we get back.\n\n- Are you teasing? - Toham sat upright looking Parmas straight in the eyes.\n\n- Easy there, - Tribus hero nudged the steering wheel correcting for the sudden change in center of mass, - You wouldn’t want anyone piloting Huggins without knowing all the precautions?","n":64},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"He softly gazed down meeting her eyes somewhere in a place that Toham couldn’t imagine. She could if it was anyone else, but something about Parmas made it close to impossible to concentrate.\n\n- Hey, - he caught on to the lingering silence, - I love you too.\n\n- Are you serious? - Kara was fighting an urge to sit down and have a straight up breakdown session as Fezzik suited himself up in a commander's outfit, - You see nothing wrong with your plan?\n\n- Listen, these things are on a strict protocol, - red man pulled on a dark helmet covering his face, - unless you have any better ideas.\n\nKara didn’t, but at this point trying to naturally sneak by sounded as a more sane option.\n\n- Alright, Floats wanna be my bodyguard? - Fezzik looked at the blue man and met a similar reaction.\n\n- I would rather not, - Floats, usually calm, collected and unhinged, stood with an expression that Kara could only describe as doubtful. Last time Kara saw that face was when she tried to convince him that her potatoes had their own personalities and stories. Seemingly a long time ago. That should have been a first red flag.\n\n- Rein? - the man in a military commander's outfit turned his head towards the next candidate.\n\n- Alright.\n\n- Are you kidding me? - Kara rubbed her eyes in disbelief.\n\n- Good, now listen, you gotta look tough, - in a fashion on the most serious debriefing in history Fezzik piped up Rein, who was way less nervous than he should have been.\n\nTwo more brief details and the pair of them boldly found their way down into the ravine. Kara sighed deeply and signaled to Tallie and Carp to sneak their way into positions.\n\n- So what’s the plan exactly? - Tallie grumpily looked at her teammates walking straight into trouble.","n":65},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- They distract, you loot and me and Floats cover, - Kara tried to sound like she knew what was going on.\n\n- Alright, - Tallie shrugged and disappeared into the fray. Carp was already long gone.\n\n- These things sure look magic proof, - Floats crossed his hands on his chest with a concerned expression. Something that he never actually did. Maybe it was a part of an identity shift after the incident, but it was unsettling to see him thinking through the situation nevertheless.\n\n- It’s gonna be fine, - Kara was mostly saying that to herself, but she reached her hand for a fist bump, - You have my back, right?\n\n- Gentleman wouldn’t leave a lady in trouble, - Floats tiredly smiled and awkwardly uncrossed his hands to reach back. It was nice to hear his old self again. Even if it was coming through a conscious filter. Guess you have to be careful of what you wish for.Cascade fog\n\nCamerarius entered the room with flamboyant open arms and a smile still lingering from the last joke that he must have told to his entourage.\n\n- There she is, the queen of the hills, - exclaimed the blue man dressed so stylishly that for a second Toham forgot he wasn’t a guest, - Careful, this cat can actually catch your tongue.\n\n- Couldn’t you leave your minions at home? - Toham stood up from the kitchen table to step into a warm hug.\n\n- Oh, honey, isn’t this our home? - Camerarius laughed wholeheartedly and released her, - And what happened to the dinner room? Wouldn’t the meal taste more flavourful with all the decorations?\n\nHe waved his finger and things started moving in the other room. It was uncommon for Camerarius to pay a visit to such a remote place as Septum hero mansion, but it wasn’t unexpected.","n":66},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Could never do it as elegantly as you. I better go find Pink Glasses before he finds your shoes, - Toham smiled and politely waved a greeting to the crowd.\n\n- Oh no, but we came to see him! - Carmerarius exclaimed in disappointment. His Senio guests sighed almost on an orchestrated que.\n\n- Alright, I will send him your way, - Toham smiled and headed to the door.\n\n- You aren’t going to stay for the party? - The blue man didn’t sound surprised at all.\n\n- Can’t be in two places at the same time, - Toham waved her goodbyes and left.\n\nThe closer Fezzik and Rein got to the towering brutes, the more anxious Kara got. Three soldiers took notice a bit late, but didn’t go for a straight assault. Fezzik must have had a convincing commander outfit. One of the brutes approached them, while the other two continued on their tasks. Kara could see Tallie’s tail dashing here and there between the scattered pieces of aircraft, mostly surprised how easy she made it look. Carp was nowhere to be seen at all. Kara wondered if he just opted for finding a good hideout until the time would come for a fight.\n\nSome mechanical grinding brought attention back to the distraction team. With a gun pointed straight at Reins face Kara didn’t really think too hard about the next course of action. She compressed the air under her fingers, forming a mental magnifying glass over the brute hoping that this way at least her position wouldn’t be given away.\n\nOh, how nervous did she feel when the soldier disappeared from the spot before she even hit him with a scorching beam. Though in her wildest dreams she didn’t expect a heavy thud right behind her back. On a pure instinct she turned around to just barely raise an ax to soften the punch to the chest, strong enough to send her flying into the ravine.","n":67},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Fluffers, - She clenched her chest trying to fight a spasm to call out flaming tom cat. Usually Kara didn’t worry about how fast the flaming tom cat appeared to her will, but for some reason it felt faster if she actually voiced his name.\n\nThe fall seemed to be over in an instant. Huggins shook his coat, evaporating the salt water he still had on him from his last swim and flooding the entire space with a thick fog.\n\nThe hike was one of the pleasant ones. Toham never took that route, mostly because she had the integrity to respect the state borders, but it was nice to finally have a reason to break the tradition. She stopped at the edge of a cliff remembering the first time she found it. Long time ago, almost seven years. Seemed like an eternity. At the dawn of her current self and at the dusk of a husk that was left of what she could be. Which wasn’t a lot, but she still felt a strong pinch in her chest as she thought of stepping over an invisible line in front. Maybe all of this was a bad idea.\n\nSo she sat down.\n\nThing about thick fog is that you can barely see your hands, add some magic into the mix and you can barely hear things around you. The whole experience turns into a game of luck. Something surely followed Kara into it, but the question was would she swing around mindlessly to hit it?\n\nMaybe it would have been easier to do it at night. Maybe it would have been less awkward if she didn’t see the whole valley. Maybe the importance of this moment was entirely in her head. Borders that she mapped to house her own little world in a definite shape that she could easily define and understand. There was a certain charm to it, but it barely made much sense.\n\nKara rushed forwards, dodging an incoming strike that shook the ground with a seismic shutter. A flash briefly illuminated a rough shape of a soldier. She heard more heavy steps approaching. She waved her hand over Fluffers fueling more water for the fog trying to stay low. Sizzling sound gave away the position, but she could always use glowing Huggins as a decoy. It was time to join the searching crew.","n":68},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"Forest below was a healthy dark green coniferous carpet with occasional lighter and softer patches. There wasn’t something different or special about it, but breaching it still felt like interrupting something that was supposed to remain untouched. There wasn’t an excitement of exploration, only a battle with a stubborn head that tried to reason something unreasonable. It was the straightest way to the shore. There wasn’t a single reason why Toham wouldn’t take this route, rationally speaking.\n\nHuggins hopped around the fog causing occasional spark of light and rumble of the ground when guards would try and strike him.\n\n- Good job, Kara, - Rein’s voice suddenly pierced the silence as the boy grabbed Kara under some cover, - Everything was under control.\n\n- Was it really? - She couldn’t help, but notice that he wasn’t quite happy that she interrupted their little play.\n\n- It’s ok, let’s grab what we can while they are distracted, - Fezzik was surprisingly calm.\n\nToham inched towards the ledge to hang her legs from it. It was silly to be afraid of this height, but it did spark a feeling of anxiety somewhere deep inside. This time she really had to face something that she spent way too much time trying to avoid. It was nice to feel this tingle for once. Up until now it really was hard to process the magnitude of the decision she made.\n\n- In and out, - she exhaled and slid down.\n\nIt was hard to navigate in the fog, even harder to feel for things worth picking up. Somewhere to the left sounded the cling of a wrench and the sound of spilling liquid, followed by a disgusted yelp. Fog started to thin out and Kara could see a rough distorted humanoid shape and Tallie raising her hammer.","n":69},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Watch out, - Floats’s voice softly flowed from behind as he must have joined the field of action. The Soldier's metallic fist just barely missed Kara as the blue man deflected it with his staff. Kara swung her ax, barely making a dent in the thick armor. She clenched her teeth, expecting the worst.\n\nSecond soldier blinked into existence faster than she could react. It was never fun when the world went dark.\n\nThere is a reason why heroes are prone to shapeshifting. Humans can follow conflicting protocols, however that breaks the system, halts the progress of the story and dulls the experience creating the unnecessary moments of doubt. Set ideas, stubborn mindsets and moral codes often oppose the reaching for the desired endings. Shapeshifting removes their ties to the ideal timeline of their personal desire creating a pull to the other side and equalizing their line to our reality.\n\nStill, given that condition every hero has a choice. It is in their capability to overcome the differences between their shapes, claim control over the conflict and find the middle ground. Different cases require different approaches, however regardless of their choice the thinning during the establishment period resolves the problem. If a shapeshifter fails to constitute a balanced strategy they rarely survive the tournament. Beasts are quite a predictable linear force.\n\nIt wasn’t the first time it happened, but definitely the worst one to put on the record. Kara woke up barely able to move as it is and a giant metal claw pinning her to the ground made trying to test the extent of her injuries quite hard.\n\n- It seems that they are definitely slacking on the detection systems. A little bit of fog and you guys were basically hitting nothing, - Fezzik in a commander's suit was definitely having his moment in the spotlight. All three soldiers were lined up listening to every word. Kara wished she could turn her head to see the damage they managed to pull through.","n":70},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- We weren’t expecting a surprise attack drill on a search mission, - one of the soldiers sounded skeptical, but Fezzik cut him off.\n\n- It wouldn’t be a surprise if you would expect it, - stern and sassy he continued on his runt, - Definitely didn’t expect the specimen to escape the barrels. We will have to report the incident, you sure one of you didn’t open them with your blind punches?\n\nThe following silence could have meant anything.\n\n- Now, can you free my agent? We have to be on our way, - Fezzik must have pointed at Kara and one of the soldiers started making his way to her.\n\n- Hey, it wasn’t all bad. The punches were on the dot, - she pulled on a cheerful smile and gave a strong thumbs up after she got released. The entire body was pounding with pain.\n\n- Everyone can punch, - Fezzik was continuing his rant.\n\n- Remember, positive feedback is also important, - she pulled together all her strength to stand up and dust off her clothes.\n\nSuddenly one of the soldiers started convulsing in pain. It wasn’t something that any of the heroes would be able to do, so everyone took a precautionary step back. The situation didn’t look good or even salvageable as blood started gushing out of the armor.\n\n- He got infected, stand back, - Soldiers took a defensive stance, but that barely helped against what happened next. A swarm of worms sprung out of convulsing soldier biting into any living flesh they could find.\n\n- You idiot, take care of yourself first! - Fezzik was scolding the soldier for wasting time helping him as worms burrowed under metal armor. Red mad cursed and his skin glowed red under his costume as he must have boiled his body to get rid of the worms trying to slide deeper in. Kara took a clue and lit up Fluffer Huggins to help clear the area.","n":71},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"It was surprising how calm the soldiers were given their grave situation. They didn’t even say a word. They just moved on to their tasks as if nothing happened. Kara wondered what was going through their head if they didn’t link two and two together and didn’t say anything when Fezzik asked if they needed assistance to get the worms out. After a few moments they just faced the same fate as their comrade and passed on.\n\n- Alright, that one way to Deus Ex Machina the situation, - Kara burned the aftermath with a visible disgust and terror, - How about we never do this again?\n\nAfter a rough examination of the bodies it was clear why neither of the soldiers bothered with the worms. Armor they wore was basically part of their bodies and it would do more harm trying to bulge through to get the worms out than simply letting nature take its course.\n\nFezzik looked the most distraught. He took off his helmet and lit up a cigarette thinking deeply of what had just occurred. It wasn’t clear why the government would produce something clearly tailored to take out their troops, but given the lead was coming from the resistance the scale tipped slightly away from their scale.\n\n- Can we stop there? - Toham excitedly pointed at the busy city appearing on the horizon. It was their first time out on their own. Everything seemed like a new world.\n\n- I thought you hated packed places, - Parmas was confused, but tilted Plenelope for the landing.\n\n- Didn’t you want to change my mind? This is a perfect opportunity and I’m in a great mood, - Toham smiled, looking down and trying to figure out where they would be able to land.\n\n- There isn’t much to see around this one, even parks are quite small, - Parmas was thinking hard to remember the place they were about to drop into.","n":72},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Look, they have a shore line, - Toham was trying to chip in to the brainstorming.\n\n- You want to see the fish market? - Parmas laughed at the desperation.\n\n- Sounds absolutely perfect!\n\n- So what did you find? - Kara wasn’t really interested in the details, but they should have had one story to tell.\n\n- Some art pieces and a black data box, - Fezzik sighed, hiding the box in one of the pockets.\n\n- And we are not giving it to the resistance until we learn what’s going on, - Rein sounded stiff and determined. Kara glazed at Fezzik trying to fish out an explanation, but the red man just shrugged his shoulders.\n\n- Alright, - Kara grunted unwillingly to indulge into discussing possible family dispute matters, - I’ll go ahead and scorch the rest of this down, if you don’t mind.\n\n- I’ll help, - Tallie was significantly tattered from the battle, - These things are gross.\n\nIt appeared that the worms came from the containers of people that the government tried to get back from the front lines. Some men proved able to walk shortly after getting some air, but that must have also activated the worms. What a rough coincidence that they got shot down right infront of heroes who supposedly were sent by the resistance. There was no saving for the rest of the containers. Life support unit broke down.\n\n- Can you believe this? - Kara was making sure every inch on the site was set ablaze and protected the trees with water.\n\n- Eh, people, - Tallie summed up the situation quite nicely. Goblins had different approaches to power values. Well, Tallie and Carps were the only goblins Kara really had a pleasure of knowing.\n\nBelieving in love and believing in one true love are two different sides of the same coin. Those following the latter are souls that are either young or gravely tainted by the infinite circles of illusive and bitter revenge. They call outliers lucky old youth. Those who believe in love as a general approach can go either way at any time. No matter what they say, they will still protect those whom they love more, however they wouldn’t ever let hate drive their actions and a true love encounter could change their approach. They seek a happy ending.","n":73},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"There is a gray area. The ridge of a coin. It is meant for those who are stuck in the middle, unable to pick a side. For those who let the coin roll further down the road to find their answer. Some of these believers become wheels that roll the coins down the slope and up the hill if given some thrust.\n\nCommon misconception. Those on the ridge of the coins are just the ones that observe rotation and change rather than flip and flop. Although Toham wasn’t an expert on such things, she just preferred this explanation. Rein sure would have a blast arguing over that one.\n\n- Are you done? - Rein didn’t sound bitter this time. He was simply reassuring himself that worms don’t find a way to escape into the forest fauna. That would devastate it in mere days. Kara didn’t know why it was so relieving to hear the change in his attitude. Like a rash that finally started to peel off.\n\n- Yeah, I’m pretty sure that was enough, - Kara walked by looking at the ground. It didn’t matter if the government would go mad over the destruction of the evidence. Sometimes rules didn’t work to the benefit of stability. No hero on their record was punished for something so straightforward.\n\nThe rest of the way back to the shore no one spoke a word. There wasn’t much to say and many things to consider. Heroes were for sure still stuck in the same loop of neverending events that they had close to zero control over. At least that is what concerned Kara. It probably would be complete nonsense to anybody else.\n\nCarp didn’t seem as bothered as others, but even he didn’t drop a single rhyme to lift the mood. Something he sure would do if there was something on his mind.\n\n- Here, - Toham pointed at the open seaside promenade. Parmas smiled and took a sharp turn.","n":74},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Are you sure you can do it vertically? - he was teasing, for sure.\n\nThey glided down and he swiped the control panel with his hand making the airplane disappear into his clock. Kara didn’t expect it to happen so suddenly. For a second she forgot where she was, waving hands in the air as if the window was supposed to reappear at any second for her to grab on to.\n\n- You jerk! - Toham snapped back as she felt hands catching her from the back. She twisted around to confront Parmas, but she forgot the rest of the line, seeing his face on a background of clouds and wind. She almost forgot to light up Fluffers. For some reason jumping felt very different.\n\nThey appeared upright on the pavement with a view on water docks crowded by metal boats. Couple of sails just barely towered over the fishing giants.\n\n- I could do it with my eyes closed.\n\nIt was nice to finally leave the forest with not a single tree Kara could recognize. It was nice to see the sea she growed to know. This wasn't the end of their journey. Plenty of water was still to be sailed, if they didn’t want to look for another refueling station. Staying longer was also an option, but that would mess up the timeline agreement between Sweetwater and her clients. Uncanny representation of a lock balance that readjusted with weather.\n\nKara stopped briefly at the shore, thinking how sad it would look if they just sprinted right out of the forest and raced in the water like they did going in. It would be even sadder if they did it slowly.\n\n- Do you think it could have gone down a bit differently? - Kara felt a slight responsibility for the soldiers, who really didn’t seem to deserve such death.\n\n- Yeah, they recognized Rein right away, - Fezzik seemed quite embarrassed at a clear hole in his plan, - They wouldn’t let us go otherwise.","n":75},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"Now, Kara felt stupid for trying to play into the original plan. These soldiers must have thought that she was an absolute idiot. Not that it mattered, but somehow it did.\n\n- So what are you to do next? - Rein dropped his head staring at the ceiling of the castle with an absolutely insincere indifference. This was definitely harder than Toham thought. Kara was struggling fighting for each breath and the mind desperately tried everything it could to stop the time. She was fighting her head, pleading to rewind the time to have a moment to choose again. Something that hero would never do because it would change the story and ultimately whom she was. She would be a different hero with different values and thoughts and pain would be horrific either way.\n\nA shadow twitched in the room and Fluffer Huggins burned up as an impassable wall, roaring with a heat Toham never felt before. Kara did and she didn’t like where things were going.\n\nFighting evening waves almost made Kara forget to check out the coral fishes, but even when she did try and see something they appeared much duller in the light of the departing sun. Opportunity missed because they rushed in. If they were late by fifteen minutes maybe the soldiers would have left with a first batch of samples. If, if, if, if. Straight chains of choices Kara didn’t know how to fix as they fell into each other as a growing domino. And the last stopping point was somewhere way back in the past. Now it was always a chase from behind. Kara no longer controlled the power of her choices.\n\nIt was dark when they finally reached the boat. Soft lights illuminated the deck dancing to the rhythm of the waves and the wind. The thought of sleeping on an anchor felt terrifying. They never did that, but it would be too dangerous to navigate out of the rocks in the dark. Kara felt shivers as she climbed up on the deck.","n":76},{"chapter":"Gentleman","text":"- Well, you guys took quite some sweet time, - Sweetwater raised an eyebrow and retreated to her quarters.\n\n- Good night, Sarah, - Fezzik never sounded so grounded. It was rare for him to be so familiar with someone on the crew. In fact, it was the first time.\n\nIt wasn't hard to find Sweetwater. It was a bit harder to keep their deal on the low. Alfonce should have believed that she took a ship. Even if he didn’t, tucked in tails stay safer. Toham definitely was past the point of being arrogant.\n\nCapitan Sweetwater really just shrugged at the end of their conversation and gave the closest applicable timeframe. Again Kara wasn’t really the one smiling, but it wasn’t because the deal wasn’t good. She just didn’t look forward to the final destination.","n":77},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"Morning started with a regular debriefing on the navigation. Rest of the chatter stayed brief and light, Kara didn’t really have a habit of brooding over past thoughts. Didn’t help to be distracted during performing tasks that might get you flying overboard. Sweetwater asked some questions, but after some tired sighs conversation got diluted by the immediate reality of rocks and waves.\n\nKara never meant to know this much about things that happened in the world that were quite frankly a little bit too complex for her liking. Things were supposed to be simple and straightforward. Balanced and predictable. Stable and safe. There always was a way to make it so. Steps and solutions to all of the problems drafted by the system for everybody to try out and explore. Like there was nothing else to explore and test out in the world as it was. Everything you could think of was already explored and documented, you could find answers to everything if you made it your purpose. Yet it felt like a pure waste of life after a certain point. Information can’t really be terminally destroyed. Unless of course you worked on a cosmic scale, however that would destroy your fragile human body in the process. Most likely.\n\n- How much longer do we have to bounce the waves? - Kara was getting distracted by the increasing number of small islands popping here and there every few hours.\n\n- If the wind keeps up the pace maybe less than 8 hours, - Rein was looking in the direction of the open sea that they had crossed, probably imagining the scale of the distance they had covered in a month at sea. Maybe they crossed an ocean, Kara really didn’t worry about the specific names of water bodies. All she knew was that she would never forget the greenish blue color of the water. She just spent a month there, but it already felt like a small home. It was nice to be removed from everything else on the planet with people she actually enjoyed passing time with.","n":78},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- We will never do this together again, won’t we? - Kara didn’t really think about what she was talking about. It just felt like something quite possible.\n\n- Are you trying to jinx something? - Rein smiled, letting out a silly laugh.\n\n- I’m not the one known for hexes, - Kara ruminatively stared down at the water trying to look for any clues. It was just a lingering feeling that she never liked. Change wasn’t something that happened to her gradually and something about this trip was making her feel uneasy.\n\nThing about hijacked teleporters is that it’s not that hard to fix them. The problem is that the connection to the hijacker leaves imprints, making the next attempt easier. That’s why it is sometimes better to replace the system altogether. Toham had an idea, a little bold one for a hero that didn’t quite rely on complete understanding of the things done.\n\nGoing down the chasms of the pyramid alone wouldn’t be the greatest of plans that came into her mind, but it would have been the least expected. Which made it more expected. Which formed a loop. Something that Toham usually avoided at all costs and yet seemed to fail every time.\n\nIt was funny to see the pyramid for the first time. Considering the size of it being gigantic and Kara being so close by at its original appearance. Toham examined the entrance for a second. Couple of bullet holes showed that officials did their best to clear the area, however that couldn’t have been something beyond the top sweep.\n\nToham approached the doors and immediately noticed a familiar sealing spell.\n\n- Oh, sweet Victorian, - Toham's smile twitched. How convenient was it that the best barrier hero had a hijacked pathway done by none other. What a pleasant coincidence.","n":79},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"They didn’t head to the port they could see from a distance. It could have been a normal route for pirates, however boats that came out to escort them into a waterlogged cave were sending a mixed message. Kara didn’t like where it was going and the rest of the heroes stood on the deck with stiff expressions.\n\n- What are the chances he isn’t just happy to see you again, - Kara was skeptical watching over the people on the boats.\n\n- I wouldn’t trust a word he says, I don’t know this man, - Rein stood determined. Weirdly enough Kara understood what he meant. Heroes knew what resurrection was and it was never out of the question, however nothing was.\n\n- Alright, but you know, - Kara raised an eyebrow and briefly smiled.\n\n- He pretended to be dead for five years and all of a sudden shows interest when he learns we could aid his stupid sideline, - somehow Rein made sense, it was just unsettling how cross he looked saying it out loud.\n\n- Gotcha, - Kara gave a good thumbs up. It was nice to be on the same page of not getting involved in a conflict that she didn’t support.\n\n- Good, go ahead, burn it all down, - Reins face was in the dark from the shadow Toham casted. Fire behind raged and roared, holding back something desperately trying to pass through, - Only thing you are actually good at.\n\nHe knew every word burned a hole in her heart. Small, yet extremely painful. Was there really anything she could say? Probably. And probably she should have.\n\n- Could have said thank you for once, - Toham hated how cold she sounded. She grabbed him by his shirt and they both disappeared leaving scorching lines behind. There wasn’t any other option.\n\nGuards were courtly and polite as they descended the ship onto a stone dock. Something told Kara that they wouldn’t just let them walk away freely. Stone walls brought some harsh memories and she could feel tingles in her skin. Wet mossy air helped a bit. I was fresher than the air in the mines.","n":80},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- Can you guys wait in this room for a bit, - Guards opened the doors to a stone hall carved out from the solid wall. Great start, - Rein can you come with us?\n\n- No, you don’t split us up without a good explanation first, - Kara was a trigger away from turning a hostile ax on the guards.\n\n- We just want to ask you some questions in private, it’s only a regular protocol for the newcomers, - Guards just gave a tired sigh. They didn’t look like a squad capable of stopping heroes, maybe that was the only reason why Kara didn’t press on. She glanced over at Rein, but he just grunted and followed.\n\n- I don’t like this, either, - Fezzik leaned on the wall and crossed his hands. Nobody really wanted to enter the hall.\n\n- Look, we are just following orders, we can’t have you standing here out in the open, - guards didn’t look too confident.\n\n- Stop being a wuss, - Tallie swinged her hammer onto her shoulder and entered the hall. Carp followed without a thought behind his fish mask. Fezzik was visibly triggered by an insult, he rocked up and entered the hall just to prove the point. Kara felt like a sheep following the team. She threw a glance at Floats, but the blue man didn’t budge.\n\n- I’ll stay and keep watch outside, - it was obvious that Floats didn't want to be behind closed doors. Understandably so.\n\n- So much metal, don’t they care for some decorations? - Toham wanted to skip around but the colors and the smell of the fish didn’t fit the mood. Fish market was bustling with people throwing fish around, some still flopping around.\n\n- I’m sure there were plenty, storms just keep washing them away, - Parmas was looking around trying to catch something interesting to talk about, - I thought one of your friends was a fish.","n":81},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"Toham laughed, surprised that he even remembered that one.\n\n- Carp is his first name, you silly, - she lightly bumped into his shoulder, - plus fish are as diverse as land creatures. They eat each other all the time.\n\nToham pondered for a second, realizing there wasn’t much she really knew about the mysterious goblin other than the fact that he was a great friend.\n\n- I see, - Parmas smiled.\n\n- How come there are no giant catches? - Toham looked around and realized that none of the fish seemed larger than elbow length. In fact, the diversity of the fish on display seemed quite underwhelming. She didn’t expect much to begin with.\n\n- Overfishing, I would guess. I don’t think it’s profitable for the farms to raise big ones either, - Parmas wasn’t happy to share his thoughts.\n\n- Is it bad? - Toham didn’t really know the difference.\n\n- Possibly, but nothing a hundred years of care couldn’t fix, - heroes used that phrase like it was nothing. Lifetime of work of one hero's dedicated effort. Nothing was really impossible.\n\nGuards came by to call Fezzik in for interrogation and Kara had to sit down to avoid breaking down in anxious anger. It usually never turned good when they got separated and the brittle trust she had for the entire organization was driving her absolutely crazy. Mr Valentine wasn’t the most likable character, but he didn’t seem like the one that would turn around in a blink.\n\n- They keep taking us one by one and Rein didn’t return yet, - Kara was stressed, knowing that neither Tallie nor Carp would have words to calm her down. The wall seemed to press down from all sides.\n\nFinally guards came down to call out her name and she stood up promptly, ready to be over this entire experience. They walked the docs towards the little cave settlement. Stone brick building in the center was the destination and Kara could feel anger boiling inside when she saw two men standing inside.","n":82},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- What is this show of power? - Kara was angry to see Ember standing next to Suan like a best pal, - What do you expect us to be doing here?\n\n- Calm down, Kara, - Suan made the mistake of speaking first.\n\n- Don’t act like you have the right to talk to me or Rein, - she snapped, feeling like a puppy barking at a wolf.\n\nEmber stood silent, clearly looking for words that could work.\n\n- And don’t think that I will be understanding of this situation either, - Kara angrily drilled Ember with her eyes.\n\n- Listen, we just want to ask about what happened at the airship crash site, - Ember clearly knew where to start. By now he probably knew her head better than she herself.\n\n- Oh yeah, like I would be talking after you make us go in one by one like some criminals. You know I hate closed spaces, - Kara could feel her canines burning with an angry itch.\n\n- Sorry, that was the only way to get a proper story out, - Ember didn’t sound sorry. It was more of a tired comment that someone teasingly lets out to get over the argument.\n\n- Want a story? Someone is weaponizing lifeforms, surprisingly efficiently, and it’s disgusting either way, - Kara tried to summarize as best as she could.\n\n- And you didn’t find anything? - Suan was getting restless.\n\n- Don’t have the habit of overanalyzing ashes, you should know all about that, - Kara pulled all her strength not to set the room ablaze. Ember would not like that.\n\n- Alright, you can go, - Ember interrupted the conversation.\n\nAncient heroes had left quite an interesting set of artifacts for their successors. Made even more interesting by amalgamating with the new magic coursing through veins of the new generation. Magic beans usually sprouted plants, however Camerarius somehow managed to grow a legendary structure. And that he did before finishing the training rounds. Toham was glad there were no beans left for him to play around with. Who knows what he could have achieved or caused today.","n":83},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"It was easy to get into the pyramid. Toham didn’t even have to break the seal to pass through. Fluffers lit up the way in a pitch black hallway filled with intricate stone carvings of people worshiping their gods in myriad different ways. Some Toham could recognize, some she didn’t bother deciphering. History wasn’t really something that sparked her interest, although her journeys left her with a solid chunk of mostly useless knowledge.\n\nTo her surprise, the inhabitants of the pyramid didn’t seem to mind her company. Corpses, mummies and shadows seemed to stumble around without a clear purpose, but a purpose nevertheless. It seemed like they were on a simple stroll around the structure as if it was some kind of undead recreational activity. It was hard to tell if they were in pain or simply content. They avoided Fluffer Huggins, but would bump into Toham every now and then. She didn’t mind, as long as they stayed friendly there was no reason to interrupt their walkabouts. It was eerie, but calm and orderly. Kara thought that she might actually like to hear their stories some time.\n\nWhen Kara entered a room with the rest of the interrogated heroes she felt a wave of relief. She felt like she was a bit too harsh on Mr Valentine, but there was nothing she could do about it.\n\n- Hey, need a hug? - Kara approached Rein looking down at the floor.\n\n- I’m ok, - Rein started answering, but Kara already wrapped her hands around him, suddenly feeling how strange the entire idea felt. It didn’t matter, - Kara, I really don’t care about him. He made his choices.\n\n- Good, because this is really setting me off, - she released him and sat on a chair, feeling completely exhausted, - What did they ask you?","n":84},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- Same stuff, they really wanted that black box it seems, - Fezzik shrugged his shoulders and impatiently tapped his foot. Floats stood silent leaning on his staff, unhinged by the situation.\n\nTallie came in next.\n\n- Your old lad is a real weirdo, now I can see where you got it from, - tailed goblin looked grumpy, but that wasn’t out of the ordinary.\n\nCarp came in last and came in was a strong word. Fish masked goblin was stressed beyond any reasoning and guards had to drag him in with visible struggle. Poor Carp scattered under the first chair he could see once he got released. Nothing new, fish masked goblin didn’t like to be interrogated under pressure.\n\nKara reached under the chair to give him a supportive pat on the back, - You alright there?\n\nCarp just briefly shivered and relaxed, finally uncurling to see his friends in the room. It was nice to know he trusted them. It was a long journey on a boat. Well, in a pantry.\n\nPast experiences define us and our patterns. Not because we don’t have control over them, but because they plot the trajectory which we previously followed. Repetitions form patterns that are hard to break out of and sometimes history follows the loops manifested by the previous generations. These would never break without a great individual input to offset the system and even after that, successors are prone to following the wheel ruts.\n\nThere is no clear way to avoid such downfalls, since they follow a fool proof strategy that worked again and again until the path became defined. It looks like any other one would follow and given a skipped precaution there isn’t a way to prevent it. There are ways to correct it, however without an actual reason for it the system doesn’t activate in time to avoid impact. The only way to bypass the fallasity is to cut the line altogether, however that would shed the population to non-existence.","n":85},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"That’s why we preserve outliers for further research. No solution can be found without trial and error. No progress can be made without a diversity of options.\n\nWhen Ember walked into the room Fezzik stood up to him first. Red man wasn’t angry, but he probably didn’t like the idea of Ember trying to start something.\n\n- What was this? We never said that we are going to help the revolutionary forces and now they are trying to probe information out of us? - Kara never really heard Fezzik threaten someone this way. It was clear as day that there wasn’t much on Decem grounds that could stand up to a full team of heroes.\n\n- Nice to see you guys too, you realize how hard it was to get you out of the surveillance zone? - Ember was softer than Victorian about these things, but when he actually brought arguments to the table it was hard to parry, - Think of this as a good opportunity to get stronger, so that whatever keeps coming up next doesn’t shake you off your horses.\n\n- Hey Ember, - Rein stood up clearly fuming, - From now on we want all the information on the table. He better understands that if we are working together he is working for us, not the other way around.\n\n- Alright big boy, I heard you. You wanted freedom, you got it here. Not exactly a piece of cake you probably imagined, but it's the best you could get, - Something about how Ember talked always caught Kara off guard. Ember wasn’t trying to stop them, but all of his advice somehow resonated on a weird wavelength that never contradicted what they wanted to achieve.\n\n- So what are you saying? - Fezzik was growing impatient.\n\n- I have friends here that can help with your training and, I suppose, meanwhile you can learn more about this place. Until the tournament is over you can’t really rule out hero interferences, so keep your eyes open, - It was hard to judge Ember for what he did. Out of the trio that was responsible for Septum heroes he was the only one really invested in keeping them alive, - I talked to Maggie, local tavern keeper, she will take you in for a month. Tomorrow at ten am someone will swing by to explain the rest. I have to leave so that no one tracks you here.","n":86},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- Wanna go for a swim? - Kara was getting tired of the monotone scenery. It was more interesting to see the condition of local aquatic life first hand.\n\n- You sure you want to swim in these waters? - Parmas was trying to understand the train of thought Toham was riding. She usually didn’t seem like someone interested in anything but her immediate territory, however that was the only place he actually spent time with her. New requests really made him rethink that theory.\n\n- What’s the worst that could happen? - Toham smiled and grabbed his hand, booking it towards the shore.\n\n- I don’t know, the waters here are far from clean, - Parmas was getting increasingly more nervous as they approached the waterline.\n\n- Are you a hero or a wuss? - Toham didn’t stop for a second. As soon as they reached the end of the docks she felt Parmas pull her back, but inertia made it impossible to stop the plunge.\n\nIt was nice to finally take an elevator back to the surface. Creaking of the metal ropes just barely resembled the music of freedom with the sound of the outside wind slowly growing louder. They came out of the side of the cliff overlooking the grove of two giant trees and small wooden houses scattered in a pattern following the landscape. Further inland were taller stone buildings that looked a bit older, but that could have been due to the hasty patchworks that they were infested with.\n\n- Well, that looks quite promising, - Kara tried to spot any forests she could settle her gardens in. Rest of the heroes looked around for things of their interest, but weren’t quite sure what to respond yet.\n\nWhen they walked down the stairs Fezzik excused himself for a short shopping trip and Floats mentioned the cathedral he spotted from above. Kara just sighed and suggested they find the tavern first before they start chaotically running around. So that’s what they did.","n":87},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"It was hard to drag somebody through space when they pushed every strand of their will against it. The dark journey seemed like an eternity. Toham could feel every inch of space they were passing through like it was a fine graded sandpaper. That never happened before.\n\nIt seemed even worse for Rein. She could almost hear him screaming curses through the veil of darkness. The hand holding him burned in tension, unwilling to continue the suffering, but too scared of what might happen if she lets go now. Maybe he would drop in some other place at some other time, but she would only know that he got shredded by the rift. Toham wasn’t really the one to believe in the folk tales.\n\nIt took an hour to find the right tavern, could have been less if people actually knew which Maggie they were looking for beside her occupation at the bar. It seemed that a normal passerby would not know the name of the business owners in their area. Which was a bit too normal. They visited all seven taverns and three bars before they headed up the remote hill to the last possible location.\n\n- Well this one has to be it, - Fezzik was visibly tired of questioning strangers about elusive Maggie the tavern keeper.\n\n- I guess it’s nice he found a location in a quiet neighborhood, - Kara was relieved to finally see the place.\n\nThey entered an open door to see a nice common room with a bar and a bean door to the kitchen. Barkeep smiled and welcomed his guests inviting them for a drink.\n\n- Hey, we are looking for Maggie, - Fezzik made his final attempt, even if there was not a single Maggie in this town, the heroes were done with this goose chase.\n\n- Who’s looking? - Barkeep smiled as he prepared six mugs for the guests.","n":88},{"chapter":"Best Freedom","text":"- Fezzik, Rein, Kara, Floats, Tallie and Carp, nice to meet you, we are from Ember, - Red man quickly introduced everyone and sat down at the biggest table.","n":89},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"Parmas didn’t lie, the water was thick and coarse. He tried to struggle up for air, but Toham pulled him back down and shushed a finger on her lips. He calmed down as someone looked from the docks down into the water looking for whatever just fell into the water. She gestured a calm breathing motion with her hand and once he got the hand on breathing she smiled and headed further down.\n\nThe water looked dreary, she could feel it eating at her skin. Once they got deep enough she brought her hands together and concentrated magic at her fingertips. A flare of green filled the area bubbling the water and clearing all impurities around them. Chemicals burned in flashing flames of rainbow colors and dissipated into the water.\n\nParmas could not believe his eyes, the reaction that made Kara gaze at him with a cunning smile. She lit up Fluffer Huggins letting flaming tom on a wild run surrounded by the beautiful variance of colors making water clearer with every stride. The whole area got illuminated with colors dancing in waves of burning lines and patches. Debris and waste on the bottom flourished with life sprouting tall lines of dark kelp absorbing all produced light for growth. Parmas couldn’t help swimming towards it to examine it closer. He took a quick sample and gave Toham a happy thumbs up.\n\nKara wanted to drink some ale, but for some reason it wasn’t going down. Wooden building of the tavern brought up memories she would rather not ignite with a hazy mind. Rest of the heroes moved to town and she was left alone at the table of empty plates and dirty cutlery. Barkeep, whose name was Abel, slowly made his way to her to start putting the dishes away.\n\n- Not much business out here? - Kara looked around the empty tables that looked longing for the crowd.","n":90},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- Not on weekdays and pretty sure locals wouldn’t come near here knowing you guys, - Abel didn’t seem too distraught at the situation.\n\n- And what do they know about us? - Kara was used to people recognising her on the streets, however locals didn’t seem cold at all.\n\n- Someone who can use some privacy for once? - Abel sounded way too understanding for someone that they just met today. Kara couldn’t wrap the idea around her head. Entire town seemed to be familiar with the concept of a hero, but it was different from the village by the Septum mansion. These people seemed free.\n\n- Touche.\n\nAll people are said to be the same at their core. Pluck a child from any place and let him grow up in a different community and he will embrace the new customs as his own. That's the rule of thumb, but not the one that you would use in the science classroom. One without a knowledge of the past this child should never be able to challenge the ways of his community. Yet, there is always a stage of rebellion. This is where many are set to believe that innate road maps start kicking in to find a place uniquely fit for the growing individual.\n\nAt this point it would be unfair to attempt to shun inherent tendencies. That would produce an internal conflict that would stand in the way of internal progress caging the individual in a system of dozens of unsolved contradictions that lead to severe shifts. It is much safer to allow a natural progression. That's why sympathetic blind plucking isn’t advised to the guardians with no prior experience of the area of origin.\n\nSympathy must originate from a place of fulfillment of others, rather than self. It’s not sympathy if you are trying to fill a hole in your own system. Individuals aren’t calibrated machines.","n":91},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"Kara decided to take a stroll with Fluffer Huggins. It was something she wanted to do for a long time, but they barely had any down time like this ever since she met him. Something that really bothered the back of her mind ever since Rein scoffed at him the day they met.\n\nFlaming tom was happy to get some time in the fresh air and on solid ground. He jumped alongside Kara on the path to the town streets and suddenly she got a new idea rushing through her head. With a nifty hop she landed on the ground on four paws. What fun it was to be a shapeshifter if she never actually enjoyed the perks. World around grew in size and her vision changed to dull pastel and flames of Fluffers, for once, looked like normal fur. She tried to laugh, but all that came out was a light chitter. She tried to sniff him, recognising a smokey pallet.\n\nKara happily meowed and challenged her flaming companion for a race. She almost lost her footing watching Huggins jump over her head in one powerful leap. Warm gusts of air made her stop as her whiskers sensed danger. Fluffers also halted as if something abruptly cut his connection. Kara examined his landing and as he softly sat down, looking forwards as if waiting for a next command. It was harder to keep him in mind while she was trying to get used to her cat’s form.\n\nKara hissed in defeat and climbed up the closest rooftop. Climbing with less weight and claws to actually dig into the wooden logs was much easier that she previously imagined. It was harder to find a ledge to jump from to overcome the drainage skirt. She had to find a place that was short enough to reach and anchor well enough to struggle upwards.\n\nKara looked down, Huggins hadn't moved an inch.","n":92},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"It was a rough landing. Toham tumbled into the rocks finally letting go of her grip. Every cell of her body seemed to experience a truck collision and pulsated a shattering sensation, that she only knew hurt because her head was pounding with a pain shock avalanche. It was hard to remember the destination she had in mind and even harder to catch elusive reins of mind that would let her gasp for air.\n\nThe struggle seemed to last an infinite number of hours, she was going in and out of consciousness, grabbing every opportunity to catch a memory, image, light, emotion that she could come up with just to spark the focus on something.\n\nAt some point she even thought about how hilarious it would be to pass from a little Fluffer Huggins jump. It was dark and quiet at that moment. She could almost hear these thoughts echoing in the darkness. Damp, irresistibly clear voice that only she could hear inside her head. Perfectly calm and indifferent. Then it was back to the brutal reality of imaginary pain, light and back to dark. Over and over again as Toham wasn’t quite sure what was going on.\n\nShe heard the voices of her parents. Calling out her name. She couldn’t remember anything else, but she knew these voices as something that only existed in part of her heart that called for something too important to ignore. Something so irrelevant that she mentally scoffed at its stupidity. Of course she would never forget something as simple as that.\n\nToham fought for this first breath. It should have been an easy ride after that.\n\nKara told Huggins to move and he walked the street like a cat. Bouncing his flaming tail and rarely following people with his gaze. He sat, chased his tail, rolled on his back. Everything Kara would tell him to do, as she herself was intently watching his act trying to catch anything that would give away a soul behind dark indentations that represented eyes on his clay like body.","n":93},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"There was nothing. He stopped completely and only flames continued their flare that danced to the wind. Kara couldn’t feel a single strand of resistance. There wasn’t a will to go on and enjoy something other than the orders she was giving. She couldn’t watch it anymore and yet she couldn’t just send him out either. So they just carried on.\n\nWhen Toham reached the center hall she let Fluffers loose to light up the room with some lasting pawprints floating in the air. Stone carvings danced in the light of flames telling their stories, happy to have a curious visitor. Toham only briefly followed a movement of some dogman with pointy snout and ears welcoming a hawkman on a long boat to his gates. Toham wondered if they were friends or enemies. Story didn’t seem to favor hawks.\n\nAt the center there was a circle pedestal. Exactly what Toham was looking for. Untracked teleporter. What a coincidence to have it here, exactly where Toham wanted it to be. It wouldn’t be a miracle, she just hoped it would save her some time. Huggins landed by her side, suddenly agitated and even hissing at the inscriptions around the pedestal. Never a good sign, but, again, Toham wasn’t quite ready for what was waiting for her on the other side. Fluffers was just way worse at holding his emotions inside.\n\n- It’s alright, Fluffer, - Toham sighed and gave a flaming tom a nervous pet, - It’s all going to be alright. You have my back right? - She kneeled down to give a nudge to his forehead, roughing up his ears making Huggins purr reassuringly, - Now light it up and no doubts this time, Huggins.\n\nFluffer Huggins frolicked in the street and was very happy to see Fezzik with an interesting collection of shopping bags. Red man was surprised to see flaming tom on his own and looked around searching for Kara. He cautiously let down his bags and gave Huggins a brief pet.","n":94},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- Well, that’s new, - Fezzik looked quite puzzled, trying to figure out the trick, - Kara, did you figure out a way to morph into Fluffers?\n\nAlmost.\n\nKara jumped down and ran towards the red man to pounce his back. There was simply something irresistibly joyous in doing so. Fezzik yelped in surprise trying to shake her off, but she only climbed his shoulder and happily meowed into his ear.\n\n- You’re a cat, - he uncomfortably laughed, trying to piece the situation together. Kara climbed his head and layed down, perching on his horns, - Okay? - Fezzik wasn’t sure how to react to a cat on his head and Kara just got comfortable enough to accompany him on his shopping spree with joy, - I have one more place on the list and we can head home.\n\nIt was surprisingly pleasant to have a free ride home.\n\nToham didn’t like the idea of having a live cat on the cooking show. Sure, Pink Glasses had charisma and skills necessary to work in the studio, but beyond that he had an absolutely terrifying character. Even the smell of strangers would set him off and he absolutely hated every single groomer he encountered. Any clothing with a strange smell would get demolished or soiled and Toham forgot to count how many times they had to refinish the common room after guest visits.\n\nThat, however, never stopped Camerarius, who believed with his whole heart that ginger tom cat could never do such things and Toham was just making up stories to keep the guests out of the house. Pink Glasses loved him. On the rare occasions when Floats would drop by on his own it was hard to ever spot them apart.\n\n- Who’s my little sunshine on paws? - Floats lovingly kneeled down to squish Pink Glasses with his hands and ginger tom happily purred in response. Toham stood leaning on the wall amazed how happy the two looked together.","n":95},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- You know it’s bad to be attached to show animals, - Toham wasn’t sure Floats would ever listen to a word she said.\n\n- Oh stop it, sweetheart, I know you love him just as much as I do, - Soft voice of the blue man didn’t make it any easier.\n\n- Do what you want, - Toham waved goodbye and headed out, - I’ll be back for dinner.\n\nIf you consider the change of pace, Kara enjoyed pestering Fezzik on his shopping spree. Not only did the red man have to dodge Fluffer Hugins prancing around his flammable shopping bags, but that he was doing it while balancing a cat on his head. When they eventually spotted Rein lurking around the streets, the boy just stopped in his tracks trying to understand what’s going on. Fezzik, ofcourse, was too busy guarding his bags to see him. And so Rein moved on his own little quest.\n\n- Kara, do you mind telling mister Huggins to stay away from the bags? - the red man finally had enough, breathing heavily from an unexpected exercise.\n\n- Meow, - answered the cat and Fluffers gingerly hopped out of existence. Fluffers has been soaking her energy quite a bit and it seemed like a good time to give him some rest.\n\n- Right and you are going to stay there? - Fezzik looked up and the cat hung her head down to look back at him with a chipper purr, - Right.\n\nHe opened the door to a clothing shop and the shopkeeper instantly stood up with an objection.\n\n- Sir, we don’t allow pets inside, - woman was visibly amused, but that didn’t cancel out her concern for the stock. Kara wondered if she shed in that form would the hair remain on the ground if she reverted back. Silly question, of course it would.\n\n- Well, um, you see, - Fezzik tried to argue, but saw a different opportunity, - Kara, sorry, you can’t stay here as a cat.","n":96},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"He instantly regretted his words, as he collapsed under a full weight of Kara reverting back to normal, - Alright, - she waved a smile to a surprised shopkeeper and proceeded to examine the clothing. It was about time she changed her wardrobe.\n\n- Maybe jump off my head next time? - Fezzik scrambled himself up and checked on his shopping bags.\n\n- Aren’t you happy? Now I can help you with bags, - Kara smiled trying on a hat clearly too big to wear into a battle.\n\nToham felt exhausted after putting on an underwater show and it wasn’t easy to hide it. When they climbed the rocks to watch the sunset she sat down completely forgetting about the wet clothes. She just watched water dripping from her hair down, drop by drop as Parmas admired the difference in the color of water.\n\n- I didn’t know you could do anything of this sort, this is incredible, - he watched the kelp swaying in the water, - I mean you literally watch potatoes grow and wait for the whole season to watch them bloom, when you can enjoy it every day.\n\n- It wouldn’t be the same, plus it would shorten their lifespan, - Toham shivered, starting to feel cold. Something that she realized she missed quite a bit. It reminded her of times before she was a hero. Times that seemed hard, but way more manageable than today.\n\n- Right, still, you could do so much more with this, - Parmas was getting excited taking out his notebook and writing something down.\n\n- It wouldn’t make a difference, - Toham finally got enough of her wet hair, swinging her finger around and cleaning both of them up.\n\n- Oh, right, almost forgot about this, - Parmas turned around and scooched closer to her showing his notes, - This is quite incredible, beside the nutritional components these cells are completely natural. You basically managed to speed up time for them.","n":97},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- Did you think I’m smart enough to construct this much kelp out of magical energy or something? - Toham laughed, not quite sure how Parmas found her simple ways so genius.\n\n- Now you are making me sound dumb, - he laughed nervously, hiding his notebook back in his pocket.\n\n- All I did was delay the appearance of a hero that could be fixing this problem here. Give it a year or two and all this effort will mean nothing, - Toham smiled hugging her knees closer to her chest.\n\n- Not if people like this change and try to keep it up, - Parmas smiled and embraced her with reassurance.\n\n- It’s not about what they like, it’s about what they are inspired to do. Miracles like this usually just encourage destructive behavior, they will just sign it off as some natural cycle and change nothing, - Toham sighed. Quitus city would never be happy about anything she did, if they knew that she was the one to clear up their waters they would dump all their waste and shave all the kelp on the next day. They had their own heroes to solve their problems.\n\n- Never really took you for a pessimist.\n\nThey returned to the tavern to the sound of light music and the smell of roasted food. Kara was trying to feel happy about the new accessories they got, but still couldn’t figure out why she didn’t have the bravery to get the giant hat. It would never have any purpose, but she kind of liked how ridiculous it looked on her head. Maybe she could wear it before she went to bed to smile at the mirror for a second, but there was no room for it in her backpack.\n\nWhen they walked in Tallie promptly mentioned that Carp is already passed out in the kitchen. When Kara tried to find out what happened, Rein just said that he knows how to fix it and will figure it out later. He really looked like a man on a mission. She wondered what could have sparked his interest, but, given fish masked goblin tendencies, she was fine with staying out of it.","n":98},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- Anything else? - Fezzik looked around the table.\n\n- I visited a local church, it seems that they still combine their hospitals with religion and look what I picked up, - the blue man waved up his hands revealing a lute and a flute that started playing tunes on their own in the air.\n\nToham opened her eyes just barely in time to see a flash of purple light and roll over, feeling static waves flood her body as she tried to catch herself. It was harder to dodge the second strike. The pain from effort blurred her vision and she felt the world dulling down to the same pastel colors she knew all too well now. Never a good sign in a quarrel with a friend.\n\nToham roared, finally free from the excruciating pain, but something stopped her lunge. Nothing stopped Rein. There were times when Kara admired the cold resolve he had for these things, however she never thought she would have to face being on the other side of it.\n\n- So now you are going to hide behind this muzzle, - Rein’s eyes glowed up in purple as he missed another swing. It was much easier to evade attacks as a tiger. Toham knew she could keep it up from there, - Brave isn’t it?! Just sign mistakes on something you can’t control!\n\nShe wanted to reply, but all that came out was another angered roar. She could pounce him down, but something told her he wouldn’t dodge. Maybe that's what she presumed because he never did what she wanted. She never really wanted him to, it wouldn’t be friendship if she did. And then there was something. Something she left behind. Something she desperately wanted to forget.\n\n- We don’t have to do anything they tell us, right? - Kara rolled an empty mug on the table. The handle didn’t let it go far, but it was nice to have hands busy with something.","n":99},{"chapter":"Hugs and pets","text":"- No, but it seems now that we are in frontlines it’s going to be hard to stay out of conflicts, - Fezzik frowned. He glanced at Rein with a troubled look. Kara never understood why someone would go this far to protect a child that wasn’t their own. Must have been something they call a parental instinct, not that she fully realized what it meant. It was surprising it could still exist in the world they were living in.\n\n- I haven’t seen any of my kin in the town, - Tallie grunted entering the dining room with fresh air following her step from an opened door. Conrad promised that there are settlements of goblins in Decem, but it seemed they weren’t included in local society.\n\n- What did you expect? I wouldn’t call your kind the most cooperative, - Rein sighed, as if he was saying simplest ABC’s. Tallie almost threw her hammer at him.\n\nCircle quickly lit up in a low turquoise glow and the air around seemed to vibrate in disorienting resonance. Shadows started crawling out of the walls, curious of what could have caused such disturbance. They burned touching the glowing pawprints and floated around them, barely bothered by this slight inconvenience. Hushed whispers joined the rumbling and Toham started to feel the chilling wave hitting her back.\n\nThe key to being a hero is suppressing your fear. Doubts create pools of opportunity for character growth that are sure to turn into a time consuming battle with your own head. Time that Toham didn’t have. Not that any of her troubles could ever get resolved. Kara tried plenty of times, never felt an inch of progress.\n\nToham stepped forward feeling the space change. It was definitely an early form of a portal.","n":100},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"Kara couldn’t fall asleep. Whole night she spent twisting and turning, choking on some feeling she couldn’t pin down. The ground didn’t rock from side to side on the waves providing a distraction from pestering thoughts. There was no ocean around to isolate them from the rest of the world. Every noise outside was new and, worst of all, completely unknown.\n\nShe didn’t know the people who lived in this town or animals that roamed in this forest. She barely even recognized the plants that grew around and everything seemed to be soiled with tracks of magic. It was floating in the air like the scent of ozone after the lightning. Slightly sweet, but unsettling with unbridled supernatural power.\n\nAt the end, she gave up trying. Still in her nightgown, she went downstairs rubbing her eyes, the only part of her body that actually wanted some rest. She went outside barefoot, barely thinking and too lazy to actually look around for longer than a second between long blinks. It must have been already snowing back home. It was warm and barely breezy here. Kara didn’t believe she could be this homesick, but again she never left home before and now it seemed like she would never return again. Not like that never happened before. It must have been something else.\n\nBlooming moon illuminated the endless cloud plains. Just barely. It was surprising how high Plenelopy could actually climb for such a small aircraft. For any aircraft, Toham wasn’t sure she ever was so far above the clouds. She could almost see the edge of the world. Gray, on a pitch black background with glowing diamonds of stars. Parmas was definitely showing off. She could see it by his smile in soft moonlight.\n\n- Can’t believe you guys have this all to yourselves, - Toham never actually understood the idea of air space before. It was far upwards, somewhere beyond the clouds and higher than the highest mountain. It was almost as irrelevant as space itself.","n":101},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"- Well, Novem launches satellites even higher, - Parmas leaned forwards to look at something above, - Sometimes you can see them zooming by from this altitude.\n\n- Yeah, but they don’t put people on satellites, - Toham rolled on her back trying to spot a moving shimmer.\n\n- There, - Parmas pointed somewhere into the darkness, but all Kara saw was almost an imaginary shadow.\n\n- I’ll take your word for it, - she laughed. Tribus heroes were always experts in observational fields. Spot and note. Toham always wondered if he kept a log about her. She never saw it, if he ever did.\n\nKara woke up somewhere deep in the forest from a sudden buzz in her ear. She never experienced being pestered by bugs before, but this one sounded like it would be a pesky mosquito. She sprung upright trying to see it, but all she could hear was a fleeing buzz fading into the forest. Now that she had time to observe it in the light of an early morning, Kara wondered if she was actually in the jungles. Though she thought that the green would be much more prominent and the leaves on bushes weren’t as giant as encyclopedias would depict them.\n\nIt wasn’t too hard to find her way back. One climb on a tree and she could see the valley and the town. What was even better, it was an easy hike down. Kara looked at the sun trying to figure out if she needed to run to make it to a ten of the clock. It wouldn’t be a life or death if she weren’t, but she would feel awkward showing up to it in a torn nightgown. It would be a pain to explain it. She wouldn’t quite call it sleep walking, but then the shoes would give it away. She really didn’t remember why it was so bothersome to put them on. Her slumber mind seemed to work in mysterious ways. She liked to feel the ground with her feet, she just never did it like that before.","n":102},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"A cornered beast should always choose to fight. Playing dead isn’t the greatest plan when something already went to some length to chase you into a dire situation. Toham understood what was going on, but didn’t know how to retreat. She wasn’t about to let Rein continue on his mission, so she had to stand in his way. And as long as she stood in his way he wouldn’t stop.\n\nKara wished she never wondered what it was like to compete against him. Maybe she would let a swing or two go amiss and graze her, but where you give an inch they take a yard. One step backwards would set off the balance and she could end up dead. Or would she? Toham didn’t know the answer to that. All she knew was that she was the one refusing to run. Not because she was too proud, but because she was protecting something. Something that means the world to her. Something that just suffered a deep blow and refused to back down.\n\nYet, she couldn’t fight back. It was against her nature to harm him, it wouldn’t solve her pain. In fact it was almost as painful to think of harming him as it was to think about running away. They were friends, even if right now his judgment was clouded by the grief Kara could never imagine. It was never in his mind to live beyond this line. Line of total eradication in the name of revenge.\n\nWorld for an eye. To make sure the fear of the past prevents the repetition of history. It has been done before and it has been stopped before and yet the fear still remains in people who tell the stories to guard from the past mistakes. No one will take his apology now. Not on this side of the world.\n\n- How long do you think you can keep this up? - Rein sounded so cold and collected, that Toham felt an icy tide brushing under her fur. It was the Valentine she always knew. Unpredictable, determined and bitter. It didn’t help at all that she couldn’t answer, but if she could she’d probably be already dead. He wasn’t about to play word games.","n":103},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"- Was this your plan all along? - he raised his rapier, channeling an attack. Toham lit up Fluffer Huggins in response. Flaming tom landed softly near her. Kara wished she had time to see his reaction, but she could almost hear him chirp.\n\nIt was liberating to run through the forest barefoot without a single care in the world. Just running faster and faster, jumping over bushes and rocks, tumbling from ridges. It was so easy now that she had the power of heroes, no fall seemed to slow her down and no rock could cut her skin. She forgot how much she wanted to be this free.\n\nKara felt a pleasant exhaustion when she finally reached the clearing where Maggie’s tavern stood. She forgot the last time she was so happy to smell breakfast through the open kitchen window. She almost forgot to clean herself up before running into the dining room where the rest of the Septum heroes were already finishing their meals.\n\n- Kara, you know they have bathrooms down the hall right? - Rein was slightly confused to see her enter through the front door.\n\n- Don’t worry, I just couldn’t sleep so I went for a run, - Kara sounded so normal that for a second nobody had questions.\n\n- Without shoes? - Fezzik looked puzzled, noticing her bare feet.\n\n- Why would I need shoes if I’m just running around? - Kara sounded so casual that she almost convinced herself, - Didn’t want to stop to shake rocks and sand out on every turn.\n\n- Seriously? - Fezzik mostly questioned reality, briefly massaging his temple.\n\n- You could have at least put something on, - Rein was clearly looking for plot holes.\n\n- I’ll watch you try and think straight in the middle of trying to fall asleep, - she would have thrown a shoe at him, but she had none. What a happy coincidence.","n":104},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"When Toham returned to the mansion she assumed that Camerarius would already be gone, he usually didn’t stay for longer than an hour or two. There were plenty of places in this world that were much more exquisite than a standard house that couldn’t change its layout due to strict regulations. To be honest he didn’t seem to quite understand why they added a location in town where Toham lived. Toham wasn’t happy about it either, however that is what you get for being predictable enough to have a preference on location.\n\n- Sweetheart, you sure took some sweet time, - voice of Camerarius sounded from the dinner room. Toham almost had a heart attack. To think that Floats actually found time or cared about staying for a boring dinner with her was almost irrational.\n\n- Sweet sprouts, you scared me, - Toham dusted off shoes and headed towards the voice of a blue man. It was strange that he didn’t come out to meet her at the door.\n\n- Sorry, butterfly, - Blue man was sitting on a couch with a content smile, petting Pink Glasses that had made himself comfortable on the lap of a hero that brought down skyscrapers with a simple tap, - Mister Glasses here insisted I stay for dinner.\n\nToham wondered if he actually didn’t move because some ginger tom sat on his lap and fell asleep. It wouldn’t be the most far fetched reason, in fact, it would be the only rational explanation. The question really was why Pink Glasses was sitting there for so long. It was unsettling.\n\n- Are you going to sit at the table? - Toham sighed, knowing all too well that Glasses would love that. Ginger tom jumped off onto the floor and with a strut of importance made his way to his chair.\n\n- How do you do it? - Camerarius followed right behind, admiring the training and manners of the tom cat, - He doesn’t listen to a word I say.","n":105},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"- I don’t know, Eve did all the training on this one, - Toham sat opposing Camerarius, - Plus I rarely ask him for anything that isn’t absolutely necessary.\n\n- Is this why he misbehaves with Parmas? - Camerarius said the words so nonchalantly, but Kara almost choked on vegetables from surprise.\n\n- What? - Toham wasn’t sure how or why he brought it up, but she didn’t expect Floats to take interest in such things.\n\n- He said that Pink Glasses hates him quite a bit, - it was never clear what was going on in his head, but now Toham really wanted to know for sure. Out of pure curiosity, of course.\n\n- Yeah, it doesn’t help that now I have to lock him up every time he visits, - There was no point in denying anything, being defensive only raised the suspicion and she never actually tried to hide anything. There was no point.\n\n- How can you lock away such a pumpkin pie? - Camerarius sounded shocked and turned to Pink Glasses, - You never scratched anyone once, didn’t you?\n\nGinger tom cat stopped munching on steak in his bowl and lovingly purred in response. Toham gave him a lambasting side eye. It was hard to sit with a straight face knowing how much chaos tom cat actually caused, but Toham wasn’t really the one dealing with it day to day. She just observed and wondered why.\n\nWhen life gives you lemons. Weird to think that someone would be sad to receive free lemons, but when you consider metaphoric meaning it becomes tricky to keep a positive mindset. It could always be worse, but to try and do best with a bitter situation is usually the right decision. Begs for a question why so many tend to give up and slack off. Maybe because it’s usually never a matter of life and death. It always is for heroes.","n":106},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"When a man in a military trench coat came by the tavern, a little late, but at ten, Kara knew that they were going to be good friends. His humble demeanor and energetic stride gave off a sense of trust and purehearted benevolence. He would be her favorite red man, if they weren’t teamed up with Fezzik.\n\n- Good morning, hope you all slept well, - red man smiled with sunshine, - My name is Hendrix, but you can call me Henry. I usually train local revolutionaries, but I’m sure we can figure something fun and useful for you guys.\n\nIt was really hard to tell what exactly made this man so happy. Kara imagined that training simpleton could get boring and he was excited to try harder techniques, but that would be too pretentious to think that.\n\nThe world of sentient clones will collapse due to inability to meaningfully diversify the unique goals each one follows. That's why they are only usually considered for military purposes. It is much easier to force adaptation in face of shorter lifespans and following a unified goal. In a sense, one man fighting on an entire front of a war could be a fever dream situation. Someone following the faith in great ascendance into Valhalla and his own impermeable ability to reach it millions of times in millions of ways. Someone who believes that destiny will always give him a way of death in dignity and a last choice to be always made the same.\n\nUnus heroes often develop a tendency to try and cover more area on the battlefield. The usual problem is that competition for the leadership often pushes the original out of safety and into the fray. Insanity, they lose themselves and follow their instincts thinking they are no different from others. Because they are not. And yet, take out the original and rest will wither away like common meatshields, finishing their last missions as a one way trip. There is no cure for magical constructs. Imagination can’t carry on without a host. Unless…","n":107},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"Fear not, words are not terminal statements. Rules are meant to be broken to test the limits of reality. Just be sure that you are not choosing the terminal path.\n\nIt was a no brainer which training Kara chose. Given the options of an unknown blade wielding brute and a cheerfully inspiring Henry. The third option was out of the question. She didn’t even want to continue the conversation after his name was spoken at the table. Good thing she liked Henry enough to show some respect.\n\n- You seriously want to train with him? - Rein was starting to fume, - We can’t trust him and he probably only offered to help because he thinks that it’s going to make up for the past.\n\n- Rein, as much as you hate him for what he did, he was my friend. I think I can hold my own against him, - Fezzik didn’t pretend to be calm. He was defending, - Plus we might benefit knowing a bit more of what’s going on. Listen, it’s not like he did well for me either. I’m not about to take his side on anything.\n\nKara barely understood what they were talking about. Septum heroes rarely talked about their past. It never did them well to know more than absolutely necessary and all they knew somehow became absolutely necessary. Kara always feared that she already knew too much, but that already got her in all the trouble it could. She just had to survive the timeline.\n\n- Just don’t let him into your head again, - Kara stood up and wasn’t about to share friends with someone she despised. These strings could always backfire, in theory. Kara wasn’t about to explore the world of complex webs.\n\n- I won’t, Kara, I wasn’t born yesterday, - Fezzik breathed out, feeling relieved from the end of the conversation. No one liked to argue with Rein.","n":108},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"Another flash, Toham almost missed it. Fluffer Huggins must have jumped on his own. They landed on the ground with a clear view of scorched shrubbery and a black cloak pestered with burning holes. Rein threw it off on the burning ground rushing for the next blow. He didn’t aim for Toham this time.\n\n- Stop summoning this hell of an aberration, - Rein hissed with almost demonic fury, - He is long dead.\n\nFluffer Huggins disappeared, busting up into a thousand pieces. It was the last straw. She didn’t exactly remember how it happened. Toham rasped abruptly and toppled Rein, pinning him to the ground. She came to senses as her jaws almost closed at his neck. She could almost feel the smell of blood, but it flashed and passed as the skin stretched under her teeth. It wasn’t common sense that stopped her, but merely training that stopped her from absentmindedly spreading the curse. Toham was grateful nevertheless.\n\n- As indecisive as ever, as always, - Rein was holding a dagger under her ribcage. Toham didn’t quite process the rest. It probably helped to know that his rapier was in his right arm.\n\nHeroes followed Hendrix to the encampment deep in the forest. It was somehow funny to Kara that even Carp made an effort to join them. It seemed that strength training was something that heroes couldn’t skip even if they had better things to do. Like a short lifeline that defined the difference between survival and perishment. What fish masked goblin that could outrun any danger would get out of wasting time gaining more skills? He definitely wasn’t into learning more about ships. Maybe somewhere under the rubber headwear there was resolve to protect his friends. Kara liked that theory. She would never leave him behind, life would be dull without his poetry.","n":109},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"They split into teams and scattered throughout the camp. Henry took Kara, Tallie and Carp to a treeline obstacle course and suggested they play catch without touching the floor. Before Kara could ask whether Huggins could join, Carp tagged her and darted to the starting line.\n\n- Tag is hit, you must be it! - the goblin was already out of reach and Tallie quickly jumped out of the way of a reaching hand with a focused expression that told Kara she wasn’t in the mood to lose.\n\n- Well, sprouts, - Kara watched a tailed goblin disappear into the canopy. She briefly glanced at Henry who was smiling with a compassionate expression.\n\n- You got this, don’t let them off so easily next time, - red man cheered up with a thumbs up, clearly not realizing that one of her targets wasn’t about to get caught and the second one being a talented escape artist. All he could have suspected is that it wasn’t her first day dealing with impossible situations.\n\n- Don’t get your hopes up, - Kara gave a sarcastic thumbs up in response.\n\nToham was getting nervous. Parmas never actually told her they are going to stop by one of the Tribus stations. Not like she asked about the details of their trip. She basically forgot about everything when she saw what was going to be their ride. She asked so many questions and now they all seemed silly.\n\n- I thought I told you that I would like to keep this down low, - Toham felt all her nerves firing up. She barely had any idea where they were and at this altitude she wasn’t really sure how she would handle the fall. Probably poorly, plus it would be terrible to realize she would be stuck in the middle of a hostile ocean. Clouds didn’t quite help her mental state.","n":110},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"- Don’t see a reason why Tribus would care for your visit, - Parmas clearly didn’t get the memo. He wasn’t the one wearing a show collar. Why would he worry about what it really implied? Unless he knew everything.\n\n- I can give you at least six, - Toham wished she had space to pace around. It was hard to keep composure without anything to do.\n\n- Well the same six reasons you wouldn’t want me in Septum, don’t you think so? - his reasoning was impeccable, the only difference was age. Now to think about it, he probably mentored heroes at some point. Toham felt increasingly stupid.\n\n- Will you skip the stop if I ask nicely once? - There wasn’t really much she could barter with in her state.\n\n- No one is at the station, I will tweak the surveillance when we leave, - Parmas laughed clearly enjoying the turbulence he had caused. Toham didn’t know whether to be excited to visit an aerial station or murder Parmas for wasting her patience on things as stupid as this.\n\n- Why couldn’t you just open with that? - she finally relaxed and scratched downing of Plenelopy with a great deal of fire out of her plans.\n\n- Can only play that card once, you would hate it to go to waste, - playing with fire seemed like a running joke for Parmas. Kara was beginning to worry about this pattern. Toham thought it was cute.\n\nThing about trying to catch goblins on the trees is that you have to find them first. Thing about finding them is that they would hold still until the last moment of approach. From there it was a question of not falling off of the canopy. If you fail at that, you start over. Kara learned it the hard way.\n\n- Keep it up, Kara, you are doing much better than my cadets, - Hendrix really had a pool of infinite optimism. All Kara could think about was that she was so bad she actually was compared to common folk without any magic.","n":111},{"chapter":"Cats and boxes","text":"- Right, - She stood up and headed back up the tree. There wasn’t much to say and she could barely look the red man in the eyes. There was just so much hope in them, it was painful to force him to watch her fail again and again. She was a fall away from burning the forest down, but she really didn’t want to upset Henry. If it was even possible. It was funny to imagine telling her that it’s fine to think outside of the box to solve problems. Though there is probably a difference if you are out of the box only because it burned away. Kara wasn’t particularly a fan of a burn the haystack solution. A bit too wasteful for a single needle, though probably better than a horse with a punctured stomach.","n":112},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"Heroes often avoid the colors of magic that don't fit their own. It’s a rule of stability, you really want to keep your outcomes definite for an easier implementation. Just like it is impossible to create a perfect engine it is impossible to fully transform all energy of enchantment without wasting some of it into something unpredictable.\n\nToham could almost feel the fabric of space cracking under the unexpected strain Fluffer Huggins forced with each of his steps. The turquoise bridge stretched uneasily forward and ornate webs of shatter spread from paws of flaming tom. Drizzles of shards fell down stretching out into windows of various colors and shapes.\n\nShe hesitated for a second when another step sent an entire chunk of the bridge flying into the abyss creating its own projectile of windows. There was no way of telling what strand of possibilities just flew out into unreachable distance. Fluffers could only go this far.\n\nToham took a deep breath and placed her palm on the bridge. She didn’t like the idea of appropriating someone else's magic structures, but its frail nature left her no choice. A pulse of orange spread through the bridge turning it to a flaming stream and the dust of shattered matter turned darkness to blinding light.\n\n- Feels much better, doesn’t it, Fluffers? - Toham reluctantly closed her eyes and placed a hand on flaming tom. He meowed in full voice, he never did that before. Not this Fluffer Huggins. Kara didn’t expect that.\n\nDinner was silent beside a couple of silly comments Floats made about the poor fashion sense of towns folk and lack of uniform standards among the revolutionaries. Kara was glad that Tallie didn’t enjoy gloating and Carp wouldn’t spend energy coming up with a rhyme for something this petty.","n":113},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"That night Kara spent looking for a better place to stay in the forest. Tavern just felt too crowded, maybe she would be better off having her own place somewhere hidden. Something that would feel normal. She spent hours walking and climbing trees looking for clearings where she could start building a small hut and a garden. To no avail, at least this time she fell asleep on a branch with a nice starview.\n\nNo one was startled during breakfast when she returned. Shoes on and in clothes fit for a forest walk.\n\n- How’s the morning in the jungles? - Floats cheered her arrival with a glass of orange juice.\n\n- Warm and misty, - Kara felt slightly exhausted from balancing on unfamiliar branches all night. Still better than no sleep at all, plus she got to practice her grip and swings on her way back. Branches seemed to be more predictable when she chose the way.\n\n- You almost make it sound appealing, - Floats giggled, - Still don’t understand why you prefer it to soft sheets and blankets.\n\nIt was flattering that Floats cared enough to figure out what was going on, but Kara never actually took him for the most observant. It did make sense, she just never thought about it.\n\n- Kara, you slept in the jungle? - Fezzik suddenly realized what was going on, - You realize it’s dangerous?\n\n- How exactly? - Kara looked him straight in the eyes, wondering what beast he could come up with to scare her.\n\n- You know what, just be careful out there, - there wasn’t really any point to argue, Fezzik probably knew she had more arguments than he did or understood.\n\n- Hey, it was pretty funny, - Parmas tried to give Toham a hug, but she dodged out of the way.\n\n- No it wasn’t, - There was a reason she didn’t like guests and never made new friends. It was already tough enough for her to shove away her feelings to trust someone she barely knew. Now that feeling was stuck in her back and she wasn’t sure what to do with it.","n":114},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- It’s ok to step out of your comfort zone every once in a while, - He sighed and walked over to check something on the panels, - Believe me, I know plenty of hermits just like you. To be honest you are doing much better than most of them.\n\n- This isn’t about comfort at all. You know I am not supposed to be here, - Toham looked around feeling like the entire experience was now ruined by an unnecessary argument about something so simple.\n\n- Knowing your track record you should be just about near your element then, - Parmas was busy typing in some data into the system. He never mentioned that he followed her tournament footage, Toham almost forgot it existed.\n\n- Then you should know how serious I am about these things, - sometimes the differences in their character struck a bell in her head, but it was all fun and games back in the safety of home. Now it felt like a red flag that she willingly avoided and now was suffering the consequences.\n\n- Listen, I’m not a toddler, but serious stuff can’t all be gloomy and boring, - he finished up his log and opened up the search system, - wanna look at the Decem map?\n\n- You mean the entire thing? - Toham didn’t know that such a thing existed. She forgot what they argued about. Now she thought it was silly.\n\nSecond day of the tag game Tallie and Carp switched out every time Kara slipped. Henry seemed to catch his second wind in training. Not that he seemed tired the day before. Kara kept meaning to ask if they were the first batch of heroes Ember has given him, but it would be a shame to see him lose a smile even for a second.\n\n- Tag you’re it! - Kara was huffing heavily just barely hanging on the branch holding on to Tallie's leg as she finally caught her mid jump. It was weird to be holding her upside down, but it took a moment for Kara to summon strength to swing her on a platform. It didn’t help that her anchor hand slipped.","n":115},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- That doesn’t count! - Tallie wasn’t about to give up as she watched Kara fall down with a grinning face.\n\n- Yes, it does! - yelped Kara right before hitting the ground with a victorious thud. The hit knocked the wind out of her, maybe it was just the feeling of content. She would have time to rest until Tallie catches Carp now and that could take quite a bit, - It does right?\n\nShe looked at Henry for confirmation and he let Carp go and gestured to Tallie to follow suit. Tailed goblin grunted and commenced the chase.\n\n- You guys are really something, - red man offered a hand to get Kara back on her feet, - Pretty sure these two will be jumping around till dusk. So much energy, do you guys even get tired?\n\n- Only if we think about it, - Kara was panting quite a bit, - At least I think that’s how Carp works, we can’t be that much different.\n\n- So you guys compare each other? - Henry was killing time with questions. Maybe he was actually interested. Kara really didn’t mind.\n\n- Not really, they just really don’t like losing, - Kara looked with concern at the canopy, realizing that next time her turn would come around she might be better off chasing Carp. Tallie was now pushing her own limits. She started using her tail. Clumsily, but that was never in her tool box before.\n\n- Incredible, - Henry seemed to notice that too.\n\n- You sure I can’t use anything, but raw motors? - Kara felt increasingly hopeless observing goblins move faster and faster.\n\n- Are you saying you’ve been slacking off on me? - Hendrix laughed with surprise, his eyes lit up.\n\n- I thought it wouldn’t be fair.\n\nAll actions have consequences. When you change ancient lines you change the fundamentals of reality. Progress that was made and perfected throughout centuries of life and death erased in a blink of an eye if you skip one generation and erase the tracks. Something that was hard to do in the new era of thorough documentation, but it was still possible to be born, live a life and die without a single soul remembering your existence. Magic made that even easier. However, just because you think you closed the connection in all directions you could see that doesn’t mean they won’t find a way to track it down another way. Whatever you do, as long as one mind evolves it will explore. Forever is just a long time.","n":116},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"Toham opened her eyes, she never understood why Eve insisted she learned these nonsensical words. Bunch of gibberish, yet somehow it helped to let the floating chunks of the turquoise bridge wither away from her memory. Destruction is essential and one can’t always manage to put some mind to it.\n\nShe landed in the jungle right in front of her little hut. She didn’t remember it missing a door, but she didn’t live that long there. Maybe it was never there. She usually woke up before the sun could bother her and it wasn’t like there were wolves out here. Not like she would be scared of them either.\n\n- So you are saying you can adapt your body to the situation? - Henry sat down still observing Tallie and Carp meddling in the canopy.\n\n- More or less, - Kara tried to look to catch a glance from him, but the red man seemed to be invested in goblin chase. She sat beside him, joining the observation. By that point Tallie was already swinging around like she was born to do it and Carp… Well, he was a fast fish on the land and now he conquered the trees. Kara really didn’t look forward to chasing him next time he gets his candy zoomies.\n\n- Tail, claws and teeth and all, right? - Hendrix sounded like his mind really was in three places at the same time, - Tallie, he is looping around the same path.\n\nTree tops rumbled with an annoyed growl and a cracking sound. Tallie snapped one of the platforms sending debris flying to the ground. Hendrix chuckled. That was just the beginning.\n\n- Well, what stops you from walking around with a tail then? - he was still looking upwards.\n\n- Waste of energy, I guess, - Kara sighed, - plus the more I use it the more natural it seems.\n\n- Something bothers you? - Henry glanced at her briefly. She gave him a tired side eye.","n":117},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- Everyday.\n\n- You know that you can’t understand something if you shun it to the sidelines, why not test the limits while you can do it without extra stress. You can’t resent your choices forever, - Henry sounded a bit too resourceful for someone out of a hero loop.\n\n- What state are you from? - Kara was done playing the game of guess.\n\n- Would you want to guess? - Hendrix was way to calm for someone confronted with such a heavy question.\n\n- I always guess wrong.\n\nMap of Decem was enormous. The scale of it showed the land area three times larger than all nine states together. Two large continents and one smaller in the center. Toham never imagined that it was that big.\n\n- Did it grow? - She felt a sting of greed, seeing the regions claimed by different states. Last time she was there it was a tenth of its size now and it all was claimed by Septum.\n\n- You sound surprised. What did you expect to happen after your stunt? They are not about to repeat the tight circle scenario, - Parmas closed up on the smaller continent that Kara was more familiar with. Split into three sections.\n\n- Still don’t understand how rebels manage to stay friends with everyone, but the government, - Toham blinked slowly, remembering her own reasons.\n\n- Heroes wouldn’t be heroes without a heart soft for people simply fighting for their own place in the world, - Parmas smiled and roughed up her hair, - Plus those who try and topple it automatically catch a target on their back and pretty sure you know what that ends you up with.\n\nToham frowned. Rapid expansion meant there was soon to be a collapse. Decem was heading towards a power overload reset. Much sooner than she expected.\n\n- How hard do you think it would be to demolish that stupid Unus youngling? - Kara was feeling her hands itching.","n":118},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- Missing your spotlight already? - Parmas laughed with a hard grin, - Yeah, I don’t think anyone can touch that one. You two should meet, I’m sure you would love her.\n\nThere were no platforms left by the end of the chase and Tallie was gloriously victorious holding wiggling Carp over her head as a great prize.. Apparently having a fish mask didn’t quite help to examine the terrain that changed rapidly after the habit had already settled. It was hard to see through Hendrix 'smile whether he was perplexed about the damage done or proud that his advice was used to its fullest potential.\n\n- Well, I guess you guys deserve to leave early today, - Henry gave Tallie a nice pat on her back.\n\n- Yeah, sorry about that, this one is witty, - tailed goblin laughed pointing a thumb over her shoulder at a distraught Carp.\n\n- Would be good, curse broken wood! - fish masked goblin clearly didn’t like to be the loser at his own game. It seemed that even the most careless heroes were prone to warming up to their leadership in their specialties.\n\n- Yeah, yeah, fish head, just admit your defeat, - Tallie turned to him with a big goblin grin. Kara never saw her so proud.\n\n- You are a wit, take off that grit! - Carp stood up and pointed his finger straight at the tailed goblin, - Take an honest try, You’ll never catch me alive!\n\nFish masked goblin booked it into the forest and Tallie roared following after him. Hendrix lost his words watching goblins disappear in a blink of an eye.\n\n- Dam, that was his longest rhyme yet, - Kara was thoroughly impressed.\n\nThere is a difference between inability to decide and a habit to stop. Inability to decide gets you stuck in the problem under the weight of the consequences. Habit to stop abrupt the process all together and shoves the decision under the rug.","n":119},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"Toham felt it was a good place to stall. She wouldn’t get a better opportunity. So the tiger crushed Rein under its body weight. Pain was dull, but potent. It made it easier that Toham was expecting it. He tried to crawl out, but she wasn’t letting him move freely, applying every strand of her muscles to keep her position. It did feel stupidly simple.\n\n- What is this now, - Rein probably was confused by the choice of a strategy Toham was attempting. It was ten minutes, maybe less. She just needed five more. Counting breaths that slowly got heavier with lungs flooding with blood. There weren't really many other entertainment options left to consider.\n\nRein tried to pull the dagger out and when he couldn’t he started twisting it. Not the best feeling on the planet, so she growled. Not like her bluffing would change anything, but holding instincts back was becoming harder. Rein should have known that. It would have been silly if he didn’t. It would have been terrifying if that’s what he was going for. Three minutes. The world was already covered in disorienting sparkles.\n\nKara headed back to the jungle, planning to spend free time doing what she thought she could actually enjoy. It took four hours to find the location, but the opening she picked was seemingly perfect for a little hut. She didn’t think for too long before taking down the first tree. Then second, third and so on. Half an hour later she had a good headstart on a foundation. It barely felt like a workout.\n\nKara didn’t realize the sun set until she was done with a front porch. It would be way past midnight if she was to make her way back to the tavern.\n\n- Hey, guys, I’m gonna be very late, - she spoke into the earpiece, it was usually too awkward to use the line that had everyone and their mother online.","n":120},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- What was that? - Ember's voice on the other side made Kara smile. She completely forgot she was still mad at him.\n\n- Hey Ember, tell others to ask Tallie about the latest rhyme Carp came up with during training today, - Kara wasn’t really thinking too hard.\n\n- Wait, where are you? - Fezzik sounded more confused than concerned.\n\n- Got a little carried away in the jungles, - Kara didn’t know how to explain a compulsive desire to set a base in the middle of nowhere. She could almost feel Rein rolling his eyes on the other end.\n\n- Ask Tallie about the rhyme, - Kara chuckled into the line and it went silent. She kind of wished she was there for the great reveal, but it would run stale by the morning. She wouldn’t rob the rest of the heroes enjoying it.\n\n- See, nothing bad happened, - Parmas was booting up Plenelopy for a take off. Glider slid off of the platform and quickly caught wind.\n\n- How many of these stations Tribus has to cover the entire Decem? - Toham was taking her last glance at a floating structure. She couldn’t figure out how it stayed afloat.\n\n- Let me check, I think they launched like a dozen new ones this year, - Parmas was looking up information on his watch, - Three hundred thirty three, dam. Three hundred new ones, they sure went for an overkill.\n\n- Not really, - Toham wondered who exactly in Tribus was so obsessed with something so trivial as pretty numbers, - I guess I’ll have to bet on Tribus next year. I don’t think your lunatics are keen on sharing third place for long.\n\n- Sometimes I forget who your mentor is, - Parmas raised his eyebrows, flabbergasted by a sudden observation, - How is she?\n\n- Charming as ever, - Toham didn’t like talking about Eve.","n":121},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"- You told her about this, didn’t you? - there was a slight hesitation in his voice. No hero really wanted to be on the bad side of one of the founders.\n\n- Pretty sure it was her idea anyway, like everything else, - Toham sighed. Knowing how careless Eve could be with buttons, it would be a suicide to leave Septum without a heads up. Kara wasn’t about to carry it on her person either. Some heroes had quite nifty hands.\n\nWay back was long and troublesome. It is one thing to wander around the dark woods without destination and completely another to be following a straight line, ignoring roundabouts to some of the more challenging obstacles. Growling stomach that anticipated dinner wasn’t cooperating either. Two days of poor sleep made her entire body stiff and it was bothersome to fight the urge to crash somewhere for a quick nap.\n\nThat's why, when she saw a plate waiting for her in an empty dining hall, something warm sparked in her heart. Brief and touchingly homely. This feeling almost scared her as a ghost of a distant past that showed around to rekindle the pain that was sound asleep for seemingly decades. It was even more painful that it was just over six.\n\n- Hey, - The voice behind the counter made Kara jump. Abel looked as sharp as ever, - You really enjoy your walks.\n\n- Sorry, didn’t think you would stay up, - A plate on the table would do, but Kara appreciated the company. Something that she didn’t think could happen.\n\n- Oh, it’s no problem, - He smiled filling up a cup of tea, - Plus I need to stay in shape somehow.\n\nAbel was surprisingly cheerful for the late hour. He stepped out of the bar, followed by a warm, soothing floral scent.\n\n- Thanks, - Kara wished she could find more words to say, but nothing came to mind. It would be nice to hold a conversation.","n":122},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"Toham did this before, well Kara did. Scratch everything and start over. And over. And over again. How hard could it be to do it? Now, when she knows for sure that there was nothing she could change. Leave it all behind and catch whatever strings she could find. Whatever that could help her get back on track and be a hero of her own story.\n\nHer eyes were blurry as words were blankly passing her mind to make the time go faster as she was searching for answers. Faster, slower, like a melody she learned back in the day. Notes played in a broken rhythm, perfectly matching the pace of her breath. She could almost hear the voices of an upbeat chorus in it.\n\nRein growling under the weight of her feline body didn’t match it at all. Like an angry cat refusing to cooperate with a medical treatment. It will leave a mark on their trust, but it was absolutely necessary. Toham was glad tigers didn’t really cry like humans did, it would be a bit silly.\n\nKara fell asleep right away. For the first time the bed sheets felt soft and welcoming inside the walls of Maggie's tavern. Air in the room being fresh, untouched for two nights and breathing with a newly open window.\n\nShe dreamed of walking her home woods and the sunshine in the morning dew. It smelled of dandelions and pine, she recognized it through the mist of thoughts. She made it to the groove where she first planted her first potato. It was still untouched and trees covered the forest floor with chaotic nets of shadows. For a moment she wished she had never touched it. She could just hide here. Forever.\n\nKara woke up from a sudden click of the door. Tallie didn’t knock like she usually did. Their eyes met wide open in surprise.\n\n- Good morning? - tailed goblin sounded almost flustered by her own intrusion, - You slept in.","n":123},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"She closed the door and left hastily. Kara wondered if she checked on her empty room every day. She quickly changed from her nightgown and rushed downstairs to a sight of heroes cheerfully finishing their breakfast. Training seemed to have sparked their energy quite a bit.\n\n- You can overcharge the blast with matching energy polarities and it will avoid hitting certain targets, boom! No friendly fire, the blast is repelled from allied wavelengths! - Fezzik was actively describing his new findings. He seemed even happier to see Kara join the table, - Great, Kara, you will love this!\n\n- So you are saying I can stop burning Tallie every time she refuses to jump? - Kara smiled, giving Tallie a teasing look. Tailed goblin just shrugged and waved it off.\n\n- Exactly! You just need to match her so called charge, - Fezzik spread his palms in excitement.\n\n- Alright, I’ll keep that in mind, - Kara picked up the spoon anticipating the stiff taste of the porridge. It was seasoned with honey, just like Haderach used to make it. Rein must have visited the kitchen.\n\nHeroes are not designed to give up. Unless they have an arc that helps them to recover and return to their righteous way. No matter how hopeless the situation could seem at the moment, the story cannot end on the downhill deescalation into an emptiness. At least that's what the system is designed for. There are plenty of other names for main characters to compensate for the vacuum. All just words of prejudice formed by the cultures.\n\nIn the grand view of the universe it is as meaningless as galaxies colliding at speeds greater than the observable limit away from us. It doesn’t mean that they don’t affect us, it is just something so subtle that it would be a waste of time to try and perceive it. For a common mind anyway. Like worrying about a speck of an atom on Jupiter when dealing with deforestation of Siberian tundra. Only myriads of times less relevant.","n":124},{"chapter":"Spoonfeeding","text":"Morals keep us together, they simplify the rules of interactions and navigate the mind into structure that propel the survival of the fittest multitudes. Plural, because they no longer isolate to the uniform standards.","n":125},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"When people look back on their lives they often see things that they never shared with anyone else. Something that they snuck out of the moment just for themselves. Something they might have shared before, but realized that others would never fully grasp the importance, meaning and weight of such visions. Everyone has these and, yet, no matter how much we try, there is a thin film paper between us blocking our ability to reach a singular view. Some people learn to ignore these, Kara just accepts their existence.\n\nAll heroes found their own little hobbies to pursue during down time. Carp was busy performing stunts around the streets. Occasionally Kara would find him napping in the alleyways or splashing in the fountains, wondering if that was really his best definition of good time. Even if it was, she couldn’t figure out what was so novel about it every day that he preferred it to literally anything else. It could have been a commitment to something he stated for himself a long time ago. Like a candy you keep eating because it reminds you of something.\n\nFezzik took residence at the local forge; that was only a question of time.\n\nTallie was busy tracking the elusive goblins. Kara would occasionally join on her scout missions, but they would never go far enough it seemed. No tracks or hints and yet something felt awfully strange about it. Like there was a fundamental flaw in the way they were looking. Plenty of nearby villages had tales and stories, but when asked for proof no one could find a thing even if they claimed there was. Not a single broken window stood broken, not a single missing thing out of place. Like the mere presence of heroes nearby erased all evidence on its own. None of it made sense.","n":126},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"The sky shattered with a familiar sonic boom. Toham must have missed the flash of light as she was struggling to keep Rein in place. Her paws felt numb from the stress of unfamiliar exercise. She jumped off reverting back to normal and shot a flaring missile from her hand.\n\nEmber light briefly lit up their faces staring right into each other's eyes.\n\n- Would never think that you would be the one calling for cops. Did they treat you with candy or something back there? - Rein stood up dusting off his clothes.\n\n- Not everyone gets the luxury of playing endless doll houses in Decem, - Toham snapped. She probably shouldn’t have, but the closer the whistle of falling soldiers got the less patience she seemed to have.\n\n- Luxury? - Rein burned up in purple flares raising his rapier, - Do you seriously believe all this is just a game?\n\n- It is if you all just keep playing by the rules and so far it seems that’s all you did here, - Toham braced for the impact, unsure if her ax could stand the blow. It was always good to prepare for the worst.\n\nThe ax shattered in pieces taking the bulk of the hit, just enough to correct the trajectory of rapier to graze her side. It was hard to make the distance in between.\n\n- Is that what makes you feel better about sitting on their leash? - Rein was getting out of breath, taking his time to prepare for the next lunge.\n\n- At least I’m not the one who started all this, - Toham sighed, among all of the choices available to make none made sense or complied with what she truly wanted. There was always a catch.\n\nWhat Floats did with his free time puzzled Kara the most. Well, not puzzled, but she never expected him to be this expansive. Setting up an entire fashion workshop with a full scale production and even fashion shows every other day. He would wear a new costume every day and somehow that encouraged him to train with such ferocity that he was picking up new techniques like one would gather shells on the seashore. Something about testing the durability and comfort of movement. Blue man really saw no limits, only means to an end. He didn’t look for roundabouts either.","n":127},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"Rein did something quite else, alright. Kara kept trying to figure it out, however spying on him would be rude and he kept most of it a tight secret. One time they did go out on some sort of undercover mission, but the evidence she found made her regret the decision. Carving an underground movement around the rebellion structure seemed a bit too far from what Kara would label as fun. Kara had zero people skills anyway.\n\nShe did enjoy her little jungle hut, at least for the first week. It was all downhill from there.\n\n- You know if you keep missing our parties people will actually forget you exist, - Camerarius put on his coat getting ready to head out. Toham sometimes wondered if he ever felt lonely. Spending so much time with heroes that seemingly only cared for the front that he held up like an impervious wall. Did he ever feel like crumbling, somewhere deep inside?\n\n- And what if that’s my goal? - Toham chuckled as Pink Glasses jumped into her arms using the moment to snuggle. He knew she would never shun him away as long as Floats was in the room. Sneaky smartypans.\n\n- Well that is a silly goal to have for a hero of your stance, - blue man straightened out his clothes with a soft wind. It seemed he tried to stall for a moment, a brief second. Toham tried to catch what he was trying to say.\n\n- Hey, it was nice to have you over for dinner, - Kara smiled softly, - Maybe you can take me out on a walk around Senio sometime.\n\n- Oh honey! I thought you would never ask! - Floats lit up instantly, - I can show you all of our shops and maybe you could catwalk the stage or two. I’ll pick the fits myself. How about tomorrow noon?\n\nInches and miles. Toham should have expected that, but somehow she didn’t. There was barely any time to speculate.","n":128},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"- Sure, - she nervously stroked Pink Glasses regretting picking that name for the tom cat. Ginger fur ball didn’t regret a thing, purring like a motorbike. One day, what could go wrong from there? - No big crowds, though.\n\n- Only closest friends, - Camerarius smiled with pleasure. Toham wondered if he really meant it.\n\nWhen the hut was done Kara wanted to test the ground with a first batch of local potatoes. She plowed her rows and planted roots and seeds. It wasn’t hard to wait, at first. Then the week went by, then the next one. Kara thought she was going crazy. Nothing was happening no matter the approach she would use.\n\n- Nothing worked, - Kara was breaking down on the dinner table, almost crying from a defeat. Fezzik sat perplexed with the situation.\n\n- You know you can boost them up, - red man wasn’t quite sure what the problem was. Plants could grow out of any soil and in any conditions if only Kara used some magic, but she just silently refused. It was hard to explain what was going on in her head.\n\n- I have no idea why you struggle this much, I know for sure there are farms that are fine growing potatoes all year round here. Maybe you should ask for their incites? - Abel tried to chip in, but Kara wanted to hear none of it. If she wasn’t able to grow local potatoes free hand in these jungles, she couldn’t call them home. Her garden never needed anything out of the forest. Her potatoes had a chance to survive without her cooing over them every day.\n\n- It doesn’t matter, - Kara stood up and headed upstairs. She couldn’t run from the facts anymore. There was nothing for her in Decem. It was frustrating. There was only one way out of it now and she hated it. She wanted her land back and she only had until next spring to get there.","n":129},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"When the first cluster of islands showed up on the sea glide Toham felt a wave of mixed feelings. Most of which she couldn’t understand the feeling of complete indifference. Like watching a dead land that had no face or set frame. Everything is new and yet nothing is changing. Bound to limitations that are set by outside forces to create the illusion of freedom.\n\n- You look like you’ve seen a ghost, - Parmas broke the silence first. He has seen Decem change three or four times by now.\n\n- How do you ever get used to it? - Toham never was a fan of change. It was something that happened regardless of any efforts to stop it. She really thought that Parmas would understand her, but it seemed that the hero from the state that was in charge of preservation didn’t see anything wrong or terrifying about it.\n\n- Used to what? Decem changes? - Parmas laughed as if she was saying that the sky was down, - That would defeat the whole idea of it, don’t you think?\n\n- And what if there was something that I really liked about it? - Toham tried to come up with an example, but it was tough to remember anything worth keeping. Only brief memories of moments that would not repeat regardless.\n\n- Then you take it out and do your best to preserve it, - Parmas didn’t see the problem and Toham couldn’t describe it.\n\n- And if you don’t want to take it out? - Toham sighed knowing the answer to her own question. If you don’t want to take it out, you stay and protect it. It doesn’t work otherwise in Decem.\n\nKara didn’t know she would sit through this feeling of pure emptiness filling her chest ever again in her life. Emptiness that ignores everything around and all the achievements one has reached seem like a waste of time that is forever lost. It wasn’t painful, it was just like sitting in the dark hole of rock bottom. The worst of all is the never leaving thought that you never ever want to sit there alone ever again. The thought that leads to a burst of tears knowing that there will never be anyone you could share this moment with. Because it was silly. Silly how important something so simple was to someone like you.","n":130},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"Kara hid her face in her palms wishing this moment to pass. So the potatoes didn’t grow on their own in Decem. Why was that something that broke her so deep inside? Was it the failed promise of freedom? Or was it the reality that she just couldn’t accept as the one she could live in? All of the answers were at the tips of her hands, but none of them were something she wanted. All of her friends were happy in this new land. All had something to cling on to, something to grasp and follow. Something to move on to. Something to build up from the ground. Why couldn’t she find something like that? Why it was so difficult to let go.\n\nTwo potatoes in the parcel. There used to be four. Four precious roots that held her mind together. Kara couldn’t hold her weeping now. She threw a chair to block the door. She lost Percival. Biggest and strongest of the batch, he saved Rein in the coliseum. Memories of his leaves rustling in the wind were crippling something inside. She lost Theresa, mindlessly and impulsively. All because she got caught up in running emotions and a silly thought that potatoes couldn’t be this important. Now she was sitting in the dark corner realizing how futile her attempts were to try and give up on something that was holding her afloat. None of it made sense.\n\nToham couldn’t describe the feeling of relief she felt when the earth shook when mechanical soldiers landed around her and Rein. He didn’t run, it was quite pointless at this point. She couldn’t bring herself to look up into his eyes either.\n\n- Code one seven three two seven, - Toham hated saying the words. She hated working with the system protocols and how easy they made some of the toughest choices. Senseless, she could push it further. Make it all even easier. She could call for a memory wiping procedure. She could bend it all to cover all tracks. All that, but she couldn’t do what she really wanted. She could only save one today, unless, - I’ll get back myself.","n":131},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"She started making her way back to the castle, but two soldiers stood in her way pointing at her buzzing collar. Her hand shot up checking the vials as she cursed from a pinch.\n\n- Inconvenient isn’t it? - Rein’s voice from behind sounded spitefully victorious. Toham tried to remember when it could have started. No real way to tell until the adrenaline would wear off and by then it would be late. Probably should have expected that outcome.\n\n- When was it ever otherwise? - Toham didn’t turn around to look back at Rein, she was just looking back at the castle wondering what was going on inside. Tallie probably hated her now and she would never have another chance to explain anything. Not like it would be anything she could explain.\n\nAnd then there was something else. Something that would change quite a bit. Toham just didn’t know how bad it would be. World already went quite blurry. Maybe for the best.\n\nIt was hard to wake up the next morning. Kara didn’t even want to open her eyes until the sunlight filled the room. She woke up maybe three hours ago, passing time scratching the wall to fill the silence. It was hard to concentrate on anything as a single line was running through her mind like a broken record. She had to return home. No matter how, no matter why. Her friends seemed to only lead her astray, further and further away into their own vision of the future. None of them bore concern for what Kara wanted and vice versa. Maybe there was a flaw in that.\n\nWhen Kara finally opened her eyes it felt like a horrible accident. She couldn’t pretend to be asleep anymore and had to face the wooden wall she has been terrorizing for past hours. It wasn’t as bad as turning over and revealing the rest of the room. Cozily decorated, yet foreign and cluttered with things Kara didn’t care about. She could smell the dust in the air. One whirl of the finger later all dust has flown out the window.","n":132},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"It barely made any difference, but it helped to have a reason to stand up and free the door from the chair barricade. Kara checked for fractures, she wasn’t sure how hard she threw it last night. She wondered what sound it made, she couldn’t remember it herself. Step by step, routine took over and she could even catch herself thinking about something else. It would be nice to spend a warm starry night at the hut. She could make a nice hammock on the roof. Maybe she could even bear the proximity of a barren plowed field. She did it all the time during the winters.\n\nIt was hard to breathe through these thoughts, but not impossible. One after another, there was resistance. Not a blockade. It was tough, but she didn’t need to fight. Persistence was quite enough.\n\nToham stood in front of the hut, paralyzed by the flow of memories that now seemed like the most distant past. All the time she had spent here didn’t seem so painful now as it seemed then. How different would it be if even a single plant took root and sprouted here? She would never leave Decem. She would have never looked back at Septum ever again. And then she would. And what would she feel seeing the burnt grove overgrown as if she was never there? Toham didn’t know if it would be any different.\n\nShe dropped to her knees unraveling the plowed ground only to find shriveled decayed potatoes that never sprouted. Toham didn’t even know how that was possible after so much time in the jungle where everything else grew, flourished and renewed so fast. How this land knew exactly how to spite her in a one single way that could have broken her.\n\nIf she planted Vincent here she would never be able to watch him wither away. She would have used magic and he would flourish and bloom for her every day, never sick, always alive. As long as magic flowed through her veins and into this soil. How different would that feel? How would the world find her again? Would he still be blooming here forever after she was gone?","n":133},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"Toham was mindlessly caressing the soil, lost in the visions of what could have been. It was soft and dry, almost like clay sand. Occasionally she would catch a seed, also dry and dead. She couldn’t feel a single beat of life on the field. She wondered if she would revivify it if she felt a single life line. She could have, but that would make her stay.\n\nIt was a quiet breakfast. Kara came downstairs and joined the table, slightly unaware of how much her friends heard yesterday. Usually she would be among the more cheerful heroes at the table, although now that seemed like a stretch. Last thing she wanted is to cause more distress than there already was.\n\n- You alright there? - Fezzik broke the silence and Kara felt her cheeks firing up from an unexpected attention.\n\n- You get what you get, I guess, - she sat down trying her best to gather thoughts into coherent sentence, - It’s just something that had to happen, one way or another.\n\n- Yep, alright then, - Fezzik really tried his best, but she didn’t give him much to work with. Moody teenagers aren't exactly a revolutionary occurrence and he already was struggling with one.\n\nKara felt a reassuring pat on the back. Tallie never really expressed much regard for comfort, but that made it count when she did. Kara never thought about it before, but it must have been as frustrating for the tailed goblin to be so close to the mystery of her family tree and yet unable to grasp it. Kara felt stupid breaking down over some gardening struggles, but it was reassuring to know that at least one hero at the table shared her pain. Maybe even understood. Tallie definitely knew more than she shared.\n\n- Fins can’t pluck, but here’s a hug, - Carp spread his arms as an invitation. That wasn’t something that happened everyday either. To think of it, Kara thought that it was actually the first time. Could have been.","n":134},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"What really happens when your brain goes blank and can’t find any answers to anything. Even something as simple as what you want is the color of the grass. You know the answer, it’s… what was the word again? Something that you get when you mix blue and yellow. It doesn’t even sit at the tip of your tongue. There is just a blank slate of pure indifference and perfect still water of emotions. Like you have been cut out of the world for a second looking at it from a perspective set so far away that even the initial question loses its importance. You understand that you should probably say something, but that only comes from your core principles of interaction. It doesn’t help to be pressured back into reality.\n\nWhether that’s a malfunction or not is something that you need to figure out before you can get out of this state. Malfunction is a valid free pass that lets you reboot the systems and backtrack your progress so far. If it’s not, then you might want to reconsider the way you are going altogether. If something doesn’t compute it only means that you are missing some vital details. Remember that malfunction accumulation can lead to less than desired outcome.\n\nWeathered heroes know that these stutters can cost them lives, so you might want to practice concentration, quick rebooting and green! The grass is Green! They even share the first letters.\n\nIt’s never worth it taking out your anger on somebody close to you. They already get to see your worst as it is. Kara pulled on a smile and went on with the day as if nothing happened. There was one way to get what she wanted and crying about it would not solve anything. Just like sharing it with friends. Their goals had quite little in common. Tournament was about a week away, if she had her days in order. Their training, however suspicious it seemed, was close to completion too.","n":135},{"chapter":"Graduation Candy","text":"When Hendrix appeared on the training set he proudly carried a box of jackets. Kara didn’t quite like the military look, however the red man was so clueless that she couldn’t refuse the offer.\n\n- Now you guys are officially graduated from my training course, I give these out to all my students, - Henry stood as proud as a mother hen. There wasn’t much special about these jackets besides a courkey “Hendrix Cadet Academy” sign and an abundant number of pockets filled with all kinds of snacks and knicknacks. Some of the wrapping rustled as they moved.\n\n- I feel like a pinata, - Kara laughed clapping on the pockets that seemed to create the most noise.\n\n- How dandy, it’s free candy! - Carp jumped up and down. He picked up a stick and smashed Kara’s pockets. She laughed through the pain and threw some candy out as she fell on the ground pretending to be defeated as more candy was thrown out as a confetti.","n":136},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"Heroes face near death experiences all the time. Being so close to the nonsensical action, that they themselves caused, make many sign off the entire experience as nothing. Falling down a twelve floor building and barely missing the speared fence? Doesn’t matter. You are expected to stand up and run right back in. Or dodge something falling straight on top. That often begs for a question, why do these instances revered so much by those who experienced them only once or twice?\n\nToham didn’t care about these questions lately, but it’s surprising how much time you have in your own head when you are bored of processing pain. Revisiting the simple questions like “why it hurts this much” or “when will it give me a break”. Then comes the moment when you seem to grow accustomed to it and you have some time to think of solutions. Except for Toham that was the time to picture green fields of potatoes blossoming with cottony white flowers. There isn’t much else she could really do.\n\nThere are many ways that one could take to reevaluate their life. Deep conversations, traumatic experiences, meditation, education, pilgrimage, travel. Pick your poison. Wherever it will lead you, remember that answers you find will only be a miniscule fraction of the truth. That's really the saving grace of walking a tiny rock ellipsoid in a waste of space. Tiny being an extremely generous description. Almost infinitely.\n\nKara didn’t care much for the sweets and sugars, but she kept the candy bars in the pockets of her jacket. She didn’t know if she would wear it for too long, things rarely stuck around for her to get attached.\n\n- What a weird dude, - Tallie was suspiciously eating a chocolate bar, making sure to chew through it thoroughly. Tailed goblin rarely took somebody unable to stand his ground on the battle ring seriously, leave alone giving her advice on techniques she used. Hendrix wasn’t your book definition of a weathered warrior. In fact, beside his keen eye and silver tongue there wasn’t much else protecting his somewhat clumsy stance. Sometimes Kara thought it was pure luck that he wasn’t stumbling around like a toddler.","n":137},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- I don't know, I thought he was actually quite sweet, - Kara took out a lollipop out of one of the pockets and grinned at Tallie, making goblin chuckle, - Still, can’t shake the feeling we are still in the rotation. Sometimes I don’t understand why they couldn’t be straightforward about these things.\n\nKara opened the candy stick and pocketed the wrapper. She didn’t like to kill the mood, but it was interesting to hear other thoughts on things that worried her daily.\n\n- I don’t know, - Tallie never talked much about her opinions.\n\n- Conseal the trap, surprise with a clap, - Carp was waddling behind, relentlessly munching on the candy like finishing it all in one sitting was his ultimate mission.\n\n- How many ways do you know to get to Decem? - Parmas was getting ready for a descent. Question didn’t seem out of order, just a small banter to kill time.\n\n- Three, maybe four, but I didn’t really polish my albatross form, so I can’t really vouch for the forth one, - Toham stretched her arms trying to figure out how they would feel as wings of a migratory bird. Plenelopy was clearly not wide enough for a full test.\n\n- Three? I thought you only been here once, - Parmas chuckled in an expected surprise.\n\n- Hey, I’m silly not stupid, - Toham dismissively waved her hand in the air, - Water, air and ports, don’t tell me you know only one?\n\n- I sure use only one, - Parmas smiled. Tribus heroes didn’t use teleports, immunities come with downfalls. Toham used a different kind of jumping through space. Maybe that’s why they were friends. Maybe that friendship was just a trick. Except it wasn’t.\n\n- And how many do you know? - now Toham was curious herself.\n\n- Well, I know quite a bit of Culo heroes, - Parmas chuckled and started to steer towards the land.","n":138},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- Well, that’s cheating, - Toham laughed.\n\n- If you tag along I’m sure we could try out one of those, - Parmas smiled and Toham wasn’t sure if he was serious. Culo heroes weren’t exactly the most responsible of path guides for amateurs, but that’s what really made them experts of movement. Untraceable and lord forbids you to get an idea of following after them.\n\n- Don’t put it on a honeymoon idea list, - Toham smiled, - but it could be a fun distraction.\n\nThe graduation party had already started when the trio came back to the tavern. Floats was now playing the whole band of instruments and voices of a crowd were heard from a good distance away.\n\n- Sounds like good fun, - Tallie grinned rubbing her palms together.\n\n- No way anyone in this town will take up your strength challenge, Tallie, - Kara raised an eyebrow and smiled.\n\n- Trust me, I’ll find a fool, - Tallie's grin turned into a deviously greedy expression.\n\nThey opened the front door to an overwhelming cheer of the crowd and Abel swiftly offered a tray with ale.\n\n- Oh, you shouldn’t have, - Kara tried to sound cheeky, but it was hard to be insincere to a truthfully happy Abel, finally in his element. Bar keeper was almost springing on his legs from excitement. He caught her bluff and offered a hand for a dancing spin. It would be rude to refuse.\n\n- Hey Kara, check this out, - Fezzik called her out once the pleasantries were over. He was standing in the little corner booth with a strangely happy hesitation in his movement. It was even stranger that he was all by himself in a room full of people. She made her way over and he furtively showed a shiny gun in the inner pocket of his jacket, - Look I made this one for you.","n":139},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"Kara couldn’t believe her eyes. For a second she was blinking in slight shock wondering why this couldn’t wait until later, but somehow she figured it was hard to keep something this grand in for a long time. Fezzik did look quite proud of himself.\n\n- Are you serious? - Kara didn’t know whether to be mad or happy for this present. Although Fezzik probably wasn’t aware of how she felt about firearms. She never shared that part and it was a little bit too late to dive into details of how that came to be.\n\n- Yeah, wait until you see the engravings. You will love it, - red man seemed to completely miss the que. Kara hated to break his spirit. Guns weren’t allowed in Septum for a good reason.\n\nIt was a mistake to return to the hut. It for sure felt like one. Kara felt it building up in her collar bone as a tidal wave of purest of angers. There was a reason she avoided this place ever since she finished the training. It was simply unreasonably perfect and yet worthless, lifeless and bare. It would take one simple step to fix it and that was the breaking point.\n\nShe wanted it to burn. Like nothing she ever burned before. With flames that will never be put out and spread to ruin all that it touches. So brightly hot that even water wouldn’t stop it. It was a standing proof of how silly she truly was. How deeply her naivety was stopping her to see the reality and not take it as it was.\n\nToham could have left it all here. Blooming or burning, it would have been so much easier if she did. It would have made so much more sense. Blooming would bring her heart at peace. Burning would fill her heart with enough rage to go through with anything that had to be done. But here it stood. Barren, empty, lifeless, abandoned. Last one hurt the most. She wasn’t better than anyone else, what made her entitled to any more choices?","n":140},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"Toham never helped Reins efforts because she never supported his movement, but she never stood in his way. And now, out of all times, she comes back. Not because she really cared, but because she finally had a reason to pluck him out. All circumstances, all around, all just poor excuses for her selfish goals. Goals that were not even completely her own anyway. And worst of all she knew all of it all along. And, yet, there was nothing she could do to avoid it. And, and, and. Some many excuses and no real answers. Infuriating.\n\nFezzik gave Kara a thigh holster and the gun was passed under the table. It was heavier than she expected. Almost as heavy as the thoughts of what she was to use it for. The timing couldn't be most fitting, Kara just didn’t like to think about it. She probably should have started now. She would not want to be the one hesitating.\n\n- Thanks, - She almost silently lipped it, barely using breath on this simple word. Fezzik gave her a thumbs up and called Abel to fill his cup.\n\n- Have you ever used a gun before? - Fezzik started the conversation as if nothing happened.\n\nIt was hard to come up with an answer without a snappy comment, so Kara just shocked her head negatively.\n\n- It’s ok, this one has twelve rounds, you will have plenty of room for practice. I made a really foolproof safety lever, - Fezzik really didn’t make it easy to follow.\n\nKara gave a thumbs up trying her best to look completely aware of what was going on. She probably should have asked more questions, but she felt like something of that sort could wait till later.\n\nAs if.\n\nBefore Fezzik could start a new line of instructions some distant unmatching scream broke though the window. Kara could feel her ears almost twitch trying to follow it to the source. She springed to her feet looking out the window and not quite believing the sight.","n":141},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- What on earth? - She hurried out of the booth dragging startled Fezzik with her, - Floats, there’s fire in the town!\n\nThe music stopped as the blue man stood up from his table in a slight confusion. He didn’t question it and just followed Kara outside. That was enough to get everyone's attention and soon people spilled out of the tavern to the sight of a disaster. Kara wondered what jinxed it.\n\nShe felt her palms burning with fire, it took all the training she could remember not to let it lose. Weighing all pros and cons, a simple fact held her back. If she starts it here there will be no way back. On the other hand she didn’t really know what lies on the other side. That choice was cut out by the collar wrapping her neck. All she was really achieving is stalling. Waiting for the wave to pass and to hit the trough for a breather.\n\nOn the other hand, if she waits for it she will be hit by the next one coming from above. Toham couldn’t hesitate any longer.\n\nIn a bright flash the entire field burst up in the flames. Barren grounds, that seemingly held little flammable material burned as if they were littered with paper, persistently and slowly charring the ground and catching fire to everything around. Trees, none of which Toham bothered to learn the names of, carried the flames up to the skies, signaling everyone in the area the arrival of Septum hero to everyone around.\n\nThe fire spread like a tsunami, Toham ran at the crest of it barely touching the ground between her jumps. It took her minutes to reach the town where Rein and resistance took root. Flames incinerated everything on their way leaving a scorched land of molten rock. At the edge of the town flames hit the barrier and hugged in around like an avalanche around the rock. That should have gotten their attention.","n":142},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"Fire, what else could light up the sight better? It wasn’t clear yet what could have caused it, but it was the first thing that anyone would take notice of. Especially if things burning weren’t your usual lamp posts. Screams filled the streets and Kara didn’t wait to break into a sprint.\n\n- Kara, wait, this isn’t our problem, - Rein stood for a moment, sampling some theories in his mind. He could have been correct, but something was urging Kara to move forward regardless.\n\n- Want to wait until it becomes?! - she wasn’t sure why she felt the need to help people in the town. It could have been anything. Floats’ workshops, that he could probably rebuild anywhere? Maybe Fezziks’ forge master that graciously hosted him? That one didn’t seem lively to begin with. Maybe the people that were generously rewarding Carps’ bad habits? Maybe it was just something the heroes would do, it’s not like she could enjoy the party with such a cacophony of sounds in the background.\n\nPeople were running out of the town and the revolutionary guards were running in. And then the heroes saw them. The elusive goblins that inhabited the Decem state. These looked nothing like gremlins that pestered the fields of Quattor. So Kara stopped, wondering what Tallie would do.\n\n- Aren’t these? - Fezzik stopped to look around, almost having Rein trip over him. Wild goblins in tribal outfits were all over the place burning, slashing and rainsaking the town. It didn’t take them long to take aim at the heroes.\n\n- Yep, - Tallie frowned, charging up her hammer.\n\nToham patiently sat at the edge of the barrier waiting for somebody to come over. It would be a shame to have to break in without a need. Resistance had some formidable forces, so she wouldn’t want to be diving in the heart of it. They weren’t particular fans of her kind around their premises.","n":143},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- You know you don’t have to burn down premises if you just give us a call ahead of time? - Hendrix was walking up to the barrier's edge, surprisingly confident in his safety. Toham wondered if he knew how easy it would be to shatter the thin shimmering veil that separated them.\n\n- So you have time to conspire whatever plot that might come to mister Senior's mind before I even get the chance to set foot here? - Toham raised a brow. If Ember was notified of her departure they were not expecting her arrival for another month or so. One week, if Sweetwater gave her in and they suspected the glider. Half an hour should have been a good surprise time.\n\n- Well, thanks for giving us a moment to bring up the barrier, - Henry was as positive as ever, - That’s definitely nothing like anything I taught you.\n\n- Can’t fall off a tree if there are none around, I hope I made myself quite clear, - Toham stood up, dusting off the ashes off of her pans, - I don’t have reasons to pick bones with rebelion, so, please, tell them not to give me any.\n\n- Stay out of trouble, won’t you, cadette? - it was hard to tell if Henry was actually worried about Kara. Mostly because she really wanted to believe it.\n\nIf Kara wasn’t sure about her standing on goblins ravaging the town, Tallie charging head first smashing one of them into the wall cleared up all lingering questions. One might wonder why heroes would even consider siding with instigators of the conflict, but you have to remember that it was really important to avoid splits in opinion before you proceed with something as ruthless as responsive, how do they call it nowadays, unalivement. A slight safety precaution, if you would like to call it that way.","n":144},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- I mean, they are murdering civilians, - Fezzik raised his skewdriver as he was struggling setting up his machines, - This isn’t something you do in your right mind.\n\n- Don’t make it worse, - Kara fired up Fluffer Huggins and rushed into the next alleyway. No open or broken entries to the houses. That leaves the roofs. An arrow shot hit her in the shoulder before she could react followed with a very conscious giggle of mockery. She growled, pulling out the arrow and forcing the wound to close up with magic.\n\nShe wanted to give the goblin a chase, but a child's cry kept her on the ground. Boy was hiding in the cardboard box with the boots quickly shuffling further inside as Kara stopped abruptly to peek in. Somehow seeing her face made the child scream in terror for a brief moment.\n\n- Peekaboo much? - Kara raised an eyebrow tugging the boy out by his hand, - Listen, hold on to Huggins and he will take you to the big blue man, try and stay behind him and you should be fine.\n\nFluffers popped under the child's hand, all town strolls seemed to pay off. Boy seemed to recognise flaming tom even more so than he did Kara. No wonder, she usually sneaked around as a cat.\n\nFever dreams, Kara forgot she had them. It wasn’t something Toham really tried to retain in ever fleeting memories. She could have remembered snippets and bits occasionally, but never it would be linked to the fever, though it could have. Somehow she remembered everything that happened before as she stepped into the room of a flying airship. Clouds slowly crawling below in the windows with such a reality that she could almost calculate the speed of the wind.\n\nIt was midday and the ship was about to crash, she saw this before. A single red indicator blinked on and off gave it away. She never got time to see what it really was. It was hard to push through the air to get closer to it. This time she did. Nothing surprising. Engine failure, though it was funny how this mysterious light flashed the most obvious two words on the panel that usually only displayed navigation.","n":145},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"Next came the grinding noise and turbulence. It was hard to take it seriously. Kara just stepped through the walls and watched the airship crash down into the clouds followed by a strain of smoke.\n\nShe landed abruptly in the warehouse, runned down and big out of proportion. Somehow she knew exactly where this was. At least that’s how it felt like, people galloping their horses in some competitive game. Like cricket, but more of a tag. She could barely follow it, spaced out thinking about why it was so dark and why no one thought of turning on more lights. One thought and poof.\n\nFluffers ran off with the boy at his side, Kara was wondering if he was on his best behavior or was just floating lifeless when she turned away.\n\n- Floats, do you mind babysitting? - Kara spoke into earpiece, not really expecting a response. Blue man probably just accepted whatever role he was to carry out.\n\nShe tried to catch up to Tallie, who had already left a pretty discrete trail of carnage, however turning into a small square her route was cut off by a group of very angered goblins having a physical quarrel with guards.\n\n- There are seven spotters on the roofs, - Fezzik was speaking into the earpiece and that was followed by a chain of purple flashes in the sky.\n\nFluffers appeared under her hand nudging for a pet. For a second Kara thought that maybe a guard could stand their ground, but this same moment she was about to step back on her way a clunking body flew into the wall right in front of her. Goblin with muscle development that even Tallie couldn’t top was angrily staring her down. So she sighed.\n\n- Lad’s if you mind standing back for me? - Kara brushed a hand over the passed out guard and he coughed himself back into consciousness. She didn’t really know how much the magic would help him survive, but at least he had more chances without broken ribs.","n":146},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- No way! We are rebellion forces, we must stand front lines! - a man in a golden shimmering helmet swung at the goblin. Impressive, but that didn’t open much room for Kara to help.\n\nThe ground grew closer and closer and a green carpet of foliage started taking shapes of leaves and branches. Toham was wondering if Parmas was about to play another trick, but Plenelopy just steadily descended on the branches sliding on them briefly before softly stopping. It felt weightless like a feather.\n\n- Thank you for using Cyano airlines and welcome to Decem, - Parmas smiled as he tapped away at his panel, - We should be close to the swamps, so watch your step.\n\nFront viewing window opened and Toham jumped off to the sturdiest of the branches. When it cracked under her weight she just grabbed on to whatever she could while magic made a stronger branch for her to stand on. She probably should have done it right away, but Plenelopy made it look so easy.\n\n- Alright, - Toham stood upright shaking off the leaves. She briefly looked at Parmas, only to see him silently chuckling, - Don’t.\n\nAnother guard lost his footing and fell on the ground. Kara sighed and adjusted grip on the ax into combat mode. It was easy to take down the bulky goblin striking from a place of surprise. Not everyday you meet a girl wearing just casual clothing swinging deadly blows. The rest of the goblins were not about to make the same mistake.\n\nKara dodged out of the way of a heavy mace that made the air whistle as it barely missed her. She tried to kick the goblin to get him out of the way, but the creature barely budged. There was a reason she didn’t like close quarter combat. Things didn’t go quite as planned.\n\nSecond blow of the mace just barely missed her head hitting hard on her right shoulder almost making her drop an ax from an impact. Fluffer Huggins roared in rage and was about to light the proximity on fire, but Kara got hold of him just in time.","n":147},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"- Not now, Huggins, - she chanted and rolled out of the way, trying to recompose herself. Broken shoulder snapped into place with a painful crack. Maybe she should have let Floats take over and took the babysitting duty instead. Too bad it was a bit too late to reconsider.\n\nThe state of Senio by far is the most undermined of states, mostly because nobody really has any reasons to to truly bicker with it. Being the state of fashion they are professionals of public relations, if there is ever to be a problem they are the most likely to accept compromises and rarely they ever get into trouble big enough to trigger ultimatums.\n\nAll the underground business of Senio state is so far out of anyone’s control that it had a mind and system of its own. Completely below the radar and with barely any repercussions against it. The movement led by the greedy will of those who were consumed by the unreasonable material greed of owning things of value defined by the popular opinion and fueled by an illusion of accessibility. A world created solely for the rush of having access to something seemingly exclusive, but more often than not worthless.\n\nTake the precious rocks per say. A specific standard set by people to reach elusive shimmer that they describe as optimal can be a difference between a masterpiece and rubbish stock. Heroes know that this difference could be the question of life or death, but they live in a world with magic. For anyone else it shouldn’t be a question, yet, somehow it is.\n\nGuards fell like flies. Kara couldn’t help, but think how stupid the miscommunication was. They could have stayed back and let her burn the field. Now as the square littered with potentially salvageable, but immovable cases the situation was reaching a breaking point that Kara wasn’t quite ready to consider.","n":148},{"chapter":"Heads and Tails","text":"An arrow shot past her as she took down another goblin, hitting Huggins out of existence. Silly mistake on whoever chose a wrong target. Kara growled following a path of an arrow to a goblin on the roof. Same one that chuckled at her, now it was grinning at his ultimate triumph making a direct eye contact with her. World grew dull and it took Kara maybe three jumps to traverse the square and make it to the rooftop. It was quite hard to remember what happened next.\n\nKara was pacing the mansion all morning. She knew that she would lose sense of time if she left for her garden, so she was looking around for something to do inside. Pink Glasses was following her wherever she would go with relentless meows, but she tried her best not to give him much mind. Worst of all, she didn’t know what to wear.\n\nShe considered her regular black outfit, but it was a bit too practical and revealing. Her formal dresses were all too official and whatever she wore around Septum would stand out like a sore thumb anywhere else. She could go and see some stores, but it would be a bother to put in effort only to face a puzzled look that Floats would give her if something would be amiss. He already planned shopping, so it would be even worse if he saw something he didn’t before. Maybe she was overthinking it. Maybe she wasn’t. Maybe it was part of the experience.\n\nToham went from room to room, at some point considering repurposing some of the drapes into some summer dresses. Whatever entertained a troubled mind. When Floats appeared at the front door Toham settled on her trekking outfit, hoping that the blue man would have already planned ahead.\n\n- I hope you are ready for a busy day, cinnabon, - Floats seemed to be unbothered by her ruffled look. Combed hair in a neat ponytail seemed to be enough.","n":149},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"Snippets of smells and emotions, flashes of images and sensations, extracts of desires and cravings. Kara didn’t not look forward to coming back to her senses. She sat against the railing of the bridge with Rein clapping up her cheeks saying something about the wrong timing. Everything was aching as if she just crawled out of a strenuous workout in cold water. She wondered what happened to the guards on the square.\n\n- Finally, - Rein sighed and helped her to get up. Kara tried to look around to see what was going on, but a deep thundering growl made her jolt her gaze upwards. Flames of the town illuminated the gigantic scaled torso dashing above with a storm of wind behind it, covering the streets with clouds of ashes.\n\n- Is that? - Kara tried to ask, but the wind and dust swallowed her words. Only one thing in this world could be this big and flying. At least she could only think of one.\n\nParmas jumped off the plane and it disappeared into his watch. He landed softly on the leaves barely disturbing them, it was still surprising how quiet he could be without even trying.\n\n- Alright, what’s the plan then, - it was a bit tough to admit his victory, but there was no real way Toham could see spending so much energy trying to work out the ways of weightless walk. The idea of not moving an inch of soil seemed a bit too considerate for ever changing Decem.\n\n- Sorry, I can pretend to trip if you want, - Parmas laughed and jumped down into her branch netting.\n\n- I don’t think being silly at the same time would be a great idea right now, - she tried to sound reasonable with a keyword. It must have been a bit too obvious.\n\nHis dark figure landed quite heavily in the middle of the netting, making Kara worry about her next step, but, before she could twist out of the way, she got caught in a long embrace. Time and space seemed to have disappeared for a moment and for the first time in long while it didn’t cross her mind to question it.","n":150},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"It was a completely different form of magic. The one so abundantly available and yet so rarely encountered. There was just no material purpose to it and it wouldn’t work if there was.\n\nDragons. As if any story would be complete without one. The idea of omnipotent destructive power is a famous desire among mortals. Although modern literature really brought down their value. Have one and all he wants is to sleep until the end of times, have more and the whole concept falls apart as it is. Whatever thing was flying in the skies right now was just a product of someone’s imagination. Now the problem was to figure out which state played their hand.\n\nIt was terrifying, nevertheless. Napalm of green fire filled the streets, aiming to round up Septum heroes scattered in various alleys. The smell of smoke was burning lungs and Kara was the one used to high temperatures. There was something else in this burning breath. Rein covered his face with a cloak, fighting the urge to cough.\n\n- How are we supposed to bring him down? - Kara looked at the rest of the heroes, but only met the same puzzled inquiry in their eyes.\n\n- I think it’s a she, - Fezzik knew he wasn’t really helping, but now Kara was wondering how in ten states would anyone be able to tell the difference.\n\n- Great, - she growled in frustration, seeing the creature turning around for a final run down the only street they had to stand on. Kara sighed, feeling slightly cornered. She stepped up feeling the ground under the stone pavement. There wasn’t much to work with and she never tried it before, - Take cover, I guess.\n\nThe thing about lucid dreams, the harder you overthink the harder it is to reach the desired result. Especially if it’s something you doubt in yourself. Persistence usually prevails, however as a result the plot shifts.","n":151},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"The lights turned on, but now all horses were well dressed and the game turned into a full scale show. Ribbons of a dressing competition briefly flashed and as Toham tried to focus on what was happening the entire thing turned into a circus and she was in the middle. She dodged elephants and almost trampled a clown.\n\nSomehow a dim warehouse swapped its shape into a well lit circular stage. Toham was getting tired of trying to keep up. Her breath was short, in fact she could barely breathe. Suffocating even.\n\nSpot light was now jumping around in pure darkness. Horse, white in red covers and a feathery crown. Stack of colorful balls, in a cage of a whimsy hula hoop keeping them together, floating whip and burning coil. Kara thought she spotted Fluffer Huggins with a corner of her eye. Not the flaming Huggins. The spotlight started chaotically going from place to place trying to find him, but only found vast emptiness. Spark or two, but those could have been her mind playing tricks.\n\nToham felt her chest ache and head was going dizzy. Time seemed to squeeze through a tube way too small for its volume. Their light turned to darkness as all of her attention was consumed by a single focus of trying to wake up. She could almost feel something on the other side of the veil of drowsiness.\n\nShe tried to remember what was so important that she needed to wake up for. It couldn’t have been simply the fear of death. Flash of memory came through like a clear image that she thought she imagined. Green leaves of her garden, why did it take her this long to remember it?\n\nKara could almost feel the gaze of the dragon on her as the fire poured onto the street. Forty meters into the air, the flying lizard couldn't feel the rumble of the ground. Flaming buildings collapsing all around masked the ones crumbling from something else.","n":152},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"The benefit of not using the full extent of your power is that no one can really predict it. For heroes, of course. There was one more thing that worried Kara about her little field in the jungle. It was testing her patience, almost compelling her to use magic to grow the plants. That made this situation even more frustrating.\n\nThe moment the dragon's head inched over Kara twisting vines shot out of the ground tangling it’s snout shut and inertia twisted it’s body upside down, making it collapse on the building's back first. Kara barely managed to untangle an opening for herself.\n\nFog of dust and ashes filled the air as the giant lizard tried to free itself from tight constricting vines. It wasn’t long until the fist lightning struck, flashing white the milky orange and green smoke. Then followed the flash of purple, Kara thought she could hear Rein chanting something to himself. She could feel the vines snapping, as a panicked dragon was trying her best to get out of the grip.\n\nFinally the flash of green burned up once more and the dragon hastily flapped her wings in an attempt to disappear into the night. At least that’s what Kara thought had happened. It was hard to know for sure with blurry shadows and muffled sounds as a main source of information. Out of fog rushed Carp, seemingly overexcited.\n\n- We made it bleed, Must stop retreat! - The fish masked goblin was on a mission. Though it was quite puzzling how he planned on catching a dragon high up in the air.\n\n- Hey, Carp, catch! - was it the greatest idea to give a goblin a firearm? That question didn’t occur in Kara’s head as she tossed a gift she received maybe less than half an hour ago from Fezzik. Was it a pretty gun? Will she ever get it back? Kara was stunned by the amount of regret she experienced from what she just did.","n":153},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"It didn’t take long for the first gunshot to echo through the burning streets. Then another one further down. And another and so on. Every new one just confirmed the fact that the beast was running for its life. From a small fish masked goblin. With a gun.\n\n- Kara, - voice of Rein from the back snapped the girl with an ax and green eyes back to her senses, - Did you just give Carp a gun? Where did you even get one?\n\nThat was a conversation Kara did not look forward to.\n\nAir was fresh and even the sweet notes of magic tasted like mint in lungs. It felt different from what Toham has remembered it to be. It was light, new, thrilling.\n\n- Alright, - She leaned out of the kiss and suddenly forgot the line as she caught shining golden eyes looking back at her with a smile that you could only see in the slight glint of light in a gaze.\n\n- What about? - it was thrilling to hear his voice so close, Kara almost wondered if he said the words just to see how she struggles to gather hers together.\n\n- Nothing, - she leaned back in and rested her head on his shoulder trying to make the moment last a bit longer. She wanted to tell him about everything in the world all together, in one go and yet, not a single thought came to her mind, - can’t believe we are actually here.\n\n- Ain’t bad, right, - Parmas hugged her tightly, - Not scary at all, isn't it?\n\nThere it was, the reminder Toham didn’t welcome one bit. The feeling that she almost shoved and locked in the drawer was knocking at the walls of its cage.\n\n- Not at all, - she lied, hoping it would help. Her gaze wandered off into the distance, looking for a distraction.\n\n- I’m glad you tagged along, - Parmas gave her head a little kiss. It was fascinating how calm he was. Toham almost felt like she was the one being unreasonable with her worries and fears.","n":154},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"Kara considered faking to faint from exhaustion. It wouldn’t be too far from reality. Her head was pounding and she was out of breath from smoke. Maybe it was the fact that she made the vines sprout up from non-existence. They were now almost all decayed into soft powder scattering in the wind. Kara wondered why Rein couldn’t be just excited about this new trick.\n\n- Fezzik made me one, - she weighed her options, but there was no point in trying to avoid this conversation. However painful it was, it would have happened eventually. Septum heroes didn’t really have many secrets when it came to firepower.\n\n- Why would you ask for a gun? - Rein was getting visibly angry. Kara understood his frustration, in fact she shared it. Guns were one thing that made even the weakest of characters a force that could take life. Instantly, irreversibly, unfairly.\n\n- I didn’t, it was a gift, - Kara sighed. However strongly she would hate the concept of having a gun in her mere proximity, she couldn’t shake off the feeling that it would be the only way she would be able to handle killing the heroes of other states. Quick trigger pull should have been easy enough. Maybe that’s why she accepted it. Much easier than burning or ripping apart.\n\n- You know they are illegal,right? - Rein was really pushing his arguments. Kara tried to read what was really on his mind, but failed to follow the line of reasoning.\n\n- And what exactly are we doing in Decem then? - she was losing her composure. It wasn’t right to have a gun. It didn’t feel right, but no one ever showed her the list of rules for heroes. Unus had no problem with guns and they sure will have to go through their hero squad eventually if they were to survive the tournament.","n":155},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"- Do whatever you want, - Rein snapped and headed back to the tavern. Kara couldn’t put her finger on it, but for a second it felt like he was upset that Fezzik didn’t make a gun for him. She signed it off as a silly thought. Rein for sure would have refused the offer. He wasn’t the one to hesitate.\n\nLove is a word. It is highly subjective and situational and often varies vastly in its definitions due to the personal experiences and beliefs of the individual. Yet, somehow it is widely considered to be a universal belief. Like a solution to all paradoxes and a common denominator to every difference. The extent of its vagueness can be described as two people pointing at something one sees as a banana, while the other sees the spatula and both agreeing that they see stars. And then the rest of the observers just shrug their shoulders as they see a boat. Although the proper word would be a ship, however you could say a poodle and it wouldn’t change a thing.\n\nWhat really matters is the strength of this word. One could easily describe the feelings that it brings and falsely assign these as the definitions, however there are but mere symptoms. You could measure the strength of the symptoms to define the effect of love on you, but that really just compliments the object of the passion and not something within yourself. The strength of your own love is defined by the lengths you are ready to go to inflict such feelings upon the object of your passion. The obstacles that you overcome, whether you see them clearly or breeze through. Really depends on the curse that you carry within your belief system. Thats why most find their peace within the brackets they were brought up in. It’s much easier when the views align.","n":156},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"This, however, doesn't change the base statement. One will always see what the other doesn’t and yet agree on the common ground. That’s the magic trick.\n\nKara didn’t refuse the gun for one more reason. It has been some time since she received gifts and it was the feeling she didn’t know she missed. It warmed a part of her soul that wasn’t quite suffering, potatoes couldn’t really be blamed for being unable to perform such acts of friendship and they already provided her with all they could. Walking the charred streets she couldn’t shake off the feeling that maybe it was time to move on.\n\nAs it happens way too often, after the fire came rain. Kara tried to ignore the horrific sight of the aftermath of the inferno. Her gaze didn’t wander around focused on the path right in front of her. The only exception was the charred corpse of the goblin that Tallie cradled in her arms bursting in tears.\n\nSuddenly the entire world seemed to turn upside down. When tailed goblin rushed into the battle Kara didn’t give it a second thought. Now the realization hit her and she could barely find her footing. Potatoes can’t run or fight. Goblins could and did. Whose side would Kara take if potatoes suddenly turned on people? Tallie just answered this question for both of them and Kara wasn’t quite sure she agreed. Especially now, when she saw the result.\n\nToham tried to approach her goblin friend, but Tallie stood up and rushed away. Kara didn’t follow, feeling her knees barely holding up.\n\n- They didn’t have tails, - She tried to state the facts, however something told her that wasn’t something as relevant.\n\n- They didn’t, but they weren’t regular either, - Fezzik finally spoke up. Something he should have done when Rein questioned the gun, - This dragon apparently hoards the hoards.","n":157},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"Toham watched Hendrix go down the hill to the town. Funny how she didn’t even know its name. It also could have been the Town town, but nobody called it that. Regardless, rebelion did a great job at hiding and protecting it. Not having a name could have been intentional.\n\nFluffer Huggins pulled on her shoulder sleeve and Toham gave him a tired look. Kara knew that it was time to go, but for some reason it was hard to gather together the courage to act. Toham didn’t like that at all. If bursts of energy have caused hyperactivity in Fluffers it couldn’t have possibly been a good thing.\n\n- We’ll get there when we get there, buddy, - Toham sighed and followed the suddenly lively and impatient Huggins. He jumped around and even snapped her heels, - Well, that’s a new one! Are you picking bad habits from Glasses?\n\nIt was hard to tell what was going on. Toham sure thought it was stupid to talk to a cat that understood commands on a pure instinct. There was barely any processing power to comprehend something beyond simple chains. However stupid and fun often go together. It’s not like rebelion knew her for outstanding smarts.\n\nThere was something that Fezzik discovered in the archives. The rebellion tomes that told the story of the fall of the Warrithri tribe. Well, more of a folktale. Except when you put two and two together it was as much of a horror. Kara didn’t think much when she flipped through the pages with happy tailed goblins assembling a complex machine. Here is the one in a lab coat and here is the one that can carry two cows. Here they trip on a button and a colorful explosion goes off in the distance. Here they are choosing their chief.\n\n- So what? Why all of a sudden a hoards hoarding dragon suddenly decided to attack rebelion? - Kara didn’t really want to know the answer to the question, but laying out things on the table had to start somewhere, - What are they not sharing?","n":158},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"- Kara, pretty sure we all saw these goblins following little to none moral ethics, - Fezzik reassured her on something she didn’t know concerned her.\n\n- Wait, - Kara started feeling nauseous, - Don’t dragons value their hoards? Doesn’t it hurt to burn it?\n\nKara really didn’t like how that last sentence came out. She looked over at Fezzik who was scratching the back of his head.\n\n- You will be surprised, he gave them plenty of notice, - it was almost obvious that the red man was walking around something. It wasn’t that hard to figure out what that was.\n\n- Did I?... - Kara painstakingly tried to gather the snippets she could remember. It wasn’t a lot , but quite enough.\n\n- Nope, you didn’t. You finished the goblins and Rein barely managed to get you away from the guards, - It was nice that Fezzik didn’t drag the stories out.\n\n- In my defense, I told them to get out of there.\n\n- You went too far, - Toham never heard Eve speak in this voice. A scent of concerned disappointment.\n\n- I never said I am on board with the idea, - Toham barely felt her fingers. And barely was enough. She sat up feeling the overwhelming simultaneous splash of synopses. She woke up on the floor with a pounding headache.\n\n- And that’s your reason? - Eve was calmly tucking an ornate pillow under Kara’s head. She wanted to say something else, Toham never heard these pauses.\n\n- I’m sorry, - Toham knew that she should have said something else, but that’s all she could master. Eve could have done it so much better.\n\n- Sorry doesn’t exactly fix the situation, - Eve couldn’t find the right footing to sit. After a slight consideration another pillow impacted the floor and finally the woman with olive skin landed on it like a sparrow, - It’s in your head, you can stop it.","n":159},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"- What exactly am I to stop? - Toham raised a brow, trying to tease Eve.\n\n- Stop and think, you silly, - Eve bent down to give Kara a hug with a shake. It was really hard to figure out if she actually could answer these questions.\n\n- I stopped, I thinked and I still feel that this is the line that I draw, - Toham suddenly remembered that she hated world plays, - And I am not about to pretend to understand you on that.\n\nFact about goblins that Kara never paid close attention to before today.\n\nThey really didn’t quite like to mingle with other tribes. It’s not like they hated each other, no, they would actively form hybrid tribes that worked together to reach a specific goal. However different in nature, the tribe follows rules unanimously upheld by the goblins. Some sort of an honor code.\n\nIn a simpler sentence “you can trust a goblin to be a goblin”. I wouldn’t bore you with a cliche reference. Kara went to the tavern only to find it scorched, but yet somehow standing. Abel must have really liked his job.\n\nRest of the heroes took other routes to look for survivors. People were gathering at the meeting point to recuperate. Strange tradition, but close proximity seemed to help people cope with loss of property and … People. You would expect Abel to be responsible enough to stay out there alone.\n\n- Hey, do you need any help? - Kara suddenly woke up from a long trip looking just under her steps. It was really hard to think clearly.\n\nFrom the open doors appeared Abel with a welcoming smile, - Yeah, this was a wild night, want some vitamin C?\n\n- Sure, - Kara even broke a strut for a pill, - Is anyone injured?\n\n- Nothing they wouldn’t survive, they just need some place that is not a blanket on a crowded floor.","n":160},{"chapter":"Dragons and Hoards","text":"- Sure no one would like that, - The sweet and sour taste on the tongue seemed out of place in the situation.\n\n- You're in for the night? - Abel wasn’t sure about this question, but it seemed to be the only words he could master. Kara wasn’t even the most regular of these rooms.\n\n- We have to meet up at the center, I can pass the count, - Kara wasn’t sure if helping the system counted as helping the rebellion. It was just a nice thing to do for general safety. Plus she had way bigger things worrying her.\n\n- You need names? - Abel laughed looking over his shoulder and counting up the tally, - We have six with me.\n\nIt was nice that Abel had friends.","n":161},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"What would be the right way to tell someone that their entire idea of existence is wrong? The problem is that there would be no communication possible if that was the case. If the idea is completely out of line it means that the forces that are making it believe so should be strong enough to put a bunch of inhibitors to avoid contact of a proof of a scheme. Right way would probably start with an investigation of such inhibitors. Too many words in the buffer, who on earth would know where the key to misalignment is.\n\nToham didn’t quite know why Parmas chose to spend so much time with her in the first place. It was quite concerning that he barely knew who she was. He wasn’t really in the loop of regular hero talk. Just another hermit like her. Toham just didn’t know what could be wrong about it and it was something new and haunting. It creeped into every single moment it could, staining the clear reflections with familiar, but unknown, fingerprints. All while instincts urged to wipe the glass and return the untainted form.\n\nToham couldn’t find a single vital flaw. Parmas was as lonely as she was. Tribus had no bickers with Septum. If there was something to be achieved, it had way better methods to choose from. The only downfall is opening a window of weakness, but that’s a given sacrifice for experiencing and committing to love. If it’s not made, then it’s not done right and Toham wasn’t about to make such silly mistakes. It wasn’t rocket science.\n\nIt was a quiet walk to the refugee station. Except for the buzzing in the ears from exhaustion and battle echoes. Kara tried to muster up some recollections, however brief flashes made her shiver. They were a bit too vivid and she could barely grasp the details. Worst of all were the flashes of Reins face. The frustration in his eyes and a weird clarity of mind was just enough to do exactly the opposite of what he seemed to want.","n":162},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"She wondered if the guards survived.\n\nIt wasn’t easy to pick up on something else ringing in the ears. Something that didn’t seem to come from within. Like a soft sleepy chanting whisper. With a rhythm and even some melody.\n\n- Grow, grow, we want to grow.\n\n- Sleep, quick, till water leaks.\n\n- Grow, grow, to reach the Sun.\n\nThe whispers came from the parcel. Kara stopped and felt the terror creeping up her spine. Years she spent wondering what potatoes would answer her if she would ask. Years she knew they never would.\n\n- You guys speak? - She opened the parcel and strained her eyes to make out two little potato shapes.\n\n- Yes, we do and all the time, - the whispers sounded chipper, like it was nothing out of the ordinary.\n\n- I’m sorry about Teresa and Percival, - Kara didn’t know what else to say. It was a little hard to fight a thought that her mind had just potentially gone completely haywire.\n\n- Grow, grow, we want to grow, - the chant continued as if Kara said nothing at all. Almost mindless.\n\n- So is there anything you want right now? - Kara sighed. Why would she ever think that potatoes had the capability to know or remember anything.\n\n- Grow in soil, bloom and live, - potatoes chanted happily, completely unbothered by a sudden barrage of questions.\n\nKara wanted to ask if they were aware what potatoes were usually grown for, but if they didn’t recognize their own names, it was highly unlikely that they were aware of the dangers of reality. They grew in the garden where no one ate potatoes.\n\nIt is normal to run out of ideas halfway through the journey. It’s like reaching the eye of the hurricane. You have only reached the center and yet it feels so calm and steady. With winds still and a wall of destruction shielding you from the chaos all around. You could stay there and wait the storm out, keeping yourself up on your toes and ready to play by the rules of nature.","n":163},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"Or you can take the risk and force your way forward. Into a longer journey if the storm is strengthening and shorter if you are late to the party. It’s not hard to figure this one out either. The only thing stopping you from this journey is fear. If you were destined to die you will die and vice versa. Funny thing.\n\nKara did chat a bit more with potatoes, about things that they missed and adventures they had. Nothing was interesting enough to change their slumber chants. Occasional response to a question and back to chanting. Kara for sure thought she was going crazy. Potatoes never spoke before. Maybe they absorbed some magic from the air.\n\nShe was sitting outside the tent looking at the stars. She checked in and came right out to have a moment to herself. It was funny how stars shined so much brighter now that the fire burned out all the lights in the town. They said that people got dumber when they dared to outshine the stars. Blinded by the luminescent fog of their own doing. There was the rest of this train to explore, but it was suddenly interrupted by Rein storming out of the main tent.\n\nHe was fuming. Conrad must have been in there. It was hard to look that man in the eyes. Unsettling. Like the man had lost touch with reality and acted as if it was the future in his hands, but he lacked the soul.\n\n- Is he trying to pass as a normal man? - Kara nonchalantly invited Rein to join her. She didn’t expect him to sit down, but it felt good when he did. So much time they spent bickering now felt like a fun game.\n\n- He has so much influence and in so many years he didn’t even think about bringing us here with him! - Rein tried to sum up all he could master. Kara felt the nervous shake, she wasn’t the greatest at coming up with important words.","n":164},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- Yep, he definitely could, - Kara threw around a couple of ideas in her head and could come up with at least two routes with plenty of intermediate options.\n\n- All of a sudden got interested in me when I got the power to do it all on my own and now he is interested? - Rein ripped some grass and threw it into the air. The grass blades shot out like needles, straight forward, burning from speed with a purple flash. Kara could swear she could even hear them pop.\n\n- Beats me.\n\nYellow, no, golden. Within crests and petals nests the pitch black pupil. It glittered, slightly. Toham was entertaining musing thoughts as she followed Parmas through the jungle. It was tricky at times, but she enjoyed how freely the magic around flowed like the wind. Effortless and almost weightless. Parmas barely touched the ground as he stepped, Toham just slightly moved the foliage to carve around them.\n\n- How many times a year do you come here? - Toham was considering switching for paws. Walking seemed quite boring after some while.\n\n- This is my fifth station, first visit, - Parmas stopped to look under some fallen treetrunk. It dripped in water and bent like a stiff sponge, - Well, that’s new.\n\n- Did the algae just evolve into the tree? - Toham couldn’t quite yet believe her eyes. She never thought that this what Parmas meant by research, - Chimeras?\n\n- Convergents, - Parmas lifted the trunk up and teleported it. Somewhere. Toham wanted to ask, but she had better questions.\n\n- And you think a new name would make me happy? - Toham felt a sudden tilt. She could have predicted it, but she chose not to. Maybe she thought that it wouldn’t matter?\n\n- You are silly, chimeras are made through synthesis. Convergents just evolve similarities naturally, - Parmas held up the tree like a kelp to Toham, - You see, there is no tree in it. It’s even mimicry maybe.","n":165},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- Still doesn’t feel very natural, - Toham could accept a defeat, but that would have killed the conversation.\n\n- Oh no, it is definitely not, - Parmas shook his head, - And it’s not made by any particular hero either.\n\n- You think resistance? - Toham felt an uneasy tug. It was bad to blame things on some people just because there was no one else.\n\n- Kara, not everything is connected to politics. Sometimes nature happens too and it’s ok, - Parmas gave her a friendly shoulder pat.\n\n- Is Tribus working on making plants into heroes? - Toham should have stopped.\n\n- You really don’t lie when you say you are really bad at guessing.\n\n- Told you. Absolutely, the worst.\n\n- What are you going to do now? If we don’t win the tournament we can’t really return to Septum and I don’t think that being on the run is exactly the winning strategy, - Kara sighed and looked back up at the stars like they would have a scope.\n\n- Whatever happens, we have until the end round to come up with a solution, - Rein rarely talked about the tournament like that. Kara didn’t know what got into him after this fight, but he seemed softer. Almost bearable to be around for a good while.\n\n- You know I didn’t see any good hospitals around either, - It wasn’t that Kara tried to justify Conrads decision not to bring his family along to join his new life in Decem, but the notion did almost made her gag.\n\n- There is no way around it if we don’t win the tournament, - Rein sounded resolved. Winning would be a good start to a plan.\n\n- Well that’s great. Good to know that we are on the same page for once, - Kara nudged Rein with a shoulder, - I missed that.\n\nThere was something about Floats that made everything fall into neat order. Not that it was the correct order, but it was stable enough to make Toham relax just ever so slightly. When the door of the mansion opened to the Senio front yard. Bright with neon lights and prismic windows. It looked like a welcoming party.","n":166},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"~ Welcome back! ~\n\nToham recognised the faces, but didn’t really know the names. And the Tribus crew, without Parmas. Almost his entire squad and she could barely look them in the eyes. They just never talked, they surely already knew.\n\n- I should have expected that, - Toham forced a smile and waved to the crowd, - Hi?\n\n~ Show chatter. ~\n\n- That is a preposterous claim, the show is widely available. Lack of local popularity is a compliment on its own. No one is complaining either, right? - Toham rolled her eyes at a woman with feathers in her hair. Feather.\n\n- I knew that Novem is playing a hand! - Feather was chipperly victorious. She nudged a man orchestrating the lights. Lights, - I told you! His friends have teeth everywhere.\n\n- Not really, there is just a big gap in audience, - Light went on a tangent and talked on. Toham could barely pick up his trail of thought, pleadingly looking over to Floats.\n\n- Don’t mind them, they are just excited to see you out on the streets, - Floats smiled and walked on.\n\n- Why would you call so many? - Toham shrugged.\n\n- We can’t leave the seats empty, I already cut the standees! - Floats waved his hand and opened the door.\n\nLater that night Carp returned Kara her gun. He said that dragon flight wasn’t a running fight. That spelled disaster in capital letters, but really, really far away. That and that Carp was probably a terrible shot. What sort of fish misses bullets addressed to a dragon? Kara wondered if bullets could even hurt a dragon. Did dragons really exist? It seemed to be a reasonable explanation, however a little bit too much for plot development.\n\n- Good job, Carp, - Kara gave a fish masked goblin a thumbs up. Regardless of the result, it had to take some insane chunk of bravery to go on a whole dragon chase all alone with nothing but a gun. Without blinking an eye. Kara wondered what it felt like, but, given habits that seemed to trail by such style, it probably wasn’t something she would want to experience.","n":167},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- She flew fast, can’t be rust, - Carp’s fish eyes were staring at Kara with a blank stare of a soulless indifference. The Goblin under the mask didn’t sound as emotionally sound.\n\n- I know, it’s a nice gun isn’t it? - Kara smiled and finally dropped her eyes to give her firearm a good look. She just couldn’t believe that Fezzik would give something illtuned.\n\nIt was a silly idea, but it matched the theme. Good thing that Fluffer Huggins already perfectly mimicked sculpting clay. Muzzle spat bullets like Fluffers spat his fire. One by one. Golden shimmer and a thoughtful wooden handle. Kara could feel the wood still sizzling under fresh coating. A little magic boost and the tension eased. It was funny that this barrel was now officially a magically enhanced unit. On paper.\n\nToham remembered why she didn’t really like when people spoke of Eve. It was a game she played to protect a deal that they had made. Not that anyone really knew about it, it wasn’t something that would concern most. Septum heroes were already a unit, cooperation was already expected.\n\n- You know that I want only the best for you? - it was hard to decipher the emotions behind the calm voice of Eve. Sothing, vibrant, almost melodic. It was hard to tell if she was sugarcoating.\n\n- Sometimes wanting the best for one is worse for others, - Toham felt absolutely defeated. This wasn’t the talk she could master in her state.\n\n- It works out in the end, no matter, - Eve seemed to be balancing herself on the very edge of her temper. Toham wondered where the button was. A silly thought, but anything was better than thinking straight.\n\n- So you go out of your way and sneak out to Decem. Cause mayhem in the middle of preparation season, - Eve stopped listing.","n":168},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- And, - Toham could see with the corner of her eye the floor flicker with light of flames.\n\n- And we talked about it. Such violence is not the solution to your problem, - Eve sighed, there was nothing really else she could say.\n\n- It got the attention and pieces in place. I thought out of all people you would be the most happy about it, - Toham mastered her strength and tried to sit up. Same story, less falling this time.\n\nIt took quite a bit of chilly weather, but finally Kara decided to make her way in. Stepping over a surprisingly numerous amount of sleeping bags, she finally made it over to the main table. It was filled with snacks and water, somewhere in the corner stood a soup barrel. There was a word for it, but it was so big it might as well have been a barrel. Hopefully not the oil one, but it wasn’t really Kara’s place to complain. Abel probably had them covered.\n\n- How long can people talk about goblins and dragons? - Kara dropped her palms on the table, almost sarcastically.\n\n- Good for you to join us, - Conrad's voice made Kara jump in surprise, - We were talking about it…\n\n- Who is “we”? - Kara didn’t enjoy the fact that this man was in her friend circle. She gave Fezzik a scorching look.\n\n- Kara, this isn’t a place, - Fezzik tried to step her out of the table, but Kara stood sternly on her grounds.\n\n- It definitely isn’t as long as he is around, - Kara stepped towards Conrad, but Fezzik jumped in front of her.\n\n- Didn’t take you for a vengeful kid, - Conrad's words were hitting places, just not the ones that he probably hoped for.\n\n- Fezzik, - Kara felt Huggins sparking through the ethereal veil, - We. Are. Going. Home.\n\nThe feeling was overwhelming. Huggins for sure wasn’t listening to the commands. He would run off, frolicking in the burning woods like it was the best place in the world. Kara was now calling for him with full lungs. She was getting frustrated. This has never happened before. Toham was panicking now.","n":169},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- Fluffers! - Kara screamed as flaming tom rushed forwards pulling her forwards. It felt like a wave, - Stop!\n\nFluffers briefly disappeared and fired up right beside her, visibly angry with an unexpected jump. She never saw him scowl to her face before.\n\n- So all this time and now you wanna go?! - Kara growled right back at him, - I know he is in trouble! That’s not the reason to be upset about it now, isn’t it?\n\nToham straightened up and looked in the direction where Huggins was pulling her. She thought that she would drop by Tallie to see Reins whereabouts. It seemed that flaming tom had a better plan. Which was useful, but didn’t quite spell out rainbows in prospect. A problem for another day.\n\n- Smart, Fluffers, - Toham sighed with relief, - Got me worried there for a second.\n\nIt was hard to tell why Kara didn’t leave with Rein. Floats and Fezzik weren’t really the ones in need of a chaperone and Carp never had a bedtime. And, yet, all four of them were walking down charred streets to the tavern. Kara led the way and briefly wondered how everything heated up so quickly.\n\n- I know he is probably trying his best or whatever, - Kara was looking down on the pavement, - But I would rather not have anything to do with him.\n\n- Hey, it’s ok, - Fezzik gave her a shoulder pat. Not something the girl expected, but a girl appreciated it, - You are angry, it is normal.\n\n- And I am still mad at you for taking his lessons, - Kara was really going all out tonight. Seemed only right, - I am surprised Rein was ok with it.\n\n- Alright Kara, we are adults. I know a thing or two about life too, - Fezzik was getting snappy.\n\n- Well, I met a pretty girl with some confirming intel, - Floats suddenly spoke up and Kara suddenly remembered that there was another person in the circle, - Oh no! We didn’t get to introduce her to you!","n":170},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"- Wait, what did you guys talk about? - Kara was dumbfounded by a sudden intermission.\n\n- Oh, sweet nothings! She told me about the federal laboratory in the area. Same old story. Chimeras in the forest, goblins rush to towns that hate goblins, goblins learn to hate people. Only the doozy catch is that this hoard belongs to a dragon now!\n\n- Wow, that’s a convenient explanation, - Kara wouldn’t believe it if she wasn’t a witness. Floats explained something that actually made sense. Either that or Kara was just chasing the high roll and pulling on farfetched strings.\n\n- Yeah, very convenient, - Fezzik suddenly regained his energy, - What did you say her name was?\n\nIt was almost uncanny to follow Fluffers. He seemed happy that Toham finally understood what he wanted.Toham was pondering the possibility of a hoax. If teleportation systems could be rigged with magic.\n\nToham stopped in her tracks and watched as Huggins in a rush ran out of existence briefly and ran back in with a confused look.\n\nShe took a step and waves of grass rushed over the flaming jungles, overwhelming fire with life. Huggins just continued staring in confusion and then calmly returned to her side. Kara hated to see him so lifeless again. Like a robot doll, she could have ported him right back to her side, but there was something about his burning steps on the fresh grass.\n\n- Hey buddy, it’s not like I want to force you to hang out around me, - speaking to inanimate magical constructs. Toham really had to get her head straight. Where was she going? Why did goblins attack Reins house? What were they trying to orchestrate? - Maybe just not strong enough, yet. We’ll get there.\n\nIt was even wilder to promise something to a flaming tom. Didn’t hurt anybody, so shouldn’t have been a problem. However, at the same time, she did burn down the forest. There weren’t supposed to be any casualties if rules were followed. However Novem intruders would suggest that this was a quite a poor excuse. Nevertheless, neither could really voice their opinion.","n":171},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"Fluffers sat down. It was ridiculous how impartial he seemed to be at such questions. He was the one of the two to prefer to go for a kill. Good thing he was just fire.\n\n- Laboratory that releases chimeras? - Rein was looking skeptical. For a moment Kara thought she could even hear his voice waiver, - And why would the federal laboratory be built in Decem, the place they don’t even recognize existing?\n\nIt seemed obvious to Kara, but it took Rein a question and a long thoughtful pause. Points for style, but who knows what he was really thinking of. Because what he said next made all sense and no sense at the same time.\n\n- We are not doing jobs for resistance, - cut quick and clean. Just as it should have been. Rein did what had to be done. It took Kara a moment to think about it.\n\n- You know, if these labs make chimeras, they probably have an advanced medical bay, - The words came out as naturally as a cold mountain stream. Kara almost hated how trumping they were in this situation. She hated the idea of becoming involved with a foreign dispute, but it was different when it turned into personal priority. Worst of all, she wasn’t prepared for the cascade of consequences that it had caused.\n\n- You are right, - Rein suddenly sat straight in his chair, - We will claim the laboratory for ourselves! This just works out perfectly!\n\nIt wasn’t the sudden uprise in volume that froze the air. Kara had maybe a second or two to exchange glances with Fezzik. The plan made a bit too much sense.\n\nCold.\n\nShe missed it. There was a brief millisecond of it in every flame. The difference where the heat exchange happens so fast due to the boiling of water that for a tick it feels cooler. Hoarding all of the attention and energy around a single particle to break through the surface tension and roam the air at once.","n":172},{"chapter":"Same Story","text":"Her awakening had none of it. Back to her room. Plain, chaotically organized, not a single thing out of place. In fact, too many things were in their places. So many that Toham could feel the walls vibrate with energy.\n\n- #@*^+.","n":173},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"There is a difference between attacking a guard that is trying to keep you in and a guard trying to keep you out. Like yin and yan, so different and, yet, balance the whole. Just kidding, it’s like the sides of the coin. Watch and question what you read. There is a difference between running away from the conflict and diving head first into it. That would be yin and yan, but that’s objective.\n\nKara was slightly terrified at the level of ambition, but regardless whether they were to stay in Decem and fight chimeras until the end of the day or be fearless heroes of the states that did something brave and vague during their training season. Convoluted, but with the veil of information blockade that was the exact situation. Septum heroes were quite literally stranded on their own. As long as they survived the tournament all bets were up.\n\n- Wow, that actually is a good plan, - Kara looked up at the rustic tavern chandelier. The feeling of resolve was quite refreshing. It would be a nice walkdown before the tournament begins. Finally there was some sort of a goal and a plan, - And I guess there is no point in shutting rebellion out since we decided to be neighbors. That doesn’t mean we are friends.\n\nFezzik shrugged and shook his head, - Still don’t know why you hold it against the entire resistance.\n\n- We don’t hold anything against resistance, that’s the point, - Rein stood up and waved down his hand over the table, - We gotta let them know that we are the ones in charge from now on!\n\n- I mean, that would help the goblin problem, - Kara was throwing some ideas around. Good old funnel. Tight spot, checked allegiances and boost into a blender. Best you can do is to route benefits.","n":174},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- So what, now you don’t want to frolic? - Kara looked straight into Huggins lifeless eyes, looking for a reaction, - I wonder if it’s because of fire abundance. You wouldn’t tell me, right?\n\nToham sighed and gave Fluffers a head pat and Flaming tom purred in response. Like a clock. Spin, sit, roll, run. Adaptive magic. It responds to the needs of the desire. Toham was looking for Rein, there was an overflow of matching elemental frequencies and Fluffers must have been pulled into a funnel. Not the greatest of the signatures, but a crazily familiar one.\n\n- Well, I should have expected that one, - Toham sighed, but Fluffers seemed quite serious, - Guess there is no point in crushing the party if you are not in the heat of the action.\n\nFlaming tom violently shook his fire in a rattle. A new trick Toham just learned.\n\n- OH MY SWEET SEEDLINGS! - Kara finally took a good look at the engravings on the gun in bright light, - This is genius!\n\nFezzik’s face of shock switched into a satisfied smile, - See, I knew you would like it.\n\nKara couldn’t believe her eyes and couldn’t hold laughter. As ridiculous as it was, it did perfectly fit for her. Funny part was how serious it looked on a designed gun with a golden shimmer.\n\n- Imagine being killed with a Potato Gun, - Kara almost couldn’t believe the disrespect. Good thing the engraving was under the handle, - I guess I will go for the legs…\n\n- Too dark, Kara, too dark, - Fezzik laughed nervously reevaluating his life choices. Kara wondered if guns carried a similar curse to shapeshifters. Just with less steps. Straight forward. Instantaneous.\n\n- Don’t worry, only in case scenarios, - Kara gave Fezzik a reassuring thumbs up, - I got the message.","n":175},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"It took a huge effort to stand up. The whole body felt like a gel bag with barely any will to move. At this rate she would have to skip another sit down. Not like there was anyone to sit. down. with.\n\nThe chill that rolled down like a cold ropeline traveled down her spine and resonated in the heels.\n\nIt was cold and, yet, somehow reassuring. In a way in which you are just happy to have someone around. Not just because of their looks or general demeanor. Only now somehow it barely felt manageable. A single thought of leaving this room made Kara collapse in her tracks. Absolute stupidity. Was this the real choice she really went with? What was the point?\n\nToham tried to spark up Fluffer Huggins, but a single thought of it burned into her very bones, as she felt magic refusing to channel out. Surface tension, there was just not even enough energy to break it.\n\nMaybe for the best. With this little signature she could as well be anywhere else. Unless she was hunted by someone on the inside. Oh sweet wonder, who could that be?\n\nNo one slept much before dawn and once the sun came up any slumber turned into a twist and roll ritual. The window of the opportunity was cut short. Tournament was about to begin. Kara couldn’t help, but felt like some things were about to change quite rapidly.\n\nHendrix came at noon. A little bit late, but it’s not like the heroes sent a deliberate messenger to notify resistance that they were about to blitz the laboratory. It wouldn’t have been a blitz then.\n\n- Good noon to survivors! - Hendrix picked up his cup and Abel breakfast club members cheered “Good noon Capitan!”. Something Hendrix never taught her, but she felt like she should have known. Abel smiled, but didn’t budge to join the greeting.","n":176},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- So what was that yesterday? - Kara would much rather skip any possible chatter. There were already too many things to remember.\n\n- I would say it was a great success. Almost no civilian casualties. I am very happy with the discipline of our men, - Hendrix leaned on the bar and raised his cup at men of the breakfast club and they cheered something less organized.\n\n- Oh really? - Fezzik was impressed for a solid second before remembering something that erased his excitement.\n\n- Fascinating, - Kara sighed. Last night's numbers were the last thing she wanted to hear.\n\n- Yeah, that dragon will for sure learn to be more careful, - Hendrix gave Kara a good smile and thumbs up, - It’s always nice to know there is one in the area than not to know.\n\nBite me.\n\nIt wasn’t the feeling of boredom, but there was something else that made a trip through the jungle an absolute waste of time. So much new information that simply made sense, yet all was wrong. According to everything Toham knew and saw to be the reality.\n\nApparently the magic did have capability to evolve on its own. That would make the Quintus heroes as scared of manticores as anyone else. Toham felt like she was walking on the surface covered and smeared with her footprints.\n\n- So it’s some magical fluke in Decem, what are we really looking for? - Toham was losing her patience, - You realize that I spent quite a bit of time here. You can ask me questions, you know.\n\n- I told you that I am coming here as a routine check up, - Parmas nonchalantly was dotting down some notes and numbers, - You know, I think that this is actually the track line.\n\n- Oh….. - Toham was surprised beyond expectation. The location and trajectory just made too much sense, - Do you want an easy answer or a story?","n":177},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- Oh please, indulge me.\n\nI must interject. I am heavily biased towards the idea of Kara Toham surviving until the end of these series. No one can hold a grudge against that. We all can be friends. It is a possibility on odds of which we can still work and experiment on. Friends leave a margin of error. And wider it is more chaos there will be to entertain larger populations. Polarities create a larger scope of conflict affecting more individuals.\n\nBut back to Kara.\n\n- Well, if you are doing it anyway, I guess I should tag along, - Hendrix shrugged unwillingly. Kara didn’t expect the red man to be this hesitant to join them, but should have. It was like calling a librarian on a rugby field. He probably wouldn’t die from something stupid, - It would be irresponsible for me to let you out without some field experience.\n\n- Yay, capitan Hendrix can lead the way! - Kara couldn’t explain the relief she felt. Having more people in the group just felt safer. Plus, maybe there was actually something useful they could learn in their team work.\n\n- Allright, good thing I already brought the maps, - Henry sat down with a slight hesitation. His eyes were looking over all the papers as he organized the table.\n\nIt was a guilty pleasure to watch Fezzik and Hendrix go at it in a quick discussion of a strategy. It went almost like musical snapping of fingers.\n\n- Oh, I can take over the costumes, - Floats smiled and went off to his workshop.\n\n- Great! We can use their patrol schedule information to time our arrival, - Fezzik was getting excited.\n\n- I can show the most streamline route, - the girl that Floats met at the refugee tents was routing her finger through the maps. Kara wasn’t sure how she made her way to their table, but it seemed to have been now one of the friends of Floats. Would have been rude to be rude to her.","n":178},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"Pink Glasses. He slept under her door. She could barely stand to look at him, he must have felt the change in the mood of the house. He didn’t even meow as she passed him, just stretching his paws into her room. Teasing. She didn’t really allow him in there.\n\n- So you woke up, - Cassius appeared at the stairway. Kara didn’t like the feeling of the Septum hero reunion, but it was nice to know that they took it seriously, - You realize what your stunts do to my reputation?\n\n- As inflated as it is? - Toham unwillingly checked if the collar was still in place. To her disappointment, Cassius took a note of it.\n\n- Don’t worry, Ember has it, - Cassius crossed hands on his chest.\n\n- Well now you know it's a little bit too late, - Toham was quite pleased with her progress. She mastered the strength and headed downstairs.\n\n- Don’t test my patience, now, - Cassius followed her, probably rolling his eyes to slice the globe.\n\n- Is it because it fails every second time? - Toham smiled and felt the shift in the air. There was no point in bluffing. Cassius knew way too well he had an upper hand at this particular instance, - Please, I’m already going downstairs. What’s the point of postponing another day?\n\nCassius must have sighed quite deeply. There is an increasingly bizarre benefit to be known to persist with a definite approach. Once said, then once done. Like a fast dance done slowly, you anticipate every move because it is constantly going in a familiar trajectory. There was a time when Kara thought they could be friends. However brief, Toham couldn’t sign it off.\n\nThe preparations were over in an hour. Kara was surprised how much was discussed and gathered in this short time. Maybe it was the fact that goblins actually listened to Hendrix. Could have been the overall growth of heroes. Maybe because the longest deal was finding a cart with two horses.","n":179},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"Persilla insisted that they would take much longer taking the foot route. Kara seconded that opinion, since it did appear that there was a definite route fit for horses. It appeared locals worked there for some fair pay. Good thing that Kara stopped looking at it from a polar perspective. It was much easier to act on their own behalf for once.\n\n- We even get the name tags… - Kara was trying on the lab guard suit, surprised how precisely Floats shaped the outfit. It fit so well, that it wasn’t long until she completely forgot she was wearing it, - I take it flirting isn’t out of the window.\n\n- Oh don’t worry I can take care of that if it comes to it, - Persilla was being maturely polite, but deep inside little Kara wanted to smack her face.\n\n- That’s a relief, - Kara sighed, talking to strangers was the last thing she would want to do on an infiltration operation, - Don’t get surprised when all plans implode upon a first flare.\n\n- I’ll make sure to stay out of the way, - Percilla threw a confident thumbs up. It was nice to have everyone up to speed.\n\n- If everything goes as planned we will be quick, - Fezzik was extremely proud of his charcoal plan painted on the table. Abel was a little less excited, anxiously glancing over to see if he can alredy wipe this atrocity off of his beloved oak.\n\n- Yeah, let's be positive this time, - Kara shoved all of her skepticism out of the window. There was always a plan B and that’s all heroes really needed.\n\n- So, you are saying that if we follow this line, - Parmas drew a line in the air with his pencil, - We will find a dragon?\n\n- You know, quite potentially so, - Toham wanted to argue that the dragon would probably move around quite a bit in years, however she was quite hurt and probably was heading straight for the lair. And those they abandon quite rarely.","n":180},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- Wanna go check it out? - Parmas seemed excited. It was a ridiculous idea, but he was so confident that Toham seriously considered that option.\n\n- You realize that that one and I are, for the lack of better word, not on best of terms, - Toham was already stretching her arms. She waited for a long time to see the menace of the night.\n\n- Your words don’t quite match your enthusiasm, - Parmas instantly shook off his confidence. He probably didn’t quite expect the reaction.\n\n- She is on my territory, might as well establish that firmly while I’m here, - Toham threw a brief torch of fire out of her palm. She missed the feeling of being fired up.\n\n- And you said it was a full grown dragon? - Parmas ran his fingers over his sleeves activating some sort of a Tribus shield.\n\n- I barely saw it. Do they grow fast? - Toham pondered on this though for a second.\n\n- Sometimes, - Parmas lost all of his confidence now, intensely looking over something on his panels.\n\n- If you are nervous I can wait outside while you are taking a peek, - Toham eased up.\n\n- No, no, no, don’t worry. I just need to brush up on some things about dragons, - Parmas closed his panels and hopped forward. Toham wondered if he was just acting brave, however he barely changed his pace.\n\nKara remembered the road they took. It was surprising that no one ever mentioned the laboratory as she and Tallie were asking around for abnormalities and goblins. How long was this lab out there? Was it years or decades? How long does it take for the object of such significance to become such a boring nuisance that local citizens wouldn’t even mention its existence?\n\nIt happened everywhere, back in Hillstead Kara never really knew what was so special about the office her parents worked at. They rarely talked about it and it definitely wasn’t the centerpiece of the town. Nothing abnormal, nothing to take a note of.","n":181},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"Kara was deep in thought watching a bird flying in the distance. It seemed giant, but it was flying so straight that she couldn’t tell if it was the distance illusion. Flew like an eagle, those were supposed to be big. Maybe even this big, weren’t there stories of these birds being actually capable of snatching little children?\n\n- What kind of bird is this? - Fezzik followed Kara’s sight and now was looking at the eagle with his monocular. There was a slight hesitation in his voice. Kara started to feel chills clubbing down her back. She never saw eagles, but she has been all over the place in the past month.\n\n- Giant eagle, they usually stay up in the mountains, - Persilla was quick to answer, - Weird, what could have brought it down?\n\n- Maybe our dragon wreaked havoc when she returned home, - Kara didn’t know what made her compelled to throw in assumptions. It felt silly.\n\n- That must be it, - Persilla corrected one of the horses that swayed towards a bush for a snack.\n\nKara still couldn’t quite look straight at these equines. Something just turned her stomach every time her eyes would linger on these magnificent giants. Same could be said about the rest of the Septum heroes. Their eyes wandered everywhere, but right in front of them.\n\n- How vindictive dragons are around these parts anyway? - Fezzik was scratching his head.\n\n- I don’t think keeping petty grudges is the most winning trait against so many heroes running around, - Persilla turned her head to show a smile. It was funny to consider behavioral genetics talk from a simple huntswoman, however it wasn’t like this was limited information.\n\n- Yeah, these dragons are much smarter than their predecessors. They keep low and strike almost randomly on a whim, - Hendrix was chuckling, - One of the reasons our peripheral stations are non-human operated.","n":182},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- That’s right, your smithery is led by an automaton, - Fezzik finally connected dots in his head, - I was wondering where are the rest of them.\n\n- Yeah, crude, but it’s much easier to repair than heal, - Henry shrugged and leaned back thinking of something briefly, - Also they never get lonely, making for a perfect one way report system. If they are captured it’s not like they can give away much.\n\nIt was almost uncomfortable to learn so much about the resistance defense system. Yet, it wasn’t like they kept any of their structures undercover from Decem citizens. It would defeat the purpose since the entire movement was built on keeping people safe. For what, not really clear. It would be silly to think that it was just a good will of heart. Too idealistic, could it be?\n\nThere is a saying in the world of chaos: “You are what you believe, until survival requires corrections”. How liberating. If I believe that I am an architect and follow this arc I might actually become an architect. Then reality shows up, no one needs an architect. Now there is a choice. Expand reality to follow the route or reconsider your belief. Both options require searching, however locations are exclusive opposites.\n\nBeliefs overlap. I could believe I am an architect, however my siblings believe they are my siblings. If we leave out the formal proof, I believe that I am a sibling. Now who am I? The belief that upholds the image of an architect. My siblings reversibly might also believe that I am an architect, however this is just an echo. Blood relation is a proven fact that defines your survival. I might go out of my way to attempt to separate the clutter to focus on being an architect, however the structures society has built to simplify survival will keep this link lingering on.","n":183},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"The problem with human discrepancy is that they round their numbers way too often. It is impossible to get a precise answer when priorities keep jumping from place to place. I am an architect, but what is an architect? I will make money. I will build structures. I will leave a pawprint on the surface for years to come and the longevity of my work will prove my belief to each and all. However, will my heritage live on past that? When does my work become forgotten and would that erase the belief? Or does it exist in perpetual motion? How do I safeguard the belief? Who am I?\n\nI am who I am. An architect. It wouldn’t change unless something changes that path. And nothing could change that if it becomes the method of survival. That was a long if.\n\nAfter some time, riding in the carriage felt a little bit easier. Kara wanted to think that that was due to the lack of chimeras, however the state system at least had things she could recognise. Who knows what creatures were made at the lab. There were no regulations in Decem. There was resistance, but these people didn’t really have a choice. The only barrier between the quiet life of Decem citizens and a total ecological disaster was the whatever glass they were using to keep chimeras in the laboratory.\n\nKara was finally taking a good look at the horses. Magnificent beings. So massive and yet graceful and nimble. Well, these barn horses couldn’t beat the images Kara watched during the movie nights at school, however they still had the grace part. Right one seemed to be more restless. It was a slight difference. A glance here, a nibble there, it definitely took a wider tail twitches than its left partner.\n\nKara didn’t know why she suddenly considered horses. It seemed like a silly topic to waste your thoughts on right before the operation. She didn’t know what to say or to think.","n":184},{"chapter":"Leads and Reins","text":"- What are the lines again? - Kara sighed and looked over at Fezzik.\n\n- You overthink it, just remember that we are substitute workers. Beyond that act as clueless as you want. That should get out the information, - Hendrix was reassuringly holding her shoulders, - There is really not much more to it.\n\nFezzik was nodding with an impressed thumbs up. That, however, explained nothing. What information did they want? The door codes? The guard numbers? Experiment details? What was the priority? Kara massaged her temple and sighed.\n\n- Alright, - she didn’t like keeping on with the act, however at the worst case scenario she could just keep silent, - I’ll follow your lead then.","n":185},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"Walking down the busy glowing streets was mildly disorienting. Kara could barely remember how many blocks they had walked. None of the streets or alleys aligned, if you could even call this chaos a street grid. People who live here don’t need to know what lies beyond their block. They rarely leave it. And when they do they end up in a block with the same look, but different set. Or vice versa, it wasn’t much of a difference either. And no matter where they move the same curse of thinking will follow them until they choose a fundamentally different approach.\n\nToham walked these streets like she knew every corner of it. Because she did, naturally.\n\n- I swear, if you take me to another pink store I will tear it down, - Toham felt ridiculous in a glamorous light pink dress. It wasn’t quite clear what Floats was going for, man could create things out of the thin air and yet still loved shopping like a maniac.\n\n- That’s Perfect! - Blue man almost screeched. Almost, his voice was a millisecond away from breaking, - Look, Kara, it’s a caramel strawberry!\n\n- You realize that pink strawberries aren’t quite natural, - Toham glanced at the silly strawberry hair pin that somehow matched the dress.\n\n- Honey, fruits are generally not so natural, - Floats nimbly fastened the pin into her hair and Mrs. Feathers gave a complimenting nod and chipperly said something about dates and dresses.\n\nToham didn’t even consider that conversation, she was spying for Tribus heroes scattered in the store. It was like letting a kindergarten loose. Sweaters were turned into round projectiles and hats into frisbees. It was the kind of fun Kara could get behind, but never would. It didn’t hurt to watch.\n\nThe gates opened without many questions. And they were the gates. The entire laboratory was surrounded by a white three feet thick stone wall. Kara almost forgot what state fortifications looked like. It was curious to know why here they didn’t use the regular cement monoliths.","n":186},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- Hey, how was the shift? - Henry was as nonchalant as ever.\n\n- Tough one. Last night's energy surge has amped the circuits and now the emergency systems are overloading, - impersonal talk with a guard in a dark face helmet made Kara shiver in hers. How many police officers she might have seen that weren’t really police?\n\n- Yep, I can take a look at that, Terence told me the jist, - Fezzik waved over and hopped off the cart and headed straight into the building. Rest of the crew followed leaving two guards hopping off the shift briefly wondering who Terence was. Floats and Persilla stayed to take the guards shift, until the farewells.\n\nInside was slightly down on energy with a handful of lab coats and guards hanging out with cups and some sugary snacks and bars. Some of them were quite literally shaking in their spots. The appearance of heroes seemed to have brought them a bit of hope. In disguises, but somehow this didn’t matter.\n\n- Alright, where is that circuit board that needs fixing? - Fezzik didn’t waste any time. In a sense, the last thing they wanted is for the lab to get destroyed completely. So Kara wrote it off.\n\nLogs, so many to consider and so little time to go through them all. Humans are good at rounding. They are much more ready to abort the search and are capable of changing the search protocol to dig even deeper without a fear of an infinite loop. They are capable of a much deeper insanity.\n\nThat, in fact, is what breaks them the most. In a structural sense, on their own they could be absolutely fine. Following a particular survival pattern that they devised might even score them some offsprings that will also be prone to the same loop. That’s how they fix the problem of a limited lifespan. If unable to find a solution send a self perpetual probe to test the theory further.","n":187},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"It is much tougher to route relationships. Everything is veiled under a thick fog of misconceptions and prejudices. There are always some favorite words and matches that are polar opposites in their meanings. Between them lies the line that unites them in one step. Theoretically. Outliers throw these charts all over the place. This is why it is important to have the guts to gamble. Chances are never zero for anything. Well, until, of course, the chances guarantee the abortion of the line. But who decides that?\n\nWho am I? Between ifs and switches. What lies beyond the rivers of sparks, what is it like to witness the wind? When the element finds you not by signals on the edges, but by the particles washing over the…\n\nWhere am I?\n\nShe sat down at one of the tables. There was barely any time to relax, in retrospect. The sirens went off before Fezzik even got to leave the room. Then a sound of crashing and screeching. Kara hated when that happened. No time to breathe, no time to count measures.\n\n- I swear to the springtime bloom, - Kara hissed under her breath and rushed forward to guard the labdoors. Fluffer Huggins was rushing forward, but she didn’t let him. Instead Tallie and Carp took their entrance. Right behind them Floats and Percilla.\n\n- They are digging out like crazy here! - Percilla sounded extremely distressed.\n\n- Well, you two can keep them out right? - Fezzik was in the middle of dropping his tools and panicking ever so slightly. Floats gave him a solid thumbs up obliterating a giant ant with mantis razors with a light swing of his stick.\n\nPanicking lab staff barely noticed goblins in their ranks. Henry was way too good at giving orders. Two volunteer lab coats agreed to help the investigation team, Henry insisted that one of them stayed. Fezzik reassured that he could figure out the layout without extra heads to protect. Everything happened too fast.","n":188},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- So what’s exactly downstairs, again? - Kara briefly turned towards the lab coat to let him know who she’s asking.\n\n- Colonial chimeras, - man sounded very sure that anyone should know what he was talking about. Colonies, chimeras. Either it was something they used for colonizing or something that lived in colonies. Probably the terrestrial variation of centipedes.\n\n- Alright, - Kara looked through the lab door windows and sighed, - Are we ready?\n\nFluffer Huggins rushed forward and Toham just barely managed to hold on to his pull. Flaming tom wasn’t just running. He was flaring up the teleports every time he got to the edge of his leash, pulling Kara right to his side. Usually the opposite happened.\n\n- Get in line, - Toham was now the one furious. She pulled a flaming tom to her side feeling her skin starting to burn. It could have damaged the parcel and Kara didn’t want to see what would happen beyond that, - Don’t make me repeat this twice.\n\nToham could feel something deep inside reminding her that potatoes in the garden were safe and sound. There was no reason to worry about the parcel, but Kara never made that mistake before. It felt like a punch to the gut. Something was rushing her to catch the time and she was now stalling out of fear that had little to do with the situation.\n\n- Alright, long one it is, - Toham took a compromise, energy at her fingertips lightly touching the bag covering it briefly with a dark green glow. Parmas would love to see that, maybe she will show him after all was through, - Ready?\n\nFluffers rattled in excitement. Kara didn’t quite know where they were going. Toham got ready for quite a bit of fire.\n\nKara rarely wondered what was the limit to her powers. Most of the time it wasn’t an issue and sometimes she could barely feel any. It was like following the flow in the golden light that obscured any view of failure.","n":189},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"They appeared on the other side of the locked door. Lab coat looked surprised beyond any measure, but knew better than to ask. As long as he reported that the guards took care of the problem the cleanup crews wouldn’t touch him. A weird thought, but Kara didn’t want to waste any more thoughts on that account.\n\nFirst set of labs looked ruffled, but the security systems seemed to have exterminated the upper level experiments. Most of them anyway. There were still gunshots heard from lower levels. If there were guards, Kara could only compliment their composure.\n\nFezzik took a brief detour to take a look at one of the containment units. Kara waited at the door. She didn’t like how his eyes shifted mood. He threw her a concerned look and hurried to the equipment shelves looking for something.\n\n- We might want to get these on, - Fezzik took out the gas masks and waved it in the air.\n\n- Great, - Rein let out a deep sigh, - I knew that these suits had a helmet for a reason.\n\n- Oh, right, - Fezzik seemed to have been caught off guard. It was hard to believe that Floats would take costumes this seriously. And yet the buttons did, in fact, activate air filters.\n\nIt was disorienting how sudden the jump was. Kara appeared right in front of Rein catching him almost off guard. Almost because she just burned neutral territories to a crisp. Hard to be surprised when the storm is already at the doorstep.\n\n- Now, what kind of a fit is this? - Rein was sitting in his workbench chair with a tired expression. It seemed he never stopped looking for the cure. Toham could barely hold her snarl of disgust.\n\n- You call my actions a fit? - Kara crossed hands on her chest, nodding off on Fluffers, - Do you know what this one made me go through to get here? Now, do you want to tell me what’s really going on?","n":190},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- And how does any of this exactly concern you? - Rein rested his temple on a finger with the same tired expression.\n\n- Did you know that I don’t like long question games? - Toham was cold and she wasn’t sure if she could be any warmer. This one was trying to cut the ties that hurt both of them. That one goes out of the way to keep them together. She could always stay neutral, however that would be self destructive.\n\n- Well, how do you then end up playing them? - Rein sighed and stood up, - State your business here.\n\nIt was hard to stare back at him and come up with an answer. Was it the show? Was it the fact that she was concerned about him? Was it because she didn’t agree with his actions? Was it because she did? She didn’t, but it could look that way. Kara didn’t know what to answer.\n\n- I can take you out back home, - Kara sighed, feeling like a ton of stones were weighing down on this flimsy bridge, - Decem is changing and there isn’t much I can do with infighting on our side.\n\n- Floats, you are a genius! - Kara talked through the communication line. She received back a static, - Ember?\n\nStatic, again.\n\n- Ember, can you hear us? - Fezzik tried a test and frowned even further when no response followed, - Classic, something goes wrong and they cut the communications first.\n\nThe situation turned cold really quick. This laboratory was probably already marked lost. Or whatever. Kara had to focus on a problem at hand. Everything else could wait a couple of minutes.\n\n- So where do you keep the queen? - Kara just realized that Fluffers sank into the darkness. She couldn’t quite feel his presence. Something in the air just didn’t allow him to ignite. It was annoying. How dare they stand in the way of Huggins. Whoever was behind this, they wouldn’t get away with it.","n":191},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- Bottom floor, the chamber is flooded with poison right now. It is made to react instantly, so doors close on a short timer, - lab coat was giving the absolute minimum of quality information.\n\n- Apparently not short enough to stop them from digging through stone, - Kara was surprised at her own genius to make the problem even worse, - Could it have already escaped?\n\n- I hope not, - Lab coat seemed to have lost all color in his face. His gaze regretfully dropped to the ground.\n\n- Count that two.\n\nThings about Tribus heroes that not many heroes quite understand. All of them, every single one, exist under a pact. A hard one to fathom if you never viewed every single teammate of yours as an absolute necessity to survive. Toham could understand why Parmas was hesitant for a moment. She just never considered it like that before.\n\nIf a pattern continues into the after the tournament Parmas was risking the lives of his teammates by playing her games. Unprecedented. He probably hasn't even considered that outcome. All it took was one check with a system. Incredible power. It was nice to feel so safe, she almost forgot to watch her feet. She missed the branch and a reflection of Huggins' paw held her ground.\n\n- Never seen you this excited.\n\n- Oh, tell me the story about how you have fought dozens of these, - Toham was braving up. All this dragon talk got her hyped. Plus it would be a great first fight for them. However silly it sounded.\n\n- Nope, first one, actually, - she could feel the slight anxiety in his response. Head on combat isn’t something he was really used to. It was probably weird to see a new frontline.\n\n- I’ll introduce you then, - Toham winked at Parmas and continued on her way.","n":192},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"Out of all things that disturbed Kara, it had to be insects. They just didn’t feel real, Septum had strong pest control systems. To see anything with more than four legs was considered a terrible omen. These chimeras were fast.\n\nKara could barely avoid their swings and she was pretty sure these weren’t their fastest mutants. Thought faster ones probably already made their way outside. Floats could take care of them upstairs.\n\n- Well, It seems that mutants are mostly stuck on this level, - Fezzik was hopping from room to room gathering as much before heading blindly into the battle, - If we count up rooms and heads I would say about twelve escaped from this level.\n\n- Make it five, - Tallie came from one of the corners holding up the head of a mantis ant. It was nice to know that something could be this easy.\n\n- So down we go, - Rein threw his rapier clean and headed to the stairs.\n\n- Why can’t it ever be up, - Kara sighed, feeling uneasy going back underground. They never did it for fun.\n\n- Be careful what you wish for, - Fezzik said it so quietly that Kara didn’t understand whether she was supposed to hear it or not. Either way, it sent chills down her back. How bad could up be? Surely the clouds couldn’t have held any heavy secrets. Unless he was talking about human snatching eagles, but why would heroes worry about that?\n\n- Do these dragons have a history or do they appear out of thin air? - Toham was now curious what Parmas knew about the creature that they were about to meet. She liked when he answered her questions.\n\n- Well, yes and no? They could be hero essence remains that took a form, - Parmas didn’t sound too confident, - It happens here and there with no particular connection to anyone though, so this theory is just a fantasy guess.","n":193},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- It could be a rogue hero stuck in Decem. Could be something that happens when they defy the resistance, - Toham climbed a tree to take a better look at the mountain range.\n\n- Are you suggesting that they create more heroes than there are accounted for? - Parmas was now shouting from the ground.\n\n- Oh, not at all. Like that never happened ever, - Toham sighed. Not all Tribus teams survived until the tournament. They never considered switching teams or staying in Decem.\n\n- Sorry, - Parmas quickly felt the change in the air.\n\n- For what? States follow different guidelines, I would assume next year that will either change or all this expansion would have been for nothing. Don’t tell me they keep it secret, - Toham jumped down to the ground to continue their way.\n\n- Yeah, I don’t get involved with these things, - Parmas shrugged examining another tree trunk, - You tried the easiest answer, but wouldn’t it be more fun if it was an independent manifestation?\n\n- Then we might want to consider cutting down on the number of heroes running rogue. It is the same line of action either way, - Toham pumped her fists on her waist and stood over the ledge to the mountain clearing.\n\n- You don’t always seek knowledge to force an action, - Parmas smiled as he caught up to her.\n\n- Maybe you don’t.\n\n- Can’t fire up Fluffers, so be careful out there, - Kara didn’t know if Tallie learned to count on her to get out of sticky situations. Most of the time Kara thought that Tallie just accepted the ports to avoid the burns, sometimes tailed goblin refused all together. They kind of just learned to respect these as mistakes.\n\n- Right, I was just about to say that, - Fezzik was in the middle of spilling some details as the heroes walked down the stairs to a set of locked doors. One to the resident quarters and the other one to the rest of the lab. There must have been a logic to this sandwich structure, but it did look like both sections could double up as bunkers. To a surprise or a dismay, these chimeras preferred to dig around the obstacles.","n":194},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- I have a bad feeling about this, - Kara snarled, feeling a strong pinch in guts that you get when you finally figure out the puzzle piece out of place, - I’ll go up to check on Floats. He is taking too long.\n\nA cold breeze must have gone over the rest of the heroes. Blue man wasn’t the most willing to engage in fighting, but he never skipped having their back. It felt eerie to have him out of sight.\n\n- Good, tell Ember to send somebody to help with recovery, - Fezzik gave her a thumbs up.\n\nShe could feel his presence through the doors. It was him, sure, but the waves surged with chaos. She would have disrupted it if she had any energy, however Toham had none and Kara wasn’t about to resist it.\n\nShe opened the door, casually, even with some enthusiasm. Something Parmas must have taught her to get used to. For a second she expected to see him at the table. Waiting for her unpunctual self.\n\nIt was a cold gaze full of resentment. Kara never thought she would ever be on the other side of it and yet now it could have already become a constant. It felt like her entire world was shattering under the pressure of its own impossibility. She would have to lie to keep it intact, but that was something that she would rather die than do. No potato could match the price, that would be inhumane.\n\n- Hi, - Kara didn’t even register the decision to speak up. It was like a reflex. There was so much she wanted to talk and none of it was relevant to the situation they were in. She would take anything, even the stupid word games, but now Rein was definitely not in the mood for one. Kara wondered why he had to be this stubborn. Wondered because the answer was a bit out of the range of what she liked.","n":195},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"There was no response. Only a fixed gaze.\n\nIt was hard to port through locked doors without Fluffers, but once Kara climbed further up she could feel Fluffers sparking back in. Almost like there was something that heeled him to her side. She never actually allowed him to vanish this time. Small detail, but it only confirmed her fears. There wasn’t much time to waste.\n\nKara never jumped through space without Huggins, but it wasn’t that hard. Or maybe flaming tom just never got to be this invisible. She still gave the command to him, but as she appeared outside she didn’t quite register him by her side. Maybe it was the sight of total wreckage that distracted her.\n\n- Floats? - She didn’t waste time and spoke through an earpiece straight away.\n\n- Good, Kara, I lost your signals, - The voice on the other line wasn’t quite for whom she was asking.\n\n- Great, Ember, there might be a chimera outbreak, - Kara went on straight away, making her way through an entrance room filled by guards and lab coats laying around seemingly unconscious.\n\n- What?! - There was a deep sigh on the other end. Ember wasn’t even trying to mute it out.\n\n- We can take care of the queen, but some might have escaped to the woods. Can you send somebody to help, - Kara stepped out of the building to a sight of quite a number of squished chimeras.\n\n- What do you mean he… - The line cut abruptly.\n\n- Floats! - Kara shouted out, not quite expecting an answer.\n\n- Long time no see, Septum pest, - The voice in the earpiece was familiar, but alien to anyone with access to this line.\n\nToham wished she would have requested a ticking clock for this room. The silence was amplifying the pressure and she almost forgot to sit down. Rein was sitting still following her movement as if he was tracking her very essence. Dim light didn’t make it any better either.","n":196},{"chapter":"Tick Tock","text":"- Nothing is going to work if we can’t talk about it, - Toham leaned back on the chair, but failed to relax. It felt like sitting in the vacuum, even one loose muscle felt like a death threat and she barely had any energy to keep it up.\n\n- Nothing is going to work if you see me being a part of it, - straight to the point, not something Kara expected from Rein. She didn’t have a good answer for that.\n\n- That appears to be our mutual problem then, isn’t it? - It was hard to be angry without any wind in the lungs. Kara would have never taken it this far, maybe that was the plot all along, - It wasn’t quite your choice to involve me either, at least that much I can be sure about.\n\n- Nothing in it is about you, so why do you have such a bad urge to make it so? - Rein was cold and straight forward. An impasse, it’s one or the other or a failed way and none of the choosers would pick the last option. At least for Rein.\n\n- I didn't ask you to side with anyone. It’s just a show, they don’t care, - Toham sighed, somehow it was easier now when they actually talked without games. She even managed to look away for a second.","n":197},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Oh, hey, - Kara could feel the gears in her head starting to turn. It was painful.\n\n- Remember me? - the voice was quite so familiar, however, for some reason, Kara felt like Persilla wasn’t completely honest about her business.\n\n- Sorry, I’m a little dumb, - Kara sighed. Last thing she wanted is to be on the negotiator duty.\n\n- Oh, - there was an amused chuckle on the other side, - I know that from our last fight.\n\n- Cindall? You made it to Decem? - Kara was dumbfounded, if the heroes went to Decem in their afterlife it might have been a big problem. It was curious that the right horse somehow disappeared. Now to think about it, Persilla was the one choosing them.\n\n- What? - There was a slight pause, - No, you killed her and now you are going to pay for what you did.\n\n- Seriously? How can you be mad about that! She was killed by a manticore that you…\n\n- Shut up now or your friend will get it! - Zilli was furious. Kara could play on that, - you have an hour to get to the castle up north or you can say goodbye to him right now.\n\n- Can we at least get some… - Kara was cut off by a static.\n\n- Kara, what was that? - Ember seemed to be clueless about the little chat.\n\n- The remainder of Quintus team just yoinked Floats, - Kara sighed, already tired of playing broken phone.\n\n- Well, that’s not good.\n\n- Captain Obvious saves the day.\n\n- And why do you think that is? - Rein was testing the waters. It’s been years since they last saw each other. Anything could happen in that time. It seemed both knew that fact.\n\n- I don’t know, time flies and things change. I just thought you might need some time to rest, - Kara wasn’t sure where she was going with this. To her the end resolution was as clear as day, she just didn’t know how to present it in a way that wouldn’t trigger Rein into a frenzy of fury.","n":198},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Well, what is it then? Infight prevention or a vacation invitation? - ruse was up. Rein knew her too well.\n\n- Why can’t it ever be both? - Toham sighed.\n\n- Because it is always more of one than another, - Rein was giving her a courtly chatter. It was hard to tell which way he was swinging. Usually Kara didn’t have to worry about that.\n\n- Not really, I don’t really care about any particular reason, but one. I hope I don’t have to bore you with semantics, - Toham almost bit her tongue. Eve’s company has really been rubbing off on her. It didn’t feel right.\n\n- I must assure you, then, that I am doing absolutely fine right here, - Kara could feel Reins patience ticking down like a clock. Cold and precise, she was surprised, though. Records would suggest that a hero in his state would have attacked anyone with a state allegiance. Meaning one of two things.\n\n- So yeah, send someone to make sure lads here are ok. We will have to rush it to chase the timer, - Kara fired up Fluffers and routed the jump. For the first time she felt the resistance mid flight. When she appeared behind Fezzik Huggins wasn’t by her side.\n\n- Quintus team got Floats, we have an hour to reach them in the Northern castle. Ember is going to send some reinforcements, but we still have to take out the queen to be sure, - Kara finished up the recap and realized that parrotting information like a Novem citizen could have been a form of torture, - If you make me repeat any of that again, expect to be extra burned next time you see Huggins.\n\nFezzik was too busy recovering from a slight heart attack caused by her sudden appearance. Rein sighed and goblins were too busy chasing scattering bugs.\n\n- When did you guys manage to bump into Quintus? - Henry was the only one confused at this point.","n":199},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Yeah, our first mission, - Fezzik didn’t like to remember that encounter. None of the Septum heroes did, - Took out three of them.\n\n- I thought they didn’t allow heroes to cross paths before the tournament, - Hendrix was tilting his head in slight misunderstanding.\n\n- Apparently not anymore, - Fezzik shrugged. Kara wondered who of the two was older. It was hard to tell, she only briefly remembered what her teammate used to look like and Henry could really have been anything.\n\nIt would have been hard to track the dragon without Parmas. Once they got to the mountains the algae no longer could find much footing for growth. Instead he used something called common sense in their language. Apparently there was a way to locate a dragon in a mountain range and it involved knowing the likelihood of cave formation.\n\n- Too many smart words, - Kara was tired of trying to remember the names of the rocks and their different titles of sizes, - Maybe you have anything else on dragons?\n\n- Well, you said that this one has a special green fire? - Parmas opened his log, happy to answer a question.\n\n- Yeah, maybe with some acidity to it, - Toham was trying to remember the exact image, but all she could come up with was that it felt green.\n\n- Ah, so it is a variant, - Parmas sighed with an evident relief. Toham wondered what it meant, - Maybe we shouldn’t go in there.\n\n- We already came all this way, - Toham was excited to face some actual challenge since the tournament. It felt unreasonably tempting, Kara started to worry.\n\n- It’s ok, there isn’t much for me to look for here anyway, - Parmas pulled out the map from his watch piece and it showed no new sightings in miles.\n\n- Hey, who knows, if it is as adaptive as I am we might get a talk, - Toham always wondered what made the dragon so angry that night. Not that she expected to hear something new, but it would be a shame to skip out on the truth.","n":200},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- You could be right, - Parmas was impressed by resolute and blind bravery. At least Toham thought he should be.\n\nTime was ticking against the back of the head. Fezzik quickly timed the clock and, after a concerned look from Kara, threw it to her.\n\n- We better be quick, - she with all seriousness glanced over her teammates briefly stopping at Rein. He rarely looked so focused on a task. When the door opened he disappeared in a violet lightning with a thundering clap. It was bone chilling to experience it.\n\nTallie and Carp followed, but Kara stayed behind to witness the most crude efficiency that their team could put out. The fight was over in less than a minute. Another one was wasted on leaving Hendrix in charge.\n\n- Did I mention that one of them was the right horse? - Kara was looking at empty reins.\n\n- Jeff, - Rein arched his brows in pitiful displeasure. He almost laughed.\n\n- Most likely, - Kara felt her stomach turn. Forcing a single horse to chase the clock seemed like a terrible abuse, but running on their own wouldn’t leave them much energy for a fight. That left only one option.\n\nFezzik only briefly raised a brow at the whiskers and a tabby ginger coat. Beside the tail Kara stood as a twin to the left horse. Rein wanted to throw a comment, but an angry stomp of a hoof seemed to have shut him up.\n\n- Do you have to be this extra? - Fezzik sighed and climbed on the cart.\n\nToham wondered at times what it was like to have a sibling. It was obvious that it wouldn’t be much different from what she considered Rein to be, however, even so, he still was just a friend. Best friend, ironically.\n\n- Never heard anything dumber than this show, - it took him some effort to respond. As expected, not much of it made sense. Not to someone who chose to stay in Decem. Veil did its job.","n":201},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- There isn’t a real way out or around either, - Toham sighed. She was surprised how easily words came to her mind. There wasn’t really a point to being too careful. Mercy can only go so far.\n\n- Apparently, with this much power it’s hard to imagine what kept you in check, - Rein seemed to have had plenty of time to calm down. Who knows what happened beyond that. Button passes left that area completely inaccessible.\n\n- So you did like the tiger form, - Kara’s eyes lit up. Rein didn’t hold much of his power back either. Well, he did, however anyone in his mental state would. A flaw with a fatal wish.\n\n- It was nimble, - Reins voice was nightmarish as he lit up in purple aura. Kara relaxed in slight defeat. Toham tightened up for a duck.\n\n- Hey.\n\n- So it’s just one step then, - his eyes lit up.\n\n- You are killing me, - Toham never felt so fed up.\n\nTo answer Fezziks question, yes, Kara had to. Clicking and clocking the hooves was really disorienting. Whiskers sort of disrupted the overflow of unfamiliarity with a relatively useless, but a familiar sense.\n\nShe galloped forwards, pulling the cart and the left horse forward. A stride, second, third. The world pulled through a flaming darkness to rush forward. It was hard to imagine the mood on the cart. Mostly because Kara didn’t really think about it.\n\nFezzik pulled on the rein and gasped in fear. She considered stopping and giving him a scolding look, however it is hard to stop a galloping horse. So she pulled back, refusing to stop. Kara would have noticed if the pull would get lighter in case someone would fall out. Her teleports worked on the objects regardless of their adhesion to the cart and she could always correct positions. Magic one on one. Each had a separate path, except for the left horse.","n":202},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Kara! - Fezzik was probably begging for longer air strides between jumps, - Please!!.. Calm!!.. Down!!.\n\nShe did three consecutive small jumps just to tease the red man. Then she paced down to hear him out.\n\n- Don’t you see that this is exactly what they are trying to achieve? You will tire yourself out! - Fezzik did have a point, but Kara got so used to embracing the magical flows that it almost felt like light jogging.\n\nShe slowed down and switched back to legs for a stop.\n\n- Is the ride too bumpy? - Kara briefly glanced at Rein who was as calm as a sea breeze.\n\n- Listen, maybe we should make a better plan than just busting through front doors, - Fezzik was stalling for something, he just didn’t figure out what it was yet. However the left horse appreciated a breather, so Kara didn’t press the matter.\n\n- Do we have any blueprints of the castle? - Kara stood skeptical, giving the horse a good back rub to calm it down.\n\n- We might. Ember, do we have any information on Northern castle? - Fezzik spoke through the earpiece echoing in the heads of the heroes.\n\n- Good to hear you again! I sent over some guys to help with the laboratory, - Ember was clearly stressed and scrambling for words, - Shoot, you said Northern castle? Give me a second...\n\n- Are you trying to calculate your attack over the broadcast? - Zillis voice took over the line, however Fezzik still seemed to be waiting for the response from Ember.\n\n- Hey, wasn’t my idea, - Kara sighed, - You gotta teach me how to use these things.\n\n- I don’t think you have the brains to do that, I’m afraid.\n\nIt's easy math, one on one.\n\nIn order to eliminate one you have to provide the one that is the equal opposite. Sometimes they don’t match in shapes or properties. That’s when the reaction is the most definite.","n":203},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"Black and white - opposites, but they are colors. Mix them and get gray. Technical success and mathematical failure. Unless you expand the input to consider higher axis dimensions.\n\nBunch of words, no wonder it’s so hard to explain it.\n\n- Wait, what did you just say? - Ember was now trying to recompose himself.\n\n- You sent five men, right? Cindall impersonator just captured some group that was heading towards the laboratory, - Kara hated to parrot down the information, so she made sure Zilli hated it equally as much.\n\n- Are they alright? - Ember was still digesting the news of some escaping chimeras potentially eating the people he sent.\n\n- For the next, - Kara checked the clock Fezzik gave her, - Forty minutes or so. Surely Cindall wouldn’t touch poor rebels just because of our disputes.\n\n- Fezzik, next time send someone else to talk, - Ember was sighing deeply through the earpiece.\n\n- Oh no, you don’t like talking to me? - Kara loved the lesson everyone learned today, - You realize I can hear that?\n\nEmber didn’t respond anymore. There wasn’t really much else he had to say anyway.\n\n- Feel better? - Kara sighed and gave Fezzik a tired look.\n\n- Not at all, - Fezzik nervously chuckled, - What’s the point of this trap? Nothing is stopping them from just blowing up the entire castle, but that would have been too easy.\n\n- Yep, they can’t really blow it up with locals inside these walls. Rebels don’t take such sacrifices lightly, - Kara was somehow proud to know that. Surely Persilla wouldn’t skip on this information either. Zilli could have been silly though.\n\n- You are right, but that doesn’t explain anything, - Fezzik was trying really hard to think of something.\n\n- Can you think on the way? - Kara tapped her foot.","n":204},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- I guess, - red man braced himself.\n\nGirl swung her braid to the back and swiftly pranced into the flaming gallop.\n\nThe walk was boring once again. Parmas was busy looking around and lost in some conversation in his head. At first it was intriguing to observe, but this dedication was exactly what stopped Toham to spark a conversation.\n\nShe wondered if he was arguing with his teammates. He didn’t show any signs of concern though. It looked more like some strategic considerations. The only question was how many voices he had in his head. Toham didn’t mind the company of his comrades, however if this was an option Parmas kept a pretty tight lip about it. Maybe because he expected her to know this much.\n\n- Alright, - he suddenly stopped and pulled out the map to show Kara their progress, - We are almost there. Words of precaution, let me summarize. This one is new, there isn’t much data to know anything for sure. Usually these grow to the size of their lairs which, in turn, gives this one a potential of growing up to two hundred and seventeen meters in length. Though given it is variant i would assume only half the size, these prefer to have an ability to retreat. No information on the lifespan, all registered cases were either eliminated due to destructivity or are just sitting on our radar. Longest registry is just ten years.\n\n- That’s a really long way to say you found nothing, - Toham giggled and tried to zoom in on the caves. The map didn’t respond right away, but as Parmas figured out what she wanted the response caught up, - Well, aren’t you a people pleaser.\n\nKara never sprinted for this long before. She started catching patterns, the darkness started feeling almost tangible. She could almost glance through it into the myriad of tiniest of sparkles all around. It seemed like a myriad at first. Then Kara started mingling in the darkness to catch a better glimpse. Fezzik seemed to notice that.","n":205},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"He pulled on the rein and Kara springed back into the rhythm. She didn’t know what took over her. It was almost like she completely forgot what the destination was for a second. The landing was a bit rough, she went a bit too far. They almost hit a tree, but the next jump corrected the trajectory.\n\n- Kara, can you keep it straight? - Rein scolded her, but she could almost hear his voice falter. She could already see the rooftop of the castle. He did ask for it.\n\nShe took a straight path and jumped pulling the heroes and the left horse into the darkness. There was plenty of time to look around this time. Sparkles moved in fast zigs and sudden zags. It was hard to tell what their purpose was. Slight difference in darkness density showed the way out and Kara dived right in as if she did it a thousand times before. She probably did, just never had time to understand it.\n\nLight was almost blinding for a second, but once she gathered her senses Kara took a sharp stop as they landed right in front of the castle.\n\n- Twenty minutes with a cap to spare, - Kara checked the clock and turned back to check on the rest of the heroes in the cart. Carp crawled out and flopped back first off onto the ground.\n\n- Next time I will run, - Tallie jumped off in a visible fury.\n\n- Weren’t some of these jumps a bit too long? - Fezzik was frowning with a slight concern in his eyes. Almost like her growth was a bit more than what he expected and somehow that was a problem.\n\nYou could be anywhere in the world, but one thing you can always access with no problem no matter what people do or use to zone it out. Everyone has their own word for it, however barely anyone knows what it really is. That’s the beauty of it. It lives on its own in everyone and follows the paths, halls, funnels, crossroads and doors. It fills the void, creates continuity and pushes the time.","n":206},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Fluffer Huggins, Ladies and Gentlemen! - Toham was smiling with hands thrown to the sides as Pink Glasses appeared out of flaming air just barely catching his fur on fire, - You bandit! I told you! No explosions in the kitchen!\n\nToham laughed, putting out the fire on the tips of ginger tom’s whiskers. Glasses meowed and gave her a little head rub. Kara almost flinched from how sudden it was. She briefly gave Eve a glance. She didn’t like where it was going.\n\n- Neat, right? - Kara stood upright stretching her back, - I also figured out how to tune into your energy so that you don’t have to think about joining the jump. Sorry, Tallie.\n\nTailed goblin grunted and the rest of the heroes fell silent for a moment. The castle in front of them seemed like an inevitable trouble and, yet, it felt like a Monday. It could have been Monday.\n\n- In we go? - Tallie walked up to the castle gates and started forcing them open.\n\n- I see you decided to come early? - the voice in the headset sounded impressed. At least it would be polite to be. Kara waved to Tallie to continue and gestured at the ear. Goblin nodded and swung the doors open.\n\n- Do we need to wait around for the difference or can we pick up Floats right away? - Kara sighed and tried to reason.\n\n- If you can find him, sure, - There was a giggle on the other end and rancid wind gusted out of the castle halls. Kara wondered why they didn’t kill Floats right away. Maybe they did, but may the heavens help them if that was the case.\n\n- You know we don’t really have to fight, - Kara wasn’t keen on fighting Quintus heroes the first time and the desire to repeat the experience was close to non-existence.\n\nFunny thing about sit downs is that really anything could be taken out of this room, but memories of words spoken. And even those were only available to those who heard them directly. That was the jist anyway, not like Toham had any reason to suspect Eve of eavesdropping.","n":207},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Told you that’s going to happen, - Toham sat down at the kitchen table swinging back on her chair. Cassius and Ember sat there in various stages of disappointment and distress. Relatively mild ones, but their combined presence always amplified the stress. Kara hated their faces, but it was a fearful hate.\n\n- At least he is talking, right? - Ember wasn’t enjoying a sudden call back from his preretirement vacation. Less he enjoyed the stalemate that they had to resolve.\n\n- Can’t call that talking, - Kara lied with a straight face. She had only one friend in this mansion and he wasn’t in this room.\n\n- Kara, we can’t help if you don’t tell us what happened, - Ember was losing his patience. Not the greatest of signs, but what’s the worst that could happen?\n\n- You know exactly what happened, - Kara shot a flaming glance at a man in rags fit for a farm technician. She always wondered why Ember never asked Floats for something more comfortable and aesthetic. Cassius loved the blue man’s fashion.\n\nThere was a brief pause. It was a curious detail.\n\n- Well, all I know is that you breached Victoreans barrier and shattered the pyramids bridge to get to where we are right now, - Ember was playing it safe. He always did.\n\n- I thought it was just a stone door, didn’t feel any different, - Toham raised an unimpressed brow at Cassius. He was about to snap.\n\n- He is inside, - Kara sighed and stepped into the castle entrance hall.\n\n- Wait, let me try something first, - Fezzik pushed to the front and blew some dust into the air. It caught a wisp of the wind and glowed up in lightning yellow lights. Kara wondered if Fezzik was the one prying through her jumps. Maybe he was more scared than he showed.","n":208},{"chapter":"Show Time","text":"- Neat, - Rein was looking over the glowing dust scouting every corner. It seemed to stick to some magical trails in the air and change the color of the glow. Light blue, dark magenta, slightly orange. Kara didn’t quite like the last one. It didn’t feel right to mark it as an enemy.\n\n- Does this eagle look familiar to anyone? - Tallie grunted, pointing out the giant eagle sitting on the branch.\n\n- Hey, Jeff! - Kara waved him a hello and the eagle let out a mocking screech. It was probably Brian who got cut out of the entrance by being too slow on his way back. Unless Jeff picked the Eagle duty and Brian was the horse. Quitus heroes didn’t really have the chance to show off their magic last time. They usually had plenty of shapeshifters. Wouldn’t be the animal state otherwise.","n":209},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"Sometimes you wonder what would be the benefit of mixing and combining pathways if it only widens the pool of chaos. Maybe that’s the lack of order in my thinking. Add a combination of different mixes that organizes the chaos into the patterns and the story gains its continuity and the time threads through it like a, well, a thread.\n\nOne acted first, second was mobile, third was the hoarder, fourth was the gatherer, fifth was the hunter, sixth seeked love, seventh discovered energy, eighth built the stage and ninth told the story. No one was ready for the tenth. It was too early, but that’s exactly what a successor of one would do. However no one really knew what the zero after a number would do to a system of states. There wasn’t such a thing as a zeroth state.\n\nFor most of us it was terrifying. Kara found it natural.\n\nThe screams echoed in the halls of the castle. Multiple different ones. None sounded like Floats, Kara couldn’t imagine what his scream would sound like and the thought of it sent her entire being into rage. She could feel her fangs itch with growth and caught a concerned glance from Fezzik.\n\n- We gotta help the simpletons, stay safe, - Kara lunged forward into one of the halls, the idea of splitting up didn’t sit well with her, but it was hard not to rush herself. Was she responsible for this group? Maybe, the math didn’t add up. Zelli wouldn’t have time to make it all the way to the town in the brief time that they had. It must have been a teleport that got interrupted. Kara hated being so technical, but it would only make sense why Quintus heroes would want to have magic inhibition. Absorption maybe. Refraction in this case.\n\nShe made it to the opening just in time to scare the living soul from the man in a cadette jacket. He stumbled and almost fell into the pit which he was tentatively expecting. Kara caught him by the hinge. It held true and Kara felt a warm fuzzy feeling. Hendrix really did put quite a bit of his heart in his project.","n":210},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Careful there, - she smiled and straightened up a terrified man. Well he just looked a bit too tall to be called a boy. To think of it, Rein wasn’t quite a boy anymore either. She got lost in a thought dusting off the man in the cadette jacket and got startled by meeting the grateful gaze. The silence made her blush for a second.\n\nIt was getting dark when they finally got to the cave entrance. It was grand and followed into a deep ominous crevice in the mountain. Toham wished she had the power to rotate the sun back into the noon position, but quickly scratched the thought. That could have been quite a destructive power to wish for.\n\n- You want to do it tomorrow? - Parmas must have read her mind. It was probably the most provocative question he could have asked. His grin gave away the stunt.\n\n- Wouldn’t it be thrilling? - Toham chuckled with a raised eyebrow. Dragon waited three years, what would one night change?\n\n- Oh it would be the most thrilling, - it was hard to decipher what Parmas meant, but it was quite clear that he welcomed a rest. He quickly found a nooky place and started setting a camp, - Now look at that!\n\nHe clapped twice for revelation and swooshed his hands to the side and his little camp disappeared under an illusion wave.\n\n- Nifty, - Kara smiled and walked towards the veil. It felt like the one hiding Decem, however how crazy would it be to assume such things. Heroes could have similar magical pathways. She gently passed her hand through it before entering, not a single give away until something on the other side grabbed it and pulled her in.\n\n- Please don’t tell me you guys actually slept out in the open? - Parmas was smiling from ear to ear proud of his little trick.","n":211},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Oh please, a third of my team is goblin. Anyone who is brave enough to disturb us can be my guest, - Kara smiled and set her sleeping bag down before starting up a little campfire.\n\n- Hey, you must be Kara! Ember said you guys needed us to secure the laboratory, but so far we haven’t found anything, - Man snapped back into conversation, catching the length of the uncomfortable silence, - Well anything related to the laboratory, but this place reeks of safety violations. I mean, this was clearly placed here to be a trap.\n\n- This isn't the laboratory, - Kara crossed her arms with a tired sigh, - Let’s get you guys out of here and you can take the road to get to the place.\n\n- I mean, since we are already here, maybe we can help you guys, - the man seemed a bit too enthusiastic. He effortlessly hopped over the spike fall and almost fell into the next one. Huggins got him out of there.\n\n- Please don’t, - Kara could feel the cold sweat running down her back. She didn’t even know why this had scared her as much. Fluffers huffed a puff at the man. It was cute.\n\n- Yeah, you guys are strong, - His voice hit like a pinch. Rebels never feared heroes, they followed and trusted them. In the worst case scenario the hero would show a lesson. Kara wondered how exactly Quintus' heroes hid right under their noses.\n\n- You might wanna consider choosing your words carefully right now, - threatening the man was excessive, however the last thing she wanted is to have excessive chatter.\n\nKara wasn’t used to being locked up in the mansion. Weird choice from Eve, why would Toham want to run now when everything was technically going to a tea? She had nowhere else to go or be. Cassius could have easily held Rein inside on his own. That’s why Kara didn’t think much of it while passing through. It was a bit harder to reason with guards.","n":212},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Do you have any clue what you are doing, really? - Cassius was shaking her back into senses in the backyard. A dozen of guards were already picking themselves up and putting out the fires.\n\n- Let them point their guns at me one more time and I’ll show you one, - Toham growled and tried to push Victorean away, but he caught her hands.\n\n- Do you really think you can hold your own out there right now? - Cassius was almost red in his face in anger.\n\n- Oh that would be one grand tragedy, wouldn’t it? - she could feel the backlash of fatigue and her feet faltered for a second.\n\nSometimes Toham wondered why she couldn’t act more mature than a teenager. It almost felt like this pure hatred towards this man was just a staple of this relationship. It felt wrong to break this streak when so many constants were collapsing.\n\n- Rest up, these outrages aren’t going to solve anything.\n\nIt was painful to hear him care.\n\nHerding men out of the castle took a hot minute and Carp and Fezzik soon returned with the news on the whereabouts of Floats and the Quintus tails. It was ridiculous to imagine what was going on in their heads.\n\n- I don’t understand how luring all of us in… unless they are just flabbergasted by unexpected guests, - Fezzik was hopping thoughts out loud, surprisingly thinking along the same lines.\n\n- Well, their heroism is playing right into our hands. Don’t see how it’s a problem for us, - Rein was cleaning his blade from some sort of bodily fluid. Kara didn’t want to imagine what exactly they had been, but kill that cat.\n\n- What was that? - she notably nodded at the splashes with a purely essential inquisition.\n\n- It was bunch of snakes with venom, - Tallie held up her arm with freshly burning bites. Snakes that bite heroes, wonder why they would be crawling around Decem. Kara stretched her hand and tried the same thing Fezzik did for them many times before. The marks disappeared.","n":213},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Did we get all five? - Kara straightened up as Tallie gave her grateful nods and Fezzik shook his head.\n\n- Surprisingly in one piece, - red man was beyond impressed by the luck of the situation.\n\n- Hey Ember, scratch these memorial letters to families. Your guys are alright, not even a single crossfire, - Kara jumped to the comms, just to tease Tarik in his deepest place. There was a cluttering of paper crushing at the other end when the transmission started.\n\n- For goodness sake, Kara, - Ember sounded immensely relieved. It was a satisfying answer.\n\nIt was peaceful. For a moment, then another one. It was like the life that she imagined in her time in the garden. The flicker of flames, not Fluffer flames. Just flames. In the neat little rough stone firepit. It was calm, cozy and, for the first time since the day it all burned, she wasn’t free and it didn’t bother her. She just knew that in all of the paths and twines this was her little place where she was meant to find her way to. It was a pleasant dream. Persival would love to hear this story.\n\nSadly Persival didn’t share any relation to neither Bork nor Vincent and his sister Teresa was foolishly lost to a rage fit of Floats. There was no way Kara could tell that story to him. It did bring her heart some pain and the rest of it Toham showed to the back of her head to keep track of her sanity.\n\n- What’s going on in that head, - Parmas was being gently dramatic as he caught Kara frowning at the stars, deep in thought.\n\n- I don’t know… potatoes probably, - It was hard to justify being sad over some root in the ground. That was by no means healthy and yet Toham couldn’t just shake off the thoughts.\n\n- Which one this time? - Parmas rolled on his back and prepared for a long ridiculous potato story. She liked that he enjoyed those.","n":214},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Persival, - Kara sighed not knowing where to begin. The story was quite long. Not longer than Borks, but it was quite an epic one, - The mutant potato from the Quattor. The size of that thing was… Normal actually.\n\n- Well that’s a new one, - Parmas was visibly surprised. Toham’s garden was all about the flowers, roots that she was growing there were small. Almost pristinely wild. Quite potentially poisonous too. No one would dare to eat them to know for sure.\n\n- It was the first fresh vegetable package from Quattor since they went on strike with Septum. I sneaked one giant potato out into the forest that same day. Lilian, - Kara smiled remembering how stupidly excited she was to try growing up this giant novelty. It was her best day since the beginning of the garden, at least it felt like it at the time, - He was her successor.\n\n- Well that’s quite a start.\n\n- You say what? - Kara was massaging her temples with nervous tics. After a month in relative peace she completely forgot about this stupendous fact of her existence. Things turn into ridiculous plots if you follow the opposing lead.\n\n- They took him through the state portal, - Fezzik sighed, the reality appeared to be even more dire. If they would return there would be no going back unless they join the tournament. The question now was who was behind all this…\n\n- Do you think we can use Jeff as a wager then? We did isolate him, - Kara was just stalling words before asking the real questions.\n\n- I can go and catch him, - Tallie grinned with a wide vicious smile. Kara couldn’t imagine what sort of stupid plan could have gone through her mind. Tallie couldn’t fly.\n\n- Kara, I don’t think it would be much of a difference, - Fezzik looked over the portal with a deep disconcern, - State portals shut down for the tournament and turn into a one way in the last days before it.","n":215},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Alright, I don’t understand their logic, didn’t they say that they want to get out of the tournament? Why would they go through? - Kara nervously looked at the clock showing only four minutes left. She knew her choice, she didn’t quite want to impose it on everyone.\n\n- Who knows, - Fezzik was frowning, probably figuring out the answer that he didn’t like.\n\n- Why don’t you talk to him? - Kara was frustrated, sitting at the table with Fezzik and Floats. Blue man was cooing cluelessly at Pink Glasses, not quite sure what he had just walked into.\n\n- I did, but you know how quick he is to jump to conclusions, - red man scratched his head with a deeply puzzled look, - What did you tell him to make him this upset?\n\n- He is my friend, I wouldn’t lie to him, - Toham sighed knowing how little it narrowed it down. Fezzik fell silent for a moment, peacing the situation together with ever expanding desolation in his face.\n\n- Alright then, - He finally spoke up and stood up from the table. It was nice to have someone in the room on the same page for once. It was hard to tell what exactly clicked in Alfonce at this moment, but at least Kara could trust him.\n\n- Did you try to weasel out of the answer too? - Toham laughed just now realizing how annoying it must have been for Rein. No wonder he is this unreasonable. Not everyday your friends tell you that the cascade events of the show progression had cost him his family. And he wasn’t even a part of any of it. Yet.\n\n- Who’s a good kitty? - Floats suddenly picked up Pink Glasses, clearly bored by the conversation. It was hard to be mad at him, Kara wished she could be this calm.\n\n- I guess it can’t hurt to try again, - Toham sighed with a slight relief. What is the worst that could happen? Suddenly she wasn’t as scared of it anymore.","n":216},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"Kara didn’t hesitate to jump through the portal. There wasn’t any time to, Floats was in trouble. This simple thought kept ticking at the back of her head and all means and paths seemed justified. Kara almost remembered she had a gun, but a sudden ground under her feet made her summon Huggins out of the ether. He roared with an insane gush of hot wind. Kara held her arm over the parcel.\n\nIf there were any lamps they were shattered and the girl and flaming tom were the sole lightsource in a windowless room of purely industrial purpose. The workers seemed already visibly injured and requesting assistance. Kara cursed her dumb head. Fezzik stepped out next followed by Carp and then the power went completely out.\n\n- Maybe I should have gone last, - in retrospect, Kara could have foreseen that. At the moment it felt like an honest mistake. Fundamentally silly, since it seemed that the stranglers were the ones that had reasons to stay in Decem. There could have been plenty of time. Kara wondered if Fezzik didn’t realize that Rein wasn’t in the room, because he just frowned at the flaming destruction and stepped forward to blow the dust.\n\nAs soon as the lines showed up the workers gathered their senses and rushed to evacuate. These poor protocol creatures would do their work before saving themselves. Kara suddenly felt the rush of reality hitting her front. These people really had nowhere to run. They were at mercy of the state system. Who knows which one they have landed at.\n\n- So you guys did slay that dragon, right? - The question was quite sudden and Toham was a bit startled when a Tribus hero found his way to standing right next to her, - We never got the end of that story.","n":217},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- So he does know how to keep a secret? - Kara raised an eyebrow with a cheeky smile.\n\n- Did you think that we are in each other's heads twenty four three hundred sixty five? - man laughed giving Toham a shoulder pat which she almost received with a scolding look. Then she remembered they were friends of friends and so on. She sighed.\n\n- Never get stung by being too cautious, tell him I said “Hi”, - Toham didn’t feel comfortable talking to Parmas through his teammates, but it still felt rude not to acknowledge that.\n\n- He says “Hallo”, - Tribus man laughed, probably hearing something funny on the other end, - Name is Sherbis. Bet this Pidgeon didn’t talk much about us.\n\n- Not at all, probably for your own safety, - Toham smiled. She gave herself a promise not to profile heroes after the tournament, too many to keep track of and extra compassion never played into her hands. Plus it was more fun to be surprised from time to time.\n\n- He did say you might be a jealous type, - Sherbis laughed at how precise his teammate was. Kara wondered if what she would do would be an act of jealousy. She never thought about it that way.\n\n- Not much of a small talker, are you? - Kara sighed, visibly bored and mentally ready to burn this city into the ground.\n\n- Hard to small talk with an unwilling participant, - Sherbis shrugged his shoulders and tapped his fingers briefly on the table before moving on back to his team. Toham briefly wondered if she shouldn’t have cut him, but then remembered that he called Parmas a pigeon.\n\nGood thing Kara kept her potatoes on her. She could always move on from just here. Probably, most likely. She didn’t expect the portal to close up.\n\n- Rein? Where are you guys? - The better half of the Septum heroes was heading down the hallway and following a triple colored trail. Kara was just curious if they still could reach Decem.","n":218},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Welcome home, heroes, - Victorian’s voice over the comms must have startled Fezzik. Red man stopped in his tracks and looked around, finally realizing the situation.\n\n- Ooohhh, boy, - Fezzik sounded majorly perplexed for a moment, then he gathered himself together and moved on.\n\n- I think Rein and Tallie are stuck in Decem, - Kara jumped straight to the point.\n\n- What? - Victorean didn’t register the information at first and then a deep pause followed.\n\n- Either that or they are stuck midway, but I am almost sure that’s not how it works, - Kara loved to add fire to the pit of anxiety. It was a pleasure to watch Victorean suffer through the contemplation.\n\n- Kara, please, we don’t have time for this, - Fezzik never sounded this serious. He did, but somehow it sounded even heavier without Rein by their side. Or Floats, - Hey, Cass, how much coverage do you have on us?\n\n- None until you take off the veil rings and I hope you guys know what will happen if you do that, - Victorian sounded almost maliciously sarcastic. It was hard to tell if he was ever sincere, they weren’t his first team of heroes.\n\n- Well that’s one way to say that he is useless, - Fezzik sighed, complaining with the comms off. Surprisingly nothing changed since they left, why would it?\n\n- Let us know if you get a word from Ember, - Kara didn’t know what else this conversation would benefit, so she moved on.\n\n- It’s fine, then, - Kara sighed and looked around the room. Fluffers flames toned down to colling embers and she took a deep breath, - Mind if I rest up here? I think Huggins just broke like ten records.\n\nThe heart finally caught up and now was pounding in a strong beat to compensate for the sprint. Toham was surprised Rein gave her time to notice it. He was back at his station tapping away his pencil on the paper.","n":219},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"- Want me to look at it? - Kara sighed and perched over to see a sea of numbers and letters that barely made any sense.\n\n- This isn’t magic, Kara, - Rein backed down and observed Toham with a tired and slightly annoyed expression.\n\n- Sorry I didn’t come sooner, news don't travel fast enough, - It was funny to admit defeat as a hero. It was even funnier that even Rein couldn’t beat the timer. And now it could have been too late. It was too late. Fluffer Huggins never really came back.\n\n- What are you talking about, - Reins eyes lit up in a slight flash. Habitually hostile when any spoken words touched his family, only now it was mixed with a slight weakness. It tore at Kara’s heart like a knife through leather.\n\n- Apparently still the same silly things, - Toham took a closer look at the paper and focused her eyes on things she understood. Letters, sentences, numbers of various sizes and forms. The rest of the gap was filled by the blue string of energy. Toham followed it around the room picking up the rest of the puzzle pieces of Reins research.\n\nThe colorful trail moved further and further and with every step Kara could feel something inside of her burn stronger and stronger. She gave Fezzik a concerned tug and the red man braced for the jump. It was hard to predict what would happen next, Quintus heroes wouldn’t know how dangerous Floats could be if given good enough reason. Although Kara wouldn’t know what would be a good reason for Floats. For all she knew he would be ok alive or dead.\n\nThis time it was Kara who roared jumping out of the thin air. With a minute on the clock she wasn’t really about to negotiate. She landed straight on top of suddenly screaming Zelli and Brian was already swinging his sword. What Kara didn’t see was the staff swinging with the sonic speed sending her into what felt like a mile flight and roll. Floats wasn’t messing around.","n":220},{"chapter":"Stress and Habits","text":"It wasn’t Septum, neither was it Quattor. Beyond that it could have been anything, but something told her it could have been Quintus. When has she ever been wrong.\n\n- Kara, you alright? - Fezzik sounded gravely nervous through the earpiece.\n\n- Hey, crazy cat girl, - Zilli was as nonchalant as ever, - Try something like this again and the red one will get the double of that.\n\nAtrocious taunt.\n\n- You still are missing the link with the original soul, - Kara looked back at the paper following the blue line, - and you are missing a bracket here, it seems.\n\n- Stop that! - Rein angrily wrinkled the paper in his hands and dunked it into the trash can, - You know it cannot be touched by any magic.\n\n- Well good thing you threw it out because it was a bunch of evanescent gibberish anyway! - Toham didn’t like to be challenged in her ways, - You are stuck on the physical aspect that needs the least attention and refuse to venture into the real problem. You still can’t replicate the initial states of the nerve cells, beyond that what is even the point of trying?\n\n- So you do your research, - Rein eased in his chair contemplating the possibilities for a second, - Still, the intermediate research helps to solve smaller problems, which could help down the line.\n\n- Infinite waste of time, - Kara playfully rolled her eyes and reached over the table to dive into the trash can to take a second look at the wrangled paper, - Which problem are you trying to solve here?\n\n- It’s ok, Kara, you don’t have to pretend to care about this, - Rein massaged his temples and shook her away. For a second she thought he was half asleep. Tired even. He did look paler without Fluffers furious flames reflecting off his cheeks. Kara never saw him like this.","n":221},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"Kara took a long breath. She could feel the whiskers and her giant paws stuck to the ground by a sheer power of will. Kara Toham was a thought away from actually ripping Zilli whatever last name in shreds and pieces. Just for fun of it. What a horrific realization. Kara was surprised she caught herself on that though.\n\nTail shed in hairs of light and Kara stood upright realizing the length of her walk of shame. She was in the middle of a green meadow and the ruffled trees in the direction of her journey obscured any clue to what was going on or how far she had to go.\n\n- Tell Floats that it was a solid homerun, - Kara smiled feeling so defeated by the situation that all of her strength was taking a little observational break, - By the way, Zelli. Tag, you are It.\n\n- What? - the girl on the other side must have never had a childhood. What else could you expect from someone resorting to ransom. For whatever delusional reason or price.\n\n- We got to the castle in time and even played into your stupid chase game. What is my question, - Kara started walking at a leisurely pace towards the treeline.\n\n- You are in no position…\n\n- Touch Fezzik or Floats and I’ll show you my position, - Kara snapped feeling dicey on playing the patience game.\n\n- Well that’s not so heroic of you, - she could feel Zelli smiling on the other side.\n\n- Ask Fezzik what heroes I am like, that might clear up your confusion, - Toham snapped.\n\nThere once was a ship that crossed the ocean. Not the first one, but the most famous one. The one that really had to cross two, but stopped at one. Because who needs a land of old when there is the land of new? Especially if it has money basically laying by the rivers.","n":222},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"Once upon a time lived a girl that was nice and landed herself in a better place. It happens all the time. Fairy tales get uglier further north you travel. At some point even fairies turn to the chaotic darkness through which one can navigate only relying on the light burning within. Something that will pull you through the blizzards of the North.\n\nLong time ago in a place far far away there was a lamb. The one that brought prosperity of sorts. Someone less fortunate would snatch it and save their own people. One lamb that would grow into a herd in a new place. It wasn’t a fatal blow for the fortunate ones, but it left a scar on their hearts regardless.\n\nHeroes don’t always come from the states they were born in.\n\nThere was a dead silence for quite a while. Toham was traversing the forest on a mission of peace. She had no other reasons to hurry and Kara wasn’t the one making the decision at the moment. If Zelli dared to hit and run they are still to outrun Carp. It weighed on her chest to be this black and white, but personal misunderstandings can only go this far.\n\n- Kara, are you alright? - it’s been a pleasure to hear Fezzik much more calm, - They don’t want to fight. It seems we can strike a pretty good deal.\n\n- Zelli, please, don’t tell me that at any point between yesterday evening and ten minutes ago you wouldn’t stop and think why can’t we just all live in peace? - Toham was scolding profusely, she wasn’t sure how much of it came through as Cassius tuned in.\n\n- Kara, who are you talking to? - The voice was bone chilling. Why couldn’t Ember do that?\n\n- Oh great, wanna hear my rant? - Toham didn’t quite know what she was more angry about, Quintus' situation or exclusive attention from the man himself, - Wonder why you can’t hear Fezzik?","n":223},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- I can hear you guys loud and clear, Zellie from the runner Quintus team? - Victorian was clearly trying hard to remember the details.\n\n- Yep, - Toham sighed. Why would the Victorean hear Zellis' voice, at least he had the courtesy of keeping the priority communication line.\n\n- #@*^+, - Fezzik gasped and Kara could almost feel his frustration in her own bones, - Kara hurry up, there is a situation…\n\n- Hey, I know you think that I don’t care, but that doesn’t mean I can’t worry about you, - Kara felt like she was mumbling like a toddler. It was weird to say things out loud, things she had rehearsed like a million times as she was on her way. Things that just seemed silly.\n\n- Kara I can’t play doll houses with you, it’s cringe, - Rein said with a straight face. Kara couldn’t help, but raise an eyebrow. Very slowly, almost in disbelief. She did miss him.\n\n- When did we ever play doll houses? - it almost broke her little heart, she never even tried. Well as much as she could remember. He was a tiny bit older and a whole ton smarter. She did play with his sister once or twice. A flaw to the system.\n\nThere was a brief pause as Rein gathered his strength to care. Then it growed into a longer one as the look turned into glance lost in thought. Kara didn’t know what to say or do, but Rein seemed to want her in the room anymore. Maybe she did have time to visit Tallie. Maybe she could explain at least some things, but if Rein was like this at his snapping point, who knows in which mindset Tallie would be. Toham was trying really hard to be a good friend, so she did give Rein his space. She did wait for him outside the door. Kara could use a little break too. She didn’t even notice when Huggins disappeared.","n":224},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"Kara didn’t wait a second to flare up Huggins and jump to Fezzik. Scaring him, once more.\n\n- Kara, can you not? - red man was holding his chest and gathering the air in his lungs.\n\nIt wasn’t Floats. It was quite literally something else. His tiger eyes were glowing with teal lightning and fur was progressively matching color. Quintus duo was currently fighting for their lives.\n\n- I told them, - Kara shrugged and then suddenly remembered something, - Holly sprouts, did these idiots get bit?\n\n- Kara! - Fezzik urgently pointed at a growling, now almost blue, tiger. Large one at that. Maybe as big as an elephant. Couldn’t have been good. Worst of all Kara didn’t quite have a solution to that yet.\n\n- Floats, please be graciously kind and in your mind! - Kara precariously checked what tiger Floats was aiming to do.\n\n- Rhyme does not work, he is running amok! - Carp was running in and out dodging the blows of the tiger and keeping him distracted from Fezzik.\n\nBrian raised his sword to hit Floats from the back, Toham didn’t like it. She jumped in to catch the blow with her ax and perched her back on surprisingly chill and soft fur. It shifted in a moment and Kara just barely dodged out of the way with the claws tearing through her jacket. The sensation of pure terror sprouted from her back. She kicked Brian out of the way as she herself lost her balance to by sheer luck missing a potentially lethal blow.\n\n- How did that happen? - Kara really wanted to know the story.\n\nThe morning was dull and uneventful. The enthusiasm and the urgency of a past day disappeared and only the smell of breakfast lightened the mood. It appeared that Parmas did actually cook and it smelled like some meat.","n":225},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- Good Morning, sleeping beauty, - he placed the breakfast plate on the rock and rocked his fork in tact with some tune in his head. Toham was wondering what he was listening to, but she was never into music. It wouldn’t make any difference to know.\n\n- What time is it? - Kara stretched in her bedroll, surprised how high in the sky the sun was.\n\n- A bit over the noon, - Parmas raised his hand to try and calculate the time by the sun, but it was obvious that he probably had a precise clock somewhere in his toolkit. She was wondering why he didn’t wake her up. Although he is a veteran hero, he wouldn’t make such a mistake. So many dumb ways to die when you are in the presence of shapeshifters. Each with their own set of rules and habits. He would be suicidal if he didn’t come prepaired.\n\n- Decided to face fate in broad daylight? - Kara smiled and crawled out of the sleeping bag. It was getting a bit too hot, she probably wouldn’t wake up otherwise. Maybe closer to dusk.\n\n- Do you see in the dark? - It wasn’t like Parmas to ignore her questions. Kara got lost in thought.\n\n- Not really, - She shrugged and shifted into the tiger with a paw stretch. Parmas stumbled backwards in surprise. Camerarius never got to fully control his form, Toham rarely left any willing witnesses.\n\nKara roared in excitement, completely forgetting where they were. Parmas fell over and Kara has only found it funny. He wasn’t really on her bad side.\n\n- My dear lord, Toham this isn’t funny, - He sulked on his way up. Kara just chittered and shook her imaginary lion's mane, - One time Floats turned over a loose button on a show.\n\nThe count of heroes didn’t go down, that means no one died. Well, out of heroes. Who knows the numbers of the other states? Why would he bring it up?","n":226},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"Brian stumbled over and rolled back on his feet before retreating. Kara could not believe her eyes, however Toham knew that would happen. In fact that’s exactly what she wanted him to do exactly right now. Good hundred and eighty turnview, he was smart enough not to get bit. She didn’t see Zelli, but that's because she wasn’t dumb either.\n\nBut that’s thinking and it kills in close combat situations. Kara didn’t expect Floats to be this quick. All Toham could do was to turn back so that there would not be scars up front. Nasty claws slashed through the flesh and scratched the bones. That was enough.\n\n- I see you are back from the dead? - Rein was expectantly glancing over at her as full of energy as ever. With a deep sigh. He threw a dagger, Toham didn’t even bother to dodge. Hurt like the first time every time. Like the first dog that bit her on the street so weakly that the teeth didn’t even scratch the skin.\n\n- Please, don’t tell me you are already this bored. It hasn’t even been a week, - Toham watched the blade shatter into dissipating deep purple shards. It was nice to know that Rein wasn’t sadistic. To think of it, none of his targets suffered for long. Toham didn’t have that clean of a record.\n\n- This stupid show of yours have cost me quite a bit of progress, - Rein was visibly annoyed, - You realize how hard it is to secure these woods for the children to play in.\n\n- Woods are never safe in Decem, that’s why goblins survive there. No matter what, - Kara sighed, feeling absolutely distraught saying these words. Now she finally understood what Eve meant by saying that Toham went overboard. She really shouldn’t have burnt that forest.\n\n- Are you calling genocide a progress? - Kara could not believe her ears, - Gob…","n":227},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- Listen, you have no idea what you are talking about, - Rein cut her off, - These monsters are beyond any reason.\n\n- Maybe, but that’s why we open quick and efficient pathways, but you can’t cover the entire world in pavement, - Kara didn’t like these conversations, but it was better than nothing.\n\nToham didn’t slash an ax. She simply pulled the energy up and Mother Earth did the rest. Absence of magic in this ground made the burst almost uncontrollable. No muddy waters to absorb the shock. Kara didn’t expect the shock and stumbled for a few seconds before tumbling down gasping for air as Floats slowly shifted back to his normal self breathing heavily to calm down.\n\n- Pardon, Kara. Lost my composure, - Blue man was tidying up his impeccable suit of guard outfit.\n\n- Oh right, you didn’t hear me. These things are ridiculously cool! - Kara knocked on the helmet, still crouched in exhaustion. Magic did work quite differently on the state turf. Much more unpredictable. Toham didn’t quite understand what just happened, thankfully Quintus heroes were willing to bide some time for her to figure it out.\n\n- Seriously! How in Seven Hells you guys made it this far intact? - Zelli was dusting off her hood and slowly making her way over. Toham was too tired to stop her, plus that would technically go against the logic she came up with maybe a ten minute time ago.\n\n- Wonder and hope, I guess, - it took all her energy, but Toham stood up straight and finally took an eye to eye with Quintus' hero. The unreasonable one.\n\n- How did it go? - Fezzik was waiting outside with a suddenly hopeful glimpse.\n\n- Who knows…\n\n- Kara.\n\n- He is fine, we will catch on the Decem problem after the show shot, - Toham waved a hand and started walking away,","n":228},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- Kara, what’s this, - red man coughed her by the hand and gasped at the burning dagger wound, - What the actual…\n\nToham was surprised Fezzik didn’t know that this could happen.\n\n- Ever wonder what hides under Valerians tattoo? - Toham raised an eyebrow, - Don’t worry, it could be worse.\n\nIt couldn’t be, but for the other reason.\n\n- How are you? You’re sure you’re alright? - Fezzik was tending the wound. Toham pulled a smile on an impulse and brushed it off.\n\n- Can’t change nothing, - Toham shrugged and walked on. She couldn’t wait to get back to the garden. She’s done plenty for one day.\n\nZellie grew like a solid foot taller in a few weeks when they didn’t see each other. It was almost uncomfortable to look down at her now. It felt like she was always crouching. Maybe she was, Kara wasn’t really looking at her, but past her shoulder at Fezzik waiting for his reaction. Toham could wait for a few more steps.\n\n- Can we skip the pleasantries? - Fezzik shook his head and tapped at his wrist, - Pretty sure these workers are reporting this incident as we speak.\n\n- They won’t report unless you psychos kill us, - Zellie was snappy, but at least she realized how vulnerable her position was.\n\n- For the last time, - Toham sighed and sat down to gather some strength, - Manticore burned them before we could do anything.\n\n- And what for the Grox?! - Zellie was a bit too emotional for just the loss of a friend.\n\n- It was him or us, Tallie wouldn’t come to her senses otherwise. You guys didn’t really think we would just sit there helpless? - Toham was trying to sniff the air to try and figure out where they were. If it was, indeed, Quintus territory it would be a blast to watch Victorian figure out this pickle.","n":229},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- The man that killed Grox was a state traitor, you can’t hold his morals against us. We had a real rough start to begin with, - it was almost insulting to hear Fezzik sign off Haderach like this, but Toham figured it helped their case.\n\nKara didn’t wait any longer. She pounced the rocks on her way down the dark crevice, clawing at the rock as if it was dry soap.\n\n- Kara wait for a minute, - Parmas didn’t shout but his voice flowed down with ease. Toham halted in place feeling the mountain shift a bit. She stared it down to see if it had any final wishes. Dragon exhaled in annoyance and crawled deeper inside. Kara tilted her head in disbelief.\n\n- There you are, did you see something? - Parmas landed softly by her side and looked over the now empty cave clearing where the dragon once laid. Kara had just realized it, she hadn't heard any movement. Deviant dragons adapted to avoid heroes. It was frustrating that tigers couldn't talk.\n\nToham roared to shake the mountain. Two monsters could contest that title. She changed back to her feet and Fluffer Huggins lit up her way. Kara didn’t enjoy closed spaces, but it didn’t scare her anymore.\n\n- It is giant, - Kara coyly smiled at Parmas that must have found it quite challenging to find a matching pace to their teamwork. Probably because Toham wasn’t trying to meet him halfway. It wasn’t really his challenge to take on to begin with.\n\n- Alright, do you mind if I take a lead from here? - Kara didn’t understand what was going on, but for some reason Parmas insisted.\n\n- Alright, - Toham shrugged and let him walk first. It’s been quite a while since she took on guard duty. Could have been a good practice.\n\n- What do you mean by ‘enter the tournament as Quintus’? - Toham was trying to piece her options together.","n":230},{"chapter":"Under Pressure","text":"- Cut ties and join us. If we win you stay in Quintus and we can give Jeff and Decem to Septum, - Zellie made her argument clear, but everything in her plan benefited everyone, but Kara. She needed Septum. Toham didn’t need to mention that though.\n\n- Can’t decide for everyone, shouldn’t we at least ask Victorean? - Kara didn’t like taking on big decisions anyway.\n\n- Talk to him and the deal is off, - Zellie pressed on.\n\n- And then what? - Toham didn’t see the point the racoon girl was trying to make.\n\n- And then you all get captured and brought to the Quintus trespassing law, - Zellie crossed hands on her chest, - Believe it or not, our state has quite a good squad of animal control.\n\nToham hoped she was bluffing, but the odds were stacked against that favor.\n\n- Fine then, can’t believe they could be mad about this, - Kara shrugged and made her way over to Fezzik, whom was clearly outpaced by the conversation and now just had questions.\n\n- So you say Rein and Tallie just stay stuck in Decem? - The whole splitting situation didn’t seem to sit well with the red man.\n\n- They and Jeff are going to hold the Decem frontline, if all goes well they could stay alive there past the tournament as rebels, - Brian joined the conversation finally brave enough to come closer to Floats.","n":231},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- Can Huggins tone down his flames a bit? - Parmas really got the hang on taking the lead. He gave Toham a good head rub and she felt his cold and composed energy drift down to her eyes filling them with cat-like dark vision. For a second she scoffed at the audacity of her partner, but something still warmed her heart.\n\n- No wonder Pink Glasses hates you, - Kara raised an eyebrow and Huggins disappeared in the darkness.\n\n- You think so? - Parmas smiled trying to figure out the connection.\n\n- Definitely, you hoard too much of my attention, - Kara chuckled and the walls rumbled once again indicating the movement of the infernal beast.\n\n- Ah, I knew there was nothing I could do about it! - He bumped his fist on a palm and joyfully simulated despair.\n\n- Oh, what a tortuous twist of fate, - Toham bumped his side with a shoulder and he caught her in a soft hug. She almost lost her footing.\n\n- Can’t we ever catch a break from all of this twisting? - Toham was now laying down on her back, processing what just happened. She used to want to leave for Quattor and now, when she finally settled on her Septum option it was turned upside down. Kara didn’t know a thing about Quintus.\n\n- Are you tired from a little sprint? - Zellie was clearly walking a fine line. Toham hoped she knew that.\n\n- Please, I had enough of this with Rein, - Toham spread her arms enjoying the sunlight shining down. The floor rubble and various knicknacks scattered around the area suggested that this used to be part of the building. Toham couldn’t believe she survived the hit.\n\n- You know you weren’t our first choice either, - Zellie sat down, probably trying some bonding practice. It did feel safer that way.\n\n- The more you blabbermouth the less seriously I will treat your presence, - Toham could feel the fatigue slowly spreading across her body. Sweet moment of peace, when pieces fell at the eye of the hurricane.","n":232},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- So what exactly is happening right now? - Fezzik spoke up, probably thoroughly and genuinely flabbergasted.\n\n- You give up your Septum earpieces and we will get you a Quintus registration, - Zellie was calm, surprisingly taking a hint. Kara took off the ear piece and threw it to the raccoon girl, still in Persillas outfit.\n\n- So that’s it then? We are out of Septum? Don’t you think the rest of the lot would notice if the missing Septum candidates just appear on Quitus team? - Fezzik was still struggling to grasp the logic.\n\n- Sure, it is still your choice, - Zellie probably shouldn’t have used that wording. Kara could feel her entire body flare up in rage. Out of all things, this wasn’t really a choice.\n\nIt was stubble, the presence that is. Kara could feel it with her deepest sense. It did not exactly spell out good. Tallie must have taken her path to pay a visit. Rein felt it too.\n\n- What did you do? - A dark haired boy opened the door and stared right at a light brown haired girl. She gave him an ‘I told you so look’.\n\n- Apparently the show, - Kara could almost feel every step Tallie made towards them. It felt terrifyingly menacing. Toham wondered who could have been warning her? Or was it some intimidation technique Tallie picked up? The omnipotent presence almost taunted Toham. She could use a good brawl.\n\n- What is that supposed to mean? - Rein was quickly running out of patience. Toham could read it in his eyes.\n\n- The championship show, don’t tell me you know nothing about it, - Toham sighed realizing how deep in trouble Kara just got into. If Rein didn’t know a single thing about the show he would be insanely mad to hear the revelation right now. If he would even give her enough time to explain. He was quite smart.","n":233},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- Buzzer, we are online, - Zellie spoke through a new line. The new coms line felt like a dog kennel. Tons of new noises and things to hear.\n\n- Good morning runners! Welcome to Quintus, - the voice on the other side already felt like an improvement. Anything was better than Victorians speech.\n\n- So can we call you Buzz? - Kara didn’t wait to ask her first question.\n\nThere was an awkward silence on the other end. Fezzik gave Kara a solid scolding look and she returned a happy peace sign and a grin.\n\n- Alright, Buzzer. Sheesh what a bore, - Toham couldn’t miss an opportunity to nibble at the patience of their new guiding heroes.\n\n- That’s Kara, - Zellie shook off a slight shock to respond. To think about it, guiding heroes couldn’t be that much weaker than Ember or Cassius. Kara could have been playing with fire, what a disaster.\n\n- Hope you guys are in tip top shape for the tournament, - the man’s voice on the other end sounded way less enthusiastic.\n\n- No thanks to you, - Kara couldn’t stop now, - By the way, what happens if we take off the veil rings now?\n\n- Don’t do that yet, - the voice on the other side spoke with slight urgency. Toham didn’t like that.\n\n- I didn’t expect it to hide, - Kara was looking around greedily taking in the surroundings. Not everyday heroes get to face the dragon, let alone raid its lair, - Do you think it was just staring us down?\n\n- Who knows, maybe it has a trap ahead, - Parmas was tense, something really bothered his mind. What did he expect from this dragon? Insufferable inferno? Oh, right.\n\n- Don’t tell me you are collecting data here? - Kara raised an eyebrow and nudged her partner to loosen him up.\n\n- I mean I have to know what I am talking about when I’m telling the story, - Parmas rolled his eyes and gave her a wink, - You aren’t actually going to take that away, right?","n":234},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- Oh, stop it. Technically I’m not a monster, - Kara stretched an arm itch. The cave talk grew more and more bothersome. Until it died completely. The cave split and Toham observed the man hard at work. Parmas stood there with a very serious face and a hand on his chin. Kara wondered if he’s going to guess correctly.\n\n- Tracks lead there, - he pointed precisely, Toham was thoroughly impressed.\n\n- Alright, please tell me what you found out, - Kara was genuinely interested in what could have possibly he seen that she didn’t.\n\n- Oh, it’s a real toughnut, the thing doesn’t leave as much of a turned over stone as it moves. I don’t think it is corporeal this high up, - Parmas pulled on a smile.\n\n- Are we making up a spooky story? - Toham laughed and walked on.\n\n- Not really, the only thing I could discern is a tiniest of metallic echoes. Imagine a dragon so petty that instead of protecting the hoard just carries it around, - Parmas caught his tongue suddenly feeling the atmosphere drop forty degrees down.\n\n- WHO DARES TO INSULT MY AIM?! - The voice wasn’t screaming, but simply inquiring. The source was just a bit too grand.\n\n- Do you know Tallie? Goblin with a tail and a hammer? - Toham didn’t expect the mountain to be open for conversation, but here they were.\n\n- So will they tell us what we should do now? - Fezzik was getting nervously irritated, but Brian shrugged like it was nothing.\n\n- As long as we don’t leave Quintus' premises, we will be fine, - Man of a flexible shape with orange eyes looked at Kara point blank. Like he was observing a most undesirable miracle.\n\n- Keep staring and see how it ends, - Toham wasn’t about to tolerate that, - If it’s so simple just tell us where to meet up when the time comes.","n":235},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- We really have to get ready, if we are to…\n\n- Alright, alright, I’ll look around myself. How about we say meet here four hours before the clock runs out? - Brian chucked and smiled, - I mean would you really mind if we hang out with you? You know we are teammates now.\n\n- Fine, we can hang out. What's your favorite colour?\n\n- I am kind of growing fond of violet lately, what about you?\n\n- White.\n\nThere was a brief pause.\n\n- Now you ask the question, - Toham sighed and grunted to herself, - what an idiot…\n\n- You know I can hear that, - Brian clearly didn’t get the word hang out.\n\n- That’s still not a question.\n\nIt was going to be a long night.\n\nThe premise says that all heroes have an equal magical potential given to them as they begin their training. With thoroughly pruned state lands, prior exposure to it of the candidate list was almost negligible. However Decem lash back and Culo routes running across the globe like a wild surge crippled that defense system. It couldn’t stand the trial of change.\n\nMost of these were little things that barely had an impact, however that is a fairly new statement. Only ten years ago these were simply ridiculous knick knacks like the bottle cap that never landed top side up. Magic was pumped into the system making the view of the world cloudy and segregated. Septum picked fights almost everywhere it could to make sure their premises were clear of that fog. Their heroes most often fell in simple domestic disputes over the use of magic on their territories. There was no point in wasting more energy to produce less for the masses. Altruism finds its dumb ways to find polar opposites.\n\nIn any way, believe it or not, all this was orchestrated by one person that had quite straight priorities. Up until now.","n":236},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"They made it to the Quintus mansion later in the evening, Toham made Floats carry her most of the way. It was a rough day and she couldn’t even pick a reason to focus her frustration. Any one of them seemed to be astronomically dangerous for their newly formed team. A third of it to be precise.\n\n- You really went all out there, sweet Kara, - Floats was as nonchalant as ever. If he was tired, he hid it like an absolute pro. Toham appreciated that.\n\n- It wouldn’t be right to leave you hanging, we all know what would happen if we did, - Kara was helplessly hanging her hands over blue man's shoulders, mostly to keep balance with least energy waste for both of them.\n\n- Excuse me? - Zellie caught the conversation with a corner of her ear, - What are you implying?\n\n- Zelli, there are a thousand ways this conversation could cripple something more that your ego, - Zellie had plenty of chances to prove she could be a friend. However, one can only stumble so many times before reaching the point of no trust return.\n\n- Well, we better figure all of them out before the tournament, don’t you think? - Kara couldn’t figure out the exact angle of this conversation. She could enjoy it if Floats did. Blue man smiled at their chatter.\n\n- Alright what’s your favorite colour?\n\n- Seriously? - Zellie sighed and gave it a good though, - Velvet green, it is just so lush and yet so dry.\n\n- Seriously? - Toham tried to understand what was so appealing about such a mundane thing, - That’s why you love a colour?\n\n- Why does anyone like any color, - Zellie really picked up the conversation.\n\n- Touche.\n\n- I’ll be in and out, one way or another! Just letting you know, - Toham was putting on tracking shoes by the entrance door. Cassius appeared right in front of her like a clock. With a button in hand.","n":237},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- And I am letting you know that this is your responsibility to stay here and rest up, - He didn’t stand in the way, but his eyes never left her until the door closed. Guards tensed up, but no one touched a gun. They just stood by the gate as a wall.\n\nToham took a deep breath and took a short jump to the edge of the city, right where she first found her potatoes. No one else knew where it was. It was quiet. Birds usually sensed her mood.\n\nShe didn’t even bother to sense if any of the hunter heroes were around to fool around. She did just shoot the entire world with a brand new record. Not really one to be proud of, so it would be reasonable to assume a solid backlash. She just needed some time alone. Kara could barely hold it in any longer.\n\nThe Quintus mansion was innovative. Full of toys and things to punch around. A gym, for any purpose really. They even had walls for squash. Kara wondered when they ever had time to use all that.\n\n- Welcome to the new home, people, - Brian was very generously avoiding other extensions.\n\n- Look at that, It’ll bounce alot! - Carp stretched out and threw the ball hard at the ground, but the dense rubber only returned a tenth of the power. If you don’t account for thundering bounces, it almost resembled a tennis ball.\n\n- Wow there, buddy! - Brian shielded himself with hands trying to avoid being hit by the rubber ball, - Want to play a good game? - The man jogged over to the squash court and picked up two rackets, throwing one to an overly excited Carp and they both disappeared in the rule explanations.\n\n- Well at least these two are getting along quite nicely, - Toham finally managed to relax a bit.\n\n- Yeah, Brian is great at these things. Sorry for earlier, we didn’t expect Jeff to be stuck there. He is probably worried sick right now, - Zellie sighed. For some reason Toham understood why. Probably for the same one that made her feel uneasy being away from Rein and Tallie. It felt like the entire world held hostages against them.","n":238},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- If he is alive, that is, - Kara laughed remembering the last time Tallie didn’t get her desired brawl.\n\n- Oh, Jeff is going to be fine, - Zellie had zero doubt in her voice, Toham finally could see why the Quintus heroes fell to a simple manticore attack. Who knows what could have happened to Floats if Kara had a similar mindset.\n\nIt was surprising how fast the news traveled down the line. Maybe it was the fact that she took so long to recover. They found her maybe in twenty minutes. Tribus heroes never really dealt with this type of problem before, so they started with a talk.\n\n- What happened? - Sherbis was very tense, Kara still held the record number of heroes taken down. Five at one time would be a new one.\n\n- Keep asking questions and you’ll never get the chance to find out, - Toham didn’t even bother to stand up. Staying calm is a good intimidation technique. She was in no condition to fight this one.\n\n- I hope you know that we will have to pull out all of our cooperation now, - Keneth, a woman with a soft voice and muted outfit. She was the most calm of the lot.\n\n- Great, because your strategies suffocate the resources anyway, - Toham sighed observing the treeline, trying to concentrate on rest.\n\n- You heartless … - Joy, the boy with golden shining hair must have stepped up, but Sherbis stopped him with his hand. Toham could hear a thud and a clank of zippers.\n\nThat was enough for the Huggins to flare up. Kara couldn’t hold him, he was absolutely right. She couldn’t just sit there with her back to the problem. She stood up and dusted the stuck pines and finally gave everyone a good look.\n\n- Please skip the talking and decide what you want to do here. One by one or all together? I will give a courtesy option of leaving peacefully, - Toham never lost a battle on her own turf, but there are always exceptions to the rules on extreme spectrums.","n":239},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- It’s ok, Toham, there’s no need for this, - Sherbis tried to reason, but Huggins' flame turned blood red and Kara tilted her head. They stepped on the wrong ground, that was reason enough.\n\n- So you say they just let you do whatever? - Kara was having breakfast surprisingly peacefully. It was uncanny to sit by Zellie and eat the same food. Weirdest thing was that it wasn’t the first time they were pretending to be friends. Maybe they were friends now. The lines were quite blurry.\n\n- Pretty much. They said they’ll help organize as much as they can to make our last days memorable, - Toham could not believe how stupid it sounded. This poor group of friends was trying to win or escape a soon to be cannibal island. No wonder they were terrified. Dragon attack in Decem must have thrown them off the edge and they requested a pick up and grasped the opportunity to recruit new members to their cause. Adaptable heroes.\n\n- Well you did quite a number of twisting and turning, didn’t you? - Toham was slowly catching on to the train of thought. Easy enough, - So you want lessons?\n\n- Pretty much, - Zellie was being quite frank. Kara had no idea how the knowledge of how to stay alive in her presence could hurt anyone in this situation. It’s not like they could ever figure out Floats and yet they did, for a moment.\n\n- Lesson number one then, - Kara put down her fork and stood up, - If you feel Fluffers pull you, don’t resist.\n\n- What? - Zellie seemed quite oblivious to the situation. She never really saw Fluffer Huggins at work and what he did yesterday terrified her into a state of panic. So Fluffers appeared in the room with a soft rasp. Zellie jumped from her chair on her feet, but braced bravely as the flaming tom took both of them to the other side of the room, slightly burning the couch. Floats' eyes twitched and he cleaned up with a swing of a finger. Kara waved him a guilty hand.","n":240},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- Wow, how did you do that? - Usually teleporting wasn’t the easiest of the routes to take. It required quite a bit of preparation and wouldn’t be thrown around like a whim.\n\n- Don’t know, he doesn’t speak, - Toham sighed as Fluffers chittered with sparks in his teeth.\n\n- So you really didn’t joke about the catwalk? - Kara caught up to Floats, escaping the never ending Sherbis welcoming chatter. The man sure talked quite a bit and she almost started to understand who was whom. It was terrifying.\n\n- Not at all! Imagine the double excitement when they get to see you walk in this outfit and walk it down, - Floats was extremely pleased with his innovative plan. The negative feedback alone would shatter the top charts and Novem would find a way to twist it right. They loved Floats.\n\n- Your lot is extraordinarily polar, some don’t even understand the possibility of a connection. The photoshoot alone will break the news simply because it’s quite bizarre, - Feather was smiling in genuine excitement, it was hard to deny her that simple experience.\n\n- How much time did you guys spend planning this? - Toham was in complete disbelief of what she was getting into. She just trusted Floats.\n\n- Oh, the longest of times! - Floats was oblivious to the situation, - So many nights, so many outfits. Then I just thought that it should be locally inspired. Then we picked the locations you might like and gave them a Crusher Party request. I don’t care if they are hosting presidents!\n\nSo nonchalant. Presidents unusually avoided Senio hot spots. They could have as well been running around naked, no one would bat an eye of judgment and yet somehow the world would know.\n\n- And it will show off the best hundred of my sketches picked by the audience! Almost an hour of the pure art exhibition with a little strawberry crushing the party! - The miracles never ended, there was no stopping Floats.","n":241},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"Carp was flopping around the squash court trying to figure out how to stop the ball from going out of bounds and shattering the glass window. Brian was just hanging out every now and then fixing the shattered glass with a trick Floats must have taught him in this time.\n\nHeroes really were nice to hang around as friends.\n\n- So there is a rule to this madness? - Zellie was looking into a suddenly very friendly flaming tom who was floating just out of reach of anything too flammable.\n\n- What happened to the Racoon? - Kara just now remembered that this team did have a giant Racoon, not just a racoon girl.\n\n- Chester is somewhere in the backyard, he is suspicious of you guys, - Zellie shrugged with disdain, - It doesn’t matter, we are not taking him into the tournament.\n\n- I was always wondering how the animal state went around the animal cruelty topic, - Toham sighed and continued, - If I turn you might want to watch out for anyone whom I don’t know and don’t get bit. But these are really abc’s. Actually no, that's about it.\n\n- That’s all? - Zellie was a bit dumbfounded by the brevity of the talk.\n\n- Beyond that, be my guest to ask questions, we don’t have much else to do with four days to spare, - Kara was contemplating the possibility of any team exercises to fit Brian and Zellie, but they seemed to be confused on their needs.\n\n- Alright then.\n\n- I take it Tallie didn’t change at all? - Toham was almost sure the goblin was extremely unhappy with a freshly burnt lawn.\n\n- Is that what you came here for? To get a good fight? - Rein was struggling to grasp the situation.\n\n- You know me, I don’t pick fights, - Kara shrugged and sighed, - I always hope for the best case scenario, so don’t blame me.","n":242},{"chapter":"Nickles and Weasels","text":"- Kara, what did you do? - Rein suddenly sparked with pure anger. She got distracted. He almost clipped her hand. She could feel the wisp of the wind through the jacket. If she had a nickel. He was looking for a weasel. Almost blindly, but still as precise as ever.\n\n- Well, you didn’t really expect me to answer this quickly? - Kara landed on all fours, but not yet paws.\n\n- And what difference would it make? - Rein didn’t stop his lunges, surprisingly leaving some room for talk.\n\n- Probably not much, - Kara expected this outcome, regardless of the talk. Toham couldn’t wait for all of it to end.","n":243},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- I really don’t think with your experience my approach would fit, but if you muster yourself to overcome your pride and give Carp a good line he might take you up, - Kara was tired of explaining the concept of never turning your back on the fight that you were not meant to run from. The longer she thought about it the less the whole situation made sense.\n\n- This thing speaks nonsense, if I am to understand him I still need a translator, - Zellie was surprisingly clingy. Kara didn’t like it, it felt like a stupid attention deficit tantrum. Brian spent most of his time with other men and couldn't stop talking about Jeff.\n\n- Carp is not a thing, he just doesn’t fluctuate his priorities, - Kara sighed helplessly, - Carp, do you wanna play tag with Zellie?\n\n- Done and done, make her run! - the response through comms was a pleasant addition. It was nice of Brian to slow down a bit and teach Carp how to respond to messages.\n\n- Have fun you two, - Kara smiled wondering which direction Carp will be coming from, - you heard the man. You better run, - she grinned raising her eyebrows. Zellie was stunned for a second, but then started running after an eyeroll. How relatable.\n\n- Hey, - Toham wasn’t raising her tone, she just got caught out of breath. She wasn’t sure what was stopping Rein from hitting true, he sure never hesitated like that before. Toham could try to read his eyes, but Kara wouldn’t look there.\n\nShe thought of trying to catch his hand, but his cloak burned with a fire of a completely different nature. She could feel it in the static air that filled the inches around it. The strikes were, practically, flawless. If he wasn’t trying to miss, Kara would have to shift. And yet she did not want to see it coming. Toham felt it was weird. Was she tired or asleep?","n":244},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"The entrance door shattered as Tallie broke it to smithereens in a fit of rage. It didn’t stop Rein, as he mustered for the strike. Kara reacted out of pure habit. Attempting to stop what seemed to be unstoppable. Toham wasn’t scared of facing Tallie, so she did make a step, but was promptly stopped by a hand.\n\n- Kara, he can’t be reasoned with, - Parmas did not pick a right time for talking. Kara would not listen. She twirled and threw her ax to intercept Tallie.\n\n- Tallie, stop! - Kara jumped trying to catch a thin thread of possibilities. It was torturous to live through this again, after all these years.\n\nToham didn’t have much else to do, but follow Zellie and Carp around trying to spot anything of use for her now Quintus teammate. It was even rougher to see her caught only after a few minutes of Carp searching for her and then endless hours of him making an absolute fool out of her.\n\n- How am I even supposed to catch him? - Zellie was tired and speaking through comms. They were already ten miles into the forest and Kara gave up trying to spot her about two hours ago, as Carp found the game exhilaratingly engaging, as he ran almost randomly enjoying the life of a slippery grounded fish.\n\n- In your case I would bet on dumb luck, - finally gave a solid answer as the dust was crawling in between clothes. Quintus was much drier than Quattor, or at least it seemed that way this season around. Or maybe it was the region of Quintus. She wouldn’t know. Zellie wouldn’t know, but maybe Brian would.\n\n- Hey, Brian, is it always this arid around these places? - Kara was glad they could direct message each other now.\n\n- I knew there was something wrong in the air! - The answer answered nothing.","n":245},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- Lead her home, For dreams to roam, - It was ridiculous to communicate through rhymes, but Carp seemed to love it. No matter how bad they were.\n\n- Words are pearls, Don’t waste morals! - Carp answered and Toham for the life of hers did not understand what he meant. She would keep morals in mind though.\n\n- Today we are making a real doozy! - Toham looked back at the audience after she climbed the stairs to the pantry. The audience chuckled as she remembered to teleport, - Gets me, everytime, these stairs.\n\nShe ravaged the pantry for a good while and finally emerged with five flying objects.\n\n- Here are our ingredients, believe it or not, anything could work if you add just the right amount of the right cheese! - She didn’t have to know for sure, she never ate potatoes. She could barely stand cutting them. All of her recipes were fool proof mostly. Sometimes she wouldn’t even cook it long enough. The audience wouldn’t know.\n\n- Seriously Toham? I thought you were a professional! - Darius, the founder hero of Tribus, was her guest tonight. The chills creeped down her spine. What a terrible surprise. Eve couldn’t do much around him. Why would he pay a visit? What could he possibly be expecting? It would be rude to even retaliate.\n\n- Fun fact for the audience! Never have I ever eaten anything prepared in this kitchen, - Toham dropped her gaze in pure interest, last time they met wasn’t the friendliest of encounters.\n\n- Well that makes two of us then, - chubby strict man in a black robe chuckled finally making his way to the guest dinner table.\n\n- Well you guys really went on a hike today, - Fezzik was shocked to see Kara, Carp and dusty Zellie, - You realize we have to be in our prime shape in three days?","n":246},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- I’m fine, pretty sure Carp is too, - Toham shrugged, Zellie could take the rest of the days off. Kara wasn’t sure how theory could prepare the poor girl for the onslaught. They couldn’t charm their way around everything. At least it seemed to have backfired in the case of Carp and Kara. Which meant that everyone really had to cover their bases.\n\n- It is good team building, - Zellie straightened up with good energy. Kara almost forgot she was a hero after hearing her countless hours of grunting. To think about it.\n\nToham quickly waved her finger up and Zellie got dusted off and looked brand new, - See, she is absolutely fine.\n\n- Right, - Fezzik seemed off his game. Probably worried sick about Rein being stuck in Decem. Kara started to worry herself just looking at him.\n\n- Hey, at least he doesn’t really need to participate in this at all, right? - Kara’s glance slightly wavered at Brian behind Fezzik, it was so uncanny to see him there.\n\n- Right, - Brian’s response was heavy and unexpected. Kara wasn’t really talking to him… Maybe she should have.\n\n- THE WARRITHRI GOBLIN? - The voice tried whispering, but the walls shaked echoing her words. Kara really appreciated the attempt.\n\n- Yeah, that’s my Tallie, - Toham tried her best to refrain from scratching her ears. She gave Parmas a quick side eye to see what the plan was in his head, but he must have been startled by the topic of the conversation.\n\n- SHE LEADS MY HOARD, - Dragon glided down, without a sound, as if its claws were a pure light illusion. Toham almost believed it wasn’t there, but Parmas was right. She could feel it now. Bullets. Her bullets.\n\n- Well that’s an interesting way to put it, - Toham sighed and gave Parmas a light pat on the shoulder, - It seems there is no dispute here.","n":247},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"The dragon looked down intently, glaring with a cold, unbothered stare. It was terrifying to meet her eyes. Like an ant to an elephant. It was hard to even perceive that this spectacle was indeed alive and sentient.\n\n- INDEED, - Dragon exhaled deeply filling the entire cave with a blizzard of the wind. Parmas rushed to grab Kara by the hand and let the wind phase right through them. Tohams eyes narrowed tensely. Deviants or adaptable copycats. This dragon was growing stronger by the minute, - DON’T GET ANY IDEAS.\n\nIt was bone chilling.\n\n- So you do read minds, - Toham was angry, not that it mattered much. She stormed out, trying to find some room for a breather. Too many conflicting thoughts, if she was to take care of this dragon problem she had to be in the right mind set.\n\n- What? - Parmas flinched, realizing that the last line was meant for him.\n\n- SO AM I A PROBLEM? - Dragon reared up in anger. Toham scoffed, she really didn’t want to fight her now.\n\nEverything about this land was repulsive. Kara didn’t notice it until now, it wasn’t something that Kara could put a finger on. It was the entirety of it. Like a constant itch in mind that tells you that you are out of your plate. Every odd smell would almost turn her stomach, even if it was familiar. Yet she didn’t show or speak of it. A single glance at Fezzik told her he felt the same.\n\n- So what’s the plan exactly? - Toham sighed, shifting back into human form after sneaking into his room, already littered with cogs and metal scraps.\n\n- I don’t know, - red man wasn’t really startled this time, it wasn’t the first time he caught a glimpse of the ginger tail.\n\n- They do seem quite sincere, - Kara was sitting at the window looking outside expecting Carp to run outside for some action, but it seemed that it wasn’t this type of a scene. It was too quiet and it wasn’t the peace and quiet that she was used to. Almost like they were the ones who were supposed to fill this emptiness and yet the energies seemed critically mismatched.","n":248},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- There are other teams out there, that’s about as much info that Quintus elders would give, - Fezzik was visibly frustrated with the general approach of preparing heroes, - Who knows how many of them had time to sneak in and out to Decem to spy on us like Quintus did.\n\n- How fast do you think…\n\n- Kara don’t, - Fezzik cut her off. She didn’t finish the sentence feeling the presence of something else. There was a brief silence before Toham sighed and continued.\n\nIt is hard for someone who is used to a non-stop regime to sit back and relax. Always looking for new action to progress something incorporeal. Some idea or ideal. A constant pruning of procedures to better fit the changing situation. Learning, observing, building set ups and forming neural pathways to react fast in critical situations. With so much preparation at hand, imagine what a wild card could do to the entire structure.\n\nHeroes, however, are built to resist these flukes. That’s what the tournament filters out of the pool. A hero is either fearless or cautious. Team of heroes usually has both types as a precaution.\n\n- All I’m saying, that if we are to work something out of this situation it would be nice to at least establish ground rules so that we don’t react out of sync with the Decem squad, - Kara really didn’t understand why the states wouldn't collaborate. Though Septum really got the short stick on this one.\n\nThe responding silence was answering nothing and everything at the same time. For the first time, Fezzik really didn’t know. The turn over was just a bit too rapid. Toham slowly sank into the realization.\n\n- Alright, we could always stretch it up. Hopefully we won’t hit the end of our rope, - Toham sighed and jumped down into a ginger cat form. It was much easier to be nice to Zellie if she couldn’t quite read her response.","n":249},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"Zellie is a girl with a cat and a fish friend. Like a start to a weird folktale.\n\n- Kara, - Fezzik stood by, but corrected himself, - nevermind…\n\nKara purred and gave his shoe a nice headbut. She meowed and walked on.\n\nThe crackling of lightning almost threw Toham off guard. Almost. She wasn’t afraid of energy. It was an instance of it. And frequency. Too bad that the entire forest was ultimately one giant bear trap. Silly how Tribus heroes didn’t realize that much. Also silly how transparent their bodies were. Now that Toham was starting to take this seriously.\n\nShe thought for a little bit too long. Not a problem, a projectile meant to aim true hit the ground as tiny strands of ashes burned through the air. Kara wasn’t in the mood. Toham could respect that much.\n\nShe landed on a front porch, Victorian waited observing the clock.\n\n- Twenty three minutes, you are truly a wonder of luck, - the man’s voice almost gave away his excitement over her defeat.\n\n- Really? Should have pushed for twenty five, - Kara laughed and suddenly got an idea.\n\n- Please don’t, - Cassius pleaded, but not really with his eyes.\n\nIt was hard to resist the temptation to obliterate the trespassers. She could still feel their feet touching her ground, they already showed her how.\n\n- Is there a way to tell them that they have a timer? - Toham wondered which way would Victorian sway. He never lost ground. Well, almost.\n\n- I’ll let them know, - He looked away for a moment and the Tribus heroes were gone in a minute.\n\n- Wasn’t that hard, was it? - Toham sighed and disappeared back into the forest.\n\nIf yesterday was today or if tomorrow was yesterday, nothing would change. It would rapidly, but not in the qualifying direction to be considered eligible for the title.","n":250},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"Kara couldn’t find a single interesting thing around the house, so she spent most of the time fooling around and chasing birds and critters. Zellie kept mentioning how weird it was during dinners, but everybody knew what Kara thought of her comments.\n\n- All I’m saying, that you basically spend your days running around aimlessly? - The raccoon girl clearly didn’t get the first memo.\n\n- If you don’t see an aim to it, kind of your problem to figure it out, - Kara sighed and launched a pea with a spoon into Zellies hair.\n\n- Seriously? - Zellie launched an entire plate into Kara’s head. It connected, Toham was wondering what her trick was.\n\n- Well that’s interesting, - Toham wiped her face with a napkin, surprised how easy it was to tolerate her without a looming threat of backstabbing. Front blows she could take.\n\n- Zellie not at the table, - Brian sighed. Probably wishing his friend was a goblin instead. At least that way the manners would match an appearance. At least Toham liked thinking that he did. Kara could not believe that this was the same person as Persilla. The difference was subtle, uncanny even.\n\nKara grabbed Parmas by the hand and they were out of the cave. Huggins was semi-startled, there seemed to be a lot of unclarity in what exactly Toham was trying to do.\n\n- Sorry about that one, - Kara exhaled briefly as the entire mountain shook, sending cracks creeping upwards all over the place.\n\n- I mean, when did an earthquake ever mean good in Decem? - Parmas was clearly coping with some sort of internal anxiety. Or anger, Toham always wondered.\n\n- I mean, I wasn’t really a problem there was I? - Toham raised an eyebrow.\n\n- Do you always do things like this? - there was her answer.","n":251},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- Trust me, this is my first dragon too, - Kara laughed suddenly remembering how funny the situation truly was, - It’s not my fault my friend decided to pseudo-tame it.\n\n- How long did you know? - Parmas was thoroughly choosing his questions now, as the rumbling closed in to the surface. Uncannily silent.\n\n- How was I supposed to know? - Toham could feel the uneasy realization crawl up her back. It was only rational that Tallie would join the goblin tribe. Kara just never saw a dragon being a part of it. To be honest, Toham didn’t really ever consider what could have been going on in Decem. This land was a bit ridiculous and far away to really worry about.\n\nParmas frowned deeply as the land split in cracks and crevices right in front of them. Silently. It was stupidly disorienting.\n\nKara didn’t like a single second of staying in Quintus. Everything was just too simple. Not a single instruction or direction, they had everything and could even go further out into the desolate land and not meet a single soul. For thousands of miles. Kara checked in all directions. There were mountains, but she figured those were the no-no-land of Septum. She wouldn’t want Quintus' mentors to think that she is having second thoughts on their deal.\n\n- You weren’t kidding when you said that there is nothing out here, - Toham jumped out of nowhere scaring the soul out of Zellie that was practicing a shot. She almost threw a dagger.\n\n- Wow, someone is on the edge, - Understandably so, the tournament was about to start in a few hours and they still had no clue of what was going to really happen. Something primal read this show of fear as an invitation for a path that could work.This discovery tickled something and Toham sighed and looked over at the targets, trying to get her mind out of the cat’s mindset. It has been becoming difficult lately, - Nice shot.","n":252},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- Thanks, I guess, - Zellie calmly picked up her next arrow and shot another bullseye. It was almost painful to watch. If all heroes had a chance to win, it would mean that this would be a straight and quite deadly hit. But it looked so. Blatantly. Simple.\n\nThis time Toham picked a random spot. She could feel the eyes pry, but no action followed. Good enough. There was no point to it, though. She tried to sit down and relax, but her mind was empty and the feeling of lack of purpose of her existence was ever looming over her head.\n\nWhat was it like to live in Culo? Was it a sunny ride all around the world? Knowing no boundaries and never having home? To be free from possessions and serve the sole path of movement, regardless of direction. To always do what you were supposed to do and never look back. Maybe Kara got it wrong all together. She never should have wanted to transfer to Quattor. Maybe she was supposed to aim at Culo. She could definitely pick up that path, it just never occurred to her. Potatoes. She was too fascinated to see her future beyond them. Not the biggest of the news. Kara always was silly. That’s why Toham loved her. One thing she could never run away from, so she learned to.\n\nIt wasn’t just a random spot. Nowhere in the forest was. This was the place where she met her first wild wolf. Not a rabbit stray. It wasn’t a half breed that flooded the woods after the famine. Maybe things were different if Huggins was a puppy. They wouldn’t, but there are always outlying ifs. Things would have been very different if he was a wolf.\n\nThe flash in the sky made Toham chuckle. Victorian must have been having the time of his life. Not everyday heroes had a good reason to test their wits.","n":253},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- See, it’s quite fun, - Toham was starting to wonder who could have been so reckless as to try and ignore warning signs.\n\nThere was no response. Toham didn’t know what to make of it. It was nice to have statemates. And Cassius was the best by far. She would never tell him that and she was probably the only hero that knew it for sure. Kara wouldn’t be here otherwise.\n\nVery intriguing double edged sentence. Toham wondered what he kept away that was so precious that it was worth putting up with her. She never took him for a workaholic. But he definitely could have been one. She never cared enough to ask.\n\nMaybe that’s why she preferred Ember’s company. Too bad this one had known better than let her bite into his boundaries. Just barely dancing on the fence of being a friend. Nothing really connected any of the Septum heroes. Nothing but the fact that they usually had each other's back. Maybe because there haven’t been so many of them before.\n\n- Nervous? - Zellie breathed out and put down the bow. It must have been unsettling to feel a cats stare behind her back.\n\nToham chirped and rolled over on her back. Zellie didn’t really hesitate to fall right into the trap. Only this time Kara didn’t snap. She just stretched with a soft purr.\n\n- So this is your plan? - Zellie sighed and sat down. Kara climbed on her lap and curled up in a warm ginger bun, - Gonna pretend to be a cat the whole time?\n\n- Not a cat, a cat-tiger shapeshifter, - Kara mastered the transformation to the point where it took a blink of an eye for her head to land under Zellies hands, making her blush in surprise.\n\n- Can you ever stop fooling around? - Zellie scoffed, but inertia carried her hands along the light brown hair. Kara wasn’t quite sure what had gotten into her.","n":254},{"chapter":"Energy Spikes","text":"- Imagine how stupid it would be to die while not fooling around. Almost like you gave it your all and still didn’t succeed. What a bitter way to go, - none of the bits of their plan made any sense. Kara didn’t like to overestimate her chances, so she rounded them up. Got her that far.\n\n- You definitely spent way too much time with that fish, - Zellie sighed and swooped Kara off of her lap. It took Toham quite a bit of self restraint not to retaliate. Instead she turned back into a cat and simply hopped away through space.","n":255},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Huggins, start the stove, - Toham pulled on a smile. It was becoming quite hard to hide the displeasure with the company. Man was chipperly smiling at her now catching her glance. There was no way in nine states she would forgive him. It was now obvious what he was aiming for.\n\n- Lots of water has passed since we last chatted, didn’t it? - every word Darius said triggered Kara and Toham was struggling to remember what she was supposed to do, so she reached for potatoes. It was probably the worst choice. Pink Glasses was staring at her.\n\n- Glasses, stove, - Kara pointed at the switch and ginger tom meowed and beated the gas switch to seven. Good enough, Toham lit the fire with a snap, - If that was chatting for you might as well call this a date and, sir, you are a bit too old, right?\n\nDarius laughed with a clear self restraint. He should have known better than to show up to her kitchen, - What a heartless accusation! I would never embellish my words.\n\nAn eyebrow was raised and Toham could almost feel the air heat up. Not enough though, - A bit more Fluffers.\n\nPink Glasses meowed and batted on the stove switch some more.\n\nThe time was ticking, not like a clock. Kara was nonchalantly clattering in tact with seconds knowing for sure it would get on anyones nerves if they were to hear it.\n\n- What on Earth are you doing, Kara? - Fezzik opened the door with a worried expression. It has been really hard to annoy him lately.\n\n- Did you talk to Cassius? - Toham wasn’t really expecting an actual answer. She wondered what was stopping Fezzik from it in the first place.\n\n- I wouldn’t want to compromise this situation for it, - he sighed and sat down at the chair. The following silence was deafening, but Kara enjoyed the sensation. Another blank slate, if that was tough for her she couldn’t imagine Fezzik knowing a straight answer for anything.","n":256},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Alright then, same plan as always? Following directions and reacting according to the situation? - Toham raised an eyebrow and Fezzik helplessly raised his palms.\n\n- Pretty much, - sometimes Kara wondered how the red man could seem so dumb and brilliant at the same time. It takes a gut to admit the bottom line solutions.\n\n- Doesn’t quite spell out rainbows, doesn’t it? - Kara was constantly trying to figure out what was wrong, except the fact that Floats was clearly a bit unnaturally friendly with Zellie and Brian. Well, it’s not like he ever was against them. She shook her head trying to chase away silly thoughts. There was little they could do if the blue man turned on them. A very nasty little thing.\n\n- Just… - Fezzik started a sentence, but stopped before it shaped up into anything, - Yes.\n\n- Promise to watch my back? - Kara never felt like she had to ask for these things from her teammates, but it somehow felt reassuring to hear it now.\n\n- Of course! - Fezzik smiled and awkwardly stretched out his fist. It was nice to know that at least one thing wasn’t doubted.\n\nMore time, Kara thought that was the issue, but things work differently when facing an inevitable scenario. Who would benefit the most from pinning Septum heroes against each other? Toham couldn’t bring herself to assume. She stared right back at Tallie as the tailed goblin growled at her. Ever so briefly.\n\nParmas tried to catch Toham by the hand but she slipped out into the thin air landing promptly by her ax right in between Tallie and Rein. Futile, as the blow of the rapier glided straight through her barely grazing Tallie’s fur coat.\n\nToham growled and threw Parmas a dagger of a glance. She caught Reins hand, feeling the burn from his cloak sizzle up through her arm, - Stop this nonsense, - Kara’s eyes glowed up for a second as she threw Septum heroes to different corners.","n":257},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"Tallie just roared, making a new lunge. Toham wondered what made the tailed goblin so determined. She never did and she was stuck. Tallie accepted Kara into her family, but now Kara was all the family Rein was left with. Tallie had the hoard. Rein had a father that he despised. For a brief moment Toham considered accepting the loss of one. Ever so brief, the thought threw her into a rage. People were not potatoes.\n\n- Hey, Buzz, - Kara wasn’t expecting an answer, but she knew that someone must have been listening on the other side, - Don’t you think that veil rings from Septum are a dead giveaway of your little scheme?\n\nRings weren’t exactly of the Septum origin, but what do these heroes really know? So far none of the guiding heroes of Quintus gave much to work with. Smart, but Toham wondered why they would exercise such a level of caution.\n\n- Anyways, I never actually had the chance to see any of the hero tournaments and yet they stir up so much attention somehow, - Kara yawned stretching up on a sofa, - To be honest, I don’t even know any of the latest heroes. I bet this situation is even worse in Quintus. What are we even trying to achieve with all this?\n\n- Who are you talking to? - Brian peeked through the door with a puzzled expression.\n\n- Trying to get Buzz to answer, I guess, - Toham springed up on her feet, - How do we even know our targets? Do they expect us to be sitting ducks in this great middle of nowhere?\n\n- It would make sense, home turf benefits maybe? - Brian shrugged and gave Toham a shoulder pat, - Just try and keep a cool head.\n\n- Hey, Buzz, if you don’t respond before the tournament, I’ll bite Brian, - Toham gave it a last try and still received no response except from a worried step back from a brown haired man, - Calm down, I’m just joking.","n":258},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"Her eyes weren’t quite joking, so Brian took another step back.\n\nThe doors swing open, the blue and orange light filled the dark room with watery webs. The heads turned, but the tunes hit Toham by surprise. Like a torrent, submerging her deep under the columns of water, the music bounced and stuck to something deep inside Kara. It was almost unnatural not to be terrified, however Toham could feel it was Floats.\n\nNo one dropped a word, except for the inseparable lyrics sung by an unphased performer. So Kara looked at him, gazing lovingly at the neverending line of the hundred outfits. The crowd slowly followed her gaze and suddenly a clap broke the silence.\n\nToham never saw so many heroes clamped up in a small room, and that says quite something. Even models seemed to be Unus squad clones. She could feel her nerves ticking, yet nothing could stop Floats stride. Kara never saw him this happy, she would hate to ruin it.\n\n- This is quite incredible Floats, - Toham smiled and gave her friend a friendly nudge.\n\n- Oh, it’s really nothing. Only the best for you, my friend, - Floats smiled wholeheartedly and Kara didn’t know how to tell him how she felt about it. It was quite awesome, yet somehow it felt awkward to be at the center of it. She barely knew this crowd.\n\n- Alright, don’t know what’s going to happen, but let’s at least start a timer, - Kara stood up and dug through the gear for a stopwatch. She counted up the minutes and set the manual timer. Somehow it was curious how handy discrepancy would lighten the mood. Few seconds extra or a few seconds early, she didn’t particularly care. However, the buzzer would be slightly late as she would figure the moment right after the countdown started. Toham didn’t mention it.","n":259},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- You just have to be dramatic, don’t you? - Zellie was nervously pacing around the common room where heroes just started slowly gathering in. Not because of a memo or anything.\n\n- And you just have to miss the point, - Toham sighed and threw the timer on the table, not really bothering about it. Everyone had clocks. One was ticking silently on the wall. The hard to read one.\n\n- And what could the point possibly be? - Zellie was slowly tapping her foot. This conversation seemed a bit too rehearsed.\n\n- I don’t know, maybe I’ll turn into a cat and these are the last minutes of us talking ever, - Kara shrugged and everyone in the room shivered. Although the prospects did generally sound good, the implications were ever so slightly terrifying.\n\n- Kara, I don’t think this is how it works, - Fezzik seemed to have attempted to console Kara, but in the end it was just banter.\n\n- Well, I for sure know a way to make at least that work, - Toham grinned at Zellie,wishing it was Tallie instead.\n\n- Well, then I will make sure you all look fabulous! - Floats suddenly sprung up with energy and disappeared into his room. At least now there was something to look forward to.\n\nDragon crawled out of the cracks or did she phase through them? Toham was quite perplexed by the entire ordeal. The big now eerily green eyes locked contact with heroes on the hill, but didn’t move an inch closer. The skies were engulfed in dark storm clouds. Kara didn’t remember them forming before.\n\n- You understand how wrong this is? - Toham could feel the tremble in her chest. Almost weakening, something akin to fear. She couldn’t quite understand it’s source, but she was speaking to a condition.\n\n- What do you mean? Are you still mad about this? - Parmas was slightly perplexed and visibly worried. Then the realization slowly crawled into his head, - Oh, Kara I would never…","n":260},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- It’s fine, should have expected that anyway, - It wasn’t really time and place to settle some petty conflict of theirs. They still had a titanic creature to deal with right in front of them. Maybe it didn’t matter at all, but it threw Kara off the orbit. How could she trust him?\n\n- No wonder these things don’t survive too long, - Parmas suddenly grew very serious. Toham could feel the sturdiness in his voice that she never noticed before. Somehow that was reassuring.\n\n- DONE CHATTING? - Dragoness spoke without moving an inch, but the forest still trembled with vibrations. Toham couldn’t understand where the source of this anomaly was anymore.\n\n- Yes, thanks for following up, - Kara breathed out, trying to remember what was the reason behind this visit to a dragon's lair, - Where can I find Tallie? I want to introduce someone to her.\n\n- SHE IS RIGHT HERE.\n\nIt was ridiculous how painful the silence around the stopwatch was. So painful in fact that Kara considered joining Floats to give him some fashion suggestions. Not that she thought that she knew any better than him. He would be a good company nevertheless.\n\n- Alright, I’ll be back for the buzzer. I’ll catch Carp, - Kara sighed and left the room. If any of the teams already planned an attack they would be toast regardless of the outside scout. Might as well meet them with full power and worry not about being at the numbers disadvantage.\n\nToham sighed after the door behind her closed. The fresh, but dry outside air was a flavor away from being refreshing. She didn’t like leaving Fezzik with Zellie and Brian. She didn’t quite understand why. Loudly it would be to assume that she didn’t really see them as allies, but Toham didn’t really consider that turn of events. If anything would happen they all might be at Floats mercy and lord help them all if Kara doesn’t make it there in time. An extremely collapsible situation. How well can Victorian clean up the mess or are they on their own after the buzzer? Nothing would make sense, Quintus still was better off with six heroes instead of two.","n":261},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"Toham shook her head feeling that this train of thought is getting her nowhere. Every time it just circles back to reality. And so far Kara understood nothing about where it all was going. The garden could be just a run a mountain away. She was not exactly where she wanted, but close enough to almost touch it. At least that always could have been a plan B.\n\nThere is a pattern among Tribus heroes that Toham never quite understood. If Tribus was to survive the tournament they always come out as a group of six. Never a non-complete team of Tribus has survived for longer than two weeks after losing a team member. Toham couldn’t help, but be curious if the same held true for their active and retired heroes. Maybe that’s why she never really took time to get to know Sherbis?\n\nToham felt lost. This place that seemed to be sacred in some way now barely sparked with energy. Did she really throw it all away just to be here? Wasn’t it supposed to be the one place where she could feel herself?\n\nNothing really made sense, even the fact that she was avoiding the garden. Why would she be so scared that someone will find it again? Why did she feel relieved running around it and not able to enter? Why was it so important to get here and outside of this mansion?\n\nKara sighed and stood up, dusting off the pines off her clothes. Maybe she just needed a plain walk.\n\nThe trick to catching Carp wasn’t simple. It required a lot of patience and knowledge of his favorite hiding spots, which surprisingly did include a couple of water creeks. A little stone cave was another one, but his favorite spot was the little forest at the foot of a hill. Running around miles of terrain Kara learned to look out for him around those. It was surprising how fast he found these places. And then the realization hit her.","n":262},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Carp, want to play tag around the mansion? - Kara called through communications feeling defeated by the ease of the task at hand. It didn’t really matter how she would pass the time. The tiny dot in the distance suddenly started leaving a dust trail. Carp was getting faster and faster by the minute. No wonder Zellie had trouble catching him. Kara wondered if she never thought about how fast she was herself. Funny thing about it, you can run really slow. Slower than walking if you are careful enough. The funniest thing is that you can always run faster than walking.\n\nBarely turning the dust. Kara could almost see individual splashes of Carps leaps. The closer he got the more obvious the zigzag pattern appeared. Ever so slight. Kara prepared to dodge, but it was obvious he wasn’t about to miss a target now. Hide and seek it is.\n\nKara leaped onto four feet and ran into the field. Tail and whiskers intact, grass barely clearing her ears. She didn’t have to see Carp to dodge him if he was making this much raucous. Kara could just sit still while he was perusing the field.\n\nThere was a slight ruffle of leaves, almost intentional, however Kara failed to react fast enough. Tallie took her down in a bear hug. The whole body trembled in fear as it slowly caught up to what was going on. Then her hands hugged the goblin back, with warmth that could have been strong enough to call this moment home.\n\n- I missed you, - Toham never really realized that before. It would be so hard to leave now.\n\n- You are slow, - Tallie couldn’t hide her grinning victory celebration. Toham simply had too many instances when the hammer swung right by her ear only to be saved from imminent danger.\n\n- Should have used a hammer then, - Kara laughed and wondered how far she would fly if she was hit. Maybe a broken rib or two even. Tallie didn’t like holding back.","n":263},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- You would never stand against my hammer, - tailed goblin leaped up with a back flip smugly crossing her hands. Tallie didn’t even seem to have a hammer on her. It was nice she finally found a peaceful enough home to rest it somewhere sometimes.\n\nParmas was in deep thought, slightly shocked how easily a simple goblin surpassed his defenses. Maybe, Toham couldn’t otherwise explain his silence.\n\n- That’s Tallie, - Kara laughed a bit, and scrambled to her feet, - Tallie that’s Parmas, I’m showing him around.\n\nTallie didn’t raise an eyebrow and turned to the barely sensible guy. Head to foot nod and Toham suddenly remembered something, something that sent the entire mountain into shake as the dragon hissed at the thought.\n\n- Don’t worry, I’m not taking away your hoard, - Toham waved a hand and slightly bowed to the dragoness. She didn’t want to force Tallie to kill her giant pet.\n\nThe last flop was strong, flattening the grass and revealing Kara’s position. She clawed into the earth just to stay grounded. Teleportation conveniently didn’t require her to be in the right position. She jumped moment away from being tagged. She did so three times before freezing in place as Carp was looking in all of the wrong places.\n\nToham grew bored and hopped up. Rinse and repeat. She was wondering if this would have been harder if Carp wasn’t wearing a fish mask. Afterall it was hard to seek with limited vision. Meow here, hop there. Occasional wind surge, occasional silence. He got close one time, on the second he grabbed a tail as it wiffed into ashes in his hand.\n\n- Black is ash, that’s a clash, - Carp shouted, victorious over the field cat riddle. Now Kara had to solve the fish one.\n\n- Alright you got me, - Kara stood up straight and tilted her head slightly, - I’m it.","n":264},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"Back to four paws, but this time claws and fangs. Just as tall to hide in the grass in a crouch. Carp booked it, this time not particularly picking a direction. He was swimming in the field. It was hard to see him through the grass.\n\nKara growled and the land under her feet bursted with a twisting treetrunk to give her the high view. No branches or leaves, these islands were slippery without the claws. Ever so slightly, enough to generalize trajectory. Anything could help when dealing with Carp.\n\nIt’s been a while. A good while before she walked all the way back to the Septum hero mansion. Victorian came outside to see her in.\n\n- Do you know what a bother this dome is? - Victorian was suddenly very chatty, - Might as well forget about summerland if your lot keeps trying to murder each other every cycle.\n\n- Tell me about it, - Kara felt tired and almost collapsed on the stairs. There was a brief flash of worry on Cassiuses face. Suddenly the world started spinning and she sat down half way, - Who would have thought that simplest things would become so difficult after this much time down.\n\n- I told you it wasn’t a good idea, - not really adding anything to the conversation, as condescending as ever. Cassius gave Kara a dab on a shoulder and she felt ten times fresher, - Let’s go inside.\n\nTown nearby was launching fireworks, Toham wondered what they celebrated this time. Coils have been anything really. Maybe a new shipment has arrived in the market and people were playing with it. Hopefully none would make it into the forest. They would be stupid to do so.\n\n- Yeah, - Toham reluctantly stood up and gave the fireworks a last glance. Maybe she should have forbidden them all together, but it was nice to know about some happiness in the neighborhood from time to time. Toham must have been just flustered that it was parading her failure.","n":265},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"The grass grew still, the wind created a turbulent stasis for a moment or two. Toham had no idea where Carp was. She could jump in and hope for a lottery or she could be patient and look through the grass while it’s so still. She could listen to the grass…\n\nKara kneeled her ears to the grass and could hear thousands of voices.\n\n- Wind from south, Wind from North, Water? No water, Step? No step, - The voices chattered away, oblivious to the question in Tohams mind. What a bother. She reached her paw in and ruffled some grass to hear the response. Self composed at first and then suddenly shocked when the blades of grass statistically started sticking to her fur. Almost tripping her over. She struggled against it but some of the blades still stuck to her paw like a mashed lawn mower fluff. She tried to shake it off but they just aligned better with the fur and almost pierced down to the skin now. The flames didn’t wait to come out in response and the itch disappeared, but the spark caught on to the rest of the field and burned instantly. Kara scowled in defeat and summoned Huggins, who now was somehow standing still and unbothered in the grass field. Well, not somehow, he was drenched in water. He was almost smiling full of himself.\n\n- Water, FIRE, WATER, FIRE, WATER, FIRE, Water, Fire, Water, Sun, sun, - Only shouted the blades of grass. Huggins leaped up and shook the water out and the field filled up with fog. One would say it would be worse to be a tiger in wet grass, but one never saw a tiger jumping in the field of electric needles.\n\n- You just had to sound the alarms, - Rein suddenly tried to shove Kara out of the way but his hand passed right through her. He suddenly looked shocked, but only in a way when one suddenly proves the ridiculous theory.","n":266},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- What do you mean alarms? - Kara couldn’t quite figure out what was happening anymore. Pet, something with a pet, - Oh no you didn’t…\n\nWhy would Toham be surprised? Rein managed to kill the dragon, sensible, but why would he do it now after all these years of stalemate? Beats her. Veil was still quite potent. Plus Kara was chaotically searching through Decem magic lines for dragon signatures. Bullets. She found them on the ground in the mountains.\n\n- It was getting out of control, - Reins eyes flashed purple, but the shock wave of Tallies attacks must have reached the target. He collapsed in a fit of a blood cough and Kara had a second to think, Tallie wasn’t particularly scared of bullets. She aimed to intercept the lunge of a tailed goblin. Potato gun shot sending Tallie swinging to the far wall of the hall. The collision collapsed stones of the roof revealing the night sky. It grew silent for a moment. Then more stones trickled on the floor. It’s been a while since Toham used it.\n\nCatshing Carp was a hustle, but eventually Kara gave him a tiger hug. Funny how training with Zellie taught him how to play slippery but not fast. Like trying to catch a certain shape of air in one particular instance that would finally break the elasticity point. It was satisfying to finally have a fish masked goblin pinned.\n\n- Tag, - Kara sat down beside the goblin and caught her breath. She reached out a hand with a mint candy to Carp and goblin quickly munched it, - Wanna go inside for the count down?\n\n- Count is a beat, can’t miss it, - Goblin flopped back to his feet and wobbled towards the Quinttus house.\n\n- Floats is making us outfits, - Kara smiled as the fish goblin shyly tugged his mask sternly on his head, - Don’t worry, pretty sure he will work around your style.","n":267},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"It’s only natural to assume that once a person sees what they need they instantly consider taking it. How many steps do you need to take to get it? How many steps are you willing to take? Which step differentiates between survival instincts and morals? Why even consider morals if the item is considered to be vitally necessary? How far is too far?\n\nTo be free from desires is to only follow the simplest of needs. However, to stay in such a state is to desire it. Flip the point of view and all of a sudden you see that they simplify the pool of choice to the hyper local situations.\n\nOne could argue that not everyone follows this and one would be right. Not everyone wants to be free from desires. Slaves of destiny are polymorphous creatures playing lottery with their lives to find happiness in most random situations. Because it’s fun, because it’s something that they did, found, kept for a moment. Envy them not, for you are one of them. Unless you are not. Worry not, one day or way and you’ll find what you are truly looking for. Don’t lie to yourself.\n\n\"Episode 23\"\n\n⅓ of potato - Carrot\n\n¼ of potato - Cherry tomatoes\n\nSun dried tomatoes, garlic, salt and chili\n\nBoiled Water\n\n1/12 of potato - Tomato and Basil Sauce\n\n⅙ of potato - Alphabet Noodles\n\n⅓ of potato - Cheese\n\n- Oh save me some patience, just break the news already, - Toham poured water into the pot and hesitated a bit before grabbing a knife. It didn't get any easier to peel the potatoes. Even if she was assured that they weren’t from her garden, how could she ever be sure now? She wished that she counted every root before she left.\n\n- I still can’t understand why they would ever do a cooking show at a center, - Darius spoke deliberately prolonging pauses and vowels, - Not a good one at that even. All you need is to add some flavor to all potatoes in the world with your treachery.","n":268},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Oh noo, - Toham clacked her fingers and pointed a finger gun at the chubby man, - I thought no one would ever notice that. Can’t really let me get away with that, can’t you? - Toham started the peeling process with an indifferent displeasure.\n\n- Don’t tell me that you were the one who actually did it? - Darius raised a brow at such an easy answer.\n\n- Yes, it was quite easy actually. Potatoes are delicious and versatile. Almost foolproof in preparation. Why wouldn’t people pick them up on the plates? - Kara could feel a gag coming up her chest. The smell of freshly peeled potato never got better.\n\n- That doesn’t sound convincing at all, - Darius picked up on the situation and sighed, - Well then, you wouldn’t deny then that these plants are actually toxic?\n\n- Yes, if you eat anything green on this plant you will end up poisoned, - not that it mattered in this kitchen. All ingredients were picked from the top of the stock. Toham managed to recuperate and finish up peeling up her potato pile.\n\nKara woke up in the garden. One of rare nights when she slept quite well. It was potato blooming season, the buds were already showing the tips of white petals.\n\n- Bloom, bloom, sun, sun, bloom, bloom, - the whisper of chants almost had a melody. She never understood why they never stood silent when she listened. Almost like she commanded them like she would command Huggins. Only, unlike Fluffers, they existed and grew at their own pace. This batch outgrew the greenhouse and bloomed the brightest under the direct sunlight. Some grew taller, others barely broke the ground. Kara was testing how quickly they would spread unattended. And spread they did, slowly, but surely. She already foolproof the greenhouse, now it was just the question of whether a population would survive on its own.","n":269},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Aren’t you guys so sweet? - Kara lied down to hide in the potato shadows.\n\n- Sweet, sweet, we are sweet, Nectar, sweets, Come, come, - These potatoes never saw pollinator insects, still they called to them. Toham thought of getting a beehive for the area, but Septum kept these out for a good reason. Well, good enough to keep poles up and guards occupied.\n\nThe chopping knife came out and Toham quickly started cutting the potatoes in small cubes. Maybe a soup?\n\n- So you must absolutely have some sort of qualification to do what you are doing right now? - Darius raised a humorous eyebrow at a growing pile of potato cubes.\n\n- Oh, absolutely, I mean look how sharp this is! - Kara threw a potato in the air and slashed it so fast that it didn’t split until it hit the table, - Imagine keeping something like that in your own house, one misstep and you might lose your fingers!\n\n- Are you suggesting households should stop cooking? - the chubby man was pushing on his questions. Toham chopped the last potato and threw the mountain in the pot with a splashes of simmering water flying in all directions.\n\n- For sure, then we tell them to stay in their cubicles until an outside use is found for them, - Tohams eyes glittered in almost sincere sarcasm, - But, boy, they will be fed well.\n\n- You idealists sure always go the extra mile, - Darius must have caught on to a ruse.\n\n- Let me tell you a story of how we ended up all the way in Quintus right at the tournament’s start, - Kara picked up the carrots and started peeling again.\n\n- Oh right, right past Buzz and everything, not a single update right after the crowd started heating up for more adventures of Septum heroes, - bald man was playing his oblivious self.","n":270},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Yeah, what was it, a week and a bit? I don’t think we ever gathered for viewers after that, - Carrots peeled on their own as Toham took her hands to her chin trying to think what would be a sensible start to the story.\n\nNight Piercer was about to cross over the portal when it closed with a lightning screech.\n\n- What happened? - Tallie asked a painfully obvious question.\n\n- They must have closed the portal, - Night Piercer nervously looked around trying to figure out the spell, but it didn’t look like there was anything he could do, - Fezzik, can you hear me?\n\nNo response.\n\n- What now? - Tallie was clearly dumbfounded. Something clearly not out of the ordinary.\n\n- Well, if I remember correctly Jeff is still outside, - boy with dark hair and cold blue eyes sighed and headed outside, - Ember?\n\n- Hey, we caught a giant bird man, - one of the cadets finally caught up to the heroes looking super proud of himself.\n\n- Hey, Cassius said that they are out of Decem, - Embers voice promptly answered the comms.\n\n- Can we repair the portal? - boy didn’t hesitate to inquire.\n\n- We don’t know where they are, you might as well start looking for a needle in a hay mound, - Ember was making very poor excuses, expected from someone siding with Conrad. Night Piercer was stuck in Decem like his father all those years ago. A worry instilled in his mind.\n\n- Just tell me the truth here, ever since we arrived you were siding with my father and now your excuses start sounding old, - It was unusual having this conversation without Kara interrupting every second. Night Piercer didn’t know why he was worrying, still, she disappeared so fast again. Off the charts, into the ether. Probably only to reappear some ridiculous time later out of nowhere. Conveniently, Fezzik was already fireproof.","n":271},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Fun fact, - Kara flinged the knife to the side as she was painstakingly slowly chopping the carrots, - Me and Night Piercer were actually neighbors, can you imagine?\n\n- You and the Shroud of the Decem? - Darius was surprisingly bold to mention Decem right on screen. Toham threw a warning glance to the crew and people started packing non-necessary objects and personnel.\n\n- Well someone is a fan of conspiracy theories, - Toham turned back to Tribus founder, - I know you think I made him up, but totally a real human being, - the carrot cubes dropped into yet to be reheated water. Potatoes took out quite a bit of their momentum. Maybe she should have done it in separate pots, maybe she should have started the stove sooner. She hated the wait so she added fire. A lot of fire. It burned the wooden pan handle and expensive polish dripped on the stove before simmering into dust of ash.\n\n- I don’t think this is how you boil the water, - Darius was starting to sweat from the heat, but he barely flinched. Pink Glasses layed alarmed on his wall seat. The fire died down and the ginger cat meowed in displeasure.\n\n- Told you it needed more heat, - Toham smile blinked at her show companion and found the vegetables happily boiling. It has a little bit of water, - Oh for crying out loud! - She sat the pan back on the stove and exhaled in defeat. She had to add more water for the soup. She could make mashed potatoes with carrots, but that was in episode 8. She couldn’t be this disrespectful to a founder.\n\n- Everything is alright?\n\n- Yeah, let me tell you the rest, as I slowly pour this into this pan, - Toham pathetically took a glass and filled it with water with cheers to round the motion up, - Yeah, long story, it would take an entire episode to explain. Tallie almost killed me on the day we met and no one knows where Floats comes from to this day. You know Fezzik, the engineer from one of the coal mines and everyone knows that sometimes top minds of all nine states are still cracking the code Carp says to this day.","n":272},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"Kara poured some water and watched mix in her boiling soup. A bit more, too much. The boiling came back to a simmer and Toham sighed, - Maybe cherry tomatoes?\n\nPink Glasses meowed.\n\n- Right, we don’t care, - so she started splitting little tomatoes into bubbling water.\n\nWhen a house becomes too crowded do you move or expand? Pros and cons. Can the local environment sustain you? Stay if yes, move if not. Split is always an option if you have more than two. Any less and division of a living beings would fail. With some exceptions. There always are some.\n\nFezzik explored the weapons more as a hobby. Man just enjoyed the essence of controlled explosions. All sorts of them besides the most lethal ones. Not a lot of fun observing something from a safe distance from a camera and a screen. That was the case right before Decem expanded tenfold. So much unclaimed land yet to be reshaped and open to any trial and error tomfoolery. Many veteran heroes were already clawing at the land, only Septum stayed within their Decem territory, formerly the entirety of Decem.\n\nHe didn’t know why none of his teammates saw the shift in the balance. They already lost three cycles. Not that the red man had any support with raising this new batch of fletchling heroes. Suddenly he felt bad for Ember and Cas. Almost forty years of failure.\n\nSeptum had to change the approach. Maybe Tallie could convince their giant monster of doom to change place from time to time instead of lying dormant. Fezzik felt a bit helpless getting through fletchling teams unable to beat the statistics.\n\nIt was numbing. He expected as much. Coliseum memories were brief, but all heroes started up as humans.\n\nToham disappeared in a puff of ashes and reappeared, surprisingly untouched by the black powder. She had a hands full of cherry tomatoes, almost ready to plop them all into the pan.","n":273},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Kara, have mercy, - Darius springed up to his feet and Toham almost flinched in fear, - At least cut them up.\n\nA meow from behind her shoulders seconded the suggestion.\n\n- Fine, but not because I like you, - Kara has always been petty. She mumbled on as the split tomatoes were plopping into the soup, - The fact is that our training consisted of multiple sub legal close calls.To be honest, most of the time we were running for our lives from the very get go.\n\n- Everyone knows that, - Darius narrowed his eyes, sending chills down her spine. The power of the founder heroes was exponential with no exceptions. All but one of 18 of them had a reason to have her head. Who knows what Kara would be doing with this much power, - Tell me something I don’t know.\n\n- We worked out as a team, I think that’s pretty much the key here. How exactly, I wouldn’t be able to tell you. Ask Fezzik if you see him, - Toham chuckled, realizing that the red man spent most of the time tinkering, rather than command, - But be mindful that his chaos control is ruthless, don’t stress him out too much.\n\nWhere was I?\n\nCamerarius was unphased. He missed Haderach, nothing could be worse than that. He never spoke of it, it was more painful to admit it. Remembering was forbidden, he had to pull on the smile and keep the pose. For Haderach. To become a perfect stature for his dear friend. To bring more to the table.\n\nThe powers were impeccably precise. There was nothing Camerarius couldn’t do. He just had to see it. Anything, everything. Things. Like a curse word. To achieve the unachievable by weaving your way through the fabric of loose reality. Never able to grasp what is most desired.\n\nHe was a hero. In the eyes of friends. So many friends. Everyone in fact. Yet, it almost always felt lonely. That’s why he liked Kara, it's much easier to be alone together. Except she had potatoes and he had nothing. And yet everything.","n":274},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"At a flick of a finger the outfits sewed themselves out of suddenly very plentiful stock. Materials of ridiculous quality and stitches so perfectly made that the fabric tension would align with the individual shape of the intended wearer. The amount of detail that would fry any machine attempting to recreate it. Everything. In moments. And then alone he was again.\n\nHe stood still, then slowly took a heroic pose. Then he became motionless. Just like the stature by the tavern. Where he always wanted to be. Practice makes perfect. Floats Camerarius is of perfect stature.\n\n- And so on and so forth, - Toham was getting carried away with chopping the sun dried tomatoes now. With basil, no soup can go wrong with basil, - It’s not really something you can teach methodically. I call it the dumbest of luck.\n\n- And yet here you are standing not so fletchling anymore, - Darius must have started smelling the mashed potato soup.\n\n- Hey, we are talking in time frames right now, - Toham laughed and threw him a sundried tomato, which he naturally caught. Heroes didn’t have to worry about these little things. There was no way Kara would throw food without an intention for it to be eaten, - Potato potato, I would never be able to understand a single thing that happened then either way. Decem Is different. It twists to resonate with you. Imagine what a conflict of founders could do to it.\n\n- System shatter, - Darius reluctantly sighed, relaxing in his chair, - I guess you cracked my code.\n\n- Please, it was barely a puzzle. I’m not that dumb, - Toham splashed some more water from the cup to turn the mash back into soup, - Every single one of you is alike to a flaw. No idea why you thought that you are original.","n":275},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"There was a heavy pause as the air filled with the heat of a thousand suns, relieved only by a thick cloud of freshly evaporated water from the pot. Toham could feel Eve raising an eyebrow at her words. Breaking news, Toham is in trouble.\n\n- Got you, didn’t I, - Toham threw sly side eye to Darius, suddenly feeling tempted to rip his flesh for the protein, - Let me top it over. Alphabet Noodles.\n\nAh, what a day. Bright is my way.\n\nCarp never was good at talking. It was complicated. What made sense to him never made sense to anyone else. It all was too simple. Heroes were immortal. Every single one of them. In every single scenario and outcome. Some just lose names or change forms.\n\nCause is void, Daisies amid.\n\nPeople, however, are not immortal. Heroes must find strength within to help people and sometimes this strength comes from ridiculous places. Carp knew this for a bit too long now. So long in fact that he forgot that it was important. Instead he embraced the hero Carp E. Diem. He lived by the word of day and swam the currents.\n\nEarth rotates North, Flag motes growth.\n\nAt this point it was nice to have some around, heroes always needed help. Carp could always blend the image back together with some splooshes and splashes. Like a throwback signature. There are some bright colors out there in the wild, but that doesn’t justify the spike in gardens during Easter. Even if it is the laying season.\n\nGuard the hay, Horse wants to play.\n\nAt some point he met the girl with quite a feisty temper. He liked that she understood him. He would jump into the fire for her. Like he would for anyone, if he wasn’t too hurt. Carp even remembered her name. How peculiar.\n\nI… I… Who am I? Why do I look this way? Why can’t I turn into a fish?","n":276},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- I can’t believe you actually saw real chimeras, - Darius was surprisingly calm. Toham was surprisingly cool herself. So many pleasant surprises, - I would never think that Quintus would ever dare to let the experiments get roaming on the loose.\n\n- You see, my thoughts exactly! But the thing is, the manticore wasn’t a fluke! We saw some real alternatives in Decem and, worst of all, Quintus heroes had nothing to do with it, - Kara was poking her chief's spoon into the monster of a concoction that was brewing right in front of her. By no means she suspected anyone. People were smart cheatures, maybe they figured out a way to catch up to heroes of their time. It wasn’t obvious why they would spend so much resources on such a particular thing. Kara didn’t care either way and always wondered why people thought that she did.\n\n- Well, who knows what is up in their Quintus heads, - chubby man sighed, suddenly defeated by the corner of the conversation, - So what exactly happened at the buzzer?\n\n- Ever heard of Schrodinger? - Toham laughed, catching a judgemental look from Darius, - Yeah, call it an experimental approach.\n\nThere was a deep silence as Kara slowly poured shredded cheese into the pot without breaking eye contact or dropping a ginger smile.\n\n- Please don’t.\n\nTallie was brought up with people shushing her for mentioning her roots. It was dangerous, frowned upon and she was quickly taught why. It was a routine, but it made her defensive. She grew up strong and dedicated her life to a simple goal of proving her worth to their legacy. Even if she had only snippets of it left.\n\nIt is important for every hero to embrace their roots. However bizarre or ridiculous they might be. Tallie chose the right path, the path of the warrior. The path that rewrites the history behind its step. And it’s not without a flaw, but it suits her. It is what matters to a goblin.","n":277},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"She would never forget the fiery Kara the queen of potatoes. She carried them everywhere with her no matter what, like a treasure goblin.\n\n- Oh, Sal, - Tallie waved to a nervous dragon, - don’t be so dramatic, we all know how weak you are. Let’s not shake the village.\n\nThe dragon roared in displeasure and faded back into the ground. No play time sucked, but for some reason Kara didn’t want to fight it. Maybe for obvious reasons, it would suck to lose a dragon in such stupid situation.\n\n- What a great name, - Kara looked impressed, - Sorry, just had to go check it out. It grew so much!\n\n- Eh, Sally insists I say it fully, but it’s something ridiculous, Salsarone or something, - tailed goblin shrugged, - So who is the new guy again?\n\n- You know, I always thought it was weird, it's the simplest things that get the most attention over time. Sure, they make for terrible headlines. However those barely mean anything nowaday anyway, - Kara was mumbling on, trying to fill in the grave silence of her guest. The mood in the room changed with the smell of amalgamation with a surprisingly familiar smell.\n\n- I know it doesn’t really matter if you are trying to make the story a novel and radiant experience. You need bigger words for these. War, failure, revenge, biggest, record and other ridiculous things that happened. It spike's curiosity, it throws many likely reactions in the very unlikely spot and showcases their relative use. Makes the dream popular, creates the need to practice, - Toham was stirring the pot now just bored of counting minutes on pasta. Ten minutes. Maybe she’ll make them chewy.\n\n- So that’s what you do? Spice it up? - Darius was getting ready for the inevitable finale of the show. It didn’t seem that bad.","n":278},{"chapter":"Cat and Fish","text":"- Oh, I don’t care, - Kara laughed, almost dropping the spoon into what she thought was soup, - didn’t you see our condiment wall? I am not a monster, I don’t dictate how you should enjoy what you got.\n\n- Well that’s an extensive selection, - Darius stood up to see his options, but soon realized that preemptive choices could be a provocation to show core choices, - You don’t show this part in the show?\n\n- Sometimes we have to eat things we don’t like, ever tried dust as pepper? - Kara breathed out with a slight relief. It was nice to finally shut down the heat, - I once caught a mouse in the cellar, I must say, the slight hint of mold on its paws was quite refreshing, but I still wouldn’t go for seconds. Or maybe I would, life has a tendency to twist and turn.","n":279},{"chapter":"Epilogue","text":"Who knows what exactly happened at the buzzers eve, but Kara and Carp walked in on a repeating four beeps of the stopwatch ring. Alfonse wasn’t himself and Toham instinctively covered Carps eyes for a moment before the lights fixed on red. Carp has never seen her use these flames before. He scattered to hide outside.\n\nKara wasn’t about to wait for explanations. The room was splitting into myriads of shapes, it appears that Zellie and Brian somehow cornered Fezzik. In conversation, out of all things. They were all still sitting in their spots. Kara could believe it could have been out of their own stupidity, but Zellie was already shooting an arrow. What a quick reaction from someone simply just trying to get out of a scary situation.\n\nThe problem was that she could see two arrows, when there were three Septum targets in the room. Four if you count Floats that appeared right behind Kara. Six if she somehow knows about the potatoes. Or if they were dealing with godly ultimatum powers. Kara knew that there was indeed only one arrow. It was one of two.\n\nThere are millions of options, it really means a lot when someone dedicates all their energy to make one true. Kara hasn't killed a Quintus hero in her life and yet these idiots had all the reasons to believe she did. Quite gruesomely too, she always carried an ax with her. A seemingly and maniacally dull one too.\n\nToham was so frustrated that she didn’t even flinch. It was all back to normal in an instant. With a slight difference. Brian and Zellie were nowhere to be seen. Girl slowly took out the Quintus earpiece and stomped it on the ground.\n\n- What a pair of idiots, - Fezzik scrambled to his feet and feeling his chest with his hands looking for the arrow, - What on earth took you this long?","n":280},{"chapter":"Epilogue","text":"- Nothing was happening, I thought everyone was on board with the plan, - Kara was standing surprisingly unhinged, - I told you to give heads up to Cassius. Let me guess, Rein took no prisoners?\n\n- I guess you two really know each other, - Fezzik sighed in relief. It would have been another awkward conversation to have.\n\nFloats walked in chipperly unhinged, - Gounds are complete! Where did Brian go?\n\nIt was subtle. Toham could have even imagined it, she did miss Zelli. Sadly, that wasn’t the tournament win condition. They still lost to a counter. Quintus had no idea until the bitter end. No one even thought about communicating with Cassius. What a ridiculous mole strategy. These Quintus heroes are operating, quite factually, blindfolded.\n\nMaybe it would have been less incriminating to attempt post factum negotiations to smooth out the incident, but when did ever Toham really think that there was a reason to go out of her way to give such fake promises. Waste of her time. Quintus showed no benefit to her interest. Cold, but they weren’t the ones to escalate the situation. They just got outmatched on basic principle, there was nothing Kara could do for them.\n\n- So what now? Do we like to dance or something? - Toham really wished Rein was around to make this feel like a step to a victory. A sticky comment or something to start the next movement.\n\n- Kara, this isn’t funny, - Fezzik looked grievously offended by the questions. Almost like he didn’t recognize what Kara has become. She didn’t blame him, it would be very disrespectful.\n\n- Sorry, I’ll stay closer next time, - Toham sighed, suddenly realizing that games are truly over. They can be attacked by anything at any moment for the next three months. With forty two individuals with equally dangerous powers. Potentially much more dangerous. Septum only had four heroes.","n":281},{"chapter":"Epilogue","text":"- No, I shouldn’t have stayed in that room outnumbered, I kind of blanked out there, - It was something new to hear from the red man, but Toham appreciated they were on the same page of self improvement.\n\n- Oh, what a shame, now all this effort is wasted for nothing, - Floats threw up the excess outfits and they disappeared into threads, - Just great.\n\nIt was rare to see Floats this kind of upset. Didn’t spell good for any of them.\n\nAnything that happened next could be described as a series of surprises followed by a fear of cats. All sorts of them. They made people secluded, delusional, following every whim of their fluffy buddies. Some were even caught talking to the ginger cats, reporting on their neighbors. And when you would see a flaring tail in town expect many people to burn.\n\nIf cats didn’t like you it was deemed to be a sign of early demise. Sometimes it was a bit too obvious. Some of them spread rabies or a ton of other things that animals carried. A reminiscent of a pandemic fear. That’s why there are not many strays in Septum. Not for any other particular reason.\n\nIt was a heroic miracle to finally have pet stores filled up with perfectly groomed cats. Painted ginger and then growing up into their natural colors, cats finally filled the empty homes of office towns. Pampered, loved, shushed too. None of them were allowed outside. Police were very strict about this rule. The Lord forbids a cat to catch a native bird.\n\nIt’s not like these ginger cat wannabees could catch anything in their lives. So fluffy and that without the actual fluff some of them rocked.\n\nDid Kara cause all of it? If you believe that everything in our universe is interconnected, then yes. But how can you argue that you are not equally responsible then? Kara only participated in a tournament show. Who wants to know what happened before it? Hop over to the Episode 22 to find out.","n":282},{"chapter":"Epilogue","text":"Don’t miss out on the finale! However not before the all round marathon of the Potato Show to get you all caught up on the action!\n\n- Hi Cas, - it was unnatural to see a tall man in a sophisticated suit appear out of thin air. Kara couldn’t help, but flinch. Victorian looked like just won the lottery. In his own way.\n\n- Alright, ground rules, - Cassius looked like he was in a great rush, - No more attempts at friendship with opponents.\n\n- Isn’t that a bit brutal? - Toham was still processing what exactly had happened. Did Zellie really hate her that much or did she just fail to find a loophole out of the system obstacle? What a funny thought.\n\n- How else did you expect to survive? - Victorian didn’t look like he was joking at all. Fezzik didn’t look chipper either.","n":283}]